\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for March 13, 2026\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Schifrin, Jonathan Capehart, David Brooks, Stephanie Sy,  Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,378 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2026\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Good evening. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: The war with Iran hits the two-week mark as all sides dig in and the death toll, including U.S. service members, rises.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The latest antisemitic attack, this time on a Michigan synagogue, highlights growing threats against the Jewish community.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the Trump administration sends pregnant migrant girls to a Texas facility, despite warnings that the shelter can`t provide adequate care.\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT, The California Newsroom and The Texas Newsroom: The trauma they`ve been through further complicates their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Around 2,500 U.S. Marines are reportedly heading for the Middle East along with an amphibious warship. Their mission is not yet clear, but it signals a marked increase in U.S. forces in the region.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The deployment comes as Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said more than 15,000 targets had been struck in Iran over nearly two weeks of relentless bombing against the regime; 13 American personnel have now been killed during the war and more than 2,000 are dead, both in Iran and from Israeli strikes in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Sy reports.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Mid-interview, a pro-regime demonstrator in Tehran interrupted. An airstrike sends smoke rising over a crowded square, but doesn`t disperse the masses. These Iranians are defiant, chanting familiar slogans, "Death to America, Death to Israel," setting fire to Israeli and American flags.\par \par \uc2 The protesters had gathered for an annual rally in solidarity with Palestinians when the midday strike hit, killing at least one person. Several top Iranian leaders, including the president, Masoud Pezeshkian, were in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Iran`s hard-line judiciary chief speaking to a reporter in the moment.\par \par \uc2 GHOLAM-HOSSEIN MOHSENI-EJEI, Head of Iranian Judiciary in Iran (through translator): We stand with the people until the last breath. People don`t fear these boom-boom sounds. Under the rain and the missiles, they will not back down at all.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: In Northern Israel, fear over cross-border retaliation. A home where a family once lived is now filled with shrapnel and debris, another Iranian missile fired by \uc2 Hezbollah in Lebanon\uc2 , this one leaving at least 58 people injured.\par \par \uc2 (SIRENS BLARING)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: In the early hours Friday, Iran targeted a U.S. air base in Southern Turkey. The ballistic missile was intercepted by \uc2 NATO\uc2  defenses, as the American death toll is rising.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Central Command said today six Americans were confirmed to cease when their aircraft was lost during Operation Epic Fury. It added: "The circumstances of the incident, which occurred in Western Iraq, are under investigation."\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said Iran`s -- quote -- "evil regime" is crumbling.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. Defense Secretary: They`re confused and we know it. Our response? We will keep pressing. We will keep pushing, keep advancing, no quarter, no mercy.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: No quarter suggests even those who surrender could be killed, a violation of international law.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, market-shaking concerns remain over the closure of the Strait of Hormuz, a critical international oil route.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH: The only thing prohibiting transit in the straits right now is Iran shooting at shipping. It is open for transit should Iran not do that.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: The U.S. is temporarily easing some of its sanctions on importing Russian oil in an effort to ease supplies. But, today, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , when meeting with French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , emphasized the U.S. sanction waivers won`t help end the war he`s fighting.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, Ukrainian President (through translator): The single easing of sanctions by America could provide Russia with approximately $10 billion for the war. This certainly does not help peace.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: German Chancellor \uc2 Friedrich Merz\uc2  today adding, it is a price problem, not a quantity problem.\par \par \uc2 FRIEDRICH MERZ\uc2 , German Chancellor (through translator): There was a very clear opinion from six members of the G7 that this is not the right signal. We then learned this morning that the American government has apparently decided otherwise. Again, we believe this is wrong.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Meanwhile, President Trump today addressed reports that Russia is aiding the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX News Anchor: Do you think Putin is helping them?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , President of the United States: I think he might be helping him a little bit, yes, I guess. And he probably thinks we`re helping Ukraine, right?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE: And you are, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Yes, we`re helping them also. And so he says that and China would say the same thing. You know, it`s like, hey, they do it and we do it, in all fairness.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: The comments followed a social media post from the president earlier today saying the Iranian regime has been -- quote -- "killing innocent people all over the world for 47 years. And now he is killing them. A great honor," he added.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. is now sending more aircraft into the Middle East, signaling the military campaign is far from over.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Stephanie Sy.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: In the day`s other headlines: The Justice Department charged a man identified as Kenya Chapman, who they believe sold the weapon used in yesterday`s shooting at Old Dominion University. The alleged gunman, Mohamed Bailor Jalloh, was barred from owning a firearm after pleading guilty back in 2016 to attempting to aid the Islamic State group.\par \par \uc2 He had been released from prison early after completing a drug treatment program. Authorities say the gun`s serial number was partially obliterated, making it hard to trace. The FBI says Jalloh shot and killed Lieutenant Colonel Brandon Shah and wounded two others. The gunman was subdued and killed by ROTC students, who are being hailed as heroes.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Washington, D.C., blocked a set of Justice Department subpoenas today related to its investigation of Fed Chair Jerome Powell. In his decision, Judge James Boasberg wrote that prosecutors presented essentially zero evidence of a crime, adding that a mountain of evidence suggests that the government served these subpoenas on the board to pressure its chair into voting for lower interest rates or resigning.\par \par \uc2 Last year, the U.S. attorney`s office issued subpoenas believed to be related to Powell`s testimony to Congress about the Fed`s renovation of its headquarters. He has denied any wrongdoing. Speaking at a news conference today, U.S. attorney Jeanine Pirro said she would appeal the ruling.\par \par \uc2 A range of weather extremes is hitting areas clear across the country, starting in the West, where it`s looking like summer in places like Los Angeles, even with one week technically left in winter. Angelenos have been flocking to the beaches and to Santa Monica Pier, with temperatures climbing to 90 degrees there.\par \par \uc2 Next week, we could see records in the triple digits from Southern California to Phoenix. Even those more accustomed to the heat say this is not normal.\par \par \uc2 DEE RICHARDSON, Los Angeles Resident: Especially in L.A., this is not typical. I`m from Texas originally, so I`m always appreciative of the heat, but this is definitely different from L.A. So, even for me, this is like, oh, this is kind of hot, right? Like, we`re sweating. Why are we sweating in early March? This is weird.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, from Washington state to the Great Lakes, more than 24 million Americans are under winter storm alerts, and a polar vortex will bring another wallop of winter for much of the Eastern half of the country, including areas like Washington, D.C., that just saw record heat a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 Cuba`s president confirmed today that his government has held talks with the Trump administration as a U.S. oil blockade further cripples the island nation`s economy. At a political meeting today, President Miguel Diaz-Canel said the talks were, as he put it, aimed at finding solutions through dialogue.\par \par \uc2 His comments confirmed prior us media reports that such discussions were taking place. Last week, Cuba was plunged into darkness as a blackout left millions of people without power. At a press conference broadcast on state TV, the Cuban president blamed that on the ongoing U.S. blockade.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL DIAZ-CANEL, Cuban President (through translator): We are generating electricity during daytime hours with domestic crude and our thermoelectric plants. The fault is not the government`s. The fault lies with the energy blockade that has been imposed on us.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Diaz-Canel also said a decision to release more than 50 prisoners in the coming days was not, as he said, imposed by another country, even though yesterday`s announcement was seen by some outsiders as an effort to win favor with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 There was new evidence today that the U.S. economy is showing signs of strain even before the start of the war with Iran. For one, the GDP grew at a slower pace at the end of last year than initially thought, growing just 0.7 percent in the fourth quarter.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the latest reading on core inflation came in at 3.1 percent. That`s well above the Fed`s 2 percent target. But there was a bit of good news from the labor market, with job openings rising more than expected in January.\par \par \uc2 That mixed economic picture only added to the downbeat mood on Wall Street. The Dow Jones industrial average fell nearly 120 points to end the week. The Nasdaq dropped more than 200 points. The S&P 500 also ended in negative territory.\par \par \uc2 And John Burns, an award-winning foreign correspondent for The New York Times, has died. Over a 40-year career, he reported on far-flung corners of the globe, from South Africa to the Soviet Union, China and the Middle East. Burns won the first of his two Pulitzer Prizes for his coverage of the Bosnian conflict in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 He was also a frequent guest here on the "News Hour," including this appearance in 2003 to discuss the Iraq War.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BURNS, The New York Times: Life under Saddam is a life lived constantly in terror, in fear, and consequence of which, for correspondents like myself, finding true voices in Iraq itself is not easy. It`s not impossible, but they have a tremendous yearning to tell their truth, and the truth is a terrible truth.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: John Burns died of pneumonia while under care in his native England. He was 81 years old.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": the wider war engulfs the border between Lebanon and Israel and the people living there; and David Brooks and Jonathan Capehart weigh in on the week`s news.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Law enforcement authorities in suburban Detroit say they`re still searching for a motive behind yesterday`s attack on a synagogue in school. FBI officials said tonight the suspect had large quantities of commercial-grade fireworks and several jugs of flammable liquid believed to be gasoline in the truck he drove into the building.\par \par \uc2 They also said he had no prior criminal history and that he died of a self- inflicted gunshot wound to the head. The FBI called it -- quote -- "a targeted act of violence against the Jewish community."\par \par \uc2 And it`s touched off a wider conversation around antisemitism in the U.S. A day after a man rammed his vehicle into a Michigan synagogue, the state`s governor was clear.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GRETCHEN WHITMER (D-MI): Yesterday`s attack was antisemitism. It was hate, plain and simple.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Governor Gretchen Whitmer with a message of support to the Jewish community in her state and nationwide.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GRETCHEN WHITMER: We must lower the rhetoric in this state and in this country, especially at this moment where we have seen such a rise in antisemitism and more attacks on the Jewish community.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Officials have not yet identified a motive, but new details have emerged about the attacker; 41-year-old Ayman Mohamad Ghazali was born in Lebanon. He came to the U.S. in 2011 on an immediate relative visa as the spouse of a U.S. citizen. He became a U.S. citizen himself in 2016.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports that an Israeli airstrike in Eastern Lebanon last week killed his two brothers, his niece and his nephew. Yesterday, armed with a rifle and explosives in the trunk, he drove his truck into the Temple Israel synagogue and preschool. None of the staff, teachers or over 100 children inside were injured.\par \par \uc2 One security officer was knocked unconscious by the car. Dozens more were treated for smoke inhalation.\par \par \uc2 Allison Jacobs sends her 18-month-old daughter to the temple`s day care.\par \par \uc2 ALLISON JACOBS, Mother: There are no words. I was in complete another shock.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Cassi Cohen was inside during the attack.\par \par \uc2 CASSI COHEN, Director of Strategic Development, Temple Israel: I heard a loud crash, and I saw some debris from the car and knew that something was very wrong. I heard a bang, which was a shot, hid under my desk and stayed there until we got the all-clear from the SWAT team.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: This comes amid a rise in antisemitism worldwide and a recent string of related attacks. Three synagogues in Canada were sprayed with gunfire in recent weeks. In January, a man set fire to a synagogue in Jackson, Mississippi. And, in December, a gunman targeting Jews killed 15 people in Bondi Beach, Australia.\par \par \uc2 Since the 2018 attack on Pittsburgh`s Tree of Life Synagogue, more Jewish communities have been stepping up security. Temple Israel had just held an active shooter prevention training in January. An official said it was temple security officers who engaged and killed the attacker.\par \par \uc2 RABBI JOSHUA BENNETT, Temple Israel: We are just absolutely amazed at the heroism of our security team. We expect these things to happen. We just never want them to be real.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Today, the investigation continues. Tonight, congregants attending Shabbat services will file by a phalanx of officers standing sentry outside their temple.\par \par \uc2 For some further perspective now, we turn to Rabbi Steven Abraham, who leads Beth El Synagogue. That`s a congregation in Omaha, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Rabbi, welcome to the "News Hour." Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM, Beth El Synagogue: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Just give us a sense of what you`ve been hearing from people in your community, your congregants in these last 48 hours about what they`re feeling, what they`re worrying about right now.\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: I think at the moment, really, there`s a -- It`s both a mixed sense of relief and gratitude, the fact that there was, of course, the security guard in Michigan who was injured, but that everybody in the synagogue and the school was safe, and that was kind of where things ended.\par \par \uc2 But clearly there is a heightened sense of fear and being scared of what is going on in our country, both abroad and locally here at home. And so you have to think twice about certain actions you take, and being in your house of worship is a place that you`re supposed to be able to be welcomed and not have fears of what took place yesterday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I mean, tell us about that difficult balance, because I should quote you. Recently, you said, talking about this: "There`s a level of PTSD with the heightened awareness. We are synagogues. We`re houses of worship. We are not Fort Knox."\par \par \uc2 So how do you keep that sense that your congregation is safe, they know they can come and pray safely, but not lose the sense that it`s an open house of worship?\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: Yes, it`s a great question.\par \par \uc2 I think that the truth of the matter becomes is that synagogues, like churches and mosques, are there, houses of worship, right? They are supposed to be places of welcoming, right? That is what we -- that`s who we are. That`s what we stand for. And that`s -- that is us acting at our very best.\par \par \uc2 So the idea of being able to have security guards, to metal detectors, to security bollards outside, to all of these extra precautions, which of course are so necessary in the world that we live in, unfortunately, kind of go against the idea of being so welcoming.\par \par \uc2 And so whether it is to your opening talking about Australia, to the Tree of Life synagogue, to Poway, to Colleyville, to what happened yesterday in the Detroit suburbs, there really is this balance of how do you figure out how to be a house of worship, where your doors are supposed to be open, and yet everything that`s happening in the world is in some ways trying to make us close our doors and maybe even be closed-minded?\par \par \uc2 And I think that we have to fight back against that. These type of actions are to make us think twice, to make me think about the kippah, or the head covering that I wear on my head, when I go outside and whether that is acceptable to wear, to make yourself known to be Jewish. And I say this for other faith groups as well.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, you have to be proud. You can`t hide. That gives the person who is a terrorist the win in their ability to make us scared. And that`s not the way that we should live our lives as Jews or as any other religious tradition.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Have you at Beth El had to step up your security in recent years?\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: Certainly.\par \par \uc2 I mean, even pre-October 7, we had done different -- we had done a number of different things in regards to our security here and in the Omaha Jewish community. And then, after October 7, clearly, there was a number of things that we did to make sure that we were both safe, that our congregation was safe, that our staff on a daily basis was being able to be safe.\par \par \uc2 When you`re in a house of worship and you`re here to pray and to celebrate, or even to mourn, but the last thing you want is to be thinking about your own personal security in those places.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: You mentioned the October 7 Hamas terrorist attack on Israel and this sense that what happens overseas inevitably has ripple effects across the rest of the globe.\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I should say that there`s nothing that excuses the targeting of civilians or people in any house of worship or certainly not children, but I have heard that there has been a sense of heightened concern, heightened fear after the U.S. and Israel launched this most recent war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Did the launching of that war make you worry more about attacks here?\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: For sure.\par \par \uc2 I think that anybody who would answer that question and say that it didn`t make you think twice is simply either naive or they`re lying to sound -- for a better sound bite. But the reality is, of course. Things that happen overseas, we have seen affect what happens in this country.\par \par \uc2 I believe quite firmly that the Jewish community in many ways has always been the canary in the coal mine to be able to understand the sense of what is going on with any number of minority groups, both in the United States and abroad. And so, when this war kicked off two weeks ago at this point, certainly, there was a concern of what that would mean for us locally and nationally.\par \par \uc2 But the idea is that, as you stated so beautifully, nothing kind of justifies attacking a house of worship, a synagogue, a preschool. And so I think we have to live in the reality of the world that we live in, but people come to the synagogue, as I hope they go to churches and mosques, to pray aspirationally for what the world may look like one day, God willing.\par \par \uc2 So we have to be realists. It`s still shocking every time it happens, but we`re becoming a little bit numb. I think you mentioned the word PTSD. I think there is a level that the Jewish community, every time these things happen, it opens the wound and you don`t actually get to mourn the previous time this happened, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, as a rabbi, I have now -- I should be proficient at writing sermons, and I`m getting proficient at writing letters to my congregation trying to explain the unexplainable.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is Rabbi Steven Abraham from the Beth El Synagogue in Omaha, Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 Rabbi, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 RABBI STEVEN ABRAHAM: My pleasure. Thank you again.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: As the war with Iran continues to escalate, Northern Israel is yet again on the conflict`s front line, facing a barrage of missile and drone fire from Hezbollah in Lebanon and Iran.\par \par \uc2 Communities still recovering from the fighting that followed the October 7 attacks now face once again the constant danger and mental trauma that comes with war.\par \par \uc2 For this report, a "News Hour" team filmed in Israel`s north, and Nick Schifrin has the story.\par \par \uc2 (SIRENS BLARING)\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: In Northern Israel, six miles from the Lebanese border, the siren has become the soundtrack. Patients and their families head into the Galilee Medical Center in Nahariya. They have only 30 seconds before a possible Hezbollah missile strike, which is why the emergency room is inside a bunker.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Zvi Sheleg is the center`s deputy director and gives producer Karl Bostic a tour.\par \par \uc2 DR. ZVI SHELEG, Deputy Director, Galilee Medical Center: We`re going now to the underground hospitalization departments.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Below ground, there are tunnels wide enough for an ambulance, a six-bed trauma room, and overall 450 beds.\par \par \uc2 DR. ZVI SHELEG: Everything here is ready at all times for any missile attack, any drone attack.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: In fewer than two weeks, Israel says Hezbollah has fired at least 850 drones and missiles into Northern Israel. Hezbollah joined the war, breaking a 16-month-long tenuous cease-fire, leading Israel to bombard Beirut and targets throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 For the Galilee Medical Center, the war has meant moving the most vulnerable underground. This is the neonatal intensive care unit, full of premature babies kept alive in incubators and on ventilators. Haya Nakan`s (ph) 2-month-old girl Razal was born weighing about one pound.\par \par \uc2 DR. VERED FLEISHER-SHEFFER, Neonatal Unit Director, Galilee Medical Center: he smallest baby now. It`s 600 grams, but he was born 450 grams.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Dr. Vered Fleisher-Sheffer is the NICU director. She knows that in here, as in war, the line between life and death can be thin.\par \par \uc2 DR. VERED FLEISHER-SHEFFER: They don`t have name. When they are bigger, the parents give names. They don`t give names when the baby`s just born. It`s very challenging for the parents. It`s very challenging for all of us in Israel.\par \par \uc2 (SIRENS BLARING)\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: The challenge to Northern Israel is overseen here, the Council of Northern Israel Communities, with Security Chief Nadav Shoshani and Director Moshe Davidowitz.\par \par \uc2 MOSHE DAVIDOWITZ, Council of Northern Israel Communities: We want to live here in the Galilee with our children and our parents in a peaceful area.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Israel has extensive air defense, but just today Hezbollah rockets hit this home in the north. Many of these residents only recently returned to their communities after the 2024 cease-fire, but now the threat is once again constant. Parts of a Hezbollah drone lie in the regional council`s lobby.\par \par \uc2 MOSHE DAVIDOWITZ: The most difficult situation for our residents, I see it, is the mental health. It`s the trauma and the post-trauma that we see increase day by day. And because of that, we demand that the war will finish soon.\par \par \uc2 COL. NADAV SHOSHANI, IDF International Spokesperson: Right now, we will stay there for as long as necessary to protect our civilians.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But overlooking Lebanon, the Israeli military`s international spokesman, Colonel Nadav Shoshani, predicted the war will not end any time soon, even if the U.S. ends its war with Iran.\par \par \uc2 COL. NADAV SHOSHANI: The timeline here is as long, as there is a threat on our civilians, we`re going to defend our civilians. That`s the timeline against Hezbollah.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Along the border, there are political divides for how best to guarantee security. But the closer you get to the border, the more it feels personal.\par \par \uc2 We visited Kibbutz Matzuva, just over one mile from the border. Batya Safian was born in California, but has lived in this community for 37 years.\par \par \uc2 BATYA SAFIAN, Resident, Kibbutz Matzuva: We have gotten used to living with a lot of trauma on a daily basis. He hears the boom, he wants to run down to the shelter.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: They have a shelter, but this community is so close to the border there are no sirens. They have to respond to the impacts. She lives with her grandkids. The local kindergarten has an attached bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 BATYA SAFIAN: I don`t want my grandchildren in another 20 years to be dealing with what I`m dealing with right now.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And yet, with neighbor 48-year-old Noa Rotem, they try to keep up their spirits. Rotem has lived here for more than a decade. After October the 7th, she evacuated and returned a year-and-a-half ago. Now the threats that she fled from have returned.\par \par \uc2 NOA ROTEM, Resident, Kibbutz Matzuva: Sometimes, I`m jumping out of bed and I saw that I have been sleeping for 10 minutes or so. Your body is like -- the fight, flight, freeze mechanism is working 24/7.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Like in many Israeli kibbutzes there is a dual desire, personal security and regional peace that`s currently out of reach.\par \par \uc2 NOA ROTEM: It`s not our reality and it`s not the reality of the kids in Lebanon or in Gaza or in Iran. No one wants I think to live in such fear.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But despite the fear, life and its rituals must go on, even if back in the hospital in a war zone. Elihu Cohen is a cleaner at Galilee Medical Center. He`s also a newlywed. But when the war started, his venue canceled. So he got married in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 ELIHU COHEN, Galilee Medical Center (through translator): There was no other choice. We got married here and everything was fine. It was very, very fun. It was nicer than doing it in a wedding hall.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And as the hospital marked a milestone of life, another life is about to begin. We met Valentina (ph) and Michael Mission (ph) just before she went into labor, hoping to give birth in a world where bomb shelters are not needed.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Do you know, is it going to be a boy or a girl?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MISSION, Father: Girl.\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA MISSION, Mother: Girl. Maria.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Oh, you already have a name for it?\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA MISSION: Yes, Maria.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And since we spoke to them, Valentina gave birth to a healthy Maria, a reminder that there is fortitude even in moments of fear.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour" I`m Nick Schifrin.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Since last July, the Trump administration has been sending all pregnant unaccompanied minors apprehended by immigration enforcement to a single group shelter in South Texas.\par \par \uc2 Some of the administration`s own child welfare officials object to this policy, saying the facility lacks specialized care the girls need.\par \par \uc2 I spoke yesterday to Mark Betancourt, a journalist who spent six months reporting to break this story for both The California Newsroom and The Texas Newsroom, which are collaborations of public media news outlets.\par \par \uc2 Mark, welcome. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT, The California Newsroom and The Texas Newsroom: Thanks for having me, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Let`s start with the girls. Tell us a little bit about who we`re talking about. How old are they, where are they from, and how do they come into the U.S. government custody?\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: So, most of the time, what happens is that, when kids cross the border unaccompanied, which means they`re not with a parent or guardian, they are transferred from Customs and Border Protection to ORR care, which is the Office of Refugee Resettlement. It`s under the Department of Health and Human Services.\par \par \uc2 They basically then distribute them among shelters throughout the country to hold them until they can be released to their sponsors, which are often family members. So the goal is to actually hold them for as little time as possible so that they can then be released while the sponsors are vetted.\par \par \uc2 So when pregnant girls are brought in, these are kids who are under 18, so they`re all minors. They would normally be placed in places that have medical facilities nearby and trained staff who can handle what are often complicated pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what is normally supposed to happen under this process.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Some of the girls you reported are as young as 13. Is that right? Many of them have been assaulted, raped on the journey that they`re making to the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 So these girls are between 13 and 17. Our -- my source within ORR said that about half of them were probably pregnant as a result of rape. So that is obviously a big concern. These girls have been through pretty traumatic experiences. In some cases, they were raped on the journey or in their home countries before they came here, and that`s how they became pregnant.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of times, they`re also apprehended by ICE in the interior of the country and separated from their families. So, while we don`t know the exact situations of the girls who have been placed at the shelter now, that is definitely a concern. And the trauma they have been through further complicates their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So your reporting is based on conversations you had with a number of people who are currently officials inside this agency. Why did they decide to speak to you? What concerns did they share?\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: So they decided to speak to me because I think they were concerned that it`s not safe for these girls to be placed here.\par \par \uc2 Their primary concern was that they have heard from experts within ORR, the Office of Refugee Resettlement, that this shelter is not set up to handle the complicated pregnancies of a 13-year-old, and also that the region that the shelter is in the South Texas Rio Grande Valley is -- has limited health care, has limited obstetric care.\par \par \uc2 And so, especially in emergencies, it could be really difficult to get these kids the care that they need, depending on what could happen with their pregnancies. So the ORR officials who spoke to me were really concerned, feeling like they needed to speak up, given that there didn`t seem to be a response from leadership within the organization.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We did reach out to HHS, which oversees ORR, the agency responsible here, to ask them about the report and request comment. A couple of key points from their statement.\par \par \uc2 They say: "To be clear, we do not send all pregnant unaccompanied minors there" to this facility. "There are others at other facilities as well." And they also say that they make those placements based on child welfare best practices. They say: "Each child is placed in a setting that meets their medical, developmental, and safety needs."\par \par \uc2 Does that line up with your sources and your reporting?\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: It doesn`t. That`s not at all what my sources within ORR said.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they made a point of saying that ORR`s mission is child welfare. It is designed to be first and foremost for the best interests of the child. And they said, this is the opposite of -- this is the opposite of what you would do if that was your mission. You wouldn`t send them to somewhere they couldn`t get the right care, especially if people within the organization were telling you not to do this.\par \par \uc2 But to their point of what they`re saying about the fact that not all girls are being sent there, my understanding is that the directive is that all the new intakes, girls who are entering ORR care for the first time, as long as ORR knows that they`re pregnant, they are still being sent there. That directive still stands, according to people within ORR.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There`s another aspect of this you include in your reporting. You spoke with advocates who say, this is a deliberate policy to shift them to Texas specifically to avoid access to abortion care or abortion services if the girls wanted them.\par \par \uc2 Why did they think that and did you find proof of that?\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: So we couldn`t find sort of direct evidence, documentation of that having been the reason for the decision, but current and former ORR officials who know this system very, very well said they couldn`t think of any other reason.\par \par \uc2 Also, the first Trump administration tried to do exactly this, tried to prevent girls from being able to get abortion while they`re in ORR care. And so it seems to be just a continuation of that policy. This is exactly what some of the officials I talked to expected, given the first Trump administration`s behavior.\par \par \uc2 And now, of course, post-Dobbs, now that Roe v. Wade is no longer an obstruction to that, it is legally a lot more possible for them to restrict the access to abortion under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And folks can, of course, read your full reporting online.\par \par \uc2 Mark Betancourt for The California Newsroom and The Texas Newsroom, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARK BETANCOURT: Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: With the war in Iran intensifying, President Trump this week delivered conflicting messages on what -- when it could end. And attacks here in the U.S. prompted a wave of anti-Muslim and anti-immigrant rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 To discuss that more, we turn to the analysis of Brooks and Capehart. That`s "The Atlantic"`s David Brooks and Jonathan Capehart of MS NOW.\par \par \uc2 It`s always great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 So, Jonathan, on Iran, if the goal was to weaken Iran and stabilize the region, the early picture looks complicated. You have got a new supreme leader in Tehran, who is the son of the original supreme leader who was killed in the initial attack. You have got higher oil prices, a widening regional war, and more than a dozen U.S. troop casualties.\par \par \uc2 What, in your view, has been achieved so far?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: I don`t know. I really don`t know.\par \par \uc2 And I`m glad you mentioned the casualties, because I was going to do something that the secretary of defense refuses to do when he -- immediately when he gets before the microphones, and acknowledge right now the 13 killed in action, including the six who lost their lives when their refueling aircraft crashed in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 I am still trying to understand what the endgame is here. The president gives a lot of -- says a lot of happy talk about this will take a short period of time, and we had to do this because they were going to attack us.\par \par \uc2 But we have not heard a single coherent rationale since this war started, what is it, two weeks ago tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: David, how do you see it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS: Yes. Well, this was not a good week. I think things were achieved in the first week with the weakening of the regime and taking out some of the ballistic missiles and all that.\par \par \uc2 But this week, two big things happened not good for the U.S. The first is every intelligence agency on Earth seems to have concluded that the odds of regime change, the odds that the Iranian people have any opportunity to rise up any time soon are very unlikely. And so that means we`re in a war of containment.\par \par \uc2 It looks a little more like the Cold War, where whatever happens in this military kinetic phase, there`s going to be a long period we`re going to have to contain Iran. The second bad thing that happened was the effective closing of the Straits of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t know what went through Pete Hegseth`s head or Donald Trump`s head, whether they anticipated this move or not, but it surely is impossible that the U.S. military did not anticipate this, because we have been talking about this for 47 years, and closing the Straits of Hormuz has always been on the table.\par \par \uc2 The Iranians have been preparing for this for decades, and they have effective ability to do it, at least partially, so far, and we see what`s happened to oil prices. We see what`s happened to the world economy. And the problem is the U.S. has no really good options here.\par \par \uc2 Ending a naval blockade, getting rid of naval mines is just not an easy thing to do. Back in 1991, during the first George H.W. Bush, the war in Kuwait and Ukraine -- I mean, Iraq -- Iraq had roughly 900 mines, and it took us nearly two months to clear them. The pre-war estimates for Iranian mines were closer to 5,000, and then they have all these things that didn`t exist in 1991, like underwater drones.\par \par \uc2 So that`s a really long struggle to try to make the Straits of Hormuz open. But if the U.S. doesn`t do that, then Iran is the effective victor, because they can say we can pull the economic string that will always deter a further U.S. attack, we are the ones in charge here, and we won this war.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And it speaks to how a weaker adversary can still impose enormous costs.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, in the introduction here, we talked about the president`s conflicting messaging. He had previously said the war would last four to five weeks. Earlier this week, he said it`s -- quote -- "very complete, already won."\par \par \uc2 In an interview released today, he said it will be over -- and this is a quote -- "when I feel it, feel it in my bones."\par \par \uc2 Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: This is -- that -- when I read that comment, I immediately thought that this, what we`re in right now, this war with Iran, it is as unserious as it is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 David just talked about the danger that is involved here. And you would think that a president of the United States, with a capable Defense Department, would have thought all of these things through. If we go after Iran, if we take out the regime, what would they do in retaliation, and then how are we going to respond to that?\par \par \uc2 I don`t think it`s in anyone`s interest -- David was saying that the best we have done is maybe created a situation where Iran has to be contained. I don`t know. To me, that sounds like we, the world, are in a much more dangerous place than we were two weeks ago tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, David, one unusual feature of this conflict has been the White House`s messaging online. They have been posting these meme-style videos, these pop culture montages celebrating the U.S. strikes. There are some of them are playing out right now on the screen.\par \par \uc2 What`s your assessment of this? I mean, does this reflect modern political communication or is something here more troubling about how this war is being framed to the public by the White House?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS: You know, the White House has pastors come in. They have prayer breakfasts they go to. They talk a lot about Christianity and upholding Christian values.\par \par \uc2 At the core of Christianity is a belief in the dignity of each person, that each human being is made in the image of God. And that`s true of all humans, not just the ones you happen to like. And what`s happening here, in Lebanon and in Iran is death, is human death. And I don`t care who`s dying, whether it`s good guys or bad guys, innocents or supposed guilties. It`s death.\par \par \uc2 And the people who fought World War II, who led our conflict in World War II, whether it was Franklin Roosevelt down to George Marshall to Omar Bradley, they understood the seriousness of this, that killing human beings is not a video game. It`s not pixels on a screen.\par \par \uc2 And whatever you think of the war -- and I`m probably a little more hopeful than most -- the way this is being described is almost barbaric. There`s a great tradition of just war theory. Sometimes, wars are just, but they`re never good. They`re never anything but horrific. And to treat them otherwise is to insult the American people and to really be unnerving.\par \par \uc2 It should be unnerving to everybody to see this level of triviality.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, we saw two attacks here in the U.S. yesterday, a shooting at Old Dominion University being investigated as terrorism and a car ramming at a synagogue in Michigan. The president, President Trump, was asked about it.\par \par \uc2 He said the perpetrators were sick people. And then in an interview with Brian Kilmeade of FOX, he added this:\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, President of the United States: They`re sick people. And a lot of them were let in here. They shouldn`t have been let in. Others are just bad. They go bad. Something wrong. There`s something wrong there. So, genetics are not exactly -- they`re not exactly your genetic -- it`s one of those problems.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Genetics, he says.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: Genetics.\par \par \uc2 And no one should be surprised to hear that kind of language come from Donald Trump. He ran -- his second presidential campaign was an openly racist, xenophobic, white nationalist campaign. So the fact that he`s talking about genetics in this circumstance, it`s not surprising.\par \par \uc2 But, again, this is the president of the United States. If we ever hope to put a lid on some of the passions and the hatreds that are in this country, we look usually, we should, to the president to be the example, to step out and say, this isn`t who we are. We band together. People are hurting. Say all the comforting words to bind the country together.\par \par \uc2 And instead, he and so many of his supporters are out there rending the garment of our American society. It`s really -- it`s as troubling as it is angering.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, in the time that remains, I want to get to the SAVE Act, which you both know. It`s this bill that would require proof of citizenship to register to vote in federal elections. It would require stricter voter I.D. rules. Supporters say it`s about election integrity. Critics say it could make voting harder for millions of eligible Americans, to include Republicans.\par \par \uc2 David, for years, there`s been this theory in Republican politics that higher turnout benefits Democrats. Recent elections have proven that not to be the case. But how much of this is still rooted in that old political thinking?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS: OK.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is true now that higher turnout benefits the Republicans and lower turnout benefits the Democrats because they`re more the party of the college-educated who vote in low-turnout elections. But what the Republicans are doing, they`re playing on this pure electoral politics.\par \par \uc2 You might agree with the SAVE Act. You may disagree with the SAVE Act. It`s hugely popular. You take every group in American society and you get 70 or 80 percent approval. It sounds decent to people that, if you can have to hand over your driver`s license to get on an airplane, you should be able to have to hand it over to show you`re voting.\par \par \uc2 And there`s some truth to that. The problem is, it`s not a problem. The studies that have been done looking at how many times U.S. noncitizens -- or the times noncitizens voted in U.S. elections, it`s like fewer than 100 cases. in the last 25 years. This is not a problem.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that we are going to paralyze the Senate for a solution looking for a problem, and the idea, especially egregious, that we`re going to get rid of the filibuster, which to me is the only thing left that gives us a shred of hope of bipartisanship in the next few years, that just seems like a mistake.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: The idea that you could have a law that says it`s OK -- you can prove that you`re able to vote with, say, like a gun permit, but if you have a college I.D., you`re not allowed to vote, or handing over voter rolls to the Department of Homeland Security, for what purpose?\par \par \uc2 When I look at the SAVE Act, I look at it as an attempt by the president, who has made it very clear that he does not want Republicans to lose the 2026 midterms, and that is what this is all about.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Jonathan Capehart, David Brooks, our thanks to you both, as always.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: Thanks, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And we will be back shortly.\par \par \uc2 But, first, take a moment to hear from your local PBS station.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: It`s a chance to offer your support, which helps to keep programs like the "News Hour" on the air.\par \par \uc2 For those of you staying with us, we take a second look now at how art is being used to help veterans with post-traumatic stress.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Stephanie Sy is back with this encore report from Seattle for our ongoing coverage of the intersection of arts and health, part of our Canvas series.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: On a recent afternoon at the Seattle Opera, a group of military veterans prepares for an upcoming performance.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: OK. Not a bad review. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: This veterans choir is part of Path with Art, a Seattle nonprofit that offers a range of programming designed to promote public health.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Let`s go again.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: For Shanda De Anda, who served 24 years in the United States Air Force, the once a week ritual of singing with fellow service members has been healing.\par \par \uc2 MASTER SGT. SHANDA L. DE ANDA (RET.), U.S. Air Force: Trauma changes who you are as a person.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: De Anda served in combat roles in Iraq, Afghanistan, and Bosnia. But it wasn`t until she retired from the Air Force in 2019 that she began to process all that she`d seen on the battlefield. She was diagnosed with PTSD.\par \par \uc2 MASTER SGT. SHANDA L. DE ANDA (RET.): But I was like, I`m sure I don`t have that. I`m sure that`s not a problem for me. And then COVID hit, and I lost the ability of being really active and in the community. And so being alone with my thoughts became more a part of my life.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: She says, when even leaving her house made her terrified, her therapist referred her to Path with Art.\par \par \uc2 MASTER SGT. SHANDA L. DE ANDA (RET.): Joining the veterans choir, where I`d be surrounded by other veterans, is a way of kind of finding my voice again.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Randy Schoesler has also found a new voice in the veterans choir. He served in the Air Force for seven years until he says he was kicked out of the military in the early `80s for being gay.\par \par \uc2 2ND LT. RANDY SCHOESLER (RET.), U.S. Air Force: Randy Schoesler, I was very much in the closet, because, at the time, this was before don`t ask, don`t tell even.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 2ND LT. RANDY SCHOESLER (RET.): There was no defense. If you were found to be gay, it was instant discharge.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: What did you go through?\par \par \uc2 2ND LT. RANDY SCHOESLER (RET.): Well, first, it was the shock. Then I lost my job because my clearance was immediately ripped.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Schoesler struggles with depression. He`s been coming to Path with Art for six years.\par \par \uc2 2ND LT. RANDY SCHOESLER (RET.): I need community. I need camaraderie. I need to be able to express myself.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Path with Art has offered classes on everything from learning the basics of podcasting...\par \par \uc2 MAN: You both have nice big voices.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Really?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: ... to pottery, acting and fashion design. The classes are available to low-income adults and all veterans for free.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY JACOBSON, CEO, Path with Art: We have a mental health crisis. There`s no one magic pill that`s going to solve it all. And so the arts can play a role in helping fill that gap.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Holly Jacobson, the CEO of Path with Art, says since launching the veterans program in 2019, participation has skyrocketed.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY JACOBSON: It really did show that there was a need. So it`s now a quarter of our program, over 500 individuals last year.\par \par \uc2 SPC. DONNA BAKER (RET.), U.S. Army: This is supposed to be a wolf.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Here veterans are also employed as teachers. Artist Donna Baker teaches art classes using mixed media, including yarn.\par \par \uc2 SPC. DONNA BAKER (RET.): When I get to the point where I can`t handle things, I go to art and it just kind of -- it calms me down. It relaxes my mind. I feel at ease. I don`t feel anxious. I don`t feel like I have to battle the world.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Baker was stationed in Germany during the late `80s and early `90s. A survivor of sexual assault, she says she still struggles with PTSD from her time in the Army.\par \par \uc2 SPC. DONNA BAKER (RET.): In a lot of ways, I can totally connect. I can totally connect with maybe what they`re feeling, their angst, their pain, their suffering.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Feelings that other military sexual assault survivors like Chris Wisdom share. She joined the Army in the late `90s.\par \par \uc2 PRIVATE 1ST CLASS CHRIS WISDOM (RET.), U.S. Army: They were telling the females not to go out at night alone because there were a lot of attacks, that there were a lot of rapes. And it wasn`t by civilians coming on post. It was by your fellow soldiers.\par \par \uc2 And so the fire represents the anger and then the tears, the sorrow and sadness.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: More than two decades later, her artwork, including this mask, expressed what she went through.\par \par \uc2 So that mask, I named Explosive Emotions. I made that to represent when you try to suppress who you are and your feelings for so long with all that you have been through that eventually it`s just going to explode.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN MAGSAMEN, Author, "Your Brain on Art": The folks that have served our country so beautifully have come home and have experienced trauma in a way that many of us can`t understand.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Susan Magsamen is the co-author of "Your Brain on Art" and an assistant professor of neurology at Johns Hopkins University. Her extensive research has included looking at Path with Arts programs and has found they are effective at improving mental health outcomes.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN MAGSAMEN: And a lot of times, people talk about well why can`t you just get over something? And trauma is not like that. And so the way to get out, it turns out that these arts and aesthetic experiences can be incredibly valuable.\par \par \uc2 Something as simple as singing, which activates your parasympathetic nervous system, activates the vagus nerve, again connects you to each other, calms your physiology and makes you just feel better.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: For Shanda De Anda, it`s working.\par \par \uc2 MASTER SGT. SHANDA L. DE ANDA (RET.): For a long time, I was trying to get back to who that version of Shanda was. But she isn`t here anymore. And mourning that loss and then celebrating the opportunity of developing a new Shanda is something I think Path with Art was extremely instrumental in making that happen.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: For so many here, it`s a step away from a painful past toward a more hopeful future.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour" I`m Stephanie Sy in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Be sure to watch "Compass Points" this weekend. Nick Schifrin and his panel discuss the consequences of the war with Iran.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, on "Horizons," William Brangham speaks with experts about the next frontier for GLP-1s, now commonly used for weight loss, with promising new advances in treating substance abuse.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: And, Dr. Lembke, are you now using this as a tool for people who are struggling with addiction?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANNA LEMBKE, Stanford School of Medicine: So we are using GLP-1s in treatment refractory alcohol use disorder, that`s to say, alcohol addiction, in our patients where we have tried all the other existing interventions, including the on-label FDA-approved drugs for alcohol addiction, and patients still haven`t been able to get into recovery.\par \par \uc2 And it`s been interesting to see that we have some patients for whom the GLP-1s really are remarkably effective, and that is very, very exciting, because we haven`t had a new tool in our toolbox for a long time. On the other hand, I will say we have some patients in whom we try the GLP-1s and they just kind of don`t work, which is also not unexpected when you`re talking about the brain, which is incredibly complicated and still -- where we still only scratch the surface of you know what`s happening in the brain.\par \par \uc2 It`s not really a surprise that our medications are only going to work for some of the people some of the time. And that in our experience so far is true for GLP-1s as well.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 For all of us here at the "PBS News Hour," thanks for spending part of your evening with us. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2026 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020260313em3d0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 21, 2025\b0\par\par\uc2 Geoff Bennett, William Brangham, Stephanie Sy, Jonathan Capehart,  Liz Landers, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,570 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2025\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Good evening. I`m Amna Nawaz. Geoff Bennett is away.\par \par \uc2 On the "NewsHour" tonight: President Trump welcomes New York City Mayor- Elect Zohran Mamdani to the White House after vilifying him during the campaign.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration gives Ukraine a deadline to accept its peace plan that includes major territorial concessions to Russia.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, Ukrainian President (through translator): Right now, Ukraine may find itself facing a very tough choice, either the loss of our dignity or the risk of losing a key partner.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And one state`s efforts to protect vaccine access as the federal government casts doubt on their effectiveness and safety.\par \par \uc2 DR. SEAN O`LEARY, \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2 : We`re already seeing lots of measles, lots of pertussis. These are vaccine-preventable diseases that don`t need to happen.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the future Mayor of New York City Zohran Mamdani met today at the White House after months of trading insults. In an Oval Office meeting, they were far more civilized, with the president at points praising the mayor-elect, despite their political differences.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , President of the United States: I expect to be helping him, not hurting him, a big help, because I want New York City to be great.\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D), New York City Mayor-Elect: And what I really appreciate about the president is, the meeting that we had focused not on places of disagreement, which there are many, and also focused on the shared purpose that we have in serving New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Both said that they will look to work together to improve the nation`s largest city.\par \par \uc2 Our White House correspondent, Liz Landers, has been following today`s meeting and joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 So, Liz, what happened today in that Oval Office meeting and what was behind the change in tone that we saw, especially from President Trump?\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: Amna, today was quite the turn of events, especially after these two men have been trading barbs through the media really for months now, in the past, President Trump calling Mamdani a communist, Mamdani in turn calling the president a despot.\par \par \uc2 Today, a much calmer tone between those two men, indicating that they plan to work together in the future. Mamdani`s spokesperson had said before this meeting that this was a customary type of meeting, that the mayor of America`s largest city would go meet at the White House with the president and that they were hoping to focus on issues like public safety, economic security and affordability.\par \par \uc2 And it was really that last part that we heard over and over from these two men today, affordability. I just have to say, Amna, throughout this whole Oval Office meeting today, it was quite a different tune, especially from President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what he said earlier today.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I don`t care about affiliations or parties or anything else. I want to see -- if this city could be unbelievable, if he could be a spectacular success, I`d be very happy.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: And one of the most surprising moments in this Oval Office meeting that happened around 3:00 p.m. today was Mamdani, the mayor-elect of New York, got a question from a reporter about the fact that he has made comments likening President Trump to a fascist in the past.\par \par \uc2 Mamdani started to explain himself and President Trump stepped in from the Resolute Desk there saying: It`s OK,. You can just say yes. That`s easier. It`s easier than explaining.\par \par \uc2 So, the president almost sort of coming to Mamdani`s defense, trying to explain this for him, just a very unusual setup that we saw there today, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Some remarkable moments in that meeting today, Liz.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: But, based on your reporting, what can you share with us about what the meeting actually focused on?\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: This largely focused on this issue of affordability. And we have heard President Trump talk about this now in the last few weeks, I think, after those special elections at the beginning of the month, where a lot of voters voted in the ballot box and said that economic issues, affordability is their top issue right now.\par \par \uc2 We heard that repeatedly throughout this meeting, President Trump saying that he hopes to work with Mamdani about lowering the cost of rent in New York City, building additional housing units there as well. They also talked about getting \uc2 Con Edison\uc2  -- that`s the energy provider in New York City -- getting those prices down too, and Mamdani echoing this as well.\par \par \uc2 ZOHRAN MAMDANI (D), New York City Mayor-Elect: When we spoke to those voters who voted for President Trump, we heard them speak about cost of living. We focused on that same cost of living. And that`s where I am really looking forward to delivering for New Yorkers in partnership with the president on the affordability agenda.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: Now, President Trump, in the last few weeks, has threatened to withhold federal funding for New York City for projects like infrastructure projects, public safety, hospitals, et cetera. There`s all kinds of federal funding that goes to various cities.\par \par \uc2 Today, though, he seemed to back off that a little bit. He was asked about that threat in particular, and he said, look, I don`t want to send money to New York if it`s being used in ways that I don`t agree with, but he said that after today that he didn`t think that was going to happen, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Liz, as you reported, they both indicated they`d like to work together moving forward.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Any takeaways from the meeting about what that looks like?\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS: Well, they were asked if they would have future meetings, and President Trump said that he certainly hopes so.\par \par \uc2 Look, the president recognized that there are topics and areas that they will disagree on, but he even went so far today as to say, when asked by reporters, that he would feel comfortable living in Mamdani`s New York City under his leadership after this meeting today.\par \par \uc2 But, Amna, one more thing to add when we`re looking ahead to the midterm elections next year in 2026, it will be very difficult for Republicans to tie the entire Democratic Party to Zohran Mamdani`s policies when we see today that President Trump agreed with much of what Mamdani was talking about, and many of these policies, they seemed to find common ground on, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, that`s our White House correspondent, Liz Landers, leading our coverage tonight.\par \par \uc2 Liz, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the day`s other headlines: In an about-face, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  will again classify swastikas and nooses as hate symbols. That was the official stance for the past few years, until earlier this month, when officials issued a draft policy document calling those emblems potentially divisive.\par \par \uc2 That policy was due to take effect in December. But after news reports surfaced about the change, the Coast Guard issued a new memorandum, saying -- quote -- "These symbols reflect hateful and prohibited conduct that undermines unit cohesion." The latest Coast Guard policy appears to take effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 In North Dakota, abortion is once again illegal after the state Supreme Court failed to reach the required majority to find a ban on the practice unconstitutional. The state`s only abortion provider left in 2022 and then challenged North Dakota`s abortion laws that same year.\par \par \uc2 A legal saga ensued, with the Republican-led legislature revising the state`s laws to ban abortion outright. With today`s decision, North Dakota once again joins 12 other states with near-total abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a police officer was acquitted of all charges in the killing of Ta`Kiya Young, a pregnant Black woman.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We the jury, upon our oaths and law and evidence in this case, find the defendant not guilty of murder.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: A jury found Connor Grubb not guilty on charges of murder, involuntary manslaughter, and felonious assault in the death of the 21- year-old. Grubb and another officer had responded to a call in August of 2023 after Young was accused of shoplifting.\par \par \uc2 Bodycam footage shows Young declining to come out of her car before it rolled toward Grubb and he fired. Ta`Kiya Young`s family denies she stole anything and plans to continue a civil suit against the local township and police chief.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas, in Nigeria, officials say gunmen kidnapped students and staff from a Catholic school this morning in the second such attack this week. The \uc2 Christian Association of Nigeria\uc2  says more than 200 schoolchildren and a dozen teachers were abducted in Niger State. It`s not clear who the attackers were.\par \par \uc2 But this follows a similar incident on Monday in which 25 girls were abducted. At least 1,500 students have been kidnapped in the region since 2014, when \uc2 Boko Haram\uc2  extremists kidnapped 276 schoolgirls.\par \par \uc2 An Indian fighter jet crashed during an air show in Dubai today, killing the pilot, who was the only person on board. Spectators captured the moment the plane plummeted to the ground and burst into a giant fireball. It happened on the last day of the largest aviation showcase in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 It`s the second known crash of the lightweight \uc2 Tejas\uc2  aircraft, which is built by a state-owned company and is a staple in India`s air force. Indian officials say they`re investigating the cause of the crash.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  convened his Security Cabinet today to discuss the recent spike of Israeli settler violence against Palestinians in the occupied West Bank. Netanyahu is facing pressure from the Trump administration amid concerns the attacks could jeopardize the fragile cease-fire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The meeting took place as Palestinians in the West Bank village of Huwara blamed settlers for setting a massive blaze overnight at a scrapyard. The owner of the site surveyed the damage today and said he`s committed to staying.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMAD DALAL, West Bank Resident (through translator): This car repair field is mine, and it`s my source of income to raise my kids. These settlers are causing destruction everywhere here, in our lands everywhere. Where can we go? We want to remain steadfast on our land no matter what.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Israel`s military says it had reports that Israelis did indeed set the fire and that police are investigating.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, the Department of Transportation released a new crash test dummy that more closely resembles the female body. The new dummy is aimed at making cars safer for women, who are 73 percent more likely to be injured in a head-on crash and 17 percent more likely to be killed.\par \par \uc2 The current standard crash test dummy was developed in 1978 and is modeled on a 5`9`` man weighing about 170 pounds. There is currently a female version, but it`s basically just a smaller version of the male one. Today`s update likely won`t be used in new car safety testing until 2027, at the earliest.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks swung higher to end an otherwise tumultuous week. The Dow Jones industrial average jumped nearly 500 points on the day. The Nasdaq rose almost 200 points. The S&P 500 also ended the week on a positive note.\par \par \uc2 And Mexico`s Fatima Bosch has been crowned Miss Universe 2025, a dramatic win in a competition marred by controversy. Bosch is the fourth Mexican woman to be crowned, but her victory was seen as vindication. Two weeks ago, she and other contestants walked out in protest after the competition`s co-owner berated her during a livestreamed event. He later apologized.\par \par \uc2 Mexico`s first woman president, \uc2 Claudia Sheinbaum\uc2 , praised Bosch`s victory back home, saying -- quote -- "Women are prettier when we speak and we participate."\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  and Matthew Continetti weigh in on the week`s political headlines; Representative James Clyburn discusses his new book on the first eight Black members of Congress; and an 80-year-old becomes the oldest woman to hike the entire Appalachian Trail.\par \par \uc2 President Trump today gave Ukraine a Thursday deadline to accept his 28- point peace plan to end the war that Russia started. Russia is cautiously welcoming the plan, which would require Ukraine to make a number of concessions it`s previously rejected.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Sy reports on the difficult decision Ukraine must now make.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Today, in Ukraine, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  addressed his nation as it feces an existential dilemma.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, Ukrainian President (through translator): Right now, Ukraine may find itself facing a very tough choice, either the loss of our dignity or the risk of losing a key partner, either the difficult 28 points or an extremely hard winter, the hardest yet and the dangers that follow.\par \par \uc2 A life without freedom, without dignity, without justice? They will expect an answer from us, though the truth is, I already gave that answer. Ukraine`s national interests must be respected.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Today, Russian President Putin praised the plan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , Russian President (through translator): We have this text and received it through existing channels of cooperation with the U.S. administration. I believe that it can also form the basis for a final peaceful settlement.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Vice President J.D. Vance and \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Secretary Daniel Driscoll discussed the U.S. proposal with Zelenskyy this morning. President Trump wants it signed by Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , President of the United States: I have had a lot of deadlines, but if things are working well, you tend to extend the deadlines. But Thursday is a -- we think it appropriate time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Later in the day, President Trump had this to say:\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : He will have to like it. And if he doesn`t like it, then they should just keep fighting, I guess.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Adding even more pressure, "PBS News Hour" has learned from senior European and U.S. officials that the U.S. is threatening to cut off the program that sells weapons to Ukraine and cease intelligence sharing if Ukraine rejects the administration`s proposal.\par \par \uc2 The 28-point plan presented to Kyiv includes longstanding Russian demands. Ukraine would have to limit the size of its military. \uc2 NATO\uc2  would not be allowed to send any troops into Ukraine. Ukraine would have to give up the portion of the Donetsk region that it still controls. That would give Russia control over the entire Donbass, which would be demilitarized.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. would recognize the Donbass, occupied Crimea, and the occupied portions of Zaporizhzhia and Kherson as de facto Russian. In addition, \uc2 NATO\uc2  would change its language to prevent Ukraine`s future membership. The U.S. would lift sanctions on Russia in stages and on a case-by-case basis.\par \par \uc2 All parties would receive full amnesty for their actions. And $100 billion of frozen Russian assets would be earmarked for Ukrainian reconstruction. Separately, a document details for the first time the Trump administration`s definition of a security guarantee.\par \par \uc2 The text, confirmed by a senior American and European officials, states that -- quote -- "The United States affirms that a significant, deliberate and sustained armed attack by the Russian Federation across the agreed armistice line into Ukrainian territory shall be regarded as an attack, threatening the peace and security of the transatlantic community, and may lead to an armed response."\par \par \uc2 European leaders today spoke to Zelenskyy via conference call and rallied around the wartime leader, with German Chancellor Merz writing, "Ukraine can count on us," and Polish President Tusk stating, "When it comes to peace, all the negotiations should include Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  also weighed in.\par \par \uc2 KEIR STARMER, British Prime Minister: But we have to work from where we are to the just and lasting peace. I know that`s what President Trump wants and he`s been working to that end, but we must reiterate the principle that matters about Ukraine must be determined by Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: The Europeans are working with Ukraine to come up with a revised plan.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Stephanie Sy.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: For more on this, we turn now to \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2 . He spent decades serving Republican administrations in the State and Defense Departments and on the national security staff. He`s now president emeritus at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  And author of the \uc2 Substack\uc2  Home & Away.\par \par \uc2 Richard, welcome back. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 , President Emeritus, Council on Foreign Relations: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So before we dive more deeply into this plan, let me just ask you about President Trump`s demand here that Ukraine accept this plan by next Thursday or risk losing weapon sales and intelligence sharing. Does a deadline ultimatum like that help move closer to a deal?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : It actually works against it because it raises questions about whether the United States is really on Ukraine`s side.\par \par \uc2 Let me give you a parallel. Imagine if President Trump had gone to Israeli Prime Minister Netanyahu and said, look, you have got a week to accept the Palestinian state. Here are the borders. You have got to withdraw from these settlements. You have to accept the sharing of Jerusalem, and so many Palestinian refugees have to come back to the Palestinian state. And if you don`t agree to that, we`re going to cut off American aid for Israel.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the reaction to that would be incredibly negative and strong. That in some ways is what we`re seeing with Ukraine. The United States is making really big,fundamental, even existential demands and is giving Ukraine a kind of one-week take-or-leave-it often.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Existential demands, as you put it.\par \par \uc2 So, when you look at what Ukraine`s being asked to give up here versus what`s being asked of Russia, just on the territorial plans, as was just laid out, they`re being asked to concede land they still control, the entire Donbass-occupied Crimea and more being recognized as de facto Russian.\par \par \uc2 Is this a plan President Zelenskyy can sell to Ukrainians?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : I will be honest with you. I don`t think he can sell it. I also don`t think he should. I don`t think aggression should be rewarded. And, again, there will be tremendous pushback after 3.5 years of this phase of the war.\par \par \uc2 I think the dilemma for President Zelenskyy is how to say no without saying no,and if you get into what you might call the yes, buts. There`s something here to work with. We have concerns here. We have concerns there. And then the question is, how does this White House react to a situation where Russia might well accept the plan, because it`s quite tilted in its favor, and Ukraine has real problems with it?\par \par \uc2 And we will have to see how that plays out. But that`s likely to be where we are in a week.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: What about the language in which \uc2 NATO\uc2  is changing its language or asked to change its language to prevent future Ukraine membership, but then the security guarantee language that includes Article 5-like protection? Sort of unclear about when an armed response would be triggered.\par \par \uc2 Is that clear to you about where that line is being drawn?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : No, it is unclear, as you say. And that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 It`s a difference almost between a security assurance and a security guarantee. There`s a lot of wiggle room about the nature of a Russian attack that would trigger a response. And then there`s further questioning about what would be the extent or nature of the response.\par \par \uc2 And what`s so critical about that here, Amna, is, we`re putting -- this proposal would put all sorts of limits on Ukraine`s ability to take care of its own defense. Essentially, it would have to trust the United States to be there for it. But these are not ironclad assurances that are being extended.\par \par \uc2 So, again, for Ukraine, it would be an enormous risk, particularly -- and you remember this. In 1994, the United States, Britain and Russia gave Ukraine guarantees, and, as we obviously know now, they turned out to be worthless.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: What about the way the European leaders have responded here, stepping into show solidarity for Ukraine, come up with a revised plan? Could that plan replace this U.S.-led plan?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : It could, but that`s up to us, to the United States to accept it.\par \par \uc2 The danger for the Europeans is, what is it -- what are they prepared to do, what can they do if the United States basically says, sorry, we`re going to walk away from Ukraine? And there`s real questions about the ability of the Europeans to step up to the role of being Ukraine`s principal external security provider, but we could well be there.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: In the minute or so we have left, I want to pull from your years of experience.\par \par \uc2 When you step back and look at the way this process has played out, the way the U.S. has led this negotiation, what do you take away from that?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : It`s inconsistent with my experience.\par \par \uc2 Normally, you negotiate and you work out through consultations a plan with your friends, and then you present it to your adversaries. This was done the other way around. So you have both the process and the content working against Ukraine. Plus, there`s the added thing that a big motive for the administration seems to be to put Ukraine on the back burner and to focus on using this to bring about a new era of U.S.-Russian relations.\par \par \uc2 So, again, Ukraine is essentially losing out here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: \uc2 Richard Haass\uc2 , always good to speak with you. Thank you so much for making the time to join us.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAASS\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: This week, the CDC changed the language on its Web site to falsely suggest that vaccines may cause autism, even though there`s no scientific evidence to prove such a link.\par \par \uc2 Health and Human Services Secretary \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  said he personally directed that reversal. It`s just the latest in a series of changes to federal vaccine guidance and policy since Kennedy took office.\par \par \uc2 In anticipation of more changes to come, a number of Democratic-led states have taken steps of their own. They`re trying to protect access to vaccines and to push back on misinformation.\par \par \uc2 William Brangham reports from one of those states, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 DR. SOPHIA MEHARENA, Every Child Pediatrics: Good question.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: At Every Child Pediatrics just outside Denver, Natalie and Melissa Barba brave their way through flu and COVID shots.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s going to be OK, I promise. I promise. Relax.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Relax.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Their mom, Sandra, has been vaccinating her kids here for years. So when she hears misinformation swirling around vaccines, she knows where to turn.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA BARBA, Mother: I just come to my doctor and talk to her about it, like, do you feel this is true? And then she just tells me like a little bit of the statistics. And I feel more comfortable and I trust her.\par \par \uc2 DR. SOPHIA MEHARENA: So, over time, that difference can get worse.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Her pediatrician is Dr. Sophia Meharena. This practice serves lots of patients who are uninsured or on Medicaid. Recently, she`s noticed a shift in some of her patients` views about vaccines.\par \par \uc2 DR. SOPHIA MEHARENA: I had a patient come in for her six-month appointment, I think, and the mom decided that she didn`t want to vaccinate. She`d vaccinated all her older children. I have known this family for years.\par \par \uc2 And when I asked her why, she said: "I don`t know. I just don`t know. I`m worried."\par \par \uc2 "Well, what are you worried about? What can we talk about?"\par \par \uc2 And she really couldn`t define her fear.\par \par \uc2 So the COVID booster is a vaccine to help you decrease your chance of getting COVID.\par \par \uc2 It feels like there is a boulder to overcome of misinformation that has somehow rooted its way in our communities. And while we have an army of pediatric care providers, public health professionals, pharmacists, nurses, even families who are willing to go over that boulder and figure it out, you don`t know how big it is or how deep it is or how it`s rooted.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Part of the problem, Meharena says, is the federal government, where longtime vaccine skeptic \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  now leads the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In May, Secretary Kennedy said the CDC was no longer recommending COVID shots for healthy kids or pregnant women. In June, he fired all 17 members of the CDC`s vaccine advisory panel, known as ACIP. Several of his replacements on that panel have held anti-vaccine views. In August, Kennedy said the government was winding down $500 million in research contracts to develop new mRNA vaccines, like for bird flu and other viruses.\par \par \uc2 And now ACIP is reportedly considering changes to the childhood vaccine schedule. Amidst all of this, policymakers, doctors, and academics here in Colorado are trying to increase access to vaccines, as well as pushback on misinformation, including some that is coming straight from the federal government.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO): I think both in the same arms probably.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Colorado`s governor, Democrat \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , has been a key player in some of those efforts. He signed a law that allows the state to consider sources besides the CDC when setting requirements for the vaccine`s kids need to attend school.\par \par \uc2 Another new law ensures state-regulated insurance plans cover the cost of some vaccines, regardless of what the CDC does. And Colorado`s chief medical officer issued a standing order allowing pharmacists to administer COVID vaccines without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS: I think it`s really about trusted messengers. It`s about data and science. It`s also about ease of access. And that`s one of the reasons we try to make it as easy as possible.\par \par \uc2 We have got COVID flu vaccines at your local grocery store, your local pharmacy.So anything we can do to make it easier and get good information in front of people to make the best decisions for them and their families, that`s exactly what we`re trying to do.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: But Polis has also faced some criticism when it comes to vaccines. Last November, he praised Trump`s pick of RFK Jr. as health secretary.\par \par \uc2 Do you still stand by that? Do you still think that he is the right person at this moment?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS: Well, I don`t think he`s who the person I supported for president would have appointed, but he`s better than having a pharmaceutical executive in there like President Trump`s prior director of human services. I disagree to the extent that he casts dispersions on vaccines or does anything to put data in front of people that isn`t true or is misleading.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Polis had said he supported Kennedy`s nomination, in part because he -- quote -- "helped us defeat vaccine mandates in Colorado in 2019."\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS: I think mandates are counterproductive, because what you have here is, if somebody refuses, what does that mean, again? Their kid is not going to be able to go to school? Then you`re doubly penalizing that kid.\par \par \uc2 We want to welcome everybody. We don`t want to hold the kids responsible for the decisions, good or bad, of the parents.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: But like much of the country, since COVID, Colorado has seen rates decline slightly for childhood vaccines against measles, polio, and whooping cough. Last year, it ranked among the bottom 10 states for kindergartners who`d been given those vaccines.\par \par \uc2 DR. SEAN O`LEARY, \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2 : A lot of the gaps in coverage are not because parents are refusing. It`s because they don`t have really good access.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Dr. Sean O`Leary, is a pediatrician and professor at the University of Colorado School of Medicine. He also serves as a liaison from the \uc2 American Academy of Pediatrics\uc2  to that federal vaccine panel ACIP.\par \par \uc2 He says the way vaccine policy is being debated today is dangerously off course.\par \par \uc2 DR. SEAN O`LEARY: It is so far off the rails. What`s going on at these meetings is chaotic, and it is clear that these people should not be making decisions about the health care of Americans. It is just beyond the pale.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One spray in one side.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: O`Leary is also leading a statewide group of experts, providers, and community leaders called Colorado Chooses Vaccines. It`ll monitor changes coming from Washington, press for new policies at the state level, and launch statewide campaigns to bust vaccine myths.\par \par \uc2 DR. SEAN O`LEARY: We`re already seeing lots of measles, lots of pertussis. These are vaccine-preventable diseases that don`t need to happen. My fear is that we`re only going to see more and more of that.\par \par \uc2 DR. AVIVA WERTKIN, Children`s Health Defense: I don`t see RFK Jr. trying to stop vaccines. I just see him trying to improve their safety.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Aviva Wertkin is a naturopathic doctor near Denver. She co-founded the Colorado chapter of Children`s Health Defense, the anti- vaccine organization that RFK Jr. used to lead.\par \par \uc2 If the state of Colorado is saying, we are getting a lot of what we believe is to be bad information coming out of the administration and we want to do educational programs, increasing access to vaccines, because we believe, as most medical institutions in the country do, that vaccines are safe and helpful and save live, you think that that`s an ill-advised effort?\par \par \uc2 DR. AVIVA WERTKIN: I think it`s an unnecessary effort to some degree. I certainly believe a lot in public education and outreach, but it seems to me that this effort is being motivated by convenience over safety.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: There is a growing concern that people seem to share that what`s coming out of the administration will mean that they will have less access to vaccines that they want. Do you think that that`s a legitimate concern?\par \par \uc2 DR. AVIVA WERTKIN: It does seem to be an anticipatory, fear-based response. I can`t imagine at this point that the administration would eliminate access. So I understand if people have their perspective where they view RFK Jr. as anti-science and totally anti-vax, even though he`s vaccinated all of his children and he doesn`t seem to be blocking access.\par \par \uc2 I understand how you can look at something and develop a fear before something is actualized.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: But providers like Dr. Sophia Meharena argue some damage has already been done.\par \par \uc2 DR. SOPHIA MEHARENA: You can put all the vaccines on the shelf if you want. If you also have a sign next to them that says, I don`t know if these work well or these could cause you problems, the fact that they`re there creates a barrier to us giving information to our patients that`s good for their health.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: For the "PBS News Hour," I`m William Brangham in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, as President Trump plays nice with one Democrat in the Oval Office, he`s called six others traitors and said the social media video that they posted could be punishable by death.\par \par \uc2 For analysis of the week, we turn now to Capehart and Continetti. That is \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  of MS NOW and Matthew Continetti, Wall Street Journal opinion columnist and scholar at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . \uc2 David Brooks\uc2  is away this evening.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: Hey, Amna.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 : Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan, let`s jump into that meeting in the Oval Office, because President Trump went from calling Zohran Mamdani a communist, threatening to strip his citizenship, to complimenting him, stepping in to defend him in the Oval Office, as Liz reported earlier.\par \par \uc2 What happened?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Threatening also to send the National Guard in if he were to win the election.\par \par \uc2 It was the most extraordinary moment -- for me, the most extraordinary moment of the Trump presidency. I was wondering if a mayor like Mamdani would be the \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  of the day. And instead what we saw was an incredible lovefest. And by lovefest, I mean the president of the United States just heaping all sorts of compliments and dare I say love on Mamdani.\par \par \uc2 And I think there`s three things happening here. Game respects game. And so I think the president looked at how Mamdani ran his campaign, how many votes he got in the election, a million votes versus President Trump got 700-something-thousand votes when he ran in a presidential election in New York City.\par \par \uc2 You could tell that the president respects Mamdani as a result of that. And the third thing, the president at various moments in that meeting totally undercut Republican arguments against Mamdani, including Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , who`s going to run for governor of New York, who had been calling the mayor-elect a jihadist for months.\par \par \uc2 And when asked, the president said no.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : It`s extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Matt, what surprised you about -- what stood out to you? And, also, is it harder now for Republicans to go after Mamdani in the same way?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: I think if you had cut to ads tonight, yes, it would be hard.\par \par \uc2 But this relationship is only just beginning. I think over the course of the Trump era, we have discovered there`s a difference between meeting Trump and rally Trump or social media Trump. And in meeting Trump, if you`re not \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , typically, meetings go very well with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , especially -- and he wants to convey that he is a good host, he`s welcoming.\par \par \uc2 And so he wanted to convey that same atmosphere meeting Mamdani, much to the surprise of the Republicans. The other thing here is, they`re both outsiders. They`re both populists. In the Democratic world, in the Democratic socialist world, populism is expressed through Israel-Palestine and through economic issues.\par \par \uc2 In national populism, Republican populism, it`s expressed on the border, cultural types. But there is still this kind of fundament that connects them. And I think that`s why Trump was a little bit nicer than a lot of us expected.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: What about the affordability issue? I mean, our latest polling shows there is no more single important issue for Americans today.\par \par \uc2 When asked about what should be the president`s top priority, lowering prices, 57 percent said that should be -- the next closest issue was immigration at 16 percent. Jonathan, this is an issue that Mayor-Elect Mamdani has been laser-focused on.\par \par \uc2 Was that informing some of this meeting too?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Oh, absolutely. Absolutely. I mean, the president`s been talking about -- quote, unquote -- "the affordability issue," this new issue that`s out there.\par \par \uc2 No, that is why Democrats won elections up and down the ballot around the country, particularly governor of New Jersey, Virginia, and obviously Mayor-Elect Mamdani in New York City. And so, again, for the president to tie himself to Mr. Affordability, Zohran Mamdani, I think the mayor-elect got more out of this.\par \par \uc2 I`m sorry. The president got a whole lot more out of this meeting, I think from his point of view, than the mayor-elect did.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: I mean, affordability elected Mamdani, but it also elected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last year. Inflation was one of the number one issues that got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the popular vote for the first time in 20 years for a Republican.\par \par \uc2 The issue is, how are you going to get to affordability? And I think that`s where the two politicians diverge. Mamdani wants to have more government- controlled solutions, more price controls. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with that big asterisk of tariffs, is usually about deregulation, tax cuts, more supply.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have an experiment, which means reach that affordability,and quicker.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I also want to ask you about this week a remarkable video we saw from six Democratic lawmakers, all of whom have national security and defense backgrounds, urging military members to disobey unlawful orders. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS DELUZIO (D-PA): Right now, the threats to our Constitution aren`t just coming from abroad...\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): ... but from right here at home.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): Our laws are clear. You can refuse illegal orders.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELISSA SLOTKIN (D-MI): You can refuse illegal orders.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRIS DELUZIO: You must refuse illegal orders.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELISSA SLOTKIN: No one has to carry out orders that violate the law...\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRISSY HOULAHAN (D-PA): ... or our Constitution.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Now, President Trump called for them to be arrested. He called it seditious behavior from traitors punishable by death. White House Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt was asked if the president was calling for the execution of members of Congress. Here`s what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, White House Press Secretary: No. Let`s be clear about what the president is responding to. You have sitting members of the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  who conspired together to orchestrate a video message to members of the United States military, to active-duty service members, to members of the national security apparatus, encouraging them to defy the president`s lawful orders.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan, I asked Congressman Crow about this yesterday, who was featured in that video, what specifically they`re asking military members to do. He said it`s not about a specific order. Is it clear to you why they put this video out?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Yes, because they`re concerned about what the president could do, what the president might ask the military to do.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not a stretch when you see judges have issued rulings telling the president and administration to do things or not do things, and the administration openly defying the courts. So it`s not unreasonable for members of Congress who have served in the military to have concern about how that might reach its way into the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 And I`m glad you played that clip from the White House press secretary, because right there in her answer you see the disingenuousness. The members of that video, they said unlawful orders. And what the press secretary said, they`re asking them to defy lawful orders. That is not what the members of Congress were asking service members to do.\par \par \uc2 And one other thing. I was talking to Senator \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  yesterday. And he made the point, what those members of Congress were doing was nothing more than what`s in the training that service members get. They are just reiterating what they get in training. There`s nothing extraordinary about what they said.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I want to ask for your response to the video, but also to put to you that Senator Slotkin, who`s in that video, said she and other members of the video have gotten close to 1,000 threats since it was posted. Congressman Crow posted some of the threats he`s been receiving calling for his family to be killed. It`s chilling stuff.\par \par \uc2 But how dangerous is the president`s rhetoric on this?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: I think a lot of the rhetoric has been very dangerous. I think both sides here have been not covering themselves in glory.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand you`re calling the president a fascist, a dictator in training, someone who`s going to issue unlawful orders, even though they can`t name a single unlawful order that the president has given. And then, of course, you have the president himself calling this sedition, treason, punishable by death.\par \par \uc2 If we want to lower the temperature, it will take responsibility on every party. But what has struck me about this is how it`s a partisan split screen. Both parties are playing to their bases here. The Democrats are rallying behind these congressmen, and the Republicans almost uniformly are rallying behind President Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think that this is going to have a long-term effect politically on the president.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan, you want to respond?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : I sort of bristle at the idea that this is a both sides thing.\par \par \uc2 What we have here in terms of the threats, particularly against these members of Congress, it`s that you have the president of the United States retweeting or whatever you want to call it threats against members of Congress. He is the one who in language and in -- if you want to go back to January 6, in deeds who is fanning, fomenting the nastiness that is out there.\par \par \uc2 If we want to lower the temperature, if we want to change the way we do politics in the country, it has to start with the chief executive. It has to start with the president. And he has shown an unwillingness to do that.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: And it also has to be followed through by all of us, including people who are betraying him -- portraying him as an authoritarian who poses an existential threat to this country.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : There`s lots of evidence that it`s not unwarranted.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Let`s talk about another moment this week that brought about some of the challenges we have seen in terms of how the president interacts with members of the press specifically.\par \par \uc2 We saw him host the crown prince of Saudi Arabia, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , at the White House. He was asked about the 2018 killing of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  journalist Jamal Khashoggi and here`s what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , President of the United States: You`re mentioning somebody that was extremely controversial. A lot of people didn`t like that gentleman that you`re talking about. Whether you like him or didn`t like him, things happen.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan, as you know, it was ABC`s Mary Bruce who asked about Khashoggi. She later asked about \uc2 Jeffrey Epstein\uc2 . The president lashed out at her, called her a terrible person, a terrible reporter, threatened to revoke ABC`s license.\par \par \uc2 That followed him calling another women reporter, Catherine Lucey of Bloomberg, "Piggy" on Air Force One. What does all of this say to you right now about the president`s relationship with journalists and how he views the press?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Well, he views the press with the lowest of regard. He certainly has a problem with women journalists who ask him tough questions, who do their jobs of trying to hold the president of the United States account -- to account.\par \par \uc2 And he takes it personally. The idea that the president of the United States would talk about my former colleague at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  as he`s a controversial guy, basically insinuating that he deserved being murdered and chopped up with a bone saw in Turkey, the president of the United States used to be the person who stood for free press, for democracy, for the role of the press in upholding a democracy.\par \par \uc2 And yet there he has no problem undermining that across from someone like - - like MBS and certainly by berating a terrific -- the terrific journalist from ABC who asked very straightforward and pointed questions.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Matt, I`m going to give you the last word here in the minute or so we have left. What do you take away from this?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: My big takeaway is, we have heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  speak this way to reporters and to women reporters since the first Republican debate in August 2015 in Megyn Kelly, who was then challenging Trump as the FOX anchor. That`s not new.\par \par \uc2 What is new is Donald Trump`s political situation. And I think he understands that the economy, affordability, these bread-and-butter issues we have been talking about, do pose a real risk to the Republican majority next year. And I think that`s why you`re seeing a more elevated, angry Trump in some of these interactions with the press.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: You think he`s lashing out more because he`s feeling more pressure? Is that right?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: I think that`s -- I can only say what has changed. What has changed is that Donald Trump`s political situation has deteriorated since these off-year elections earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Matthew Continetti, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , great to have you both at the table. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Thanks, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Congressman James Clyburn has spent more than three decades in Congress. And in his new book, he turns his attention to the trailblazing Black men who were the first to walk those halls.\par \par \uc2 Geoff Bennett recently sat down with the congressmen to learn more.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: They were the first eight Black members of Congress elected during Reconstruction, when American democracy was being rebuilt after the Civil War. Their presence was revolutionary, their power was short-lived, and their stories were nearly erased.\par \par \uc2 In his new book, "The First Eight," Clyburn restores their legacy and draws a straight line from their struggle to today`s fight over voting rights, representation, and democracy itself.\par \par \uc2 Congressman James Clyburn joins us.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: You say that, when people would visit your office and see portraits of the first eight on your conference room wall, people were often surprised because they assumed that you were the first Black South Carolinian elected to Congress.\par \par \uc2 Was that the catalyst for writing this book?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: It was.\par \par \uc2 And I had just finished my -- or just released my memoir. And so one particular episode led me to open a conversation with my staff. And I said to them, my next book is going to be about those eight people. And I started writing it just to inform about their existence and what happened during their service.\par \par \uc2 And then the election of 2020 came. And the aftermath of that election led me to start over and rewrite the book, to me, a little more instructive, rather than just informative.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Reconstruction, to me, is among the most fascinating chapters of American history. For folks who are less familiar with that time period, what about that moment allowed these men to be elected and serve?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Well, when Reconstruction came after the Emancipation Proclamation of 18 -- or became effective, the second one became effective in 1863.\par \par \uc2 Well, we had to redo the government. In South Carolina, of course, they had to come up with a new Constitution, which was done in 1868. And because South Carolina had been at the center of the slave trade as it relates to the United States of America, South Carolina became the only state ever to be majority Black.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, when the new government came, two-thirds of the South Carolina House of Representatives was Black. And at one point in its history, we had five congresspeople. Four of them were black. And so these people shaped the state.\par \par \uc2 And no matter what people may say about it, this state had the leading economy, the second most productive economy in the country. And they did everything they possibly could to restore, I used the word throughout the book to redeem the South.\par \par \uc2 They themselves, I call them redeemers, because they call themselves. They wanted to redeem the South, the pre-Civil War days.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the book argues that we are in a third reconstruction. So when you look at current battles over voting access, over representation, over what can be taught in schools, what patterns do you see emerging? What forces that undermined Reconstruction back then do you see now?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Exactly the same things.\par \par \uc2 I say that one of the things I want people to get from this book is the power of one vote. The three most significant things that happened to the Blacks during this period were -- all three were decided by one single vote.\par \par \uc2 The impeachment of \uc2 Andrew Johnson\uc2 , he failed to be convicted in the Senate by one vote. The committee that recommended to the House of Representatives that they give the 20 disputed votes to Rutherford B. Hayes, who then was able to get elected president by one vote, 185 to 184 in the Electoral College, that committee, it was a 15-member committee, and they voted eight to seven, one vote, to give those votes to Rutherford B. Hayes\par \par \uc2 So Reconstruction came to an end by one vote. And then Jim Crow came about as a result of that 185 to 184. Jim Crow began by one vote. And so I hope people will get from this the power of a single vote. And if you see what is now taking place in this country, a Supreme Court that I think has run amuck, undoing the Civil Rights Act of `64, undoing the Voting Rights Act of `65 -- of course, the Supreme Court is doing more with this Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 It`s the Congress and the presidency that seem to be undoing the Civil Rights Act of `64.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We`re in this moment right now where President Trump is faced with sagging approval numbers. There`s all of this fallout surrounding the Epstein files. There`s public fatigue over the high cost of living.\par \par \uc2 Do Democrats, in your view, have real leverage in this moment? And, if so, how do you maintain it?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Well, you got to stay vigilant.\par \par \uc2 If you look at historically, Democrats seem to think that once you win an election, you sit back and wait on the results to come. That`s not the way you do it. Once you win an election, you build upon that win. You keep the wind, that W-I-N-D, into the sails.\par \par \uc2 And that is what it will take to sustain. Yes, I saw numbers just this morning that, on the generic polling, Democrats are up by 14 points. I have never seen it that high before.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: That was a PBS/\uc2 NPR\uc2 /Marist poll.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Yes, congratulations for...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Of being what I feel to be accurate.\par \par \uc2 But the fact of the matter is, how do you maintain that? If it can go up, it can go down. In fact, there`s the old saying, what goes up must come down. So it`s going to come down. So the question is whether or not we are going to prepare ourselves to keep the wind in our sails. We will just have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The book is "The First Eight: A Personal History of the Pioneering Black Congressmen Who Shaped a Nation."\par \par \uc2 Thanks for coming in. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN: Well, thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Each year, only about one out of every four hikers who set out to complete the Appalachian Trail reaches that goal.\par \par \uc2 A thru-hike, as it`s known, means traversing nearly 2,200 miles across 14 states in a single 12-month period. It`s a monumental feat of physical and emotional endurance, but it`s not just a journey for the young, as a retired schoolteacher from Michigan explains.\par \par \uc2 BETTY KELLENBERGER, Appalachian Trail Thru-Hiker: My name is Betty Kellenberger. I have just completed a through hike of the Appalachian Trail. And in doing so, because I`m 80, I set a record. I`m the oldest female to have completed the thru-hike.\par \par \uc2 I read about it in my little weekly reader in elementary school. And I just thought that would be wonderful, had that thought and kind of buried it, lived with it, but buried it. I had lived through COVID, and somewhere along the line, the idea of doing the \uc2 Appalachian Trail\uc2  reentered my vision.\par \par \uc2 And initially, I thought, oh, yes, right. And then I thought, well, how long do you think you have? Did I think I had another 10 years to think about it? How long? And so I just got busy and started training and got out there.\par \par \uc2 Early on, in Georgia, I tripped on a rock, face-planted, ended up with two Black eyes and a bloody nose. And one of my first thoughts was, I didn`t quit. At least I didn`t quit. So I got up and I continued hiking. And on that one -- that was in `22 -- I was able to get from Georgia to Harpers Ferry, which is considered halfway.\par \par \uc2 And then I had picked up a partner, a trail partner, and we flew to Maine and we were going to hike south. He fell coming down off of Katahdin, hurt himself. I went on then by myself, which probably wasn`t the brightest thing I have ever done.\par \par \uc2 But I had Lyme. I had gotten myself severely dehydrated. I had a concussion. And I met a couple of hikers who pointed that out to me. They realized I was in trouble and we got me into town. The town got me into the hospital and the doctor said: "No, you need to get off."\par \par \uc2 So then I went out last year, started at Harpers Ferry and headed south. And that particular time I ran into a little lady named Helene, the hurricane that just destroyed the South. And they didn`t want us right there in the midst of everything and making the mess worse, and then offered us a deal.\par \par \uc2 They said, if you get off now, when you come back in `25, your first step on the trail will be your step one, and you can bring with it your accumulated mileage. So I did. I came back this spring and finished up.\par \par \uc2 And once I got close -- people kept saying, how old are you? This might be a record. And I thought, I don`t think so. But then pretty soon it came back, yes, it was. It was going to establish a record, if I could live through Maine and get to Katahdin. Then it was just a whole new level of excitement.\par \par \uc2 I think we were made for movement. We were made to think. We were made as spiritual beings. It doesn`t mean they have to go out and hike the Appalachian Trail, but they have to move. Life is a journey. And we travel a lot of journeys.\par \par \uc2 And what we want to do as we`re traveling is not focus on the end, but focus on the journey itself. Because, once it ends, it`s over. And the only thing you have are your memories. Make use of the time that you have been given.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Man, I want to be more like Betty.\par \par \uc2 Well, be sure to tune into "Washington Week With The Atlantic" later tonight right here on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Jeffrey Goldberg and his panel discuss how and why President Trump lashed out in new ways this week.\par \par \uc2 And watch "PBS News Weekend" tomorrow for a look at rising tensions on the Israel-Lebanon border, even as the cease-fire with \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  nears the one- year mark.\par \par \uc2 And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team, thank you for joining us, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020251122elbl00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for January 31, 2025\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeffrey Brown, Leila Molana-Allen, Nick Schifrin, David Brooks,  Jonathan Capehart, Laura Barron-Lopez, John Yang, Geoff Bennett, Amna  Nawaz \par \uc2 9,160 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 January 2025\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: Officials stop most helicopters from flying near Washington`s National Airport while they search for clues into what caused the deadly collision with a passenger jet.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: A United Nations relief agency vows to keep providing aid to Gaza, despite an Israeli ban on the organization taking effect.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And Syria faces the daunting task of rebuilding after 13 years of civil war. How one revolutionary envisions his nation`s future.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA, Syrian Professor and Activist: I feel that I am a human being again. I am free again. All of us believe that we were born just now.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 The black box from the Army helicopter that collided with a passenger jet near Washington`s Reagan National Airport this week has been recovered.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Investigators hope to gather valuable data as they try to understand what led to the deadly midair crash.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 John Yang\uc2  reports, families were also briefed today by officials and met with the medical examiner.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG\uc2 : Police boats were back on the Potomac River today as divers searched for the remaining bodies of the 67 people who died in the midair collision of a passenger jet and an Army helicopter, both aircrafts still submerged in the icy waters.\par \par \uc2 And, today, the first major change since the crash. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  shut down most low-altitude helicopter flights near the busy Washington Reagan National Airport, one of the nation`s most congested airspaces shared until now by commercial jets and government helicopters.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigators began analyzing the passenger jet`s cockpit voice and flight data recorders, the so-called black boxes. On FOX News this morning, Defense secretary Pete Hegseth said the Pentagon is trying to find out if the crew was using night-vision goggles.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. Defense Secretary: The qualification was a night flight. They could have been used. You have a lot of ambient light, a lot of things happening around Reagan. There can be depth perception problems. Again, that`s speculation. Our investigation will tell us more.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG\uc2 : Several news outlets say that, at the time of the crash, one of the controllers in the airport`s tower was handling both planes and helicopters, tasks normally performed by two individuals. That practice is allowed, but typically later in the evening, when traffic is slower.\par \par \uc2 At the White House, reporters asked whether the flying public should be concerned about \uc2 FAA\uc2  staffing levels. In response, Press Secretary Karoline Leavitt doubled down on President Trump`s controversial comments yesterday about diversity and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 KAROLINE LEAVITT, White House Press Secretary: When you are flying on an airplane with your loved ones, which every one of us in this room has, do you pray that your plane lands safely and gets you to your destination or do you pray that the pilot has a certain skin color? I think we all know the answer to that question. And, as President Trump said yesterday, it`s common sense.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG\uc2 : More than 40 bodies of the crash`s 67 victims have been recovered. And we`re learning more about those victims.\par \par \uc2 Ian Epstein and Danasia Elder were the flight attendants on the passenger jet; 13-year-old figure skater Jinna Han and her mother, Jin, were among those returning from were among those returning from a skating camp. Civil rights attorney Kiah Duggins had been in Wichita for her mother`s surgery. This fall, she was to become a professor at Howard University Law School.\par \par \uc2 Michael Stovall was one of seven friends on Flight 5342 returning from a duck hunting trip. And Chief Warrant Officer Andrew Eaves and Staff Sergeant Ryan O`Hara, both husbands and fathers, were two of the three soldiers aboard the Black Hawk helicopter.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m \uc2 John Yang\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the crash is spurring many more questions about the safety of the U.S. aviation system and whether other changes might be needed.\par \par \uc2 For a pilot`s perspective, we`re joined tonight by Les Abend, a retired \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  777 captain. He`s also a contributing editor to "Flying" magazine.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 LES ABEND, Contributing Editor, "Flying": Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The altitude of this military helicopter is coming under scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is reporting today that the helicopter that collided with the passenger jet appears to have been flying too high and outside of its approved flight path at the time of the crash. Add to that President Trump posted on social media that the helicopter was flying -- quote -- "flying too high by a lot."\par \par \uc2 As we await the findings of this investigation based on your experience and your review of the new videos of the crash, what`s your best assessment of what transpired?\par \par \uc2 LES ABEND: Well, listen, everything is pure speculation at this point. None of us should be making conjectures or anything of that nature with reference to what`s happened.\par \par \uc2 The altitudes that this helicopter might have been flying, I think is being reported by data networks like FlightAware and so on and so forth. That`s using GPS altitude, which can be different than regular altitude. Indeed, it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 My gut feel when I first saw this accident was that these pilots of the Black Hawk might have identified the wrong aircraft and didn`t realize it until the last minute. Now, if they were using NVG goggles...\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Night-vision goggles.\par \par \uc2 LES ABEND: ... and you get -- night-vision goggles, correct. If you use those goggles, ambient light, especially coming from a landing light of another aircraft, can potentially blind you.\par \par \uc2 So that`s a possibility as well. But this is what the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is doing. We will find there are a number of contributing factors to this terrible, terrible tragedy.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has indefinitely shut down the low- altitude helicopter corridor that was in use at the time of the collision. In your view, is the airspace around Reagan National, is it too crowded? What other changes need to be made?\par \par \uc2 LES ABEND: Well, it`s crowded. And it`s been that way for decades. And we have managed to get through decades with no midair collisions of any consequence that have actually had fatalities.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, it`s busy, busy airspace. We have got military operations. We have got civilian operations. We have restrictive airspace. I think what we could do is, let`s take a look at what we have now. What is it that potentially caused this problem? And evaluate that first. Where can we make changes?\par \par \uc2 Is it possible that we could have more restrictions on what we call airplane and trail, in other words, the distance between the airplane ahead of us and the airplane behind us? Do we need to increase that spacing? It`s increased during weather operations because airplanes have to slow down and configure early when they don`t see the ground initially.\par \par \uc2 So it can be a complicated and challenging experience, but a lot of times there`s visual approaches which follow the Potomac River right to the particular runway, especially if they`re coming from the north.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: This also has raised questions about the air traffic controller shortage. Again, we don`t know to the degree that that played a role in this collision, but the shortage is so severe that you have many air traffic controllers working 10-hour shifts six days a week.\par \par \uc2 Big picture, what danger does that present, if at all, to the flying public?\par \par \uc2 LES ABEND: Yes, no doubt that this could be a potential issue, but it could be a potential issue in any facility.\par \par \uc2 This is a very busy facility. There`s some really, really sharp controllers that work for this particular facility. If indeed it`s a staff shortage, it could be related to this accident. Once again, we don`t know, but anybody working on a job that`s as intense and as stressful as this one is -- has the potential to be fatigued, just as pilots have the potential to be fatigued.\par \par \uc2 So there are regulations that restrict pilots and air traffic controllers on how long they can work, controllers, how long they can be on a counsel at one particular time. Are they stretching it and pushing it to the limit? It`s possible. We don`t know yet.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Les Abend, thanks again for joining us with your insights. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 President Trump today said he`s moving forward with his plans to slap tariffs on three major U.S. trading partners.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Speaking to reporters, he confirmed 25 percent tariffs on goods from Mexico and Canada and 10 percent on those coming from China, with many to go into effect as soon as Saturday. The president also said that tariffs on the \uc2 European Union\uc2  are to be expected.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , President of the United States: The tariffs are going to make us very rich and very strong and we`re going to treat other countries very fairly. But if you think about it, other countries charge us tariffs. We don`t charge them tariffs, and it`s about time that that changes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Our White House correspondent, Laura Barron-Lopez, has been following all this and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, what are the potential impacts of the tariffs? How sweeping are they?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: They`re very sweeping, Amna. And they`re unprecedented, according to the economists that we talked to, nothing like we have seen in our lifetimes in terms of the scope of the tariffs, especially being exacted on allies like Mexico and Canada.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke to \uc2 Erica York\uc2 , the V.P. of federal tax policy at the \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2 , and she said that these are going to be extremely disruptive because of the fact that these tariffs are on all goods on -- to Mexico and Canada. And she and other economists said that they could upend the supply chain relationships that the United States has and that ultimately this is going to have a lot of impact on the American consumer.\par \par \uc2 I should note that Trump also said there would be future tariffs on the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , and should note that some economists believe that targeted tariffs are good. President Biden kept in place a number of President Trump`s first-term tariffs.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Erica York\uc2  said that she and her team crunched the numbers at \uc2 Tax Foundation\uc2  and that this exact percentage of tariffs on Mexico, Canada and China could cost the average American household an extra $830 a year.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, Mr. Trump has said that he would do this as president. Now President Trump is doubling down on the threat to do this. What`s been the reaction to these threats?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  said that Canada will be forceful, but reasonable and have an immediate response that they`re prepared to implement.\par \par \uc2 And another Canadian official told the "News Hour" that they are in D.C. and prepared to negotiate with President Trump up until the last minute to avoid tariffs, but that they don`t see a single American official that`s willing to negotiate with them right now.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: While we have you, Laura, I need to ask about some news tonight. The AP is reporting that a top Department of Justice official has ordered the firing of some prosecutors who worked on January 6 rioter cases. There`s also AP reporting that a number of \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, including some who investigated the federal cases against President Trump, are at risk of being fired.\par \par \uc2 What do we know?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: What we know so far is that the Trump administration is expected to fire dozens of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents. This is according to multiple reports and also some reporting by our own Stephanie Sy.\par \par \uc2 But the president was asked about this today and he essentially said he didn`t -- wasn`t really aware of the potential of these firings. And he said that there are some very bad people over at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and that he thinks that this is a good idea, but it was, frankly, stunning, Amna, because he didn`t appear to be aware of these coming firings.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Laura Barron-Lopez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We start the day`s other headlines with the Trump administration`s efforts to freeze trillions of dollars in grants and loans.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, a judge in Rhode Island sided with nearly two dozen states that had requested a temporary restraining order to pause the measure. That comes after a judge in Washington had halted the plan, but only until Monday.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, a government memo called on agencies to freeze funding to make sure it`s in line with President Trump`s agenda. That sent shockwaves through the states, schools and organizations that rely on those funds. That memo was then rescinded, though the White House press secretary has insisted that a funding freeze is still in the works.\par \par \uc2 A senior Trump administration official traveled to Venezuela today to speak with President Nicolas Maduro about migrants. Venezuelan state TV showed the envoy for special missions \uc2 Richard Grenell\uc2  meeting with the authoritarian leader in Caracas. White House officials say he urged Maduro to take back deported migrants who`ve committed crimes in the U.S. and pushed for the release of several imprisoned Americans.\par \par \uc2 The meeting comes after -- weeks after Maduro was sworn in for a third term following last year`s highly disputed election. The U.S. and several other Western nations do not recognize his victory.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the Middle East, where Israeli officials have confirmed the names of three hostages set to be released tomorrow by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 . They include American Israeli Keith Siegel, French Israeli Ofer Calderon, and Yarden Bibas. The news that Bibas will be released has raised questions about the fate of his wife and two young sons also taken captive on October 7.\par \par \uc2 Hamas\uc2  said earlier in the war that they`d been killed in an Israeli airstrike. Israel has not verified that claim. In the meantime, Palestinian officials say that Israel has agreed to release 90 prisoners as part of the fourth such exchange between the two sides.\par \par \uc2 The United Nations says that Rwandan-backed rebels are expanding their presence in the Democratic Republic of Congo. The M23 group took the major Eastern city of Goma earlier this week and has advanced into South Kivu province. Congolese forces were able to repel and attack late last night, but the rebels have vowed to march to Congo`s capital of Kinshasa about 1,000 miles away.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the rebel-controlled Goma remains largely without water and electricity. The city has been a humanitarian hub for more than six million people in the region who have been displaced by the conflict.\par \par \uc2 KAVIRA ALINE, Congo Resident (through translator): We no longer had people to protect us when fighting broke out. I hope M23 have pity on us. May they bring us peace. What we are going through is not meant for children of God. We live like animals. We are suffering.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  also said today that it`s concerned about growing human rights violations stemming from the conflict. This includes reports of executions at the hands of the rebels, as well as sexual violence carried out by the Congolese government forces.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, a New York doctor was indicted today for allegedly prescribing an abortion pill online to a teenager in Louisiana, where the procedure is banned with few exceptions. A grand jury there charged Dr. Margaret Carpenter and her company with -- quote -- "criminal abortion by means of abortion-inducing drugs," a felony.\par \par \uc2 Louisiana classifies the abortion drugs mifepristone and misoprostol as controlled dangerous substances. This appears to be the first case of criminal charges against a doctor for sending abortion pills to another state since Roe v. Wade was overturned in 2022.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks ended lower amid concerns about President Trump`s tariffs. The Dow Jones industrial average lost more than 300 points on the day. The Nasdaq dropped more than 50 points. The S&P 500 also ended the week in negative territory.\par \par \uc2 And today marks the fifth anniversary of not one, but two moments that changed our world forever. First up, Brexit. At 11:00 p.m. London time on this day in 2020, Big Ben marked the moment when the U.K. officially left the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . Five years later, the economic, social and cultural shift is still playing out.\par \par \uc2 Also today:\par \par \uc2 ALEX AZAR, Former U.S. Health and Human Services Secretary: I have today declared that the coronavirus presents a public health emergency in the United States.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: It is five years since U.S. health officials declared a public health emergency over the newly reported coronavirus. COVID went on to shut down schools and offices nationwide and claim the lives of more than 1.2 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": \uc2 David Brooks\uc2  and \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  weigh in on the week`s political headlines; and musicians work to forge better relations between the U.S. and Cuba.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Today the U.N. Palestinian relief agency \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  said it remains operational, providing humanitarian aid in Gaza, as well as the occupied West Bank and East Jerusalem. That`s despite a ban that went into effect yesterday.\par \par \uc2 An Israeli law passed in October forbids \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  from operating on Israeli land and forbids contact with Israeli authorities.\par \par \uc2 Nick Schifrin has more.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  was established after the creation of the state of Israel as the primary \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency to serve the Palestinian population. In the decades since, it has come to provide some six million Palestinians in Gaza and the occupied territories not only humanitarian assistance, but education and health care.\par \par \uc2 Israel has argued that \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  staff helped commit the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terror attacks on October the 7th, 2023. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  has said nine \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  staff may have been involved and were fired.\par \par \uc2 To understand the status of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  and its future, we get two views, William Deere, director of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 `s Washington, D.C., office, and Assaf Orion, a retired brigadier general with the \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 , now a fellow with the Washington Institute for Near East Policy.\par \par \uc2 Thanks very much to both of you. Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Bill Deere, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 What is going on with \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 ? As we said earlier, there is an official ban that has gone in effect, but you`re still operating. So what`s its status?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DEERE, Washington Operations Director, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 : The first day of the ban, all \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  facilities in East Jerusalem and the West Bank remained open, save our field headquarters in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we had 400 medical consultations in East Jerusalem alone. is going to reopen again on Sunday. In Gaza, we are -- our international staff remain, and we continue to stay and deliver. We are a major part of the humanitarian assistance effort, particularly since the cease-fire.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: So, Assaf Orion, what should we make of that, in your opinion, that there is an official ban on this organization, yet it continues to operate?\par \par \uc2 BRIG GEN. ASSAF ORION (RET.), The Washington Institute: I think it is important to understand the Israeli view of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And to put it shortly, it`s a poisoned apple. The apple part is the services that we heard, education, food and health, which are a necessity and everybody recognizes the need to do so. The poisoned part is connected to negative aspects of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , the involvement and actually the repurposing and overtake of \uc2 Hamas\uc2  of parts of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  operations in Gaza, so involvement in terror, participation in the October 7 massacre, in hostage-taking.\par \par \uc2 We have a hostage who said I was held inside an \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  site.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: So, Bill Deere, take those arguments on. Israel says that \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  has been infiltrated by \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , and then we have seen things like tunnels that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has used underneath \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  schools.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DEERE: The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  nor \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  want \uc2 Hamas\uc2  members as part of the organization.\par \par \uc2 When Israel in January of last year brought us the initial names, the first thing that our commissioner general said was, this is a betrayal of the United Nations. This is a betrayal of the work that we do for Palestinian refugees.\par \par \uc2 As a result, we move swiftly and decisively, both us and the secretary- general. The secretary-general ordered his equivalent of an inspector general to look at this. The secretary-general also commissioned an independent outside review of \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 `s neutrality operations.\par \par \uc2 The record is clear. When we find, for example, a tunnel under one of our facilities -- and, let`s be honest, these tunnels are everywhere -- what we do is, we notify the Israeli government of what we found. We notify the United States and all the other major donors. We demarche the Palestinian - - or the de facto authority.\par \par \uc2 And then, frankly, at some personal risk, we inject cement into those tunnels and such.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: So, Assaf Orion, have the steps that \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  have taken been sufficient? And, again, does the fact that they are still operating, the Israeli government appears to be allowing them to operate, mean that the Israeli government in some ways needs \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BRIG GEN. ASSAF ORION (RET.): I think we need to address two issues, the tactical issues that have been mentioned here, of involvement or the use of \uc2 U.N.\uc2  sites for terror and military purposes.\par \par \uc2 The second pertains to the strategic aspects, when this agency, unlike the High Commissioner for Refugees, its sister agency in the \uc2 U.N.\uc2 , is not about finding long-term solutions for the Palestinian refugees. Its inheritance or hereditary status given to Palestinians just by the fact that their ancestors were among the refugees in `48 and `67.\par \par \uc2 No other refugee population is still around. There are some positive services which were mentioned, but it comes with a problematic load of strategic issues which actually impede the peace process or peacemaking. And, instead, it is perpetuating the right of return as an issue and inciting through its education system.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Bill Deere, is there an alternative to \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  that exists?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DEERE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  performs a unique function in the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  system. We are a direct service provider. We run, as Assaf noted, a health care network. We run an education system. We provide relief and social services.\par \par \uc2 Nick, every \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  commissioner general wants to be the last \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  commissioner general. Assaf is right. This was established as a temporary agency until there was a permanent solution. The member states of the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  across 75 years have failed to come to that permanent solution. In fact, \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  is by design built to be turned over to Palestinian institutions.\par \par \uc2 We -- it`s why we pay, for example, local comparator wages. We don`t want to be there forever. And, in fact,we see our role in Gaza as hopefully being a bridge to whatever emerges as the governance there.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Assaf Orion, I mean, all \uc2 U.N.\uc2  officials say you cannot replace \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRIG GEN. ASSAF ORION (RET.): Sure you can. Of course you can.\par \par \uc2 These services can be provided in the West Bank by the PA, in Jordan by the \uc2 Jordanian government\uc2 , in Lebanon by the Lebanese government, in Gaza, where, when there will be an alternative government, it will be this government. It can be alternative \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agencies like the food programs, health programs, or refugee programs, the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And the main thing that the -- that \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  should do to make it possible for one day to solve the problem is to stop perpetuating the problem by printing more and more refugee certificates for the sons, granddaughters, grandchildren of the original ones.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Assaf Orion, Bill Deere, thank you very much to you both.\par \par \uc2 BRIG GEN. ASSAF ORION (RET.): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM DEERE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: As Syria recovers from 13 years of civil war and decades under the brutal Assad regime, its people must now rebuild their nation.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: One of those facing that task is Saleh Hawa, a Syrian literature professor and revolutionary who the "News Hour" met in 2012 and has spoken with many times since.\par \par \uc2 Special correspondent Leila Molana-Allen spoke with him recently in the new free Syria.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: A free Syria for all its people.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, Saleh Hawa was an idealistic young father, teacher and protester, with high hopes for a free future for his country.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA, Syrian Professor and Activist: We`re singing, we`re chanting in the streets peacefully without carrying any weapons. Every day, the Assad regime forces, the security, the Mukhabarat forces kill people on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: What he saw in the 12 years since left him dejected and hopeless, his hometown of Haritan devastated by Russian airstrikes, and hundreds of his friends and colleagues killed and displaced.\par \par \uc2 This was once Saleh`s home, where he raised his three oldest children and dreamed of a bright future for them. Now his community, like thousands across the country, lies in pieces. The earthquake two years ago took much of what the warplanes and artillery shells hadn`t already destroyed.\par \par \uc2 Saleh still thinks much death and destruction could have been avoided if he and his friends were given the help they needed in the early days of the revolution.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: Nobody needs to blame them for being militants, because what they saw pushes everybody to be militant, when you see that all the people around you are bombed every day, and nobody is trying to help. We do not deny that we got good support from Western governments and Western organizations.\par \par \uc2 But everything they gave was food, some blanket, but what we needed at that time was some kind of weaponry. If the U.S. at that time had supported us militarily, we would have prevented Iran from smuggling weapons to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: Saleh, who once believed the world, and especially the U.S., would stand behind Syrian protesters, spoke multiple times to the "News Hour" as the civil war spiralled, telling us his country had been abandoned to horror.\par \par \uc2 But, he says, now is their chance to make amends. He fears, if there`s a power vacuum, Iran and Russia could come back to fill it.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: Particularly the West, particularly America, right now have, let me say, a very good chance to start a new page with the Syrian people, to atone for their sins with the Syrian people.\par \par \uc2 They let us down in the past. I believe that pro-Assad forces are still waiting, they are still weaponized, and lots of Iranians are still in Syria, by the way. This means that right now the West mustn`t lose this chance.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: After the sudden fall of the dictator who he says destroyed his country, now Saleh`s hope is back, rejoicing alongside millions of Syrians at home and displaced abroad who now hope to return.\par \par \uc2 But so many like him have been left homeless and penniless by this war, and his family is luckier than most. Since fleeing Haritan, they have been living in this small house in Azaz that the whole extended family shares. Rebuilding will be an almighty task in a country with an economy that`s in ruins too.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: Eighty percent of our houses had been destroyed by the Russian airplanes. I cannot rebuild my house because it needs thousands of dollars, and you know this is impossible nowadays because we do not have salaries.\par \par \uc2 The economic cycle right now has broken down. The economic situation needs to be reactivated, needs to be starting new businesses, starting factories, because we have lots of young men here are unemployed.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: When rebel-held Idlib faced extermination in 2020, Saleh begged then-President Trump for help.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: I`m calling for President Mr. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , please do something, please. I beg you.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: Now he has high hopes that a Trump presidency will be in Syria`s favor, mostly because of his firm stance against Iran.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: The nuclear agreement was under the Obama administration, and Mr. Trump stopped all that agreement. That`s why we as Syrians believe that, if we think of who is better for Syria in terms of the relationship with Iran, no, it`s -- definitely, it`s President Trump. Even if we are not his friends, at least he is an enemy of our enemy.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: Since 2016, Saleh has been teaching at what people in rebel-held territory called the University of Free Aleppo. A literature professor, now he hopes his future will be dominated by books, not war.\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: I feel that I am still in a dream. The tyrannical regime, the dictator whom we worked hard to topple down for 13 years right now is erased from Syria in 13 days. That was miraculous. I believe that this is the first time we as Syrian people and, personally, I feel that I am a human being again. I am free again.\par \par \uc2 All of us believe that we were born just now. We have been born maybe for the first time in our life. We can speak freely.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: There`s so much work to be done. But when and if you get there, what does life in a free Syria look like for you?\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: As revolutionaries, we don`t think about ourselves. We think about our society, about the benefits of our society, the welfare of our society.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we started the revolution, because we wanted to liberate our society, to open new horizons for the generations to come. Imagine, after 13 years of being chased from one place to the other, displaced outside my home and living under bombardment, waiting for the Russian planes to come and attack us, the ideas, the feelings, the emotions are crowded in our heads, and not knowing what to do, what to say, how to feel even.\par \par \uc2 So I cannot imagine how my future life will be.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: And how about for your kids? You grew up under the Assads. What do you want for them?\par \par \uc2 SALEH HAWA: At least we got rid of the Assad regime and we let them live freely now. They can say what they want. They can feel what they want. They can speak freely. Now they are not afraid of any oppression. They are not afraid to be detained anymore. And I believe that the future is ahead of them.\par \par \uc2 LEILA MOLANA-ALLEN: A future he never thought they would see. Now he hopes he and his countrymen can build the nation they promised.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Leila Molana-Allen in Aleppo, Syria.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Contentious confirmation hearings and confusing policy rollouts marked the second week of this Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 For insights into it all, we`re joined by Brooks and Capehart. That is New York Times columnist \uc2 David Brooks\uc2 , and \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , associate editor of \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART: Hey, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: A lot happened this week.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The week began, for those of you who are following along, with the introduction of this OMB memo, right, rescinding -- and, rather, saying that some federal funds and grants had to be frozen. It was rescinded within 48 hours of being issued after mass confusion about what exactly was impacted.\par \par \uc2 The week is ending with a lot of chaos and confusion around President Trump`s tariff threats, including against some of our allies, like Canada and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 David, this is the second week of the Trump administration. What are you taking away just from the scope of the way things have been messaged and rolled out this week?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : We have already hit peak incompetence.\par \par \uc2 So, if you say the Trump administration wants to defund DEI, fine, they ran on that. They can try to do that. You find the DEI programs or any programs you don`t like, and you target them and you say, we`re going to try to get rid of these programs.\par \par \uc2 But, instead, they have a two-page memo to eliminate, allegedly, 3 -- what they call $3 trillion in federal spending.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : This is like trying to cure acne with decapitation. Like, it`s just massive. And it threw the whole country, universities, nonprofits, government officials, into total chaos.\par \par \uc2 And so it seems like nobody had the sense to say, well, what would happen next if we threw out in two pages with no clarity what`s about to happen? Well, of course the world`s going to blow up at you. And, of course -- and this, I hope, is the pattern for the Trump administration. When they do something that`s hurts \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  standing, he pulls it back.\par \par \uc2 Then they end just today with tariffs. And G.K. Chesterton, the British writer, said that civilizations decline when they forget the obvious. And one of the obvious things is, tariffs are really bad for a country. In 1930, we had the Smoot-Hawley Tariff, we all learned this in high school, which turned a financial crisis into a global recession by cutting trade flows.\par \par \uc2 Now, this won`t be that bad, but it`ll have the effect of a financial crisis on Canada and on Mexico. All the things you want in an economy that are good get worse under tariffs. You get lower wages, you get lower productivity, you get lower supply chain flows.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, it`s another self-destructive policy. And so we have seen these two cases of just basic reasonable competence not being followed.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan, how do you look at it?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : We`re 11 days in, and I`m resisting calling this chaos or confusion, because what this really is, is, this is what he ran on, taking a wrecking ball to the government.\par \par \uc2 This is what happens when you believe that the government is completely woke, however they`re defining it. And so you put out a memo saying, we`re freezing funding. We`re doing all these things that will hurt people.\par \par \uc2 That press conference yesterday -- let`s just zero in on the press conference the president did yesterday, talking about a tragedy in the Potomac, 67 souls gone, and he gives lip service.\par \par \uc2 He does a -- fine, he does a moment of silence, and then spends the next 25 minutes saying some of the most racist things I have ever heard come out of the White House, come out of the White House Press Briefing Room, come out of the mouth of the president of the United States, scapegoating anyone who isn`t white, Christian, male, straight, for problems in the country, and then deflecting blame onto past presidents, Democratic presidents.\par \par \uc2 This is all -- he ran this way. He said he was going to do these things. And, right now, all we can do is sit back and pray that the wrecking ball doesn`t destroy us.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, he`s still continuing to pull his team together as well. We know a number of his key nominees had their confirmation hearings on Capitol Hill, including \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  for \uc2 HHS\uc2 , Kash Patel to lead the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  as the -- as DNI.\par \par \uc2 There were a couple of moments I want to get your take on, though, that seemed to reveal some Republican concerns about Gabbard and Kennedy in particular. Just take a listen to these.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Will you reassure mothers, unequivocally and without qualification, that the measles and hepatitis B vaccines do not cause autism?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., Health and Human Services Secretary Nominee: Senator, I am not going into the agency with any...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY: Well, that`s kind of a yes-or-no question, because -- so, if you`re -- because the data is there. And that`s kind of a yes or no. And I don`t mean to cut you off, but that really is a yes or no.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 .: If the data is there, I will absolutely do that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JERRY MORAN\uc2  (R-KS): I want to make certain that in no way does Russia get a pass in either your mind or your heart or in any policy recommendation you would make or not make.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. TULSI GABBARD (HI): Senator, I`m offended by the question, because my sole focus, commitment and responsibility is about our own nation.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Just as a reminder, the overwhelming data shows no link between vaccines and autism. And Gabbard has previously blamed the U.S. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  for Russia`s war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But, David, when you look at this and you hear some of those concerns, do you think these senators still back these nominees, despite those concerns?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Yes, what I heard in these two cases in particular was senators, Republican senators, saying to the nominees, that crazy thing you believe, you don`t really believe that, right? Would you please just make it easier for me to vote for you by telling me you don`t really believe that?\par \par \uc2 And, in both cases, the nominees said, man, I`m not going to tell you I believe it, but I`m not going to tell you I don`t believe it. And so they didn`t make it very easy on these Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 I actually think Tulsi Gabbard`s in a little harder place, because, A, there`s a much broader phalanx of Republicans who are skeptical about her. Second, the Intelligence Committee is a serious committee. That`s a committee that usually meets in private. So it`s not usually a committee filled with histrionic behavior. It`s filled with reasonable behavior.\par \par \uc2 And they really do know intelligence. And there are parts of the intelligence effort that she really is a threat to, a potential threat to. There`s something called 702 of the FISA thing...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : ... which is a way to -- we have to look at foreign terrorists and follow what they`re doing and sort of head off a terrorist act. And she wants to raise the bar allowing us to use that ability.\par \par \uc2 And so that is a serious threat to people who subsequently care about intel issues. And so I think she`s in a little more danger. Kennedy, it looks to me like he may go through it, unless you get somebody outside the committee, like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , come in and do something.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: But you think Republican senators may vote against \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 ? Is that what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : What do I know? But 60/40 they vote for her, which is low for -- by Republican standards, that`s pretty low odds for her.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Look, if -- one of the three could go down, the -- Kash Patel for \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which I don`t think, given the hearings, he might not -- he probably might get through.\par \par \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , she`s the one we have consistently talked about being the one on the edge, and then RFK Jr. But, look, I remember when Pete Hegseth was named as Donald Trump`s pick for defense secretary and immediately Senator \uc2 Joni Ernst\uc2  expressed concerns, valid concerns, and expressed them clearly.\par \par \uc2 He`s now secretary of defense and she voted for him. So I would love to be -- I`m always just sitting here saying...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We should note three Republicans did vote against him, right...\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: ... Collins, Murkowski, and McConnell.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Correct. And yet he is still secretary of defense.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Vice President Vance broke the tie.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 I say this often on this program. I hope I`m proven wrong that no one is going to vote against any of these people. But we will see.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Before we let you go, I need to ask you about the Democrats, because they are choosing really their direction forward tomorrow. They will vote for their DNC chair.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, you and your colleagues at MSNBC just hosted the forum for the candidates last night. What`s your takeaway? Does this party have a direction?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : The party has a direction.\par \par \uc2 Let me say this. I hesitated a bit. They are not unclear on what they stand for. What they are struggling with is, how do they convince the American people that they are where the American people said in the election they didn`t think they were, that somehow the Republicans are now the party of the working class and care about the American people and the Democrats are the ones who are in league with billionaires and the elites?\par \par \uc2 What I took away from that -- from the forum last night is that they are more concerned about misinformation, disinformation, getting the message out and how do they catch up with the incredible infrastructure that is there on the right.\par \par \uc2 And so one thing I noticed is that, as each candidate was talking about what they wanted to do or what they thought or the direction of the party, you saw heads nodding all over the stage. And so while there are eight people, maybe half of them are -- actually stand a chance. Maybe half of the half stand a chance. We will see.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats -- trust me, the Democrats aren`t in trouble.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I want to give David 30 seconds to respond here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Democrats are in big trouble.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Listen, their whole world view has been blown apart. They thought of themselves as the party of the marginalized. They`re now the party of the college-educated on the coast. They thought they could spend a lot of money, but it turns out that`s kind of inflationary.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t think a lot of Black and brown voters were going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Like, they should take a year off, my Democratic colleagues, take -- do some serious thinking, because a lot of your mental categories have to be reinvented and reimagined.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : They don`t have a year.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We will come back to talk about this some more.\par \par \uc2 David Brooks\uc2 , \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , always great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Thanks, Amna.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The future path of Cuba has been in the headlines lately. Can music be the gateway to closer relations between Americans and Cubans and help provide relief amid the economic downturn and isolation?\par \par \uc2 That`s the hope of a group of high-profile musicians who`ve been forging a bond between young students on and off the island.\par \par \uc2 Jeffrey Brown has the first of three reports from Havana for our Canvas coverage and Art in Action, exploring the intersection of art and democracy.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: On a rooftop under the stars in Havana, the sounds of a youthful New Orleans brass band, followed by those of Cuban teens, members of a group called Primera Linea. meaning First Line, named after the famous second line musical tradition in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 A battle of the bands? Not really. More a blast of joy and connections.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA HERNANDEZ, Musician (through translator): Every time we hear them, we learn about their music, because we like learning about that culture, and it helps us teach them about what we do so they can learn as well.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY JACKSON, Musician: They`re just so energetic, and they`re very passionate about it, and that`s what I really love. I like to see people who are passionate about what they do, doing what they love, like how I do.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: A connector in chief of this four-day gathering, Troy Andrews, better known as Trombone Shorty, a Grammy-winning international star who fuses jazz, punk and more.\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS, Musician: Some of these kids` music is life. When I come here, it`s the same thing as New Orleans. What they play is life here.\par \par \uc2 You can hear the culture. You can hear the struggle. You can hear the pain and the happiness through that music.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: We first met Andrews seven years ago in New Orleans to learn of how he got his nickname -- he started out as a child, a shorty, playing in the streets of the famed Treme neighborhood -- of his commitment to music and to his Trombone Shorty Foundation, an after-school program offering local students music and life lessons.\par \par \uc2 As a 12-year-old himself, he`d had the opportunity to come to Cuba as part of a cultural exchange program, a formative experience.\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: This place and the music has never left me since that day in 1998, since that trip. And my goal was to always be able to come back because it had that major of an impact on me at that young age, very impressionable age, that it never left me.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Five years ago, he began bringing student musicians from the foundation to Havana to play for and interact with students here. That`s now grown into an event called Getting Funky in Havana, an exchange in music and broader culture for the young musicians, for a group of American music lovers basking in the chance to visit Cuba, and for Cuban fans attending a series of outdoor concerts.\par \par \uc2 Participating all along the way, stars like legendary blues man Taj Mahal, soaking in the performances of the young Cubans, and later performing at an old Havana church, before joining a panel discussion that included the Funkadelic master himself George Clinton.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE CLINTON, Musician: And as a songwriter, you start employing all those different cultures and different tones and the way we converse with each other.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Also serving as one of the leaders of this gathering, the rising Cuban superstar Cimafunk, who is seemingly everywhere, performing with the young musicians, taking in a jam at a local school, even walking the runway in a fashion show.\par \par \uc2 He`s made his name mixing the sounds of Cuban salsa and American funk. And he sees this musical exchange as crucial for young people here.\par \par \uc2 CIMAFUNK, Musician: Imagine that you`re a kid in Cuba, for example, or in a country that doesn`t have a lot of resource, and you start to feel that after so many years without connection with all the musicians, with no -- nothing about connection, nothing to see some musicians from outside, and you can see how kids are like saying, like, yes, OK, we`re part of the world.\par \par \uc2 Even if we are here or we`re dealing with things, we`re part of the world, and we can do it, because people believe in us.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: In fact, any highs amid this gathering had to be balanced against the realities of Cuba today, a deeply depressed economy where electrical power shortages have crippled the country, and lines to get gas, money from the bank, and subsidized bread are the norm.\par \par \uc2 More than a million have left. Official data show a stunning 10 percent drop in population in recent years. Actual numbers are likely higher. And the one-party government is still led by the heirs of \uc2 Fidel Castro\uc2 , his image still prominently displayed around Havana.\par \par \uc2 Mass demonstrations in 2021 protesting the power and other shortages were quickly put down. There`s also the continuing whipsaw of U.S.-Cuban relations. I last visited 10 years ago, soon after President Obama restored diplomatic relations and relaxed strict rules that had kept most Americans from visiting the island.\par \par \uc2 The grand squares and streets of Old Havana were alive with tourists, spending much-needed dollars. Today, they`re still dancing, but the numbers are way off. Cuban officials say just two million tourists visited in 2024, down from some four million in 2019.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN LAEYRE, Shopkeeper (through translator): There`s not a lot of tourism. There`s less tourism with the problems that you know already exist. But we`re here. The problem is getting by and supporting oneself.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: The streets are quieter, the cruise ships mostly gone. Even the famous classic cars aren`t doing much cruising. Milton Telles has been driving for 15 years.\par \par \uc2 MILTON TELLES, Antique Car Driver: In comparison of the first time, when I start, it`s down. It`s in the floor.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: It`s in the floor?\par \par \uc2 MILTON TELLES: Yes, tourists, it`s not a lot of visit. The people complain. I hope it change.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Since President Trump reversed course on diplomatic ties and sanctions in his first term, strict travel restrictions have applied.\par \par \uc2 And the back-and-forth continued even in recent days, with outgoing President Biden ending Cuba`s designation as a state sponsor of terror, only to have incoming President Trump immediately restore it.\par \par \uc2 MILTON TELLES: Everybody is afraid of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can do in his presidential time, because the people is who`s suffering those political issues.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Against this backdrop, the cultural exchange program focuses on smaller, more manageable goals, including bringing new instruments to Cuban students at a leading music school on Havana`s outskirts.\par \par \uc2 Crumbling and outdated facilities didn`t diminish the enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: Some of the instruments are beat up and they`re making the best of it, but I`m no stranger to that. As they`re continuing to play and grow, every year we come back, those kids are phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: You can hear the difference?\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: Oh, I definitely hear the difference.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: New Orleans student musicians led a second line parade through the crowd, and players from both countries joined in to perform and dance inside and out.\par \par \uc2 Fifteen-year-old trombonist Daniela Hernandez lives nearby with her very proud extended family. She`s also a member of the Primera Linea band that performed for the visitors and locals alike.\par \par \uc2 DANIELA HERNANDEZ (through translator): When they listen to us, I want them to be like, wow, how delightful, and that they start dancing and enjoy what we do to the fullest, because in the end we do this so they can have fun with us.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Also in the band, 14-year-old percussionist Hollden Ortiz.\par \par \uc2 HOLLDEN ORTIZ, Musician (through translator): It has been my passion since I was small. I dream with the music. I dream with everything I can create when I have a few more years in. I dream of continuing with my music, with other bandmates, with being a great musician.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: That`s a universal dream, of course, and 17-year-old New Orleanian Jarnell Demesme brought along a personal dream of seeing the homeland of his father`s family. He was also getting a taste of being a role model.\par \par \uc2 JARNELL DEMESME, Musician: You can tell they love what they do. They love to play their horn. They love to learn. They love to interact with music with other people. It`s really inspiring, because, if they can look up to me like that at my age now, I can just imagine, even when I grow up, how people will look up to me then, and how I can give back to the community.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: That`s what it`s all about for Trombone Shorty as well, who could be found throughout interacting with young musicians, including them in super-jam performances at major Havana venues.\par \par \uc2 This kind of cultural exchange, what can it actually accomplish? I mean, we can all have a good time.\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: And we are.\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: But what does it accomplish, do you think, in the end?\par \par \uc2 TROY "TROMBONE SHORTY" ANDREWS: We have been able to have a great impact on some of the kids, and, plus, some of the kids that we brought here from New Orleans, they won`t forget this experience.\par \par \uc2 And hopefully the accomplishments that we`re trying to get out here is helping these kids go to be professional musicians, and they can come back and also have an impact on the next generation to do the same thing, and we can save lives through music.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Trombone Shorty says he doesn`t know what the future will bring for U.S.-Cuba relations or its impact on the work of his foundation, but he vows to return as often as he can.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Jeffrey Brown in Havana, Cuba.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Terrific story.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, be sure to watch "Washington Week With The Atlantic" tonight right here on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Moderator Jeffrey Goldberg and his panel discuss President Trump`s handling of the first real test of his second term.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team, thank you for joining us, and have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2025 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020250201el1v00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 14, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Schifrin, Sarah Varney, Lisa Desjardins, Laura Barron-Lopez,  Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,304 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  chooses anti- vaccine activist \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  to lead the nation`s health agency. We delve into the former presidential candidate turned Trump supporter`s record.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Republicans retain their majority in the House, giving the GOP full control of the federal government. What they could do with their newfound power.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And a trend that`s on the rise since the overturning Roe v. Wade, women being prosecuted for what they do while they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH, South Carolina Resident: This has caused so much strain on my family, on my kids. It`s affected so much. Me, mentally and emotionally, like, I will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, president-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to roll out nominees to his Cabinet. \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  is Mr. Trump`s pick for secretary of the \uc2 Health and Human Services Department\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Mr. Kennedy ran for president in 2024, first as a Democrat, then as an independent, before dropping out in August and endorsing then- candidate Trump. He`s also an anti-vaccine activist and has pushed several conspiracies about the COVID-19 virus, including that it was designed to target certain races.\par \par \uc2 To help us understand the impact of this nomination, we`re joined by our White House correspondent, Laura Barron-Lopez.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, if Kennedy is confirmed to lead \uc2 HHS\uc2 , what kind of power does that give him? And what has he said about some of the other agencies that would be under his charge?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: It`ll give him a lot of power, Amna.\par \par \uc2 He will be overseeing with -- being the secretary of \uc2 HHS\uc2 , he will be overseeing 13 agencies that are under \uc2 HHS\uc2 , including the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 , and the Food and Drug Administration. And \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  has said that he wants to fire experts at a number of these agencies.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT F. KENNEDY JR\uc2 ., Health and Human Services Secretary Nominee: In some categories, there are entire departments like the nutrition departments at \uc2 FDA\uc2  that are -- that have to go, that are not doing their job. They`re not protecting our kids.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: RFK Jr. also recently said that the Trump transition needs to act fast because they have to be ready with their own loyalists so that on January 21, 600 people are going to walk into offices at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  and 600 people are going to leave, meaning they`re going to be fired.\par \par \uc2 One source close to RFK Jr. told me that he also wants to focus on more standard priorities, like stricter food requirements in terms of food health safety and those health effects associated with ultra-processed foods.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  has also said a lot of -- spread a lot of conspiracy theories, things like baseless claims that vaccines cause autism. He has blamed school shootings on anti-depressant use. And he`s claimed that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is actively suppressing the use of raw milk.\par \par \uc2 Public health experts have warned that drinking raw milk can lead to illnesses.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So you have been talking to public health experts here. What kind of impact are they saying that Kennedy could have on Americans` health and also preparedness for another potential pandemic?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: RFK Jr. has spread a lot of conspiracies, like the ones I just talked about, Amna.\par \par \uc2 But he`s also said that COVID-19 was engineered so, that that way, Ashkenazi Jews and Chinese people would be immune to it.\par \par \uc2 And I spoke to Dr. \uc2 Georges Benjamin\uc2 , executive director of the \uc2 American Public Health Association\uc2 , and he said that RFK Jr.`s anti-vaccine rhetoric and the potential policies he might prioritize could have very dangerous ramifications.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 GEORGES BENJAMIN\uc2 , Executive Director, \uc2 American Public Health Association\uc2 : RFK Jr. has been on the record of saying there`s no safe and effective vaccine. And, because of that, people are not going to take vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Even if he was to change his tune and promote vaccines, no one will know what to believe. So RFK is not the person for this job. He doesn`t have the medical training, the skill of managing a large organization, and he is not a well-trusted individual. And because of that, we`re going to see more people get sick, and I`m really, really concerned that more people will die.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Dr. Benjamin said that a lot of what the federal government does is by influence. It`s not necessarily by regulation.\par \par \uc2 So when health guidances are put out or encouragement of an education of the public to receive vaccines, and so doctors like \uc2 Georges Benjamin\uc2 , as well as pediatrician Paul Offit, who I spoke to earlier this year, said that there are big dangerous ramifications when someone like RFK Jr. says that people shouldn`t take vaccines, ramifications where there is a downtick in the number of people and children who get polio and measles vaccines, which can lead to deaths, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, Laura, we have now seen a number of names put forward by president-elect Trump, people he wants to join his administration. Where does Kennedy`s nomination fit into those picks? Is there a pattern here?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Above all else, president-elect Trump is prioritizing loyalty. And sources close to the Trump transition told me that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised disruption to the status quo, that RFK Jr. is a very disruptive force, and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants D.C. to be scrambling.\par \par \uc2 So when you look across these, that is what he`s focused on.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Also today, president-elect Trump announced he`s nominating \uc2 Jay Clayton\uc2  as his pick for U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York. Tell us about him.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Clayton served as chair of the SEC, \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2 , during Donald Trump`s first administration.\par \par \uc2 And Clayton has no criminal law experience and he`s going to have to shift to work on that for what is known as the sovereign district, one of the most independent districts, with this nomination. His role is notable because this office handles a lot of high-profile cases, particularly federal crimes and corruption cases.\par \par \uc2 And so the big question with this nomination, Amna, is, does Clayton help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pursue what he campaigned on in terms of prosecuting his enemies? And then another announcement that we just got moments ago was that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has nominated Todd Blanche to be deputy attorney general, so the second highest ranking official at the Justice Department, Todd Blanche, who is Donald Trump`s personal attorney, who represented him for 18 months during the criminal hush money trial in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to responses to a lot of these nominations so far, Amna, we did have one that our Lisa Desjardins got from Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 , a doctor, in response to RFK Jr. He`s the chair of the Senate Health, Education, Labor Pensions Committee. And he said ultimately he`s open to RFK Jr.`s nomination and that he wants to learn more about it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s kind of what we`re hearing a decent amount from Senate Republicans, which is that they are open to a number of these nominations, despite the fact that many of these people appear to have very little experience in the roles that they may take.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Laura, if it speaks at all to the pace at which these names are coming out, we have also just gotten word the president-elect Trump has nominated former Congressman \uc2 Doug Collins\uc2  to be his VA secretary. The shape of this next administration is coming together.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Laura Barron-Lopez, always good to see you. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: House Republicans will maintain their hold on the Lower Chamber next year, giving Republicans a political trifecta in Washington, controlling both chambers of Congress and the presidency come January.\par \par \uc2 Our Lisa Desjardins has been following it all and joins us now from our PBS News super screen.\par \par \uc2 All right, Lisa, so Republicans retain control of the House. What are the margins looking like?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: OK, let`s talk about this.\par \par \uc2 We have -- just having some changes in the last day right now with the Associated Press calling the races it has. Republicans have 218, the exact amount they need for a majority, Democrats 209. There are eight races for Congress, for the House of Representatives left to be called.\par \par \uc2 And at this moment, the Associated Press math is zero change, the exact same distribution in the House right now, according to their calls. There are some races, of course, that are going to go, one in Iowa going to a recount. We`re still waiting for some vote counting in ranked-choice in two states as well.\par \par \uc2 But I want to look at it this way. These eight races that are left, right now, who`s leading in them? Four Democrats are leading in those eight races. And how about it? Four Republicans. So that gets us basically to the same exact pattern we have right now in the House.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s look at some of these races called just today, this race in Oregon. I`m talking about the Happy Valley up here. This is a Democratic flip, where Janelle Bynum, who is the co-owner of four McDonald`s restaurants, was able to flip this district from Lori Chavez-Deremer, very hard-fought race here. Democrats happy about this.\par \par \uc2 One place where Democrats are more concerned, however, and Republicans are happy is in Alaska, where Nick Begich, the grandson of former Senator, late Senator Begich, is ahead by four points. Now, this is a ranked-choice state, so they`re going to count now people`s second choice, those who didn`t vote for either candidate. How many folks is that? That`s 5 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 That means for \uc2 Mary Peltola\uc2  to hold on to her seat, she needs to win a huge percentage of that. That`s going to be hard to do.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa, I know you have been talking with lawmakers as they take in some of Donald Trump`s nominations, including that of \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  as attorney general. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Quite a lot to say here about \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . He is a controversial figure. He does have some allies in Congress, but he is someone who has rubbed a lot of people the wrong way. And the Senate, talking to them, has taken pause on this, especially with the news that their House investigation by the Ethics Committee has been hanging over.\par \par \uc2 And it does -- there is reporting that the House Ethics Committee was getting ready to perhaps reveal their report on him. Now, that report no longer is in play because of his resignation, which was official today. Now, I talked to some U.S. senators. They will decide whether \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  becomes the attorney general or not.\par \par \uc2 And among Republicans, there is divide over how much scrutiny he should get. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): My presumption is that, listen, he`s the leader of my party, Trump. He just won a resounding victory. I support him and his agenda. I think he should be able to choose his Cabinet.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): He`s a smart, clever guy. I usually vote for Cabinet picks of all presidents, but, in this case, hearings will be in order. He will have some hard questions to answer. We will see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: All right, so let`s talk about the confirmations. Anyone would need 50 votes. Republicans have 53. So they can spare three votes and still get a nomination.\par \par \uc2 But, listen, by my count, there are at least 10, maybe more Republicans with doubts about this candidate right now. Two senators, Ernst and Mullin, told me today they don`t think he has the votes right now.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We will see how this nomination progresses, but how could it play out?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: OK, first step is the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , where senators, all of them Republicans, told me today they do expect they will in fact want hearings with \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , no bypassing it.\par \par \uc2 We`re watching Senator Cornyn, Senator Tillis, perhaps Senator Cotton and Senator Graham in that committee. And also, because of the new majority, there will be a new member of this important committee not yet named. Also, we had a text or social media post from an attorney for -- representing a young woman who has made accusations against Gaetz.\par \par \uc2 And he is writing that they want this Ethics Committee report to be revealed immediately, the report that the House Ethics Committee had been working on for months.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, even though Gaetz has resigned, could the House Ethics Committee still release this report, Lisa?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Well, Democratic senators have written this letter just in the last couple of hours asking that the House release that report. The House Ethics Committee generally does not release reports after someone has resigned, but there is precedent.\par \par \uc2 So let`s answer a couple questions really quick here. Can they release the Gaetz report? Yes, they can. There is precedent for this. But they meet secretly. We don`t even know when they meet. Now, will they release a statement? Often, instead of a full report, they will release some kind of statement. So this is a maybe.\par \par \uc2 And then question now, we`re hearing from some senators that they may even want to subpoena the House of Representatives for this Ethics report about this important top prosecutor position. That is an unprecedented idea. As you can see, we`re in for a lot of questions and tricky times ahead on this nomination.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Desjardins, thanks, as always.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Now for the day`s other headlines, starting in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Syrian state media says that Israel carried out two airstrikes today, killing at least 15 people. The attacks happened in the capital city of Damascus and in one of its northwest suburbs. Israel has ramped up attacks on Iran-linked targets in Syria since the October 7 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks on Israel.\par \par \uc2 Israeli military officials claim they struck strongholds of the \uc2 Palestinian Islamic Jihad\uc2 , which supports both \uc2 Hamas\uc2  and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, Spokesperson, \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  (through translator): We are identifying rockets and other weapons that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is launching at Israeli territory that were manufactured in Syria and given to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  from Syria. We will attack all infrastructure we identify in Syria whose purpose is to produce weapons for \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Explosions also rumbled through the suburbs of Lebanon`s capital, Beirut, where Israel has carried out intense bombing for a third straight day. State media said a separate strike in the east killed at least nine people.\par \par \uc2 Also today, \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  accused Israel of war crimes and crimes against humanity in the Gaza Strip. The group points to the forced displacement by Israel of nearly two million Palestinians, amounting to what it called ethnic cleansing. Israel has rejected similar such accusations in the past.\par \par \uc2 Suicides within the U.S. military increased last year, continuing a long- term trend that the Pentagon has struggled to bring under control. A \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  report out today shows there were 523 suicides reported in 2023. That is up from 493 such deaths in 2022, when the number of suicides had actually gone down.\par \par \uc2 Most of the 2023 cases involved young men who used a firearm. The long- running rise in suicides comes despite efforts by military leaders to expand mental health assistance and gun safety education.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey is under a drought warning, as parts of the Northeast experience the driest conditions they have seen in nearly 120 years. Unprecedented blazes are burning in places that haven`t seen significant rain since August. That includes a brushfire on the northern tip of Manhattan. It`s now contained after a fire boat shot water onto the flames.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, New York and New Jersey officials have brought charges against two people accused of starting separate smaller fires in those two states.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon is pouring cold water on recent reports of alien sightings. In an annual report on unidentified anomalous phenomena, or UAPs, \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  officials say they have discovered no evidence of extraterrestrial beings, activity or technology. But the report acknowledges 21 cases that -- quote -- "merit further analysis."\par \par \uc2 This comes after congressional hearings yesterday on reports of UAPs, often referred to in the public as UFOs. One former \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  official insists that the government knows more than it`s letting on.\par \par \uc2 LUIS ELIZONDO, Former Defense Department Official: Let me be clear. UAP are real. advanced technologies not made by our government or any other government are monitoring sensitive military installations around the globe. Furthermore, the U.S. is in possession of UAP technologies, as are some of our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yesterday`s hearing comes more than a year after a whistle- blower accused the Pentagon of running a secret UFO retrieval program, which the Pentagon has denied.\par \par \uc2 The satirical news publication The Onion is buying Alex Jones` conspiracy theory platform Infowars for an undisclosed price after a bankruptcy auction. Families of Sandy Hook school shooting victims helped to complete the sale. Jones owes them more than $1 billion in defamation judgments for calling the elementary school massacre a hoax.\par \par \uc2 The father of one of the victims said -- quote -- "The death of Infowars is the justice we have long awaited and fought for." The CEO of the company that owns The Onion says they plan to relaunch the Web site in January with satire aimed at conspiracy theorists.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, CEO, The Onion: By the end of the day, it was us or Alex Jones. That`s who could either continue this Web site unabated, basically unpunished for what he`s done to these families over the years, or we can make a dumb, stupid Web site. And we decided to do the second thing.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Within hours of the deal`s announcement, the Infowars Web site was down and Jones was broadcasting from what he said was a new studio. A bankruptcy judge has ordered a hearing on the sale after Jones challenged the terms of the auction.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the economy, the number of Americans filing for unemployment benefits fell last week to a six-month low. First-time jobless claims dipped by 4,000 to a total of 217,000. That is slightly lower than expected and signals ongoing stability in the U.S. labor market.\par \par \uc2 Separately, wholesale prices ticked up 0.2 percent in October when compared to the month before. Economists say higher costs for services are behind the latest rise.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks ended lower after Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  signaled there`s no need to rush further interest rate cuts. The Dow Jones industrial average fell more than 200 points, or nearly half-a-percent. The Nasdaq gave back more than 120 points on the day. The S&P 500 also ended in negative territory.\par \par \uc2 And Lindsey Vonn is coming out of retirement and heading back to the slopes. The three-time Olympic medalist bowed out of competitive skiing in 2019 after a record-setting career that included 82 World Cup wins and a range of serious injuries. Now, at the age of 40, she will return to the U.S. ski team just in time for the World Cup circuit this winter. Vonn had partial knee replacement surgery earlier this year. She says her training sessions have been pain-free.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": what we know about Trump`s controversial choice for director of national intelligence, \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 ; House Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  discusses Democrats` priorities and his new children`s book; Marines reflect on the brutal battle for Fallujah in Iraq 20 years later.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We`re going to take a closer look now at president-elect Donald Trump`s pick of former Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  to be the next director of national intelligence.\par \par \uc2 If confirmed, Gabbard would oversee 18 intelligence organizations, including the CIA and NSA. But her nomination is expected to set off a confirmation fight, since she has no experience in the intelligence world and has been accused of defending dictators and parroting Russian disinformation, which she denies.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by Michael Leiter, former director of the \uc2 U.S. National Counterterrorism Center\uc2  who served in both the Bush and Obama administrations.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made clear that he wants to clean House. He wants to overhaul the nation`s intelligence services. We also know that he is rewarding fealty as he staffs these top positions. So help us understand, in that context, what he sees in \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  serving as director of national intelligence.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEITER, Former Director, \uc2 National Counterterrorism Center\uc2 : Well, as you noted, President Trump, president-elect Trump, is clearly looking for people who are loyal to him.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure that that`s fully different from any other president. I think there probably is an extra concern for intelligence positions, because, at their very core, the director of national intelligence and the director of CIA, although, of course, loyal to the president, are also there in their positions to, as everyone has said, speak truth to power, make sure that they are providing an objective analysis of the circumstances and allowing the president to make the policy choices that he`s empowered to do.\par \par \uc2 And I think some of the concern here is that whether \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  has both the expertise and the experience, but also the inclination to speak truth to power.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The role of director of national intelligence was created after 9/11 because there was a concern that other intelligence agencies weren`t sharing information, that information had been siloed.\par \par \uc2 And so the federal government created this role. Help us understand what the DNI actually does.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEITER: The director of national intelligence, the DNI, was really a result of decades of challenges and fights between the intelligence organizations, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the CIA, the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And those were really epitomized in the failures of the weapons of mass destruction debacle in Iraq and 9/11. So the DNI was supposed to take this very large enterprise, coordinate budgets, coordinate efforts, and make sure that the president and the Cabinet had the best intelligence possible, regardless of where it came from, and making sure that differing views were coming to the president.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s been a subject of criticism over its 20 years as being overly bureaucratic and not very efficient, but I think those core needs for the intelligence community to be well-coordinated and to make sure, again, that differing views are presented to the president, that`s still critical.\par \par \uc2 And I think the president is probably going to dig in to see if the DNI is fulfilling that role or if it`s necessary at all.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  has been accused of echoing Russian propaganda. She has traded in conspiracy theories about Ukraine. She was widely criticized back in 2017 for meeting with Syrian President \uc2 Bashar al- Assad\uc2 , who had been accused of war crimes.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s former National Security Adviser \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  said that she should not sit for a Senate confirmation hearing until the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigates her because he said that she presents a national security threat. Is that a concern that you share?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEITER: Well, I certainly am very concerned with anyone in any position, national security or otherwise, who isn`t thinking very critically and questioning what enemies of the United States, like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Bashar al-Assad in Syria, say to them.\par \par \uc2 Any official needs to realize that other countries and their leaders will try to manipulate our leadership for their benefit. So I think it`s going to be up to Ms. Gabbard to prove that she was not just echoing their propaganda, but she can actually reflect critically as an intelligence officer and will listen to all of the analysts who serve the \uc2 United States government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And she will ultimately get to make her own judgments. But she absolutely does need to be free of any influence from foreign governments. And that should be true of every U.S. official.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: What might a second Trump term, in your view, mean for intelligence sharing? I mean, will our allies, \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, the members of the so-called Five Eyes alliance, will they be as willing to share closely held secrets, sources and methods, not just because of \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  potentially serving as DNI, but Donald Trump`s own cavalier approach to guarding classified documents?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEITER: Your question is critical.\par \par \uc2 And the critical premise is that we do rely on our allies, whether it`s the United Kingdom or countries around the world, to share with us intelligence to protect our national security interests. And the U.S. has a terrible record of protecting secrets. And I think there`s legitimate concern that a Trump administration will have challenges with that.\par \par \uc2 And Ms. Gabbard does not have a history of working in this. So I think there`s going to be real concern both from allies, but also within the intelligence community. So she`s going to have to establish to the Senate that she can protect our nation`s most sensitive secrets and those secrets of our allies, because otherwise we will ultimately be weaker, have worse intelligence, and it will harm our national interests.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Michael Leiter, thanks, as always, for your insights. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL LEITER: My pleasure. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Plans for president-elect Trump`s return to Washington and the Republican Party`s return to power are well under way, but for Democrats, who are now in the minority, the path forward is not as clear.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of New York is the House Democratic leader and he`s also the author of the new children`s book "The ABCs of Democracy." He joins us here now.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Leader Jeffries.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Good evening. Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, you said earlier today, I want to start with the news of the day, that you don`t think RFK Jr. is qualified to serve in the role that Mr. Trump has selected him for as \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary. What is it specifically about him that worries you? And do you think he will be confirmed?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, on the campaign trail, President Trump was very clear. He promised the American people that we would have the best economy, the best border security, the best military, and the best administration.\par \par \uc2 And we all have to ask a simple question right now. Is \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2  the best-qualified person in the United States of America to be the secretary of health and human services? Is he the very best available to the incoming president to look out for the health and the safety and the well-being of the American people? Of course not.\par \par \uc2 And so the question has to be asked, why? Why this nomination and why some of the other nominations? Our hope as Democrats is to work together with the incoming administration whenever and wherever possible to make life better for the American people. But we`re also committed to strongly disagreeing and pushing back against the extremism whenever necessary.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I want to ask you specifically about one of the other nominees, and that is \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , who you know from serving with in the House. He`s been tapped to be the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 ABC News is reporting late today that the woman who was at the center of a Justice Department sex trafficking probe into Mr. Gaetz, that that woman testified to the House Ethics Committee that was also investigating him. And she testified that he had had sex with her when she was 17 years old and in high school. That report is citing sources familiar.\par \par \uc2 I want to get your reaction to that. Do you believe the House Ethics report should be made public? And should that testimony disqualify him?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, transparency is always the preferred approach, particularly when it comes to high-level government positions, such as the Department of Justice or the DNI or the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and a wide variety of other positions that the incoming president is preparing to fulfill.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of the House Ethics Committee, it`s traditionally operated in a strongly bipartisan way. And it`s my hope that the Democrats and the Republicans on the committee will get together. It`s my understanding they may be meeting as soon as tomorrow to try to figure out a path forward and if there is information that they can present to the American people.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to get out ahead of that discussion. But I do place my trust in both the principal Democrats and the principal Republicans who are on that committee to do the right thing, so that the American people have all of the available information necessary, and so do the members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Do you think that report is necessary, though, to be made public before he`s considered for a post like attorney general?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Seems like a reasonable suggestion that`s been made by some of the members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . But let`s see what the Ethics Committee does before members of leadership weigh in aggressively one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So Republicans have now officially one control of the House, meaning they have a governing trifecta here, the White House and both chambers of Congress.\par \par \uc2 It`s now been more than a week since Election Day. So you have had some time to reflect. Does it surprise you, the success that they had in this last election, and also that Mr. Trump himself in his third run, knowing all we know about him, that he was able to win the popular vote?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, we will see where that ultimately lands. And I think the election probably at the popular vote level will be closer than is being projected right now.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: But he could still win it.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 And, notwithstanding, however that turns out, he had a decisive Electoral College victory. We have to ask the question, what happened? Why did it happen? How do we prevent this type of outcome that has so many Americans disturbed from ever happening again?\par \par \uc2 My view is that we have to work decisively to address the economic challenges that everyday Americans are facing in this country. Far too many people are struggling to live paycheck to paycheck all across America, in urban America, in suburban America, rural America, exurban America, certainly small town America and the heartland of America.\par \par \uc2 And we`re prepared to work with the incoming administrations to decisively deal with that issue. It clearly is an issue that the American people have said loudly and clearly around inflation and high prices, we need Washington to act.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, you have said that it left some people upset, his win, but most of the American electorate backed him. They are not upset about this. This is what they wanted. Did that outcome surprise you? What`s your takeaway from that message?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, my takeaway is that, at least as Democrats, we have got to do a better job of communicating that we are in lockstep with the American people, who want to see decisive action as it relates to lowering housing costs, lowering gas prices, lowering food prices.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Was this a messaging failure, you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, no. We`re going to assess qualitatively and quantitatively sort of what happened on Election Day and we`re in the middle of that process right now.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: What I am saying is, what is clear, the American people want and deserve this decisive action.\par \par \uc2 Far too many people believe that the American dream is out of reach. That`s not acceptable in a country that has given us the greatest middle class in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, let me ask you about what Americans are seeing right now, just in the last few days, because there`s a very sort of dramatic split screen.\par \par \uc2 You have a Republican Party that`s just won control of both chambers of Congress and the White House. They have hit the ground running. The next government is coming together. And then you have a Democratic Party that seems largely leaderless for the party and really rudderless, sort of directionless, as you say, still figuring out what to do next.\par \par \uc2 So what is the case that you will make for why you should be leader when House Democrats vote on their leadership next week?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Well, I will make that case internally and I haven`t officially announced my candidacy because I`m in the middle of having conversations with the various parts of the House Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Is there a chance you don`t run?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: No, well, what I will say is that House Democrats clearly overperformed the national environment. Those are facts. This is not a decisive electoral victory for House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was sworn in as president in 2017, there were 241 House Republicans and only 194 Democrats. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is sworn in this time around, notwithstanding the presidential wave election, at most, there will be 222 Democrats. At minimum, there will -- Republicans. There will be 213 Democrats in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 It`s a close margin. It`s a close election. We fell a few seats short, and we`re going to work to rectify that situation, so that we give ourselves the best possible opportunity in the next midterm.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I think it`s safe to say this is probably not the political landscape you thought you would be releasing this book under, "The ABCs of Democracy."\par \par \uc2 But you do write in this, in the introduction, that: "America is at a fork in the road. Which direction will we choose?" That`s the question that you pose.\par \par \uc2 Does this last election answer that question for you?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: No, I think what the book is in part about is the fact that we have had a 248-year journey as a country, the greatest democracy in the history of the world. And throughout that journey, of course, there are trials and tribulations. There`s been turbulence.\par \par \uc2 But there have been American values that have been a consistent part of that journey. And as we are processing this particular electoral result, we have to lean into those values and lean into the strength and resilience of our institutions, of what we have been about as Americans as we approach our 250th birthday on July 4 of 2026.\par \par \uc2 And, hopefully, I have laid that out as an illustrated book for people of all ages, but with a focus on our younger Americans, on our children, and on those who are processing this moment and where we go from here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is the House minority leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Leader Jeffries, always great to see you here. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: In the first year after the Supreme Court ended the federal right to an abortion, a record 200 women faced criminal charges for behavior related to pregnancy, abortion, pregnancy loss, or birth.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court decision emboldened prosecutors to develop more aggressive legal strategies to charge and imprison pregnant women and new mothers. But even prior to the overturning of Roe, hundreds of women faced such charges.\par \par \uc2 Special correspondent Sarah Varney traveled to South Carolina to speak with one family still grappling with the impact.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: There`s an empty seat at the table every time Lauren Smith and her family go out for lunch in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Lauren delivered a healthy newborn baby she named Audrey. A few days later, a case worker told Lauren she would not be bringing her home.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH, Mother: I was completely blindsided. Never in a million years would I have thought that`s what would have been told to me, never. It was so bad I couldn`t even, like, look at diaper commercials. I would just cry.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: A urine drug screen taken without her permission showed she had used marijuana some time during her pregnancy. After two rounds of drug testing, her baby tested positive for THC, a compound found in marijuana.\par \par \uc2 How did you think about marijuana use in your pregnancies, all of them?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: It was a way for me to be able to keep food down. I worked full time up until I was seven months. So dealing with all the discomfort and the pain, it helped. It helped with my anxiety, my depression.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: The CDC cautions against using marijuana during pregnancy, but it says that more research is needed to fully understand the impacts of THC on fetal development.\par \par \uc2 Still, six months after giving birth, Lauren was arrested and charged with felony child neglect. She has waited five years for a trial set for next month. She faces up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: This has caused so much strain on my family, on my kids. It`s affected so much. Me, mentally and emotionally, like, I will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: And Lauren is just one of hundreds of women across the country who have been arrested or lost custody of their children for reasons related to their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, Author, "Policing the Womb: Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood": There are these women that are being surveilled and charged and sentenced in these ways.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Michele Goodwin is a law professor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and author of the book "Policing the Womb." She says these cases rely on a legal concept called fetal personhood, a once fringe idea now at the center of anti-abortion advocacy.\par \par \uc2 The fetal personhood movement aims to grant full legal rights and protections to fetuses and in some cases embryos. About a third of states have established fetal personhood by law or judicial decision. And Goodwin says this concept applies to more than alleged drug offenses.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN: So if a woman is driving a car and there is an accident and it`s perceived that she was not acting responsibly enough, considering that she is pregnant, then that is enforced upon her.\par \par \uc2 If there is an instance in which she is in some form of a fight, which we have seen, and she has been injured by another party, that she is the person who is responsible because she`s been negligent towards fetal life.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Brittany Watts overcome with emotion after learning her case is moving forward.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Just last year, Brittany Watts in Ohio was arrested on charges of abuse of a corpse after she suffered a miscarriage in the bathroom of her home.\par \par \uc2 And in 2022, Hali Burns in Alabama was arrested when she tested positive for what her lawyers argued were legally prescribed substances during her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN: That`s one of the problems with these laws, is that they`re selective. They select out women because of their condition, which is unlawful.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Between 1973 and 2023, at least 2,000 women were investigated, arrested, or prosecuted for circumstances surrounding their pregnancies and pregnancy outcomes.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATRINA MARK, University of Maryland: If you take a step back and look at the bigger picture, it`s hard not to view it as its really actually about control of women.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Dr. Katrina Mark is director of the support clinic at the University of Maryland, which offers care for pregnant and postpartum women who use drugs.\par \par \uc2 She says separating mother from baby can cause lifelong trauma for everyone involved.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATRINA MARK: Separating that child from its mother is almost always going to be worse than the risk of that baby being exposed to any drugs that the mother took. Disruption of that maternal and infant bond really increases the risk of neurologic and behavioral problems and relationship and social problems for that child for the long term.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: And she says, since the overturning of Roe, pregnant women and mothers are under even more intense scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATRINA MARK: Scaring the mom to the point that she`s not coming to get health care during her pregnancy or not disclosing her drug use so that she can get the support and treatment that she needs, none of those things are protecting the baby. Putting the mom in jail and taking the baby away is not protecting the baby.\par \par \uc2 There are lots of unsafe things that women do during pregnancy. I might recommend that they take a certain medication for diabetes, and they don`t take it, and their sugars are really high, and that`s very harmful to them and their baby. But no one is putting them in jail or taking their baby away.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JOHN MCCRAVY (R-SC): We strongly support the idea that a child is a person in utero.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: South Carolina State House Representative John McCravy founded the Family Caucus, an influential anti-abortion group of lawmakers in a state with the third highest pregnancy-related prosecutions. He believes these types of laws serve as a deterrent to drug use.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JOHN MCCRAVY: You use the carrot and the stick. The stick is, hey, this is hanging over my head. If I don`t get clean, if I don`t get rid of this drug use, then I`m going to go to jail and I may lose my child.\par \par \uc2 But if I test negative, if I do a good job of getting clean, I`m going to get to lead a normal life with my child. So, it`s a powerful thing.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Lauren Smith says she did pass repeated drug tests, and attended required parenting classes. But she still does not have custody of her daughter.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: With the drug testing, it was so demeaning. But I was willing and happily going to do it if it meant getting my daughter back. I would have given my left toe if it meant being reunited with her.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Representative McCravy says he`s not familiar with Lauren`s case, and that the system in place protects children in South Carolina. He says it should be up to social services if women in these situations can see their children. But he believes there should be a limit to the state`s role.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious if you`re worried about the sort of slippery slope, that you have good intentions to start with, but that this program could expand to a kind of surveillance almost of pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JOHN MCCRAVY: If you have a child that`s been in danger, the state has an interest in protecting that child and doing what it needs to do. But I would never be in favor of a program that would go around testing women that are pregnant for illegal drugs and trying to make a big deal out of that. You know, I think that would go too far.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Attorneys for patients caught up in these cases today, though, say the practice of drug testing pregnant women without their consent remains widespread.\par \par \uc2 Did anybody ever ask your permission to test your urine?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: No. No, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Or to test Audrey?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: No.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: Today, Lauren`s daughter, Audrey, is a healthy 5.5 year-old who lives with her paternal grandmother. Lauren has little contact with her. And the delay in Lauren`s trial has made it difficult for her to even get a job delivering groceries.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: Being able to \uc2 Instacart\uc2 , I was told I can`t do due to my background check. Being able to find adequate housing for myself and my children, it`s affected in every way. Every background check that I do, it comes up on every job I apply for.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: She says, every day, she thinks about seeing and hugging her daughter.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SMITH: Am I unworthy of being a mother to my daughter? Am I unworthy of being in her life? I have missed everything. I have missed every first there is to miss. I will be reunited with my daughter. And I don`t care how long that takes. It`s already been 5.5 years, but I`m never going to stop fighting.\par \par \uc2 SARAH VARNEY: For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Sarah Varney in Greenville, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Twenty years ago this month, U.S. Marines began fighting the largest urban battle since the Vietnam War. More than 12,000 American, British, and Iraqi troops fought for Fallujah just west of Baghdad, which had become an insurgent stronghold.\par \par \uc2 Now, in collaboration with the news organization the War Horse, Nick Schifrin spent time with the Marines who still remember and feel that battle like it was yesterday.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Section 60 of Arlington National Cemetery is filled with men and women who never returned from Afghanistan and Iraq. It is filled with sacrifice. It is filled with memories.\par \par \uc2 Twenty years ago, Corporal Mike Ergo and the men of Alpha Company, 1st Battalion, 8th Marine Regiment, assaulted Fallujah. Their mission, take the mayor`s compound in the city center. The fight was through narrow streets.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That was incoming. Incoming!\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: It was deadly, and as seen in this BBC footage, dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`m fine, man.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.), U.S. Marines: I ran off first, and took a couple of steps and then immediately tripped and fell on my face. And I looked to see what I`d fallen on, and it was a body of an insurgent.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Then-21-year-old Ergo and the team`s mission was to clear the city of al-Qaida backed insurgents without overwhelming airpower. So they went house to house.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): Originally, before we deployed, we were still concerned about winning the hearts and minds of the Iraqi people. And so we obviously didn`t want to destroy it to save it.\par \par \uc2 And as we walk through the city and were clearing it house to house, I had already developed the sense that I was expendable, just like my teammates were.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: We spoke in the \uc2 National Museum of the Marine Corps\uc2  in Quantico, Virginia. A new exhibit dedicated to the battle for Fallujah mimics its streets, down to the trash. War can be imitated, but it cannot be recreated.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): I felt the whoosh of air going by me, of bullets creeping between my point man and I. And I just assumed that was it. The door opens and they toss a grenade out. And we clear the area. And I make sure everyone gets out, especially the guys on the stairs.\par \par \uc2 And as I turn around, the explosion happens, and I get a little shrapnel in my body, and most of its stopped by my armor, but a little piece of it gets into my neck.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. AARON CUNNINGHAM (RET.), U.S. Marines: At any given time, there may have been a half-dozen 360-degree firefights going on in our narrow sector.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Then-31-year-old Aaron Cunningham was Alpha Company`s commander. Fallujah was the largest battle that he or any of Alpha Company had ever fought.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. AARON CUNNINGHAM (RET.): Brutal, absolutely brutal. It was a close fight. Imagine turning a corner, and see how close you and I are? That`s how close. And the toughest guy wins that fight.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And not everyone came home. In six weeks of combat, the battalion lost at least 20 Marines. Alpha Company lost three, including Lieutenant Dan Malcom, the company fire support team leader, shot in the back while briefing Cunningham.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. AARON CUNNINGHAM (RET.): Just a good -- a good man. He was killed in the execution of this job, and he was doing it admirably. That`s one of the many pieces of my soul that got left in that city. You can`t leave an environment like that without some trauma. I mean, it exists.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Fifteen days later, Lance Corporal David Houck from the 1st Battalion, 8th Marines died in Fallujah. He was Ergo`s friend. For years, Ergo was too overwhelmed to visit his gravestone. On this visit, he released 20 years of grief.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): You know, I asked my friend Robbie (ph), "Hey, where`s Dave?" because I just wanted to see him. And he was like: "Didn`t make it, man. He got shot."\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s just that surprise, just thinking I was out of it, thinking he was out of it, made it safe. He didn`t.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And you never got to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): Never got to say goodbye. Never got to go to a funeral, I mean, never really got to tell him how much he meant to me as friend. So, I did today.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And behind every Marine lost, there is a family, often themselves lost.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN FAIRCLOTH, Mother of Fallen U.S. Soldier: Bradley was a rebel with a cause. After 9/11, he was determined to go into the service. And I was determined he was not. And he won. But he was part of something bigger than him, and I think that`s what he really wanted.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Kathleen Faircloth is the mother of Alpha Company`s Lance Corporal Bradley Faircloth, killed in Fallujah on Thanksgiving day. His death sent his mother into despair.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN FAIRCLOTH: Bradley was my reason. He was all the stuff that made me get up and function on a daily basis. And so, once he was gone, it just completely deflated and waking up pissed me off. And I tried repeatedly over and over to not wake up. I`m like, God come on, give me a break.\par \par \uc2 There`s lots of people dying. I bet they like live living. You can take me. If you got a quota to fill, I`m raising my hand. Get me out of here. And the days just kept going, like, ugh. You feel like such a failure that you cannot end your own life.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Since 9/11, American service members and veterans have been four times as likely to die by suicide than in combat. War shadows its survivors long after guns go silent. Ergo returned from Iraq in 2005.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): I would ride my motorcycle, blacked out, come in and out of consciousness as I was riding around and hoping someone would hit me, hoping I`d drive off the corner or drive -- took a turn too hard and be done with it.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And yet battle breeds brotherhood. And two decades after Fallujah, Alpha Company held a reunion. They are linked by loss, but also love that can help heal.\par \par \uc2 They call Kathleen Faircloth Squad Mama. She calls them the family that saved her.\par \par \uc2 KATHLEEN FAIRCLOTH: I would die for them, willingly die for them, in a heartbeat, if it would help them. I would lay down my life for them, because they will never know what they have done for me. They will never know that they can`t -- they sent me flowers the first Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes knowing that there`s somebody on the other side of the America that might come and go, hey, mama, what are you doing, you know what I mean? And I can`t pay them back. But I would die for them if it helped, willingly, in a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Mike Ergo healed at home.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): One day, my wife told me that she couldn`t go down the path with me anymore in terms of how much I was drinking and using drugs. She said I`d have to make a choice and either keep using or choose her. And I stopped drinking and using drugs on July 11 of 2012.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Your wife saved you?\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): My wife saved my life. If she hadn`t had the courage to tell me, demand that I stop, I would not be alive. There`s not a doubt in my mind. I would be dead. I`d be a statistic.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And now Ergo has transformed his pain into purpose. Today, he directs a VA vet center to help veterans who suffered like him.\par \par \uc2 SGT. MIKE ERGO (RET.): I talk to men and women and help them understand that there is good stuff right below that hurt, and that if they have the courage to feel some more discomfort, it`s almost like washing out an infected wound. It`s going to sting a little at first, but we`re going to make the result even better and help them connect to their values, the things they love, the people they love and care about, the person that they want to be.\par \par \uc2 And so that became my new mission, my new why, is to help other people feel that hope again.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And that, he says, is the best way to honor those who never came home.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Nick Schifrin in Arlington, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241115ekbe00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 11, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Sy, Lisa Desjardins, Nick Schifrin, Laura Barron-Lopez,  Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Good evening. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: President-elect Trump begins to shape his administration, naming immigration hard-liner \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  and others to top posts.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: As the vote count continues, Republicans edge closer to winning control of both congressional chambers and giving Trump broad powers to implement his agenda.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the United Nations urges immediate action in Gaza to avoid famine. We hear from a former \uc2 U.N.\uc2  official who just returned from the region.\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 , Secretary-General, \uc2 Norwegian Refugee Council\uc2 : This is a catastrophe that has been announced for a very long time, and it will come to haunt Israel and its allies.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is quickly picking the people he wants to advise him when he enters the White House again in January. That includes who he has chosen to lead his mass deportation effort, a campaign promise he said he`d begin carrying out on day one.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: On Sunday, Mr. Trump announced on TRUTH Social that Tom Homan will be what he called his border czar. Trump said the former acting ICE director would be -- quote -- "in charge of our nation`s borders and in charge of all deportation of illegal aliens back to their country of origin."\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the president-elect is also expected to formally name \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  as his deputy chief of staff for policy in the coming days. Vice president-elect J.D. Vance confirmed the selection the social media site X.\par \par \uc2 Miller worked as an adviser during Mr. Trump`s first term and is known as an immigration hard-liner.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Our White House correspondent, Laura Barron-Lopez, has been covering all of this and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, we know immigration has been a core part of Mr. Trump`s message. What is he pledging to do as soon as he enters the White House?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made a host of promises during the campaign, Amna.\par \par \uc2 And so based on statements that he made while campaigning, as well as what I have heard from sources close to the Trump incoming administration, what we expect him to prioritize are a number of things, including mass deportations of undocumented immigrants, restarting border wall construction, invoking the 1798 Alien Enemies Act, and directing more money and agents to the border.\par \par \uc2 A note on that Alien Enemies Act, Amna, it`s only been invoked during times of war, notably when the U.S. carried out Japanese internment. And it would be used to rapidly deport alleged gang members without due process. It`s going to likely be met with immediate lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 Another thing I`m told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will prioritize is a reconciliation package with Congress to allocate more money for this deportation effort and to add thousands of agents to the border.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And who would be carrying out that kind of effort when you talk about mass deportations?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Tom Homan, who you just mentioned, Amna, the former acting ICE director, will lead this effort. And Homan was recently asked what that mass deportation effort would look like by \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "60 Minutes."\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Is there a way to carry out mass deportation without separating families?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS HOMAN, Former Acting U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement Director: Of course there is. Families can be deported together. Their parent, obviously, entered the country illegally, had a child knowing he was in the country illegally, so he created that crisis.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Homan is a -- was a supporter of the first-term effort to separate families, that zero tolerance policy at the border, Amna, which ended up separating some 5,500 families.\par \par \uc2 He was also a contributor to Heritage Foundation`s Project 2025. And Homan is expected to work alongside \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , who, as we just said, is expected to be named the deputy chief of staff for policy. And Miller is going to also be overseeing immigration policy. He was known as one of the architects of that family separation agenda during Trump`s first term.\par \par \uc2 And he is someone who supports very harsh immigration policy, recently saying at the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally that America is for America and Americans only. And sources close to Trump world told me that Homan and \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  approach things a little bit differently. Homan is seen more as a cop, someone who very much wants to enforce the law and is less ideological than \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , whose positions, whose anti-immigrant positions are based on culture and identity.\par \par \uc2 Now, when it comes to who may be the secretary for the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 , Amna, sources close to the transition told me that \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2  is a top contender. He served as acting director of homeland security during the first Trump administration, but other names in the mix include Ken Cuccinelli, as well as potentially \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And then, Laura, when it comes to mass deportations, is that something president-elect Trump can do on day one, as he has pledged to do, and is a mass deportation effort even possible?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: As one source close to the transition told me, Amna, the planning is starting now, so that way implementation can happen on day one.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to current estimates, there are roughly 11 million undocumented immigrants in this country, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and J.D. Vance both had previously talked about deporting anywhere from one million to 11 million undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, Tom Homan and other Trump allies have said that their first priority for those deportations would be those who pose public safety threats and national security threats.\par \par \uc2 But John Sandweg, who is a former director of ICE, acting director of ICE, said that if Trump goes through with those deportations of one million or more immigrants, it will reach far beyond just those who pose public safety threats.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SANDWEG, Former Acting ICE Director: I`m grateful that the administration says we want to focus first on public safety cases, but the bottom line is, you can`t deport a million people in a year, and you can certainly cannot deport 11 million people without getting into these really tough cases, these cases where someone has been here a long time, has never been convicted of any criminal offense, has a U.S. citizen, oftentimes a minor child, who is a U.S. citizen.\par \par \uc2 And now you`re saying, hey, we`re going to take you into custody and remove you from the country, and putting these families in these incredibly difficult positions.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: So, again, Amna, the big question is, beyond deporting people who pose public safety threats, beyond deporting undocumented migrants who have committed violent or property crimes, who exactly is Trump going to target?\par \par \uc2 Is it going to also include people whose visas have expired? A former DHS official told me that Trump and Tom Homan may target employers who have large undocumented migrant workers, so that could result in conducting raids. Of course, logistics are a big question here, Amna. It`s going to require more agents, more transportation, more detention beds, and a lot of more money.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Laura, put this in context for us. Have we ever seen anything like this before?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: No, nothing that would be at this scale,if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if the president-elect is able to carry out something that would be deportations of one million or more undocumented migrants.\par \par \uc2 And the big picture overall, Amna, is that people view Donald Trump`s goals as one that would make it harder, harder not just for people to migrate here illegally, but also legal migration will be restricted severely under this administration.\par \par \uc2 And there were a few other areas that I want to point out, Amna. One is that multiple former DHS officials and sources close to Trump world said that the administration may target states by blocking FEMA funding or funding for local law enforcement if those states are ones that provide driver`s licenses to undocumented migrants.\par \par \uc2 Other things that are on the table are ending birthright citizenship.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Before we let you go, Mr. Trump has named a few more people to serve in his administration. Who do we know?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Today, the president-elect announced that he is going to be appointing former New York Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  to lead the \uc2 EPA\uc2  and Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York to be the U.S. ambassador to the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We expect more names in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Laura Barron-Lopez, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So how much congressional support will Mr. Trump have to implement his agenda? That remains to be seen with more than a dozen congressional races yet to be called.\par \par \uc2 Republicans hoping to maintain their majority in the House, and if they do, it would lead to Republicans holding a political trifecta over Washington.\par \par \uc2 Our Lisa Desjardins has been following the latest and joins us now from our PBS News super screen.\par \par \uc2 All right, Lisa, so where do things stand in the race to control the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: We have had more races called over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Right now, as it stands, according to the Associated Press, Republicans have 214 seats they have won in the House. As many of our viewers know, that means they need four more in order to clinch control, keep control. How many races are left? There are 17 races left to be called around the country at this hour.\par \par \uc2 They`re all over the place. There`s a couple that are going to overtime, Maine and Alaska. Those are ranked-choice elections, where we`re going to go into the next phase of that. There`s a couple that are getting official review, potentially recounts possible in Ohio and Iowa. But the largest number still, looking at you, California, 10 races left in the Golden State.\par \par \uc2 And that will decide who wins and also the margin. So let`s take a look at some of those. Here`s a Republican race critical for Republican control. David Valadao, incumbent here, in House district 22. That`s up here, sort of in the center of California. Right now, he`s out ahead of Rudy Salas, looking good. Republicans like that.\par \par \uc2 Not as good news for Republicans, better news for Democrats in District 27. That is Northern Los Angeles County, right around there. \uc2 George Whitesides\uc2 , former chief of staff at \uc2 NASA\uc2 , is now leading. He pulled ahead over the weekend. This could go back and forth, but Democrats think they`re going to hold on to this lead.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, Lisa, you have been looking at the data surrounding the House results. What do you see when you look more deeply?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Yes, there is something historic that happened in this election. Took my spreadsheet, looked at all of the competitive races that had incumbents in them. These are the ones that have been called by the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 You look at all of these dots representing Democrat incumbents, Republican incumbents. Of all of these, only five of these incumbents lost their races. The rest of them were winning incumbents. It`s a 98.5 percent win rate for incumbents, according to Open Secrets.\par \par \uc2 That is a modern record. This is one reason Democrats have had trouble. Incumbents have just had incredible staying power. So right now, Republicans essentially are up one. Democrats really have a lot of catching up to do if they`re going to get -- they need to swing five more seats in order to get control. Republicans feeling good.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right, let`s shift our focus to the Upper Chamber. We know Republicans will control the Senate. The question now is by how much.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Right. Let`s look at it.\par \par \uc2 OK, here we are. The Senate, according to the Associated Press, to their calls, 53 Republican seats right now in their hands. The remaining decision is in Arizona, and we are waiting to see what happens there. So, this week, Republicans will vote on their majority leader replacing \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . That happens Wednesday. To win that, you need a majority of the sitting Republicans, more than half.\par \par \uc2 Who`s in that race? \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas, \uc2 John Thune\uc2  of South Dakota, both have served as the number two Republican in the past. But a lot of conversation about \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida, who has entered the race, saying he represents the MAGA wing of the party. And some from that MAGA wing have been endorsing \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  in the last couple of days, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , podcaster Charlie Kirk, RFK Jr. and \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, talking to my sources, however, this is a secret ballot vote, Geoff. And everyone can say what they want about \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  in public. Behind the scenes, I hear more senators inclined not to vote for him at this moment, but he`s really making a run of it.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: It`s an interesting point because the Senate has typically been insulated from outside pressure. That has to do partly with the way the founders designed it. I mean, does that still hold true in this Trump era?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: We have seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  clearly wants to push control of the Senate right now. He sent out this post on X just over this weekend, saying that he is expecting any Republican senator who wants to have the leadership position must agree to recess appointments.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? Recess appointments is a way that you get around Senate confirmation. And you have to have both chambers in order to do it. A reminder, the president appoints more than 1,200 total positions. And high-level nominees usually face hearings in scrutiny, but a recess appointment would get around that.\par \par \uc2 So this is also a decision that Republican senators will have to make. Trump wants them to give him sort of carte blanche on his appointments.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa Desjardins, thanks to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We start the day`s other headlines with Veterans Day, and, in particular, the first joint appearance by President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  since last week`s election. They appeared together to honor those who serve the nation in uniform.\par \par \uc2 At Arlington National Cemetery outside Washington this morning, Mr. Biden laid a wreath at the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier. And with Vice President Harris looking on, Mr. Biden reflected on the service of our nation`s veterans as his own time in public service comes to an end.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , President of the United States: This is the last time I will stand here at Arlington as commander in chief. It`s been the greatest honor of my life to lead you, to serve you, to care for you, to defend you, just as you defended us.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, British Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  joined French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  in Paris to mark the 106th anniversary of the armistice that ended World War I. It`s said to be the first such joint appearance on Armistice Day since General Charles de Gaulle hosted Winston Churchill in 1944.\par \par \uc2 America`s top climate envoy told a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  conference today that the nation will continue its environmental efforts, despite what he called a bitterly disappointing election result. \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  was speaking at the COP 29 conference as it got under way in Azerbaijan.\par \par \uc2 He warned that president-elect Trump will try to reverse President Biden`s climate policies, including the Inflation Reduction Act, which funneled $375 billion into climate spending. During the campaign, Mr. Trump vowed to take the U.S. out of the Paris climate agreement once again and increase oil drilling. Mr. Podesta said Trump should be taken at his word, but that the broader fight for a cleaner environment will continue.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PODESTA\uc2 , White House Senior Adviser For Clean Energy: While the United States federal government under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may put climate action the back burner, the work to contain climate change is going to continue in the United States with commitment and passion and belief.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Podesta was speaking as world leaders from around 200 nations gather over the next two weeks to discuss climate concerns, but the leaders of two of the world`s biggest polluters -- that`s the U.S. and China -- are not planning to attend.\par \par \uc2 Back here at home, fire crews are battling blazes on both coasts. In the Northeast, an 18-year-old state parks employee died while fighting a large fire that`s burned almost eight square miles near the New York-New Jersey border. Numerous brushfires have broken out around New Jersey since last week fueled by bone-dry conditions. Officials say that a quarter-inch of rain fell last night, giving firefighters a bit of a break, but they say it`s not nearly enough.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN GALLAGHER, Forest Ranger: So, right now, you get a little bit of rain, puts that surface fire out, but if it`s in the duff, it`s going to stay there. It`s going to smoke like a cigar until it gets dry enough, and then that fire can pop up again.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And in the West, much larger wildfires rage on in California, though firefighters are making more progress there. The 32- square-mile Mountain Fire has destroyed more than 170 structures, most of them homes, and at last check is only about a third contained.\par \par \uc2 A county coroner in Alabama has identified the man killed during a shooting at \uc2 Tuskegee University\uc2  as an 18-year-old who was not affiliated with the school. Yesterday`s shooting erupted as the historically Black university was wrapping up its 100th homecoming week celebrations; 16 people were injured, many of them students.\par \par \uc2 A 25-year-old has been taken into custody. He was found with a handgun and faces a federal gun possession charge, but has not been accused of the shooting itself. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has joined the investigation and is seeking tips from the public.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  said today that his forces are holding back enemy troops in Russia`s Kursk border region. He said that Moscow had sent 50,000 fighters to retake areas that Ukraine captured in a surprise incursion earlier this year. The Ukrainian military released drone footage today that claims to show Russian troops under fire in the area.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a barrage of Russian missiles, drones and glide bombs pounded several cities in Ukraine`s south and east earlier today. Officials say at least six people were killed and 30 others injured.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin is denying that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and president- elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spoke on the phone last week. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  first reported the call yesterday, saying that Trump advised Putin not to escalate the war in Ukraine. But Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov told journalists today that the report is -- quote -- "completely untrue and pure fiction."\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Trump said -- quote -- "We do not comment on private calls between President Trump and other world leaders."\par \par \uc2 Haiti`s international airport shut down temporarily today after gangs opened fire at a \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  flight trying to land in Port-au-Prince. A flight attendant was wounded and the plane was diverted to the Dominican Republic. Meantime, on the streets of the capital, firefights broke out today between police and gangs as civilians fled in terror.\par \par \uc2 Some homes were set on fire in upper-class neighborhoods. It all comes as the country`s new prime minister was sworn in following weeks of political chaos and uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks closed at new milestones to start the week. The Dow Jones industrial average added more than 300 points to end above 44000 for the first time ever. The Nasdaq inched new numbers and nearly 12 points higher, so just a small gain there. The S&P 500 managed a small gain for its first ever close above 6000.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": we examine the influence billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  could have in the incoming Trump administration; Tamara Keith and Amy Walter break down the latest political headlines; and Idaho`s strict abortion law goes to trial, with potential implications nationwide.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Today, the de facto leader of Saudi Arabia, Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , accused Israel of committing genocide in Gaza and called for Israel to respect the sovereignty of -- quote -- "sisterly Iran."\par \par \uc2 It marked the strongest criticism of Israel by the man known as MBS since the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  October 7 attack and signaled a turn away from Saudi normalization with Jerusalem, all this as a U.N.-affiliated organization is warning of -- quote -- "imminent famine in Gaza."\par \par \uc2 Here`s Nick Schifrin with more.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: This weekend, an independent famine review committee affiliated with the United Nations declared that across Northern Gaza, starvation, malnutrition and excess mortality are -- quote -- "rapidly increasing" and famine thresholds may have already been crossed or else will be in the near future.\par \par \uc2 Little to no aid has reached Northern Gaza over the last month, since Israel launched a new operation and called on all residents to leave, saying \uc2 Hamas\uc2  had regrouped.\par \par \uc2 Jan Egeland\uc2 , a longtime diplomat and humanitarian who is the secretary- general of the \uc2 Norwegian Refugee Council\uc2 , recently traveled to Gaza and joins me now from Amman.\par \par \uc2 Jan Egeland\uc2 , thank you very much. Welcome back to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Let me first show you the areas of Northern Gaza that we`re going to be talking about. The Israeli operation last month has focused on Jabalia, Beit Lahia, Beit Hanoun. You were only able to travel as far north as Gaza City. What did you see?\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 , Secretary-General, \uc2 Norwegian Refugee Council\uc2 : Well, Gaza City looks like Stalingrad after the Second World War. It was much worse than I feared.\par \par \uc2 I`m really shattered by seeing how humanity has been trampled upon through Gaza. More than two million people are trapped in one of the most confined areas on the planet and they`re under the most intense and indiscriminate bombardment by enormous Israeli air force.\par \par \uc2 And they`re using indiscriminate bombs provided by the United States. This will come to haunt Israel and the United States because this is so much against every principle of civilization and of law.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: The Israeli operation in the farther most part of Gaza, the \uc2 IDF\uc2  says that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has regrouped there. Herzi Halevi, Israel`s top general, said they have killed 1,000 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  over the last month, detained 1,000 more.\par \par \uc2 In a visit this weekend to that area, he added this:\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. HERZI HALEVI, Chief of Staff, \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2  (through translator): We are providing the Israeli residents near the northern Gaza border with greater security and creating conditions for the security to endure, to not be fleeting. We are not stopping or slowing down.\par \par \uc2 This is to bring back the hostages, to ensure security for the surrounding communities.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Is that what you saw when you were there?\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : I understand that the Israeli wants to have safety for the citizens and also to have the long-suffering hostages out.\par \par \uc2 But they cannot starve deliberately women and children to try to make Israel safer, at the cost of thousands of innocent Palestinian children`s lives, will only smear Israel forever and not make the Israelis more safe. By destroying the neighborhood in Gaza and in Lebanon will not make Israel more safe.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: As I said, you couldn`t go farther north than Gaza City. When you requested to go north, what did they say? What are the conditions, as far as you can tell, just north of Gaza City?\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : There is a siege there. It`s a little bit like what the Assad regime in Syria did against the besieged areas in Syria, which outraged the United States, \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Britain, Germany, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Israel is doing exactly the same. It`s starvation tactics against an area which I`m sure have some very bad guys inside, but many more babies. This is not the way to exercise self-defense nor precise counterterrorism operations like the United States have asked for.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Israeli officials, of course, deny that they are conducting any kind of starvation campaign.\par \par \uc2 And on the warning from the famine review committee, specifically, Israel`s coordinator in Gaza said this weekend -- quote -- "The researchers Continue to rely on partial, biased data and superficial sources with vested interests."\par \par \uc2 Israel points out there is aid from Jordan arriving into Northern Gaza, some 700 trucks over the last month. They have also facilitated the movement of the entire population from Northern Gaza.\par \par \uc2 Again, does any of that match what you are seeing?\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : No, it doesn`t at all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I`m amazed how journalists sort of takes one party in a very dirty war as a good source. Don`t believe the Israeli propaganda. Don`t believe \uc2 Hamas\uc2  propaganda. Don`t believe \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  propaganda. There is a reason Israel is denying \uc2 PBS\uc2  and all other independent journalism in the world access to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 They don`t want independent witnesses. We who are there who are independent, neutral, impartial, all \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agencies, all Red Cross agencies, all nongovernmental agencies, American, European, et cetera, we are unanimous in that Israel is deliberately starving the population and having an indiscriminate, excessive warfare that are killing thousands of women and children.\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt that this has been documented. Israel is not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And, finally, \uc2 Jan Egeland\uc2 , winter is coming to Gaza.\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: I wonder how much that will exacerbate all the things that we have been talking about.\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : After having seen all of these families, all of these mothers who were crying to get a tent, a blanket, a mattress after their home has been destroyed by the carpet-bombing, many will die this winter in the area where Israel is asking people to go, and no water, no sanitation.\par \par \uc2 Epidemic disease is spreading. This is a catastrophe that has been announced for a very long time, and it will come to haunt Israel and its allies.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: \uc2 Jan Egeland\uc2 , secretary-general of the \uc2 Norwegian Refugee Council\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JAN EGELAND\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Among Donald Trump`s most visible and powerful surrogates on the campaign trail was \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the tech billionaire who owns the platform X, formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Now with president-elect Trump, Musk remains within the inner circle, joining calls with world leaders and weighing in on staffing decisions.\par \par \uc2 For more on the influence Musk could exert on the next administration and what he stands to gain, I`m joined by Vittoria Elliott, a reporter from Wired who has long covered \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Vittoria, welcome. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 VITTORIA ELLIOTT, Wired: Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So let`s just start with the influence Musk has already had on getting Trump back into the White House. We mentioned he owns and controls the platform X. He donated over $118 million to the reelection bid.\par \par \uc2 How instrumental would you say he was in getting Trump reelected?\par \par \uc2 VITTORIA ELLIOTT: You know, obviously, he plowed a lot of money into the campaign and he really brought the full weight of his own personal celebrity, because before Musk even owned \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he was one of the top 10 most followed people on the platform.\par \par \uc2 So just his audience, even without the sort of super juicing that now we have seen as he owned it, as he`s changed the algorithm, would have been incredibly powerful. There was a really great report from the Center for Countering Digital hate that found that the posts on X from Musk alone between July 13, when he endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and October 25 reached so many people that if a campaign were to like pay for that kind of visibility, it would have been the equivalent of about $24 million in advertisements.\par \par \uc2 So even the use of his platform alone to boost the Trump campaign`s talking points, to boost its visibility was incredibly valuable. But then, if you zoom out from that, he also gave permission for a lot of these other more right-leaning people within Silicon Valley, people like \uc2 David Sacks\uc2  and Shaun Maguire Sequoia Capital, to be really public about their support for Trump and to put their money behind his campaign too.\par \par \uc2 So, I think he sort of really was at the vanguard of being willing to take the public heat and attention for this, such that other people in his circle felt comfortable backing Trump. And that`s also true for bringing the people that have followed him for years, including his fan base.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Musk is also the richest man in the world. He`s the CEO of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX.\uc2  He also relies on the federal government for a big chunk of his wealth, Right? He`s gotten billions of dollars in federal government contracts over the years.\par \par \uc2 From what we know about how former President Trump, president-elect Trump, plans to govern, how could Musk and his businesses benefit from a Trump administration?\par \par \uc2 VITTORIA ELLIOTT: Well, definitely, what we know from how the first Trump administration went is that Trump values loyalty and he is more than willing to use his position as president to reward the people who have been very loyal to him.\par \par \uc2 So I think a really great place that we may see this is in space exploration. \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  competes with other companies like \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 , which is run by \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , for contracts. I think we could definitely see him getting more contracts for that. Tariffs, which have been a huge talking point, would be incredibly helpful for Musk, particularly tariffs on China and Chinese electric vehicles, because that really helps Tesla`s market share, even though \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has sort of taken a more pro-fossil fuel vision for how he envisions his administration and is not as hot on electric cars as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 Still, some of these policies will be really good for his businesses. So I think this rollback in regulation, regardless of who you are, if you are a billionaire, if you have a big business, it is an ultimate win. And particularly, if you read Project 2025, the rollback on labor rights and labor protections, Musk is part of several labor lawsuits and is trying to currently dismantle the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that, in itself, being free from some of these suits, being free from the ability of his workers to make claims, are all boons for him. So I think there are many ways in which a Trump administration could really benefit him.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We have also seen over the years a lot of people who got very close to Mr. Trump who ended up getting burned by him in the long run. You`re talking about two very big personalities, two men who are used to being in control and getting their way.\par \par \uc2 What do you think happens in the future? Do you see this alliance continuing between these two men?\par \par \uc2 VITTORIA ELLIOTT: Both of them are very sensitive. We have seen them really value loyalty and be willing to fire or cut ties with people over even disagreements or perceived slights.\par \par \uc2 I think it could really go either way. Though Musk has said he would be in charge of some form of government efficiency commission, I think we may see him much more as a floating adviser to the Trump administration, rather than someone who`s actually installed within it.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s, frankly, because these are two people who are used to being in charge, who kind of conceive of themselves as alpha men. And I don`t see either one of them being comfortable willing to work in a position that feels like it`s beneath the other.\par \par \uc2 Trump has no problem letting go of people who have previously supported him if he feels that their loyalty or their support is in question. And I don`t think Musk would be any different, particularly once Trump is in office and has the full power of the regulatory state that he can then he could sort of rely upon.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is Vittoria Elliott, a reporter from Wired, joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much. Good to speak with you.\par \par \uc2 VITTORIA ELLIOTT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: For more analysis now of Democrats` losses in last week`s election and the new Trump team that`s taking shape, we`re joined now by our Political Stakes duo -- I almost said Politics Monday -- that`s Amy Walter from The Cook Political Report With Amy Walter and Tamara Keith from NPR.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH, National Public Radio: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So Democrats are working to regroup and figure out what went wrong in last week`s election. Here`s some of what Democrats said yesterday on the Sunday shows.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): Bottom line, if you`re an average working person out there, do you really think that the Democratic Party is going to the mats, taking on powerful special interest and fighting for you? I think the overwhelming answer is no.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): The reason we didn`t win ultimately is we didn`t listen enough to people on the ground, people like Chris Deluzio, Pat Ryan, who were saying, talk about the economy, talk about people`s economic struggles, convince people you have the better policies and better vision.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DEAN PHILLIPS\uc2  (D-MN): We have used condemnation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus invitation to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters. I have never known any industry, political or professional, in which a strategy of condemnation works better than invitation.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, Tam, how are Democrats coming to terms with this? Because there are still Harris campaign aides who say they outlined policy after policy that was targeted to working-class voters, and they did make this outreach to Republicans, and yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won all of the battleground states.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 I think that what you`re seeing is there are a lot of Democrats pointing at a lot of different things, and a lot of the things that they`re pointing at confirm their priors. So, if they`re progressive, then they believe that trying to win over \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  voters was a fool`s errand and that simply the Harris campaign was too moderate.\par \par \uc2 And if they`re moderate, then they think that they went way too far to the left, and that wokeness is taking down the Democratic Party. The reality is that this is going to be an argument that is going to continue to be argued for years.\par \par \uc2 If you remember, after 2012 when \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  lost in the -- and former President Obama was reelected, they did that autopsy report, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  did. And they said they need to do a better job of winning over Latino voters, and they need to be more sensitive on immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 Well, then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came down the golden escalator, and he became the party`s nominee. He set the party`s policy. In essence, the way this will be decided is by the next Democratic nominee who Democratic voters choose, and if that person wins. Of course, if that person loses, then there will be a whole new round of this sort of finger-pointing and reckoning, really.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Amy, when you look at the data, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, The Cook Political Report: You know, the Harris campaign and her allies spent a lot of money, hundreds of millions of dollars with many of those messages that the critics that you played in that clip are accusing her of not doing.\par \par \uc2 And I do think -- I agree with Tam that this idea of she moved too far to the left or she needed to move from the center to talk to working-class voters is going to be a central part of the debate going forward.\par \par \uc2 But I think, at its very core, the thing that was hurting Harris, the campaign, the most was the one thing she had absolutely no control over, which was inflation itself and the frustration that voters had with the very issue that was impacting them day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 You can put all the plans you want forward. You can talk about a positive message. But at the end of the day, what voters knew was that she was in the White House when inflation went up and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn`t. And when he was in the White House, lower inflation was the norm.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Amy, Democrat -- or, rather, Republicans -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won across the map, improving his margins nearly everywhere. He delivered on his promise to win over more nonwhite voters. How daunting a picture is this for Democrats with their national coalition really fractured now?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes, when I look at where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was most successful, you`re right. He basically broke up what was known as -- at the time of the Obama era the coalition of the ascendant, younger voters, voters of color, this growing group of folks in the population.\par \par \uc2 And where Trump did best was younger voters and Latino voters. But I think what`s also important to appreciate is that, in the states where, say, Harris struggled the most or Trump gained the most, Democrats also either held on or picked up some seats.\par \par \uc2 So in a state like Nevada, Arizona, where you have Senate candidates, Democratic Senate candidates who were able to outrun the Harris campaign, or, in some cases, we`re seeing Republicans who were unable to match Trump numbers. So I think we`re going to talk about that for a while.\par \par \uc2 The other piece that really did hurt Democrats, especially Latino voters, was being on offense. In place like California, where they were hoping to pick up multiple seats in districts that are overwhelmingly Latino, it looks like they`re going to come up short in all but maybe one of them.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Tam, meantime, Mr. Trump is starting to assemble his team, as Laura Barron-Lopez was reporting on this broadcast earlier. For any presidential administration, personnel is policy. That`s especially true when it comes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: What will you be watching for?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: I`m certainly watching who those personnel are.\par \par \uc2 Already, the picks that we have heard, all of them fall into the same category of people who`ve been incredibly loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I think that that is the key lesson that he learned from his last transition, when he was wowed by generals, where he was persuaded by people who said, Oh, yes, you have got to give this guy a try.\par \par \uc2 And he ultimately ended up with an administration full of people who did not agree with him on policy. This time, loyalty to Trump, agreement with him on policy is going to be the most key qualifier. And that`s what you`re seeing already with the people that he`s named.\par \par \uc2 The one thing that I will note is that, and it`s very technical, but there are these memoranda of understanding that an incoming administration is supposed to sign with the current administration, essentially to give them the keys, so that they can look under the hood, they can have landing teams in place at various agencies, so that they can really hit the ground running. So far, Trump`s team has not signed these.\par \par \uc2 They also govern things like conflict of interest, allow background checks. A lot of different items come with these memoranda. They haven`t been signed yet. This is a total break from precedent. Even in 2016, which is widely seen as a truly terrible transition because Trump`s team threw out the plan two days after the election, back then, they did sign those.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s not clear at this point whether or not they will sign them. Trump will be sworn in on January 20 regardless. But people who watch this who say that transition is really the most perilous time in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , they are worried about it, and they are raising -- they`re sounding the alarm.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: In the minute we have left, help us understand why he`s focused on recess appointments, especially given the fact that he`s going to have at least 53 -- a 53-seat majority in the Senate. He shouldn`t have too much trouble getting his nominees through.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: He could have trouble with some of them, depending on how big that majority is in the Senate. Some of these people that have been extremely loyal to him are also extremely controversial and have a lot of controversial history and statements and all of these things that may make them hard to confirm.\par \par \uc2 But also this is another one of these tests where Trump is trying to assert his power over the Congress, where he is trying to have ultimate power and trying to get the potential leaders of the Republicans in the Senate to agree to this in advance. So that is as much as what it`s about. It`s a power that hasn`t been used in a long time, in part because senators on both sides of the aisle have tried to prevent presidents from using this power.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Tamara Keith and Amy Walter, our thanks to you both.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: An Idaho state court will hear arguments tomorrow in a case brought by four women who allege they were denied emergency care because of the state`s abortion laws, which are some of the most restrictive in the nation. The women argue that the medical exceptions in the state`s laws are too narrow to protect the health of pregnant patients, including in cases of fatal fetal diagnoses.\par \par \uc2 For more on the case, I`m joined now by the lead plaintiff, Jennifer Adkins, and Marc Hearron, senior counsel for the Center for Reproductive Rights, which is representing the women in this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 And, Jennifer, I want to start with you and your experience, because you were 12 weeks pregnant with your second child when you received multiple diagnoses for the fetus, including Turner`s syndrome, which I understand ends in miscarriage in the vast majority of cases. I`m so sorry for that.\par \par \uc2 Can you just tell us what it was like to receive that news?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADKINS, Plaintiff: I mean, it was it was devastating to find out that a baby that we were really excited to welcome into our family was not going to survive the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And it was just -- it was news that no parent wants to get. And knowing that we couldn`t get health care in our home state was just added heartbreak to us.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Marc, the state attorney general here has argued that the law says -- quote -- it allows a doctor to perform an abortion if he or she has a "subjective good-faith belief that there is a risk of death to the mother if it`s not done."\par \par \uc2 You`re representing three other women in this case, but you`re also representing two physicians and a local medical association. What did they tell you about how they`re interpreting this exception?\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON, Senior Counsel, Center for Reproductive Rights: So, actually, let me just say that the attorney general`s quotation of the law is wrong.\par \par \uc2 The law only allows an abortion if it is -- quote -- "necessary to prevent the death of the pregnant woman." And doctors all across Idaho or anywhere in the United States are not trained to draw the line about what medical care is necessary just to prevent their patient`S death.\par \par \uc2 They are trained to provide all necessary treatment options and health care that would prevent their patient`S health from deteriorating. So these abortion bands are handcuffing doctors, preventing them from providing the full spectrum of care to their patients, and under threat of criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 Doctors do not understand what this law means. They don`t understand how to apply it to their practice. What they do know is that if they provide care in reliance on the exceptions, they are subjecting themselves to threat of criminal prosecution, massive civil fines, loss of their medical license.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s no wonder that you have seen an exodus of obstetricians and gynecologists and maternal fetal medicine specialists from the state of Idaho. You have seen closure of labor and delivery wards. And there is a health care crisis going on in Idaho because of some of the most restrictive abortion bans in the United States.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jennifer, in your case, you were, as you mentioned, forced to leave the state. You traveled to Oregon, some six hours away, to receive an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Just help us understand what that process was like to figure out where to go and to organize it all and to pay for it all as well.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADKINS: Right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you`re forced to in that kind of situation immediately start scrambling and kind of ignore the grief that you`re experiencing, knowing that you`re not going to take home a baby, and instead juggle logistics and, like you said, coordinate the travel, find the funds to travel and call a bunch of different clinics that you have never called before,that you don`t have a relationship with.\par \par \uc2 And, especially when you`re not referred by your doctor, they don`t know anything about your story. They don`t know anything about you as a patient. And so you have to explain yourself over and over again and try to find appointment availability and those types of logistics.\par \par \uc2 And it`s the last thing that grieving family and a bereaved family is wanting to do is walk through all of these mechanical logistics.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Can I ask, Jennifer, at any point in all of this, did you ever consider carrying the pregnancy further, seeing if you could carry to term?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADKINS: You know, it was it was explained to me that even if I attempted to continue the pregnancy that my baby would not survive to term, and that I was likely to miscarry.\par \par \uc2 And miscarriage itself has risks of certain things like hemorrhaging and sepsis and other risks. I also understood that I could develop something called mirror syndrome and develop high blood pressure and edema, which is swelling under the skin. And so, even if I attempted, it would be a risk to my health.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Marc, the goal of this lawsuit, lay this out for us. And what would it change in Idaho and what would be the broader impact, especially given this context we have of an incoming administration that signaled there could be further restrictions on abortion access?\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON: Look, what we`re asking for in this lawsuit is pretty simple. We`re asking that doctors be able to practice medicine as they have been trained to.\par \par \uc2 And that includes abortion care where such care is within the standard of care to treat a patient and prevent them -- to prevent a serious health risk or a threat to their lives. And the laws currently are inadequate for doctors to feel comfortable with providing care like they have been trained to do.\par \par \uc2 I do think this case -- the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  has filed similar cases in Tennessee and in Texas. And this is important at a national level because it highlights what could happen if there is a national abortion ban enacted by the new Congress at any gestation.\par \par \uc2 You know, I have heard politicians throw out, well, how about 15 weeks, a 15-week abortion ban or 16-week abortion ban as a compromise?\par \par \uc2 These medical complications that arise that are opposing serious health risks to patients often, they`re arising at 17, 18, 19 weeks in pregnancy. So what you`re seeing in Idaho will go on tour all across the United States and will come to every state, regardless of the abortion laws within that state, if we have a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is Marc Hearron from the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  and the lead plaintiff in their Idaho case, Jennifer Adkins.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to you both. Really appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER ADKINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: On this Veterans Day, we celebrate the approximately 6 percent of the population who`ve served in our nation`s military, a number that`s been declining for the past four decades.\par \par \uc2 In cities and towns across the country, the nation`s oldest veterans group, the \uc2 VFW\uc2 , or Veterans of Foreign Wars, provide spaces known as posts for these remaining war veterans to connect and socialize. But as their numbers dwindle, the \uc2 VFW\uc2  seeks to expand its ranks by looking to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Sy has that story.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: As the Ohio State Buckeyes sail to victory over Perdue, veterans and their families mingle over a potluck. It`s part of the weekly tailgate at the Whitehall VFW Post just outside of Columbus, Ohio. It`s a little more inclusive than the traditional \uc2 VFW\uc2  post, where old veterans trade war stories over a beer.\par \par \uc2 But that`s the point.\par \par \uc2 SGT. 1ST CLASS CHRISTINE CURRY (RET.), Whitehall Memorial Post 8794: We realized that to reach out to that younger generation, they`re family- oriented. Then we need to be family-oriented.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Christine Curry and her husband, Richard, served in Iraq and were the first post-9/11 veterans to lead this \uc2 VFW\uc2  location. They have seen their membership rise in the last few years simply by opening their doors to veterans who didn`t feel welcome before.\par \par \uc2 COL. RICHARD CURRY (RET.): Whitehall Memorial Post 8794: We came here and made sure that we were a welcoming place not only for families, but obviously for people of all beliefs and all races. And we have a good mix here.\par \par \uc2 Ray Rodenfels was a Korean War veteran, recently passed away. We miss him. He`s a good member.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Richard Curry says when, he took over the post, there had been a slow decline in membership for decades. Since the draft ended, there are simply fewer military service members. And to qualify to join, one has to have served in a conflict.\par \par \uc2 COL. RICHARD CURRY (RET.): We have lost a lot of veterans from World War II era. We`re losing the Korean War veterans. So we have declined over the years because obviously people have died. Off.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Now in its 125th year, the \uc2 VFW\uc2  was founded by veterans from the Spanish-American War and Philippine insurrection to support and advocate for those who fought for their country.\par \par \uc2 But since its peak in 1992, the \uc2 VFW\uc2  has lost nearly half its membership nationally, now at nearly 1.5 million. And hundreds of posts across the country have closed.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY DYMOND, VFW: Veterans are veterans, but just like any other group, needs change over time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Brittany Dymond is a Navy veteran on the VFW`s communications team.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY DYMOND: A lot of posts are getting really, really creative with how they appeal to newer veterans. There`s one post that has a community garden. There are other posts that are introducing really amazing programs like yoga and meditation. Thank\par \par \uc2 MAN: you to the \uc2 VFW\uc2  for hosting this.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Rather than sitting at bars, modern veterans often connect over video games, says Dymond. So the \uc2 VFW\uc2  started a gaming community for its members.\par \par \uc2 Posts have also increased community service opportunities, like providing relief to communities hit by Hurricane Helene last month in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY DYMOND: A big through line that unites veterans is a core of service. As veterans, we can just fall back on -- essentially, it`s kind of muscle memory.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: According to the \uc2 VFW\uc2 , these efforts have worked. Like the Whitehall VFW, posts nationally have recently seen their membership grow. Vietnam veterans still account for the bulk of this membership; 85-year-old Sidney Logan has frequented the white hall \uc2 VFW\uc2  posts since he retired from the Air Force in the late 70s.\par \par \uc2 Logan spent six years in Vietnam flying equipment and deceased and wounded troops across the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 SIDNEY LOGAN, Vietnam Veteran: You have bad memories. You have good memories. And it`s a place to talk to people, because some of the nightmares, you bring 30 or 40 human remains across the Pacific. And it`s a place to vent oftentimes.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ALFRED LIPPHARDT, \uc2 VFW\uc2 : Those smoky, beer-drinking bars, there`s times that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Alfred Lipphardt is a Vietnam veteran and the national commander of the \uc2 VFW\uc2 . He says, even with the efforts to reach out to a new generation, the old ways still have their place.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ALFRED LIPPHARDT: There are times when a combat veteran needs to talk to another combat veteran. When one veteran meets another veteran, we pretty much understand. We know each other.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: And so while families cheer for the Buckeyes over homemade chili, there`s still a spot at the bar to meet veteran to veteran and share a drink and a story.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Stephanie Sy.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 For all of us here at the "PBS News Hour," thanks for spending part of your evening with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241112ekbb00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 5, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Sy, John Yang, William Brangham, Laura Barron-Lopez,  Lisa Desjardins, Jonathan Capehart, David Brooks, Judy Woodruff, Geoff  Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 10,855 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And welcome back to PBS News` special live coverage on election night. I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 It is now 9:00 p.m. Eastern. Polls have closed in three more of the biggest presidential battlegrounds, which include Arizona, Michigan, and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We can at this hour call several more states for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Those include Nebraska, which has five electoral votes, but, of course, also awards several of them by congressional districts. So Vice President Harris is trying to win one of those votes out of this reliably red state.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump has also won the states of Louisiana, North Dakota, South Dakota, and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has claimed the states of New York. Polls have also closed this hour, but it`s still too early to call the winner in these following states, Colorado, Iowa, Kansas, Minnesota, New Mexico, and Texas.\par \par \uc2 So if we take a look at the electoral map, that`s where it stands at 9:00 p.m. Eastern as the vote is still coming in and still being counted.\par \par \uc2 We mentioned Arizona as a race we couldn`t call yet. It`s one of the big battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Lucky for us, our friend and colleague Stephanie Sy is at one election center in the most populous county, Maricopa County. That`s in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Well, Geoff, polls have closed here in battleground to Arizona. That means, if you`re in line by now, you will be able to vote, or if you have dropped off your ballot at 7:00 p.m., you will have that ballot counted.\par \par \uc2 I will want to give you an update now on Apache County. That is on the Navajo Nation. The Navajo Nation reported that they were having problems at many of their polling places earlier this morning. A judge has now granted the Navajo Nation`s request to keep their polls open for two more hours. That is critical. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won Apache County by 66 -- he had 66 percent of the vote in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Most of the voters, 43,000 of them in Apache County are Navajo. So they will be given two more hours. Everywhere else, the polls are closed now in Arizona. What happens next? Well, we`re at the tabulation center in Maricopa County. The largest competitive jurisdiction and voting in the country. What will happen now, the polls will close.\par \par \uc2 There is new legislation in Arizona that requires that any of the dropped- off ballots today, those envelopes are counted. Then the memory cards holding the data from their tabulation machines at polling places throughout the county will end up here tonight, and they will begin to both signature-verify those early dropped-off ballots, dropped off late, as well as begin to tabulate the in-person voting today.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Stephanie, thank you for laying out that process. I think it`s so important for folks to understand how it works in each of those key states in particular.\par \par \uc2 I do want to ask you about something that relates back to what we heard from the reporter in Pennsylvania, which was this idea about election denialism and misinformation making its way through the system. \uc2 John Yang\uc2  was also reporting out on that from Pennsylvania, because we know Arizona back in 2020 was really the hub of a lot of that election denialism and misinformation.\par \par \uc2 It led to threats against election workers and others. Are you seeing any of that, any of those efforts again this year?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: You know, what`s so interesting is, you talk about 2020 and Maricopa County the day after Election Day being ground zero for a lot of misinformation, as well as election fraud conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 But you have to remember that, in the 2022 midterms, the Republicans that went up against Democrats who were Trump-endorsed election deniers pretty much all lost. That`s why you have in this historically red state the top three state offices all held by Democrats now.\par \par \uc2 Now, several of those election deniers in 2022 are running again. For example, Kari Lake, who lost the governor`s race, has never conceded that she lost that race, by the way, is in the Arizona Senate race with Democrat \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 . She has really played down her election denialism in this election cycle. In fact, I saw that she was asked today whether she would accept the results of this election, as she said she would.\par \par \uc2 So we`re not hearing Republican candidates necessarily lead with that in this particular cycle, because it was not successful for those candidates in 2022. Now, there are certainly polls that show that there are a large number of voters here in Arizona who still have questions and distrust of the election system.\par \par \uc2 That`s why election officials here have tried so hard to get out ahead of that, a lot of them, by the way, elected Republican county election officials that have been doing really hard work to get ahead of that. We`re really just not hearing that be the leading issue here.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes, that`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Stephanie, earlier today, I was seeing long lines on television at \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: \uc2 ASU\uc2  has, like, what, four campuses in metro Phoenix. Do you have a sense of the Gen Z voter turnout?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Yes. Oh, gosh, in the early turnout, Gen Z was very low. And we saw a preponderance of the early ballots coming from Arizona voters that were 65-plus. And the younger turnout was very low, 20, 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 I saw the same video on the campus, the main campus of \uc2 ASU\uc2  today during lunchtime. There were very, very long lines. And so it`ll be really interesting to see where those voters landed. I did speak to two \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  students this week. They were both voting for Harris, and they were voting Democrat down the line.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Democrats in Arizona and throughout the country believe that younger voters favor Harris. So it`ll be interesting to see where that demographic lands today. We`re also watching another group, Geoff, and that is that out of all of the voters that turned out to vote in-person in Maricopa today, the biggest group right now, our latest data, which is from an hour ago, is independents.\par \par \uc2 That`s unaffiliated voters. So even though Republicans have a huge advantage in registration in Arizona, 200,000 registrations more than the Democrats, there`s a large group of independents that turned out today. The second highest group that turned out today in Maricopa County were Republicans. Democratic turnout from our latest numbers were only about 30 percent in-person.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Steph, before we let you go, I got to ask, because Arizona is one of those 10 states that has an abortion measure on the ballot. What`s your sense of how much of an impact that could have?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: You know, it`s so interesting.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to three women in a row at one of the polling stations today, all of whom were passionate about the abortion issue. In fact, two of them were women in their mid-70s who said that is the most important issue for them. One of them even shared with me that 40 years ago she had a miscarriage and they had to do a DNC.\par \par \uc2 And she would have lost her life if they had not been able to do the D&C procedure. And tears welled up in her eyes. That issue is so pervasive among the women I spoke to. Having said that, it is really hard to stereotype by identity, I think, a lot of the voters this year in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to another voter. She`s a Latina woman who identified as a Christian, but said she is pro-choice and voted for the proposition that will legalize abortion again and make it a constitutional right in the state of Arizona. But she voted for Trump at the top of the ticket because she said, "Even though I believe that women should have this choice," she believes that her predominant issue when it comes to voting for the president is the economy and crime.\par \par \uc2 And even as a Latina woman, she said she connects crime to immigration and illegal immigration. But this is the interesting thing, Amna and Geoff. She said she didn`t want to talk about it. She was what I would call the shy Trump voter.\par \par \uc2 So she talked to me readily, but she did not want to be on camera and she said, "I don`t want to talk to my friends about it, but this is how I`m voting."\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Stephanie Sy reporting for us from Maricopa County, Arizona, the battleground state too early to call even though polls have closed there.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, now we can make another race call at this hour. The state of Ohio has gone for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . When you take a look at the national map at this hour, just past 9:00 here on the East Coast, brings the current electoral vote stand for former President Trump to 137, Vice President Harris with 99.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the studio with our panel.\par \par \uc2 Judy Woodruff, can I just get you to weigh in on what we heard from Stephanie Sy reporting on? Because I think one of the questions you mentioned we want to try to answer tonight is, how much of an animating issue is this abortion rights issue? Will this matter, make a dent in even in red states?\par \par \uc2 This idea she mentioned that for older women in particular, it could bring them out in larger droves because they remember a life and what it took to get to those reproductive rights in the first place.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And you`re right, Amna. I think I misspoke earlier and said 13 -- it`s on the ballot in states. It`s on the ballot in 10 states, as you said.\par \par \uc2 There`s been so much attention and news coverage in the last couple of years, but especially during this election cycle, about not just abortion, but about women`s experiences. And you see it in the advertising that has come out of the Harris campaign, but these other advocacy groups have come forward with as -- and we just heard this from Stephanie -- very personal stories, where women have been willing to speak about their experience.\par \par \uc2 We saw it at the Democratic Convention in Chicago. So, again, it`s such a personal issue. I think for -- it`s clearly going to make a difference for many women voters and men. Is it going to be the issue that makes the difference? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think we`re still waiting. You heard -- you did hear Stephanie say that a woman said, it`s important to me, but not as important as the economy.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: It`s an interesting point, and especially the degree to which it`s an animating issue for men. And we often don`t talk enough about how men see this issue, how girl dads might be driven to vote on this issue or Dobbs dad, the Dobbs dads, to use that phrase.\par \par \uc2 How do you see that, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Yes, I mean, it used to be -- I haven`t looked at the whole -- back 10 years ago, women were more pro-life than men were. And so it was a mistake to say it was a women`s issue, that it`s both genders are involved, obviously.\par \par \uc2 You know, I would -- the one thing I`d say, it`s not just Dobbs. If the Republicans had said, OK, we`re going to take the states and we`re going to do -- where the median voter is, I think, is like a 20-to-25-week ban.\par \par \uc2 And if Republicans had said that, OK, we will be roughly where the median voter is on this issue, legal until 20 weeks, say, it would be a very different landscape, I think. It`s when they had states going to six weeks or zero weeks, basically. Like, then you`re really drifting away from the median voter rule.\par \par \uc2 And the median voter rule is get to where the middle is and you will be OK. And the Republicans just ran away from that. And it`s hurting them. As Amy said earlier, Florida`s now a deep red -- it`s a pretty deep red state, 10- point Trump margin. And that initiative on abortion is 57 percent support.\par \par \uc2 So that shows there are an awful lot of Trump Republicans who are like, we`re not going there.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, we know, as Judy mentioned as well, 10 states have different ballot measures related to abortion rights tonight. Florida is among them.\par \par \uc2 And we can now report in Florida there is no wins on the right to abortion there. Abortion rights will not be enshrined in the state constitution. It got 57 percent support, but fell short of the 60 percent needed.\par \par \uc2 And, Amy Walter, this is a noteworthy moment in many ways, because so far, any time we have seen an abortion measure on the ballot, even in a state like Kansas, it has passed the bar.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, The Cook Political Report: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 And so the 60 percent threshold was really the challenge. And the governor of the state, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he had campaigned very hard to make this 60 percent threshold in place. Remember, this is the six-week ban that is in place was one that came from his administration. And so it is a win for DeSantis in that way, that this is something that went through a Republican legislature.\par \par \uc2 He obviously is a sitting governor. He campaigned strongly about turning back this amendment. And he was successful. And it is also just a reminder. I mean, it was not that long ago we were talking about who was going to win Florida.\par \par \uc2 This was the bellwether of bellwethers, and how dramatically it has shifted in such a short period of time from a real toss-up state to one where Republicans have just been really dominant up and down the ticket.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: It`s interesting. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voted today, he was asked by a reporter how he voted on the abortion measure and said something to the effect of, well, let`s not talk about that, and kept walking.\par \par \uc2 We have one more race call to share with you. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has won the state of Texas with its 40 electoral votes. Let`s take a look at the overall electoral vote map at this hour. We see there \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the moment with 177 votes to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` 99. That is a number that will shift over the course of this evening and into the early morning.\par \par \uc2 When we talk about reproductive rights, you were making the point in Politics Monday that the way the Harris campaign has framed it in many instances, it`s less about abortion and more about this issue of freedom.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And that has been resonant for a subset of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And I think it was very purposeful that she did not talk about abortion rights or even reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 What she talked a lot about is not going back, turning the page, freedom, this idea when we talk about so many of these issues, whether we`re talking about what does it mean for you when you say that this is an election about democracy?\par \par \uc2 For some people, that`s -- including conservatives, they say, look, this is about freedom of my speech. I feel like liberals, Democrats are actively trying to ratchet back conservatives and their free speech or I feel like my freedom is impinged when you are trying to make it harder to get a gun, right, in the same way that you will get other voters saying what freedom means to me is not whether I want an abortion, need an abortion, talk about this issue.\par \par \uc2 It`s really more the idea of somebody putting restrictions on. And that`s - - a couple of weeks ago I sent a note out to all the people that I communicate with who are strategists and campaign types, and I said, so if you had to say to a group of eighth graders what this election was about, you can`t go in with your partisan hat on, right? You had to say what is this election about.\par \par \uc2 What was so fascinating was that they came back really with a similar thing to what we have been talking about at this table of, what is at stake is not -- it wasn`t really about -- this isn`t about the economy necessarily, about abortion necessarily.\par \par \uc2 It`s this idea about control, having some sense of control over their lives, feeling as if things are so out of control right now in the world that this person, electing this person will make it worse and electing this person, maybe it won`t make it that much better, but it won`t make it worse.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right, let`s shift our focus back to Pennsylvania for a moment and return to "PBS News Weekend" anchor \uc2 John Yang\uc2 , who`s in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, at a ballot processing center.\par \par \uc2 John, what`s the latest where you are?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG\uc2 : Well, they`re still working on the ballots here.\par \par \uc2 They have gotten the most of the mail ballots counted and posted and now they`re going to turn to the in-person ballots. Actually, the spokesman for the Board of Elections just gave me a little briefing over here. Producer Sam Weber is listening in, so I may have more for you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 But also earlier I talked about some of the long lines around colleges and universities in Lehigh -- at \uc2 Lehigh University\uc2  -- yes, \uc2 Lehigh University\uc2 , which is in Bethlehem, which straddles Northampton County and Lehigh County.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and the Democrats went to a county judge asking him to extend voting hours. He rejected their request. His reasoning was that there are people in line at and if they`re in line at 8:00, they can vote. They will be able to go in and vote. It may take them a while, but the Board of Elections is sending more people and more equipment to that ward. It`s Ward 3 in Bethlehem to try to speed things up.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, that is \uc2 John Yang\uc2  for us in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 John, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head over to the super screen and Lisa Desjardins, who can give us a deeper look at some of those results coming in from Pennsylvania at this hour -- Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: First of all, Amy, you`re welcome over here any time. Any time. Come on over. All right.\par \par \uc2 OK, Pennsylvania, for a lot of people, the whole ball game, 20 percent of the vote now in Pennsylvania. So most of the vote not in, not by a long shot. But right now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is up by 19 points. Now let`s take a look at where we`re starting to get more idea of what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 This is Berks County. This is that county with 25 percent Hispanic population, including some Puerto Rican. Right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is up there. Let`s see how that compares in the past. He`s actually lost some ground at the moment in this county over his past two performances there. That`s something Democrats want to see.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, more important probably for Democrats are these big counties right here around Philadelphia that we keep talking about. Harris continues to do well there. Look at these large margins, Philadelphia. Bucks County, just 14 percent of the vote in. Bucks County, parts of it, as Amy has said, I think, are very purple.\par \par \uc2 Some of them are red. So this is a place we will be watching very carefully. Allegheny County, this is a huge Democratic county. Let`s see how much of the vote is in there, 40 percent of the vote. So this is a place where \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  can continue to make up ground. But a lot of these counties down here, Lancaster, here`s Franklin, all of these kinds of places, Adams, are places where we think -- this is a red county right here, Lancaster County.\par \par \uc2 These are early votes. So this could very well flip coming up. I want us to do a quick check-in on the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Where are we right now?\par \par \uc2 Here we have from races called and races that were not on the board this year, 46 Republican Senate seats. That, of course, is only four seats away from 50, which could be a majority, depending on who wins the presidency. This is where we expected things to be at this moment, because West Virginia has been called.\par \par \uc2 This is the first flip of the night in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . It was expected. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  has retired there. \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2 , the governor, has picked up that seat. Now, the race you all have been talking about quite a lot is Ohio`s \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race.\par \par \uc2 I want to say something about what`s going on here. Right now, \uc2 Bernie Moreno\uc2 , the Republican, is leading incumbent Senate Democrat and banking chairman, as you have noted, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . But just a half-hour ago, we were watching this and it was the other way around.\par \par \uc2 So this is a race that I think we could see go back and forth over the next couple of hours. Right now, 56 percent of the vote is in there. And we are seeing some of these Democratic areas come in, Cuyahoga County up here, right here on Lake Erie, all of this starting to fill in.\par \par \uc2 This is more of a red, purple area that we haven`t seen numbers from. While we`re in Ohio, let`s check out an important House race as well. This is Ohio`s 13th District, right up here, Akron, Summit County. It`s redrawn. It`s a sort of a new map up there, 42 percent of the expected vote in.\par \par \uc2 Emilia Sykes, freshman House representative, someone who Republicans were hoping to unseat, right now, she`s up by a nice, comfortable margin. But, again, we still have the majority of the vote to come in. Kevin Coughlin and his crew are probably watching right now, hoping that it goes their way.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, Lisa Desjardins, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now and take a deeper look at the state of Wisconsin. We have been checking in with our colleagues and public media reporters across the country.\par \par \uc2 In battleground Wisconsin, we`re joined by Zac Schultz of Wisconsin PBS. Of course, we`re still awaiting results for both the presidential and Senate races there.\par \par \uc2 Zac, good to see you. And thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Just give us a sense of what your hearing. Polls have closed there. What do we know about turnout and what does it look like on the ground so far?\par \par \uc2 ZAC SCHULTZ, PBS Wisconsin: Well, we spent the day traveling across the state, starting up in Green Bay and coming all the way back down to Madison. And the one thing we ran into everywhere was rain and voters waiting in rain. Their turnout was really good in a lot of the places.\par \par \uc2 We hit some rural places that had long lines. Ripon, the birthplace of the Republican Party, or so it lays a claim to be so, had lines stretching through their halls and out and around the block, people passing umbrellas back to wait it out up to a half-hour at times so people could get in and vote.\par \par \uc2 There was a really large early in-person absentee vote in Wisconsin that lasted a couple weeks. It`s closed now, but we`re not quite to 2020 pandemic-style turnout for absentee ballots, but it`s very high, higher than the normal presidential.\par \par \uc2 And what that means, it`s just going to be a long time before we have results from a lot of these communities. The city of Milwaukee does a central count for all of their 100,000-plus absentee ballots, and they actually had a tabulator air error in the day. So 30,000 that they`d already counted, they`re going to redo.\par \par \uc2 So we`re looking at late into the night, probably tomorrow morning before we have results. Some of these smaller communities, we`re not expecting to have as many absentee ballots. Some of them, more than 53 percent of registered voters, went absentee. I checked in at one. They had nine people to process 8,000 ballots.\par \par \uc2 They`re expecting a long night as well.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Zac, you mentioned some of those larger metro areas and counties. Milwaukee and Dane tend to come in later, and as we expected, it could be a very long night for you as well. But help us understand what other counties you`re watching much more closely as the night progresses to give you a sense of where this might go, especially if Wisconsin expects another incredibly close margin of victory for either candidate.\par \par \uc2 ZAC SCHULTZ: Well, one county that we have looked at throughout this election in the last couple of cycles is Ozaukee County. It`s just to the north of Milwaukee. It has a rapidly growing Democratic population, that typical, educated, college-educated work force, turning blue in a lot of those areas.\par \par \uc2 And it used to be a stronghold of the Republican WOW counties, Washington, Waukesha, and Ozaukee. If you have ever heard the Wisconsin WOW counties, it was the deepest red traditional Republicans. And it`s really blood a lot of voters in the Trump era. And the question there is whether it`ll keep going.\par \par \uc2 We were there earlier this election cycle, and they couldn`t keep up with the yard signs, getting 1,000 in at a time for the Harris campaign. So Democrats are really looking there. As for the rest of the state where Wisconsinites are -- or Republicans are looking, it`s Western Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 They have got a very competitive congressional race there, and they`re really hoping that the rural areas of Wisconsin turn out strong for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s where they`re going to be watching for, a better margin than last time around.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes, Zac, this is the time the rest of the country gets to talk about the WOW counties. This is our favorite time of year.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to ask you about the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race as well. I believe you`re at the campaign of Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  for her watch party tonight. She`s running for a third term there. She`s being challenged by Republican \uc2 Eric Hovde\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There`s been tens of millions of dollars spent in -- on ads there. And this is really a seat Republicans have been targeting. Can you give us a sense of where that race stands?\par \par \uc2 ZAC SCHULTZ: Well, the latest polling shows Baldwin up a couple points, depending on which poll you`re looking at. Now, the question really is, will she be able to run ahead of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the top of the ticket?\par \par \uc2 Because, historically, especially in 2018, which was an off-year, it was an off-cycle for her, she ran way ahead of all the other Democrats on the ticket, doing really well in some of those rural Republican areas. And the question is, will that happen again or will that race get nationalized in the same way a lot of the other races are getting nationalized and voters are tying them together, or will they separate those out?\par \par \uc2 That`s part of Eric Hovde`s campaign strategy is telling people through his ads, she`s not the same \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  that she was that you voted for before, trying to split some of those off, whereas Baldwin spent her campaign traveling every corner of the state. Even when the DNC was in Chicago, she spent the first part of that week back in the farthest corners, the most rural northern corners of Wisconsin, trying to court those voters and say, hey, I`m still the same one you voted for six years ago.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Zac Schultz from PBS Wisconsin joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Zac, thank you. Appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn over now to Lisa Desjardins for a closer look. And pick up where Zac left off there, Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Take us through what we know about Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Wisconsin, this -- guys, I know we have been waiting for results, but this is the part, fasten your seat belts. I think we`re going to start getting more and more results, including here from Wisconsin, including from those WOW counties that you were just talking about.\par \par \uc2 Look at this one that we heard him talk about right here of Ozaukee County. There`s Milwaukee, first of all, to give people a sense of place. Here we go. There`s Milwaukee right next -- up top there, Ozaukee County. We have not gotten any results from there yet. We will watch it closely.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, they still -- they have not been -- had the polls closed there long. But Waukesha County next door, look at this important county in Wisconsin. Right now, we have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tracking with his 2016 results there, outperforming 2020.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is somewhere in between. She`s kind of between what \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  did and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So we have got to watch this carefully. This is good news, early good news in Waukesha County, just one county, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While we`re here, let`s do a quick check-in on Pennsylvania, the big one, 25 percent of the vote in. Harris is still retaining her lead. But, as I say, we will just keep watching it carefully.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Lisa Desjardins, thank you so much. Always good to get a deeper look at Wisconsin there.\par \par \uc2 Amy, this is the part of the night I think you have been dreading, when I bring up Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And see what it means.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Because we know at 9:00, obviously, polls closed in a number of key states, Arizona, Michigan and Wisconsin we are tracking.\par \par \uc2 But we spoke earlier about older women voters in particular, older white women voters. We talked about that poll from over the weekend from a renowned well-respected poll sir in Iowa that sent political \uc2 Twitter\uc2  all a flutter.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Will we know soon what that really means on the ground?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Whether or not -- what that means.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is -- right? This was a big outlier.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And I think people were taking it -- and what it may show -- and this is sort of interesting as we`re watching some of these states come in. As I said, the states that are not tied to the big story, which is they`re not one of the seven swing states...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... behaving a little bit differently than we have seen them in the past.\par \par \uc2 So a state like Florida going even more red, but a state like Virginia or New Hampshire is likely to be a smaller Democratic margin in those states, even though they are pretty blue states. So what we have in Iowa is a state that, if it`s going to behave the way, say, some of these other states have done, which is, they`re not sort of following -- or when they don`t have a -- when they`re not focused on by the presidential candidates, they behave maybe a little bit differently.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: But there`s nothing that I`m seeing right now, whether it`s out of Ohio or whether it`s out of Wisconsin or these other states, to suggest that Iowa is suddenly going to pop up and be this one great big outlier, right, that even as everywhere else is sort of tracking with what we assumed it would be in terms of the demographic and how these states have performed in the past, that would be quite remarkable if Iowa did not.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: David, we didn`t get to talk about it because it came out over the weekend. What`s your take?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Yes, I was a little -- she`s a great pollster. I was a little dubious. Everybody has rogue polls.\par \par \uc2 If I could just bring us to Texas...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : ... we have got 65 percent of the vote in and right now Trump is up by 11 points. If he maintains a double-digit lead, there`s no way Ted Cruz loses.\par \par \uc2 And so the -- Texas is always about to turn blue over the course of my lifetime. It looks like we will still be waiting. So this margin in Texas is this kind of impressive.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Back to Iowa and the poll. I don`t know if it`s an outlier or not. I`m not I`m not a pollster. I will look to you, Amy, to say whether that is that is the case.\par \par \uc2 But I think it was a bit of a political earthquake and something that at least among Democrats was hopeful, not necessarily that -- not that the vice president would win Iowa, but what it portends for the rest for the rest of the country, in particular, demographics.\par \par \uc2 I mean, for me, it was akin to the abortion vote in Kansas, and the fact that ruby-red Kansas voted to protect abortion rights sent a signal to me about how important that issue would be across the country. And so the Iowa poll, the Selzer poll, to come out and to show the vice president a head by 3 or 4 made me wonder, what does that mean for the battleground states?\par \par \uc2 What -- more importantly, what does it mean to white -- to me, for white women voters? And so with all these results coming in, the picture is being filled in.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Picture is being filled in.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And the takeaway of the Selzer poll was that \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  had expanded her margins among independent white women and older white women. In order to win this evening, she would have to really historically run up those numbers, one would imagine, right?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, so this is where my math skills really are a problem.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Because they`re very low.\par \par \uc2 Again, as many of us -- I won`t speak for anybody else here, but those of us who got into this profession did so because we did not do particularly well on the math SAT.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I was told there would be no math, correct.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 And so we like to do the other part, the writing piece, not so much the math piece. What is interesting is -- and let`s see if this plays out, but in state after state, women are making up -- they do traditionally make up a bigger percent of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 But so in a state like Pennsylvania, women are making up, say, 54 percent of the electorate. She`s winning women by nine points, but winning or -- but losing men by nine points. But because women make up a bigger percent of the overall electorate, that is a benefit, right?\par \par \uc2 So losing men by nine, if it were 50/50, and that would be a bigger problem. But what is also pretty clear is that it is not that -- we`re not seeing that she`s winning women by 15 points, while there`s also an increase in the number of women who turn out, right? So we have more women that are turning out. The margin isn`t dramatic, but that ultimately could be the difference.\par \par \uc2 This is why I say the math becomes the problem here, because if at the end of the day it`s not that she wins them by a bigger margin, but they made up a bigger percent of the electorate, that would matter a lot.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Kevin and Faiz, how are you looking at that gender gap?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: Well, first of all, I have to get my word in on the Iowa poll.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Please. Please.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: I mean, look, the Iowa poll had everybody shook, because I think the fact that there are so many independents and women breaking towards Harris, I think, was a surprise for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 But I always think back to Mike Murphy. Mike Murphy, who is a grand wizard on the political strategy side, on the Republican side, had taught me. He said, if you wanted -- if aliens wanted to invade Earth, one of the first things they should do is buy up all the pollsters, because you set the zeitgeist in one direction with just one little poll like that.\par \par \uc2 And what I tried to tell everybody after that poll was, like, we have an election Tuesday. We`re going to find out what happened and how -- and whether or not these campaigns matter. And what you have is billions of dollars pointed by these elections, pointed by these campaigns at engaging these voters and trying to make the case to them on these elections.\par \par \uc2 And I think the thing that we`re seeing is that the economy mattered, the issues like abortion matter, issues like immigration matter. And those independent voters are more likely to vote based on their station in their local economy than they are on what some Iowa pollster tells them which way they should be breaking.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s just a more of a reflection that these campaigns actually matter.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: ... what they are breaking.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: Well, you know this about me. I`m interested in class politics. As much as there`s gender politics to discuss, class politics is always at the top of my mind.\par \par \uc2 And it pains me to watch more Black working-class people, more Latino working-class people, at least by exits, and I think maybe we will see tonight moving away from the Democratic Party. It`s not great. We have to analyze how and why that`s happening.\par \par \uc2 I think you would win without them, but I think it`s better that we win with them. And one of the reasons is because the Democratic Party has long been believed and understood to be the protectors of government, the executors of government, believers in government. And to believe in government right now, there`s a lot of dissatisfaction with it, feeling the government is corrupt and broken and not delivering in a disruptive way for people who have been left behind.\par \par \uc2 We talk about freedom. We have talked about abortion freedom, abortion rights freedom, but there`s economic freedom that play very much on people`s mind, whether -- do I have the liberty as a middle-class person to make it in this economy?\par \par \uc2 And the Democratic message on it has been cautious. It has been status quo under -- and I think we got to continue to push that envelope. We can`t leave the playing field to say, OK, we`re going to do abortion. We`re going to fight for democracy. But on economics, we`re going to sound status quo.\par \par \uc2 You sound like just like the disrupter that we need to be and say -- I get that there`s some working-class people who say Biden`s going to -- or -- sorry -- Trump`s going to come in, he`s going to disrupt government. And yet he`s standing here with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the richest guy in the world. He`s going to turn over government.\par \par \uc2 It seems like it`s going to make some kind of co-presidency if they were to win, right, that he`d be running government officials. How can you not fight that fight and explain to working-class people, no, no, no, there`s a difference of a choice here?\par \par \uc2 And I wish -- that I want more of a Democratic Party that`s on the class issues as much as I do.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, I`d be curious your take because of the messaging on the economy, because we have talked almost exclusively about women, about abortion.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: But if you look at the ads run, both by the Harris campaign and by her super PAC, it was almost all on middle class, helping the middle class, getting the economy back on track, getting prices down.\par \par \uc2 What do you think? Was that the wrong economic message?\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: No, no, that...\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Or was it that they should have been...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: It didn`t go far enough, in your view?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Far enough.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: Here just -- that was good.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: And I think hopefully we will win because of it too. Also, the number one poll issue polled on the economy in terms of both Trump and Harris putting out ideas, you know what the number one was? Trump saying no tax on Social Security.\par \par \uc2 Breaks my heart that, on Social Security, are you kidding me, that you`re - - that Donald Trump`s going to outflank you? The Democratic response on that has been Project 2025. We will do the X, Y, and Z to Social Security, undermine it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has proposed budgets that would cut Social Security. All true.\par \par \uc2 But what was left on the table is say, we`re going to expand Social Security. Guess what? Here`s a plan. Here`s a proposal. Here`s how we`re going to fight. Here`s how we`re going to change that, right?\par \par \uc2 Because if you read, what does no tax on Social Security sounds like? To -- it codes like an increase in the benefit, right? And you`re like, well, we got to offer. We step up to that and fight that fight.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We are going to return to this. There`s a lot more to talk about on all this.\par \par \uc2 But I have to break in now, because just past 9:30 here on the East Coast, and we can share another race call when it comes to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And that is that the state of Maryland has been won by the Democratic candidate, Angela Alsobrooks. That`s a Democrat hold, the first time, by the way, that we will have two Black women in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 We reported earlier that \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  of Delaware had won her race as well. Here`s where control of the Senate stands at this hour. Republicans have 46 seats. They have gained one. Democrats, of course, lost one, and now have 36 seats.\par \par \uc2 And, Jonathan, guys, we`re going to talk about the politics of all this in a minute. We`re going to talk about what it took for her to beat a popular moderate Republican governor there in \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 . But we can`t lose the history of this moment, Jonathan, two Black women for the first time in this country`s history serving in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  together.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : And elected on the same day.\par \par \uc2 This is huge. And so no matter what happens tonight at the presidential level, and I know from a lot of my friends there`s a lot of angstiness out there. But akin to 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got elected, but so did \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in 2016. She was elected to the Senate.\par \par \uc2 This is a big day for those states, but also for that chamber, because it`s always good when there are more women in institutions, and it gets real good when there are more Black women in institutions, because you get -- you just get a different perspective, a different way of looking at things.\par \par \uc2 And it is my hope that both senator-elect Alsobrooks, and senator-elect Blunt Rochester will bring all of themselves to the table -- to help push back against what`s happening to the Senate, which is a Trumpification of the Senate, and a lot of the Newt Gingrich bad boy ways of the House moving into the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And I hope that those two women and Senator Kim, senator-elect Kim of New Jersey, another history-making elected official now in the Senate, will improve things there. It`s my hope.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Judy, your take on this.\par \par \uc2 You have covered a lot of these races. These are big moments worth noting.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: It is a big moment. And it is worth noting this election.\par \par \uc2 And we know \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  put a lot of money and a lot of effort and he is a very popular former governor, that his belief was that would overcome, even though Maryland, we know, is an incredibly blue state. But the headwinds were just too much.\par \par \uc2 I will say that the Senate that these women are going to be serving in is looking more and more like it -- we don`t know yet, but it might not -- the Democrats might not hold on to their majority. We don`t know yet, but being in the minority and depending on who`s president and dealing with appointments confirmations, it`s a different place when you`re in the minority.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : I will just say, I think abortion was a giant issue for Alsobrooks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : We all -- those of us who live here saw a lot of those ads, grainy pictures of \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  saying things that make him look pro-life and then immediately would come out a Hogan ad, don`t worry. I`m pro- choice. I`m pro-choice. Don`t worry. Don`t worry.\par \par \uc2 But she really hit that really hard.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, and, actually, I think her most important message at the very beginning was simply, you all, he`s a Republican and he`d be in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And her ads -- and this to me for all of us olds around the table that remember there were times when people would say this is a Maryland Republican. We can send a Maryland Republican. It`s not a national Republican or a Montana Democrat. A West Virginia Democrat isn`t a national.\par \par \uc2 Those days are pretty much gone. And so her ads basically said exactly that. They said, we know you like \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  as governor. I did too. I voted for him.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: But this is the Senate. And the Senate is now -- we have to be very careful because...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: And it also shows Maryland voters because we`re some of the suburbs of Washington, D.C., they have a little bit more sophisticated view about Senate control.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: I think that weighed very heavily in a lot of these votes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: I also think this is also a case of a Republican who tried to run outside of the Trump model in a state and really to try to tailor his appeal, his legacy name in the state to voters.\par \par \uc2 And I think it just showed that this is still a Republican Party that is built in Trump`s image, built with Trump`s tone and style. And even \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  couldn`t get ahead of that here in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Yes, I mean, all the ads I saw, Alsobrooks ads I saw were about, Larry Hogan`s a Republican, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is going to be a vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the Senate, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is going to be a vote for a Supreme Court justice if Trump is president, and just hammered away at it for weeks.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, let`s shift our focus to Michigan just for a moment, because Michigan is being closely watched tonight for the presidential race obviously, but also a Senate seat and several House seats that will be critical for control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Let`s check in with Zoe Clark of Michigan Public.\par \par \uc2 So, Zoe, bring us up to speed on the Senate race with the Democrat \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who is running against the Republican, \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 ZOE CLARK, Michigan Public Radio: well, First of all, let`s just talk about the money game, right? I mean, they`re just millions and millions and millions of dollars that has been spent on this race.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit media market, for example, you cannot turn on a television and not see back-to-back ads. And yet what we know is that polling has continued to show it incredibly close. And with very few precincts reporting yet it continues to appear like it`s going to be very close.\par \par \uc2 But much like what this theme of the night talking sort of about Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and whether or not she can get center and right- leaning suburban women, it very much could be the same for \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  here in the state .\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: I understand you have been tracking reports of long lines around Michigan. What can you tell us on that front?\par \par \uc2 ZOE CLARK: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, first, we should say there have been long lines, but Michigan, this was the first presidential election where we had early in-person voting, nine days of early in-person voting, no-reason absentee voting. So more than 40 percent of registered voters had already voted before even beginning to see Election Day.\par \par \uc2 But we have been seeing lines, but overall things have been moving and working very smoothly, including in Detroit, in Huntington Place, where there was in 2020, probably many of your viewers will remember, pounding on glass doors and windows as absentee ballots were being processed and counted\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, lastly, the Biden administration and the Harris campaign have faced criticism from some Arab American and Muslim American voters in Michigan over the administration`s handling of the war in Gaza and the humanitarian crisis there.\par \par \uc2 How has that issue evolved to Election Day?\par \par \uc2 ZOE CLARK: Well, this is what we`re also watching, right? I mean, within Michigan, which is a microcosm sort of the country as a whole, there`s a lot of these different demographic groups, whether it`s suburban women, right, whether it`s Detroit voters, or whether it`s Arab American voters.\par \par \uc2 And what we saw was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becoming the first of the two candidates to actually visit Dearborn this past weekend. And so it`s something we`re closely watching, particularly when you consider 100,000 voters voted for uncommitted back in the February presidential primary; 100,000 votes means something in Michigan when you consider that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  lost the state by less than 11,000 votes in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  then won Michigan by some 150,000 votes in 2020.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Zoey Clark of Michigan Public, our thanks to you for your time this evening.\par \par \uc2 ZOE CLARK: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, as we have been covering in the last several months and stressing to you at home, Americans should have confidence in the integrity of this election. That is the overwhelming message tonight from both intelligence officials and election workers.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: For a closer look at how tonight has gone from a security perspective, we turn to Juliette Kayyem, former assistant secretary at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  and faculty chair of the Homeland Security Program at Harvard`s Kennedy School, and Jessica Huseman, editorial director for Votebeat. That`s a nonprofit newsroom covering voting rights.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to both of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, Juliette, you and I spoke not too long ago about the possibility of violence, about threats to election workers and others.\par \par \uc2 And we did see some hoax bomb threats earlier, some threats to election officials, of course, many people still in line, a lot more votes to be counted. But when you look back at how the day has unfolded, how prepared have we been as a system for these institutions?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM, Former \uc2 U.S. Department of Homeland Security\uc2  Assistant Secretary: I think very prepared.\par \par \uc2 Well, we will talk about the threats, which are likely Russia-inspired, and they are targeting Black and minority areas in these swing states. So that`s not a surprise or a coincidence. But in terms of what the reaction is, it`s exactly what was being prepared for, was the potential hoax disruption or at least a disruption that they would have to ensure was a hoax that would delay voting times, disrupt voting.\par \par \uc2 And so what you saw in almost all of these instances was a quick response, a quick sweep to ensure that no one was in harm`s way, a relatively fast reopening, anywhere between 20, 30 to 45 minutes, a request to a court simultaneously to ask for more time to vote. So you`re seeing that now, and then get people back to vote, whether Democrat or Republican.\par \par \uc2 It`s a -- I don`t want to say it`s good news. This is just, we should not be accepting this as normal. As we have seen in the nighttime hours, these bomb threats are getting faster and more furious. But, nonetheless, fortunately, all of them appear to be hoaxes inspired or at least coming from Russia, at least according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, Jessica Huseman, we have seen very few disruptions or at least reports about disruptions to voting infrastructure, the tabulators, the printers, that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 The actual administering of the vote has gone off for the most part without a hitch. Talk about what we have seen and what an effect it`s had on the vote.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA HUSEMAN, Editorial Director, Votebeat: You know, I think that you are exactly right. We have seen a couple of errors here and there.\par \par \uc2 There was a ballot printer error in Pennsylvania. There was some scanning issues as well, but nothing that has materially slowed down processing or anything that would allow us to question the results or the or our confidence in them.\par \par \uc2 And I think that election administrators are really to be lauded for how seamlessly today went from an election administration standpoint.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Juliette, we know some of the disinformation that we have seen. And you referenced this a little bit earlier. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has now come out and made multiple statements attributing that online disinformation to Russia.\par \par \uc2 There`s also reports about National Guard deployments for Election Day support in a number of states. As people see these reports and these kinds of updates going on, is there any concern about violence or unrest moving forward? Or is this business as usual?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: Well, it`s not business as usual just because of January 6.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a -- what you sort of learn from the past. So you`re seeing about 18 states have deployed their National Guard. And just to be clear, the mission is quite limited. It`s just to ensure that people are protected if people go out in the streets for lawful protest to make sure that they don`t become violent.\par \par \uc2 D.C. obviously has activated its National Guard. And starting around January 6, all of D.C. will be what`s called a national special security event designated between January 6 and January 20. Those of you who live there might start to experience this a little bit now, in which that area, because of the electoral vote count, if there`s any question, if there`s any legal issues going on, through January 20 will be designated.\par \par \uc2 These are right now not based on specific threats, especially specific threats of a mass unrest, but the night`s going to unfold in ways that we don`t know. And we know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has already alleged voter fraud in -- at least in Pennsylvania. How do people respond to that?\par \par \uc2 We don`t know because we don`t know what the vote is yet, but this isn`t over. And so what you`re really seeing is the prepositioning and anticipating of what happens on the street, but also what`s going to be happening in terms of the candidates.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, Jessica, what are you looking for in the hours and days ahead as this vote continues to be tabulated?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA HUSEMAN: I think I`m really interested in how the counting goes tonight at the central count locations, especially in places like Arizona, which are expected to take quite a while.\par \par \uc2 It will be interesting to see how people react outside of those locations as the counting continues into the night. As you remember from 2020, we had some disruptions in both Pennsylvania -- I`m sorry -- in both Michigan and Arizona. Hopefully, we won`t see those again tonight.\par \par \uc2 Counties and states are much better prepared, I think, for the possibility of disruption.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Jessica Huseman and Juliette Kayyem, thank you both. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And we have another race call to share at this hour.\par \par \uc2 The state of Missouri has gone for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He puts that in his win column. Let`s take a look at the electoral map overall, at the moment, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with 188 votes, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with 99 votes. And, again, that number will continue to shift.\par \par \uc2 Let`s check in with Laura Barron-Lopez the Harris election watch event tonight over at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  in Washington, D.C., the vice president`s alma mater.\par \par \uc2 Lisa (sic), what`s the latest? What are you hearing from the campaign?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Well, the campaign, again, Geoff, is still being very patient, very calm. And they are saying that, again, they are seeing good suburban support, specifically when they`re looking at Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Yes, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might be up right now. But they are saying that in some of the larger suburban counties, they`re feeling good. They say, the campaign says that Harris is currently overperforming Biden`s 2020 margin in those suburban counties. They`re looking at ones like Douglas. They say that she`s running ahead of him, in Rockdale.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s what they`re pointing to right now. They also say that the rural turnout in Georgia is not -- is looking about what they expected. So they don`t expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to run away too much with that rural turnout as of right now. They also say that there are late-arriving ballots in North Carolina that they are paying attention to that they say should break in their favor.\par \par \uc2 So, so far, the campaign is being pretty calm about all this, saying that they`re waiting for more votes to come in, that they are not worried at all about what they`re seeing in the numbers so far.\par \par \uc2 I was also, Geoff, checking in with some voters in Michigan today, two young voters. And as we have talked about a lot tonight, young voters are going to be a key part of Harris` coalition and whether or not she is going to win in these swing states.\par \par \uc2 And these two young voters in Michigan, one of them is a college student who told me that they ultimately -- she ended up voting for Jill Stein, not for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She was very upset about Harris` position Gaza and Israel and ultimately voted third party there.\par \par \uc2 But the other young woman, a young Black woman who is around 28 years old, she told me that she ended up deciding to vote for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , despite being hesitant months ago and not thinking that she would vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She said that she decided to vote for Harris, especially because she liked the fact that Harris had \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  join her as vice presidential nominee.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, that is Laura Barron-Lopez at the Harris watch party in Washington, D.C., tonight.\par \par \uc2 We also have William Brangham with us. He`s in West Palm Beach, Florida, with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 William, we`re coming up on 10:00 Eastern here. Polls have closed in all the battleground states except Nevada. How would you describe the mood in the room? What are you hearing from campaign officials?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: The mood here is very, very positive. I mean, I haven`t been able to poll everybody here in the room, but the sense you get walking around and talking to voters is that the news keeps getting better and better and better for them.\par \par \uc2 I talked with a couple of older veterans who were here proudly wearing their military medals and insignias who felt that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was exactly the kind of leader that we need in a world that is at war and full of conflict in Ukraine and the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 I asked them, what about these allegations that Trump has said denigrating things about veterans? They didn`t believe any of those stories.\par \par \uc2 A lot of Jewish voters here saying that they believe that Donald Trump`s stout defense of Israel is a principal, overriding reason that they want to vote for him, and they think he will deliver for them.\par \par \uc2 In fact, one voter that I talked to said that she actually voted for the abortion extension, the expansion of abortion access, which many Democrats thought would draw people here in Florida, but also voted for Trump on the issue of Israel.\par \par \uc2 I had people who were also pulling out their phones and showing me lovingly photographs they have of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from prior rallies of him sitting on one of their motorcycles. And so there is just a great deal of sense that things are going well for them, the mood is great, and with one exception.\par \par \uc2 They did have -- most of the night tonight, they have been playing FOX News constantly here in the room. Recently, they have switched over to CNN, I am not sure exactly why they are doing that. Maybe they like the results that even CNN is reporting to them. And it did prompt a little bit of a chant of "CNN sucks" by a small group of people here, but otherwise people feeling very, very confident and eager for the continued results that they think are going to deliver Trump to the White House once again.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, that is our William Brangham in West Palm Beach, Florida, with the Trump campaign headquarters there. We will continue to check in with both him and Laura Barron-Lopez with the Harris campaign over the course of the evening.\par \par \uc2 Polls have closed in almost every single battleground state now, still too early to make those calls. Stay with us and we will have more.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, right now, we are going to take a short break.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: That is right, so \uc2 PBS\uc2  stations can report on some of their state and local races.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241106ekb50008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 5, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Miles O`Brien, John Yang, William Brangham, Laura Barron-Lopez,  Lisa Desjardins, Jonathan Capehart, David Brooks, Judy Woodruff, Geoff  Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 11,639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome back to PBS News special live coverage on this election night. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 It`s now 8:00 p.m. Eastern. Polls have closed in 16 additional states and Washington, D.C. It`s still too early to call in the swing state of Pennsylvania, which, of course, we are tracking very closely tonight. It`s the biggest prize of the battleground states, part of this so-called blue wall of states as well.\par \par \uc2 We can, however, call several states at this hour. Former President Trump has won in Florida with its 30 electoral votes. He`s also won Oklahoma, Tennessee, Mississippi and Alabama.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has won the states of Massachusetts, Connecticut, Maryland, and Rhode Island, so no surprises there.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Polls have also now closed in these states where it`s too early to call. We take a look at the national map in just a moment here in Delaware, in Illinois, in Maine, Missouri, New Hampshire, New Jersey and in Washington, D.C., still too early to call. So there you have the current updated electoral vote counts, former President Trump with 56, Vice President Harris with 13.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, "PBS News Weekend" anchor and our correspondent tonight, John Yang, is out in the field. He`s inside a vote-counting center in Allegheny County. That`s home to Pittsburgh and a county where both presidential candidates have held rallies just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So, John, we have been hearing a lot about long wait times in different parts of Pennsylvania. Have you seen those there and what should we know about those?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG: That`s right. The reports are all around college campuses of long wait times. There was at Villanova reports of 90 minutes, Temple, two hours.\par \par \uc2 And at Lehigh in Bethlehem. Some students told reporters they had been in line for seven hours. The \uc2 ACLU\uc2  was gone to court trying to extend voting hours at those places. But, of course, if they`re in line at 8:00 when the polls close, they will vote. They will get to vote.\par \par \uc2 We have also been seeing some problems in -- from early in the day in Cambria County, which is east and west of here. That`s where Johnstown is, if that helps places for people.\par \par \uc2 They had software problems earlier in the day, so they are extending voting by two hours until 10:00. But you`re about to see the big drop of all of the mail-in ballots, the work that county boards, the 67 county boards across the state have been doing all day, while simultaneously running a live election, counting these mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 And people should be cautioned that these are heavily Democratic. The applications for mail-in ballots were about 60/40 Democratic. Obviously, we don`t know who they voted for, but we do know that they were registered Democrats.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: John, stay with us just a moment. I just want to update our viewers on another presidential race call we can make at this hour. That is that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also won the state of South Carolina with its nine electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 That additionally goes into his win column. When you take a look at the national map, that has now been added to his electoral total there, 95 to Vice President Harris` 35 -- back to you, John.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, John, in the meantime, I understand \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been making unsubstantiated claims of voter fraud in and around Philadelphia. Bring us up to speed and give us a fact-check.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG: He went on social media in the -- early this afternoon and said that there was massive fraud or massive cheating, is his words, going on in Philadelphia, and that law enforcement was on the way. The first denial I saw came from Seth Bluestein.\par \par \uc2 Who`s Seth Bluestein? He`s a Republican city commissioner in the city of Philadelphia. He said there`s no substance to it and that he has been in touch with the \uc2 RNC\uc2  all day to assure them and reassure them that things are going smoothly in Philadelphia and that the vote is safe and sound.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, John, can I also ask you? You mentioned some of the delays because of long wait times and et cetera. We were already tracking and trying to understand how long it would take Pennsylvania to count, ultimately.\par \par \uc2 What do we know about how that might delay the entire counting and tallying process?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YANG: Well, obviously, if these votes -- these voters -- it doesn`t matter what time they close, the polls close. They close at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 But if they`re in line, they`re going to be able to vote. So that means a lot of ballots are going to be coming in late and that could delay things even more. There is some confidence in this state that they can get all the mail-in ballots counted by midnight, which would be a big step forward from 2020.\par \par \uc2 But then the in-person vote has to be counted.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: John Yang in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 John, our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring it back to our panel here and get your early reaction to what we have reported so far, starting with you, Amy.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, The Cook Political Report: Oh, well, I was deep into crosstabs.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: So I feel like I got called out in class for reading, trying to figure out what`s coming in.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Should we come back to you?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: You come back to me.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, Faiz, over to you, friend.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR, Democratic Strategist: Well, going into tonight, North Carolina obviously closed. And we`re looking at the results there. Trump visited there, I think, 21 times. It was one of the highest visits.\par \par \uc2 They indicated their level of insecurity about that state, given Mark Robinson`s weaker performance in the state. We will see how that holds. By contrast, Harris did a far fewer number of visits there. Even yesterday, I think Trump started his day in North Carolina. So they were telling you, we are insecure.\par \par \uc2 And I`m watching that state to see if there are insecurity holds, because if it obviously moves in a different direction from Trump, then this election will be quick.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes, what about that, Kevin? Because the vice president spent all of her day yesterday in Pennsylvania, and the Trump campaign did have that one stop in Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 You can tell a lot about the campaign strategy and how they`re feeling by how they spend their time in the waning days.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN, Republican Strategist: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: You seem skeptical of the point I was trying to make.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: Yes. Well, no, I don`t. No, I agree with the point you were just trying to make.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Oh, OK.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: I was kind of thinking about earlier in the week, when you started to hear talk of going to places like New Hampshire and Virginia, trying to expand the map.\par \par \uc2 And I think that had much more to do with the attitude of the Trump campaign than maybe some of the internal numbers. But that`s always been their approach, which is to end the week or end the campaign looking like you`re the campaign that`s on offense, that you`re the campaign that`s sort of expanding the map.\par \par \uc2 But one of the worst ways to wake up the day after Election Day is realizing that you deployed resources improperly, and that in a close race like this, you probably spent -- you could have spent one more day in Allentown versus up in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 But we will see. I think this is one of those things where if you look at how the campaigns closed, I would say one of the criticisms I had over the Trump campaign was the last two weeks were a distraction, starting with the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally. It really did take sort of 48, 72 hours of critical time away from them driving a closing message.\par \par \uc2 And the issues that are going to matter in this campaign, the economy, housing, even the abortion issue, I think were largely sort of left in the background, while Trump kind of still focused on a lot of his grievance politics and relitigating all of the past elections. And that`s always a problem.\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: On that point, just very quickly, because that was really important, think about how Trump closed this, because it was awful.\par \par \uc2 I mean, those last 10 days, not only that \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  event, which got its own -- but then the enemy within, you had the shooting of journalists or whatever the heck he was talking about, women, whether they like it or not, votes are rigged. You had all kinds of nonsense of antics around Arnold Palmer and microphones and all kinds of stuff that was such - - it affirmed for a lot of people, this is not what we want in office.\par \par \uc2 And I think it was just an awful close for him, because, as Kevin says, in a normal environment -- I think we were talking about an earlier panel about the economy -- you would have just focused on economic contrast. You talk about your vision and feeling good about whatever Trump felt about his economy.\par \par \uc2 And he could do none of that. He had lack of discipline. And I do think it hurt them in a big way.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes, and that`s what I -- speaking of digging into the exit polls, one of the questions they ask is, when did you make up your mind?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And so you`re always looking at those people who made up their mind at the end, how did they break? Where -- and so that`s what I was looking for.\par \par \uc2 And that goes to that point, which is, just from a campaign strategy perspective, you could not look at the last week-and-a-half of the Trump campaign and say, boy, they`re closing strong. And whether it mattered is really going to be the question.\par \par \uc2 And I just keep coming back to this idea of who are in the states that are getting all the attention, both from the candidates and the advertising and the targeting, and who are the states where they`re not? And so we`re looking at some of these results out of Florida in -- and I think Lisa talked about this -- Osceola County, heavily Puerto Rican county, where it does not look as if that did any damage at all.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Trump -- to Trump`s margins, in fact, he did a little bit better, with not all the votes counted yet, but at least it looks at this point that it did. So is it going to have a different impact in the places where people are actually talking about the election a lot more and where they`re communicating these sorts of things to the candidates -- to the voters?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And, Judy Woodruff, you have been on the ground in a number of those states talking to people.\par \par \uc2 And this idea of the lack of message discipline, to put it kindly, I think, from former President Trump in these last few days, was that something voters were attuned to, something that could push them away from him?\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: I`m really interested in this question about how much voters are affected by the kind of language that he uses, and these late in the campaign, and even earlier in the campaign, frankly, harsh rhetoric, ugly language.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if the exit polls -- you all are from more familiar. Amy, I`m looking at you. Are they going to tell us anything about whether that turns some people off? Because as I travel the country and talk to people, so many people who are voting for Trump saying they planned to vote for him, I`d ask them, well, what do you think about, what he says about X and Y?\par \par \uc2 And they would say, well, that`s the way he talks. I still like the way he handled the economy, or I like what he`s doing about immigration.\par \par \uc2 So I`m really interested in getting inside people`s decision-making on this.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And that`s something we will learn?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, not necessarily, but I think one thing we can get at is, if you look at some of these numbers of Donald Trump`s approval ratings, they`re asking that in the exit polls, who will do better job on the economy, if he`s winning on those metrics, but not winning, or winning by a smaller margin, than he`s winning on the economy, that suggests that there are plenty of people who are saying, yes, I think he`d do a much better job on the economy.\par \par \uc2 I think he`d do a better job on these issues, but I`m not supporting him because of that. Why would you not support first -- if economy`s the number one issue, you think he`s doing a better job on the economy, what would be the reason not to vote for him?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Yes, I hear that a lot.\par \par \uc2 But just to introduce a wrinkle, the 1980s were a golden age for conservative books. There was like "The Closing of the American Mind." They were doing really well. Then I talked to a conservative publisher in the early 1990s and said, our strategy for selling conservative books is not publishing good books. It`s getting liberals mad.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : And so their books would come out with titles like "Liberal Fascism." And the theory was, if you can get liberals mad and they attack you, then the conservative readers will rally around you.\par \par \uc2 And so \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does a lot of that. He gets progressives or Democrats to attack him, and it causes Trump voters to rally around him. And the massive version of this was, frankly, all the trials he -- which did not hurt him.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s not -- it`s not totally insane what he`s doing. It`s a manipulative of way of getting his people to rally around him, rather than getting them to drift off from him.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: That \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally, though, to use your phrase, really did get liberals mad. And it`s interesting because we`re living in this universe where the competition for attention is so fierce and people`s perceptions of these candidates, more so with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is so calcified, that it`s hard for really anything to break through.\par \par \uc2 But what that insult comic said about Puerto Rico really broke through, Jonathan. And we will see if -- the degree of the blowback tonight. But why do you think that resonated, when \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  saying that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fits the textbook definition of a fascist, you could say didn`t resonate in the same way?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : It didn`t resonate in the same way, but I do think it resonated.\par \par \uc2 Look, that \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally was just a hate fest from beginning to end. And the fact that you had this comic there to say these jokes that were racist, xenophobic, sexist, and that`s the beginning of the night, and then it just kept going in that tone for the rest of the night, including from the headliner of the evening, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And for that to be part of the closing message -- and I`m with Judy. I would like to know how much this language impacts the way people are going -- going into the voting booth and impacts the way they`re thinking about who they want to lead them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think that`s also why this election is such a stark contrast. Do you want someone who is saying, I`m here for everybody, I want us to work together, or someone who says there are all these problems and everything`s going to hell and it`s that person`s fault, that person`s fault, that person`s fault, and I`m the one who`s going to fix it?\par \par \uc2 And none of -- your proposals don`t speak to unity, do not speak to bringing us together. And so I think that`s -- liberals getting mad is one thing. But I also think that that \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  concerned Americans. And let`s not put the labels on it. But I do think a lot of Americans looked at that and thought, what the entire hell is happening, if they hadn`t been paying attention up until that point?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And we will see how much of an impact those remarks did make.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, coming up on 8:15 here on the East Coast. Polls have closed in a number of key states that we are tracking. And that includes the state of North Carolina, which is a battleground this year.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by Colin Campbell, who`s at WUNC covering the election there.\par \par \uc2 Colin, thanks for being with us. It`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 COLIN CAMPBELL, WUNC: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Give us a sense of what you`re seeing there on the ground in terms of turnout and what it could mean for how the results go in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 COLIN CAMPBELL: Yes, we have seen pretty heavy turnout today, some long lines of the polls, most notably a campus polling site at \uc2 Appalachian State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At one point today, had a four-hour line of mostly students trying to get their ballots cast in the final hours of voting, so certainly a heavy surge in the final hours here in North Carolina. We`re starting to get some results back, with Harris just ahead in the results we`re seeing so far, but the caveat there is, of course, those are mostly early votes, people who cast their votes early.\par \par \uc2 And those votes got reported faster than the votes from today, which I think may favor Republicans more, so still a very close race in the state and probably a long time before we get a race call tonight for North Carolina in the presidential.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And we should clarify still too early to call in this key state, which we think could be a bellwether to some degree.\par \par \uc2 But we should also note, the last time Democrats won here was back in 2008 with President Obama. Former President Trump won there in 2016 and in 2020. Just help us understand what happened in North Carolina in the last few years that makes it a place where Democrats think they have a chance.\par \par \uc2 COLIN CAMPBELL: Yes, we have had a lot of in-migration in the state, a lot of sort of college-educated, left-leaning folks moving to some of the urban areas of the state that have been some of the fastest growing metro areas in the country.\par \par \uc2 So that`s made Democrats more optimistic that they can start to shift the state more in their direction in this particular presidential election. We have also got some downballot races where the Republican candidates are pretty far to the right. Democrats are energized around that.\par \par \uc2 And you certainly see the Trump campaign not taking for the state for granted at all. We had three different days of rallies in the final days. He was here Saturday, he was here Sunday, he was here Monday, and that`s not what you do if you feel like you have got a pretty good confidence that you`re going to win a state.\par \par \uc2 That shows that we really are in play this year in ways that we weren`t the last couple presidential cycles.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Meanwhile, Colin, I know we reported on the devastating impact of Hurricane Helene. Of the 15 counties that were impacted, previously, two had broken for President Biden. But the rest were all counties where former President Trump won one by wide margins.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about the impact of that hurricane on the ability of election officials there to carry out the election and for people to vote?\par \par \uc2 COLIN CAMPBELL: Yes, it certainly created some challenge.\par \par \uc2 There were polling places that would have been used today that got flooded or were inaccessible. So some of those counties actually had to move where people were voting and then make sure all those voters knew where their new polling site would be.\par \par \uc2 What we have seen of the turnout, both in early voting and today in those storm-affected counties, we don`t see a huge impact on turnout. People, even though they`re having to go to an extra hassle to vote, they`re dealing with a lot in their personal lives. They`re making the time to vote in those areas.\par \par \uc2 So we haven`t seen an appreciable decrease in turnout from a normal year in some of these counties, even as they have had to scramble to make the polling process work this year.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is Colin Campbell from WUNC joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Colin, thank you so much. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 COLIN CAMPBELL: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Meantime, let`s hop back over to Lisa Desjardins at our super screen for a closer look at the state of North Carolina -- Lisa.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 Yes, let`s take a look at the beautiful Tar Heel State. What`s going on right now? As you showed viewers, we have got 9 percent of the expected vote in, so still a long way to go. Right now, Harris is up by five points. We do think this is a lot of early vote, but it`s not just that.\par \par \uc2 It`s that she`s getting -- some of her key counties are coming in. Mecklenburg County has 6 percent coming in. Up here, Wake County, that`s Raleigh, that`s got 6 percent in. Some very big Trump counties, Union County, that county where he got the largest number of votes in 2020, we don`t have any results yet.\par \par \uc2 So, caution, of course, this is still early, but this is where the presidential race stands. Let`s keep an eye on this number for Harris for a second, because I`m going to take a look now at the gubernatorial race, Harris with 52 percent at the moment, gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 , the attorney general of the state, now 58 percent. He`s six points ahead of Harris.\par \par \uc2 I think some folks might have expected her to be running even more ahead of her than that. But, obviously, he has a very comfortable margin, still with just 8 percent of the vote in right now in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of governor`s race, it`s not the only state that has one right now. New Hampshire, where polls have closed, we have got looks like a really close race between former U.S. Senator \uc2 Kelly Ayotte\uc2  here, who is running, who is right now behind two points.\par \par \uc2 One reason I want to look at this race is because if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is able to win the full blue wall, Pennsylvania, Michigan, Wisconsin, and also pick up -- keep Nebraska`s Second District, that essentially closes a major path for the Trump campaign. We saw vice presidential candidate J.D. Vance in New Hampshire just in the last couple of days because they are hoping that perhaps there`s a backdoor path to the presidency in New Hampshire`s four electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 They are counting on \uc2 Kelly Ayotte\uc2  to do well in this gubernatorial race to give them a chance. It`s a bit of a long shot, say a lot of people, but they -- Republicans I talked to think they have a real shot certainly for \uc2 Kelly Ayotte\uc2  to win the governor`s mansion there, but also potentially something the Trump campaign is watching.\par \par \uc2 All right, Pennsylvania, we`re going to be talking a lot about this tonight. We are actually getting real results in. This is what I want, 7 percent, though. I know it`s not as much as we like. But here we got almost a 50 point margin early here for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is, as John Yang was saying, we have seen some of the mail vote coming in, but also this is because this is key Harris areas, these collar counties that Kevin was talking about. Philadelphia County, 15 percent of the vote is in. Look at that margin for Harris, 65 points. Here we go, Delaware County. Bucks County you guys have been talking about, we have got a 33-point advantage for Harris right now.\par \par \uc2 Allegheny County, that`s Pittsburgh. John Yang, that`s where he`s at, also a big margin. So, this is -- she`s running up the tables early on in Pennsylvania, but we still have a long way to go. These are places that are favorable to her. We`re waiting for the Trump counties to come in.\par \par \uc2 Finally, let`s talk about that House race, Virginia 7 that we have been watching, just an indicator of where the House will go. Right now, we have got 44 percent of the vote in, and remember this is sort of suburban Washington, up here, Fredericksburg. Right now, the Democrat, Eugene Vindman, is ahead in this open seat fight with Derrick Anderson.\par \par \uc2 We will see, 44 percent in. Democrats probably like this right now, but I`m going to have to keep saying this. We still have a long ways to go -- back to you guys.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: A long ways to go. Lisa Desjardins, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we want to update you now with the winners of some of the Senate races we`re tracking tonight. In the state of Florida, Republican \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  has won his race. In Tennessee, Republican \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  is the winner. In Mississippi, also, Republican Roger Wicker has won that race.\par \par \uc2 In Delaware, Democrat \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  has won her race and made history as the first Black person to represent Delaware in the Senate. In the state of Connecticut, Democrat \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  has won his reelection bid. In Massachusetts, so has Democrat Elizabeth Warren. In Rhode Island, Democrat Sheldon Whitehouse has won again. And in New Jersey, Democrat \uc2 Andy Kim\uc2 , the former congressman, has won that race.\par \par \uc2 All of those are holds for their respective party, so no changes in the seats there. And here you see the Senate map updated. Again, control of the Senate is on the line tonight, Republicans with 43 seats so far and Democrats with 34.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn back now to the presidential race and take a deeper look at the nation`s largest battleground state. That is Pennsylvania, of course,\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Katie Meyer of Spotlight PA. That`s a nonprofit investigative journalism site in that state. She joins us from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Katie, thanks for being with us. This is obviously the state we`re going to be talking about more than any other tonight.\par \par \uc2 Just give us a sense of what you`re hearing from voters on the ground. We have been hearing a lot about some of the long lines and frustrations. What are the issues animating people you talk to there?\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER, Spotlight Pennsylvania: Yes, I have been out at the polls a lot today.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that you hear over and over again is abortion. I heard that from men. I heard that from women. I have been pretty much exclusively in Philadelphia, but it`s really top of mind here.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I have heard, and I was in sort of Republican areas of the city, as well as Democratic areas, the economy was the big one. Those are the two issues that I kept hearing over and over again.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Do voters there get a sense of how closely the rest of the country is paying attention to them right now?\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER: I think everybody knows. Yes, we have been getting a lot of texts and calls and door-knockers. So, yes, everyone knows that there`s a lot of attention here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Katie, we have also been tracking the Senate race. Of course, the incumbent Bob Casey there is running for reelection against Republican challenger in \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Help us understand why Casey finds himself in such a tight race this year.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER: Yes, I mean, one of the big things is money. \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  is an extremely well-funded candidate. Casey, of course, has been in politics for a very long time and has a lot of reserves to tap into, a lot of deep relationships to tap into, but it`s been an extremely expensive race.\par \par \uc2 And I also think Pennsylvania is just an extremely purple state. So any race, especially against with two well-funded candidates like this, can be very close, and that`s what you`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We also just want to get your take now because we saw Lisa Desjardins sort of breaking down what we have seen in terms of the expected vote that`s come in from Pennsylvania so far, which is a very small amount.\par \par \uc2 But you do see a big lead for Vice President Harris because of the way the votes are tabulated. Again, for our viewers` edification here, every state counts and tallies differently. Help us understand for their sake how Pennsylvania counts so we know what we`re seeing over the course of the night.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 So one of the big things to know about Pennsylvania is that we have widespread mail voting, but we cannot start counting those ballots until 7:00 a.m. on Election Day. So that was 7:00 a.m. today. It`s a lot of ballots to get through.\par \par \uc2 And big counties especially have a kind of a long road for those ballots. So, in Philadelphia, for instance, they`re hoping to get through about 100,000 ballots, up to 100,000 ballots, I should say, by the end of tonight.\par \par \uc2 Ballpark, they`re expecting to see 200,000 overall. Again, these are kind of broad numbers, but it gives you a sense of how soon to expect results here. The answer is, if the race is very far apart, there could be results sooner. If it`s very close, it`s going to take a minute.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, Philadelphia and Pennsylvania broadly just counts ballots relatively slowly.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Katie Meyer from Spotlight PA, a long night ahead for you. We wish you well and good luck. Thank you for joining us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Back here in the panel, before we move on, I know we`re going to talk about Pennsylvania a lot. We will return to it.\par \par \uc2 But, Jonathan, can I just get your take on that little bit of history that we reported there and I don`t want to go unnoted, the fact that \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  made history in her Senate race in Delaware?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Right, made history, first Black person elected to the Senate from Delaware, this -- the home state of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But she also made even more history. She is now the third -- she will be the fourth Black woman to serve in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , the third to be elected. Senator Laphonza Butler of California, who is there now, she was appointed by Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  to fulfill -- to serve out the rest of the term from the late Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So the first Black woman elected to the Senate was Carol Moseley Braun in 1992. That was 32, is my math right, 32 years ago. And we should also note that there is one other Black woman running for the Senate, and that is Angela Alsobrooks, a Prince George`s County executive, in Maryland. And so even though the state has been called apparently for the vice president.\par \par \uc2 Still waiting to see whether more history is made tonight.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And it was President Biden who encouraged \uc2 Lisa Blunt Rochester\uc2  to run for that seat, in large part because he knew that she would win and make history.\par \par \uc2 Can we return to something that Katie Meyer about the degree to which, Faiz, abortion is an animating issue in Pennsylvania? And I raise it because, talking to a Harris campaign aide over the weekend, they said that there are really three things driving this race, Donald, democracy and Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, I agree with that. And we will I think hopefully learn at the end of tonight that how -- what a motivating factor it continues to be. When you look at a lot of Democratic victories over the past couple of years, Geoff, almost all of them can be tied directly to the Dobbs decision and the intensity of turnout and the persuasion effect it`s having, where you have conservative-leaning women, conservative-leaning men saying, well, hey I might be conservative, but this issue is too big, too important. I`m going to vote this way.\par \par \uc2 But just to make one point that, for his part, Trump has not done much on persuasion this. To anybody who was concerned about the fact that Trump came in, overturned Dobbs, he didn`t give much to work with in trying to suggest he had learned anything or suggested a different course.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he kind of fed back in today. He couldn`t even tell you where he was on the Florida ballot measure. And I would say it was a genuine problem for Trump all the way through is, he couldn`t talk to a persuasion universe. And instead he buckled down on saying, here`s my die-hards, I`m going to continue to affirm to them.\par \par \uc2 There are some lessons to learn about, when you are in that kind of an approach, he went very hard at talking to people in alternative media, the working-class people through Joe Rogan, through \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , who talked to \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2 . The other factor was \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  there. You know who was there last night? Megyn Kelly is another right-wing influencer.\par \par \uc2 He spent a lot of time trying to talk directly to people through TikTok, Adin Ross, Logan Paul, a professional wrestler. This was the way in which he conducted his campaign. To the extent he`s going to get new voters, there`s something to learn about the approach that they took with media and how they tried to reach people.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: How do you see it, Kevin?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: Well, I think, first of all, if you have been living in the Philadelphia area for the last six months, you have probably seen a million gross rating points in advertising from the Harris campaign on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 They think it`s a key issue, as Faiz has mentioned, to winning those suburban women voters. And I think one of the big things that they have exploited is that for years I have worked on presidential campaigns, state senate campaigns. I have worked on city council campaigns for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 We have always had a very clear understanding of where we stood on the messaging as it related to the abortion platform of the party. That has changed dramatically now. There`s really no through line. And also you have a very sort of compromised messenger at the top in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in that he`s never come up through the pro-life movement, through the party.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t spend a whole lot of time focusing on policy or Supreme Court decisions. And what he`s doing now is sort of the way he ran his real estate industry. He`s like, what do I got to do to get you into this property, right?\par \par \uc2 And so he`s trying now, he`s like, telling voters, what do I need to do to get you into my position on this issue and how is it going to appeal to you? And that comes off as very inauthentic. And so there is no through line between the presidential candidate down through the rest of the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And that has been -- I think it`ll prove to have been a challenge, I think, in the Philadelphia suburbs and suburbs like it around the country on that issue.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, that is going to be really fascinating to watch just in a place like Pennsylvania, where the Republican candidate for the Senate is someone who lost the primary last time.\par \par \uc2 But when he was campaigning for that primary in 2022, that was before the Dobbs decision had come out. He had made some pretty conservative statements on abortion that now become part of the campaign in 2024 that weren`t part of it when he first ran for office.\par \par \uc2 Trump, as you say, he`s changed his position so many times that it just becomes sort of the background noise. The other thing that`s important to note that Florida is one of the states that has a referendum on the ballot, which would -- this is -- right now, Florida has a six-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 This would enshrine abortion rights in to the state constitution. And it needs 60 percent to pass. It`s at 57 percent right now. There`s about 88 percent of the vote in. But that is a high bar to get 60 percent, but kind of right knocking on the door.\par \par \uc2 So, again, this goes to that exact point. You have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may win there by eight, nine points. And the abortion referendum will win. At least, even if it loses, it will be getting 57 percent, 58 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And, if I may, that`s one -- that`s a place where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was slow to declare himself.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And he was -- wouldn`t be pinned down on it. He finally said he would vote against it. But it took several asks.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, it is a little after 8:30 p.m. Eastern here on the East Coast, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And the polls in Arkansas have closed. And we can now say that the state`s six electoral votes will go to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Meanwhile, we can also report that Vice President Harris has won the state of New Jersey. Polls there, of course, closed at 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 So here is a quick update, the national electoral map at this point in the race, former President Trump with 101 to Vice President Harris` 49 electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Let`s bring in Harris campaign co-chair Marcia Fudge, the former secretary of housing and urban development in the Biden administration and a former Democratic member of Congress from Ohio.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you back with us. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE, Former U.S. Housing and Urban Development Secretary: Thank you so much. It`s good to be with you, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: I know that you and the vice president are close. Have you spoken with her this evening or recently? How is she feeling about her chances and how is she really feeling about this moment?\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: I haven`t spoken to her today, but she`s feeling good. I mean, just think about it, Geoff. She`s -- in 107 days, she changed the entire dynamic of politics in this country.\par \par \uc2 She gave it everything she had. We left it all on the field. She feels good about her work. She feels good about the work of the campaign and very, very optimistic tonight.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We were talking here among the panel about the impact that abortion and reproductive rights might have on this race. Do you think that women will be decisive in this election?\par \par \uc2 The loss of Roe was really seismic to women, especially women who were alive before 1973, and saw a right extended to them as it relates to bodily autonomy that was later removed for their daughters and granddaughters.\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: Certainly, it`s a major issue.\par \par \uc2 But I think that, as I have been looking at exit polls, more women are saying that democracy is their number one issue, democracy and then freedom. Yes, it`s going to be important because no one wants to live in a nation where we are rolling back the ability to control our lives, our family`s lives.\par \par \uc2 No one wants to live in that kind of a nation. So I think that it is going to be important, but it`s not the only important issue. And so I`m really glad that she`s talked about freedom of choice, but also about, what kind of country do we want to live in, Geoff?\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Her closing argument was really focused on pragmatism and compromise. In fact, I don`t think she even mentioned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by name yesterday.\par \par \uc2 What was really fueling that strategy?\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: Well, the strategy was that everybody knows who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. We wanted them to know who she is and where she stands and what she believes in and the kind of America she would lead.\par \par \uc2 And I think it came across very, very well. I thought the speech was excellent. We were very pleased with the reception. And I think it just became time to just turn the page, as she says, on what is in the past and looks solely at the future. She did a good job with that.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: You know, as we look at the arc of history, \uc2 Shirley Chisholm\uc2  on this day back in 1968, became the first Black woman elected to Congress. You watched her run. You were heavily involved in Hillary Clinton`s campaign. You`re now the national co-chair of the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling this evening?\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: I`m a wreck, actually.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: I mean, I have watched them all.\par \par \uc2 But what I do believe is that Kamala now has an opportunity because \uc2 Shirley Chisholm\uc2  ran and because \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  ran. And they just kept breaking that glass. And I think now it is time for her to just shatter it all. But, yes, I am. I`m a little anxious.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right. That is Secretary Marcia Fudge.\par \par \uc2 Always good to see you. Thanks for being back with us.\par \par \uc2 MARCIA FUDGE: Thank you. Good to be seen.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right, be well.\par \par \uc2 And we have another race call. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has won the state of Delaware, President Biden`s home state. No surprise there.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring it back to the panel.\par \par \uc2 Amy Walter, I see you poring through Excel sheets.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I know. I`m poring through some numbers. I know.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: What do you see?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I wanted to go back to this point. Faiz was talking about the outreach that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done with talking to Joe Rogan and YouTubers and all of this as a way to reach out to...\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The young men vote.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... the really young men vote.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m just looking right now in Pennsylvania. And the biggest gender gap -- and we`d seen this in some polls. But it is holding up that the biggest gender gap is between 18-to-29-year-old men and 18-to-29-year-old women, the women voting for Harris by 27 points and the men voting for Trump by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 So, even among -- if we go into the 30 to 44, it`s not much different, men voting for Trump by two points, women voting for Harris by eight points. So that`s a pretty narrow range. It is in older voters. It`s a little bit bigger than sort of middle-age voters, but that young voter gap.\par \par \uc2 And that was the whole reason to go on to those shows. Now, they make up a small -- that`s 7 percent of the overall electorate. Women -- young women are 9 percent. So, obviously, I can`t do that math right now.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: But the point being, yes, there are more younger women who are voting, but that the percentage to which women are voting for Harris and that younger men are voting for Trump is quite notable.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And it`s not represented in any other generation.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: But, Kevin, is it enough for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to sit down for a couple hours with Theo Von or three hours with Joe Rogan? Is that enough to drive out the young male vote? Or do you have to do more than that?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: That`s the test. And this goes back to the conversation that we had earlier about getting out the vote in your infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 And going and talking to those voters, I think, is very important. And some of them, many of them are very disaffected. And, also, many of them are casual voters. They`re not as necessarily dedicated to making sure that they have their ballot ordered and mailed in or showing up to the right polling place on time.\par \par \uc2 So they`re sort of a lower-propensity audience to begin with. The challenge that you have is, now that you have talked to them and you have got them on the hook with your ideas and you have motivated them to support you, how do you make sure that they get to the polls and that they take 10 friends with them?\par \par \uc2 And that`s where the organizational advantage of having a campaign where you own the infrastructure and you have been building it over two years inside the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and in an integrated fashion with your own campaign. It makes a big difference.\par \par \uc2 So this will be a big test of whether or not they were able to do that. They have definitely knew that they had an opportunity with these voters. It`s were they able to take the curiosity and convert it into actual votes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, the other thing we`re seeing, though, again, in the exits is Trump is doing better with Black voters than he did in his previous election in places like Georgia and North Carolina, so going from nine to 15.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s not a huge jump. This is not -- he`s not winning these voters, but in a state especially like Georgia, where to win their Democrats need to get not just Black voters to show up, but to vote overwhelmingly for them, that is probably a good sign for Trump.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Can we go back to this idea of that gender gap you pointed to, particularly among young voters?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And, David and Jonathan, I`d love to get your take on this, because we have seen this trend across the country, right, of younger women becoming more progressive, younger men going further to the right.\par \par \uc2 It was interesting too. Faiz mentioned Megyn Kelly, who appeared at a Trump rally, who`s previously been a very vocal critic of former President Trump, saying, you`re not doing enough to reach out to women. I think she called one of his rallies, like, brotastic and urged him to do more. He has not.\par \par \uc2 He`s resisted at every turn. We have seen this persistent gender gap. Will that be the death knell for him in some states?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : So far, no. I don`t see it. I mean, it`s sort of been awash so far.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : And I don`t think the campaign had much to do with why young men are going for Trump.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s because, if you look at two-thirds of the people in the bottom of high school classes, two-thirds are men. If you go to the top of high school classes, two-thirds are women.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes, so this is a trend we have been seeing anyway, that lines up politically.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : So this is a trend.\par \par \uc2 So I think the young men are just like, the system is out to get me, and they`re voting. I`m looking at all -- \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has a very handy piece, where they take counties where all the votes are in. And they do a red arrow if Trump is doing better than four years ago, and a blue arrow if Harris is doing better.\par \par \uc2 And I`m looking at a sea of red arrows. Now, most of these are in Trump country. So it`s Trump doing better in Trump country. That`s not important. The one that leaps out at me here is Loudoun County, Virginia, which is an exurb of Washington.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : It used to be the fastest-growing county in the country. It`s now solidly Democratic. Harris is up by 16. But compared to four years ago, Trump is performing nine points better.\par \par \uc2 And so if we see a lot of red arrows in places like Loudoun County, then it`s a -- that`s a warning sign for Harris.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Jonathan?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : What?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : After that...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Can we also clarify here, these are places where you say most of the vote is already in. It would tend to come in faster in some of these rural counties, correct?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : It`s plus-95. They`re rural counties.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : But Loudoun is a straight-up suburban county.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, I look at the fact that Trump is doing better in Loudoun and better in these red counties already. I can`t remember who said this, who said it on another network, that really what`s happening tonight is America is basically on trial, in that we`re going to find out who we are through this election.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that, despite the things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said on the campaign trail, the things that he`s promised on the campaign trail, a lot of things that are concerning to millions of people, and he`s still getting more votes than he did the last go-round, that to me is more concerning than the vice president`s not doing as well, say, even though she`s up plus-16, plus-16, even though it`s not as good as 2020.\par \par \uc2 And so, for me, that`s what I`m looking at and what I`m most concerned about, about what I`m about to learn about the American people through this election.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: A lot of questions about what`s going on and what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 I have to say I brought a little cheat sheet here. To the extent the economy is driving where people are, we know the Democrats point out the numbers, the inflation number, the rate. It`s down to 2.4. That`s better than it`s been, a lot better than it`s been compared to, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected 1.7, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected, 1.2.\par \par \uc2 But average price of a loaf of bread, it was $1.36 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected, $1.51 when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was elected. It`s $1.97 now. The average price -- median price of a house sold in the United States this quarter, it was -- eight years ago, it was $303,000. It`s $420,000.\par \par \uc2 I mean, those are the practical things in people`s lives that people are dealing with. So we don`t know what`s going to happen, but just pointing it out.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We certainly don`t want to get ahead of where the results are.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: But it`s a good point. There is this sense that it really is a luxury to be concerned about the fate of democracy when you`re trying to pull together two or three jobs in the gig economy and put food on the table for some folks.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BROOKS\uc2 : Yes, people can care about both things, though.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so I think a lot of this argument that it`s a luxury if you can`t afford bread, you don`t think about democracy? No, you think about your country. But I do think you think about how`s my community doing? It`s not only how you`re doing individually. It`s how my community is doing.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re an affluent person in a community -- I was in West Texas where the community is just -- the grocery stores are leaving, the shopping stores. It doesn`t matter how rich you are. You see your community failing, and you`re upset about it. And you definitely want change.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know. I`d be curious to know if Amy has run across, which candidate is the candidate of change in the exits?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right. I have not seen that yet, but that`s a great point.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We will continue to come back to this issue, I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I do want to update viewers with another race call we can make at this hour, coming up on 8:45 here on the east coast. That is that Vice President Harris has won the state of Illinois, 9 percent of the expected vote in there so far, but the winner can be called, again, AP calling those based on historical data, trends and votes as they have come in.\par \par \uc2 So when you look at the national map right now, here`s where the electoral vote count stands, former President Trump with 101 to Vice President Harris` 71.\par \par \uc2 Amy, I want to return to the state of Georgia if we can. Polls there closed back at 7:00. We`re still waiting, obviously, to be able to make a call.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes. For a while, we will be waiting.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We have seen this phenomenon of split-ticket voters in a state like Georgia. Do we expect to see those again, where voters made a difference between who they were voting for at the state level versus the federal officials?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And for the first time in a long time, we do not have a Senate race on the ballot in Georgia. It feels like every five minutes there`s a Senate race in Georgia for various reasons.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  would agree with you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes, he would, yes. Same with Arizona. There`s been a Senate race there forever and ever.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: But -- for retirements and such.\par \par \uc2 But the -- and this was why Democrats were actually concerned from the very beginning about Georgia, which was that it was a state where, without \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  on the ticket...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... without a governor`s race, without other things, especially without an African American candidate on the ballot helping to juice that turnout, you were not going to get that same kind of intensity among Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 And that is -- that may come -- turn out to play. As I said, the one data point we do have is just the fact that Trump is doing better among Black voters. And, thus far, it doesn`t look like other issues have -- I mean, other groups of voters have changed all that much.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Let`s bring in our Lisa Desjardins.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, the polls in Georgia closed at 7:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: You have Georgia there up on the super screen. What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Yes. Yes, more results, which is, of course, what I want.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go on a three-minute tour of what we know in this moment, Trump up, but we`re getting now 55 percent of the result in, in the state of Georgia. I want to look at a couple of places to judge sort of what`s happening here.\par \par \uc2 First of all, Liberty County, this is a rural Democratic county. We spoke to a Black farmer there that broke -- he broke for Harris. Right now, she`s at 59 percent in Liberty County. Where is that? That`s where \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was. That`s not where the Democrats want her to be in a county like this.\par \par \uc2 It`s a smaller county, but this is a place where they need to be getting closer to Biden`s numbers if they`re going to win the state of Georgia. So let`s look at another county, though, where Democrats see something more that they like. This is Douglas County. This is suburban Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at where they are now versus where she was, where they were in 2016, this is where, for the moment, Harris is outperforming \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in this suburban county. Big difference here, this is 40,000 votes, many more votes here than in those rural counties like \uc2 Liberty\uc2 . And, look, 80 percent of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 This is a place that Democrats like to see this kind of change going on for them. Let`s take a look at what else is going on in some other states. Pennsylvania, we`re getting more results in, but still it`s only 13 percent. But we are starting to see this even out a little bit as some of the Trump areas are coming in.\par \par \uc2 I want to take a look closely at also the Pennsylvania Senate race. Here we see Bob Casey actually underperforming \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the moment. But we have a lot of results to come in, including we don`t have anything from Chester County yet. So there`s some big areas to come in and just a couple more things to look at.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, this is another key race that we`re watching, Susan Wild, the incumbent there, right now with 9 percent in. She`s crushing it, but this is the early vote. Ryan Mackenzie is a rising star, state representative there in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And then, look, we have got a few results coming on in Michigan. Let`s take a look at Washtenaw County. I have mentioned that as a key county, Ann Arbor. Students there, we were wondering how they feel about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the moment. With about a third of the vote in from Washtenaw County, we`re seeing her underperformed by a lot even \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Again, still many more votes to come in. And we will just keep following this closely.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, that`s our Lisa Dejar -- Desjardins.\par \par \uc2 Man, guys, I need my coffee.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Over at the super screen.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we have got reporters found out across the country, including two with both of the campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Laura Barron-Lopez is with the Harris campaign at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, give us a sense now. We`re coming up at 9:00 here on the East Coast. What are you hearing from the campaign about where things stand so far?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Yes, so Harris` campaign is still exuding cautious optimism. They aren`t reading too much into these early numbers, but they are sending still updates from what they`re seeing on the ground, where their surrogates are, where their ground operation is.\par \par \uc2 And so the campaign shared with us that right now there are long lines still at universities across Nevada, that they have deployed surrogates to those universities to make sure that people stay in line. Also, in Wisconsin, they said that they had one-to-two-hour lines there and were trying to make sure that people were staying in line to vote.\par \par \uc2 They deployed some of their brand names surrogates, some big names like Paul Rudd, the actor, to universities in Wisconsin. They also had others, like Jennifer Garner, FaceTime in to Pennsylvania. And they continue to keep pointing out that the Puerto Rican population turnout is high in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that they`re pointing out, Amna, is that they like the numbers that they`re seeing in the suburbs of Atlanta. I was also texting with a House Democrat, a lawmaker from Michigan, who is also saying that they are cautiously optimistic and said that they think that it`s going to come down, more than anything, it will be the gender gap, which I know that you and everyone there on the panel was just talking about.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: All right, let`s head down to Florida, where William Brangham is with the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 William, what are Trump campaign sources telling you?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Well, this is going to be no surprise to you, Geoff and Amna.\par \par \uc2 That`s -- just as Laura is reporting that the Harris campaign is expressing optimism, so is the Trump campaign. Specifically, Trump campaign officials have been telling me that they are very bullish on Pennsylvania. And, specifically, they were pointing out that they think that turnout in Philadelphia, while it is high, which is something that would normally favor Democrats and the Harris campaign, is, they`re saying that it`s showing high for them in areas that are strong for Trump, south and northeast parts of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 The other Harris strongholds, they say, they aren`t doing as well. That goes a little bit of what Lisa was saying, that Casey`s Senate campaign was seemingly underperforming. The Trump campaign seems to be echoing that same idea.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the campaign is saying also that across the country, the rural turnout, which again was to be expected to go for Trump, is very, very strong. Monster and huge were the adjectives that the campaign officially used.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that the campaign is pushing is that they have argued that low-propensity voters, as we talked about earlier tonight, would be critical for their turnout. They say that the numbers that they have seen show that those voters turned out early and that they turned out today, which is critical because the concern always is that if you have a strong early turnout, that that ends up cannibalizing a lot of your actual Election Day turnout.\par \par \uc2 And the Trump campaign is saying so far that has not turned out to be the case, that those voters turned out early and more of them are now turning out today.\par \par \uc2 So, overall, again, as to be expected, a bullish update from the Trump campaign here.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: William Brangham, our thanks to you.\par \par \uc2 And we have a governor`s race call to share with you. North Carolina`s governor`s mansion will go to Democrat \uc2 Josh Stein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Faiz Shakir\uc2 , when there is a seven-hour-long line at \uc2 Lehigh University\uc2 , as both John Yang and the reporter from Pennsylvania told us earlier this evening, whose fault is that? I mean, should the campaign have known that there was going to be significant turnout? And could they have coordinated with the local election officials to account for that?\par \par \uc2 FAIZ SHAKIR: Well, election officials, this is their responsibility, right, to make sure that they have accounted for over the past historical numbers of elections, that there are enough poll watchers, poll, basically, voting assistants at these poll sites.\par \par \uc2 So that`s a failure of the state if there`s too long a line. But it`s also a good problem to have, from my perspective. You want people voting, you want them to stay in line. So I urge everyone who was watching who`s in line or watching any of this, just please stay in line. Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, Kevin, take us inside. I`m curious from both of your campaign experience here, as Laura was reporting, that they`re deploying surrogates to get people to stay in line.\par \par \uc2 I mean, imagine being out there in line and the actor Paul Rudd walks up to you and says, hi, I`m asking you to please stay here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I mean, is that literally what`s going on in each of these places? Is that -- it seems so haphazard in some ways, whether or not someone will stay in a long line to cast their ballot.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: It is, yes.\par \par \uc2 But it goes. It`s actually -- if you go behind the pipe and drape in a campaign, that`s going on, on all day. I just remember, 2004, my -- our political director, Mike Hartley, kind of just walking around the headquarters in Ohio, where we were stationed on election night, just like a raving madman.\par \par \uc2 He looked like Mike Ditka on the sideline of a Bears game with like a headset on talking and yelling to people.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MADDEN: But that`s the big part of the campaigns.\par \par \uc2 And I think with the campaign sort of modeling that you go through early on and the practice that you put into that and the work that you put into that so that, on Election Day, you do know where.\par \par \uc2 You`re not just randomly haphazardly calling volunteers and asking them to go out and get people in line, but that you have anticipated where the turnout could be, where the turnout needs to be so that you have your volunteers out there making sure that your -- that everybody`s voting as best they can, and that they have the opportunity to vote in all of these crucial battleground counties inside the states.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Amy, when William was sharing his reporting from the Trump campaign that they feel good about the turnout for them in North and Southeast Philadelphia and the rural parts of Pennsylvania, is there anything that you`re seeing that tracks with that?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, I need to dig into Lisa`s map. I wish I could just go on over there and just type it in.\par \par \uc2 But I think what we`re really going to be looking for -- and if you are the Harris campaign, I think this has been their key all along -- is doing just well enough in those rural areas, holding on there, holding on to Biden`s margins with that white working-class voter, even if you`re not getting the same margins out of Scranton or Erie, but really running up the score in and around the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 And if Philadelphia is coming in, and if those people are standing in line, remember, Philadelphia has been a real sore spot for Democrats for the last few cycles, because it used to be this huge vote driver, and two things have been happening, one, fewer voters for -- fewer voters are turning out, so lower turnout out of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 And then as we have seen in these last couple of elections, and this is what Trump was leaning into as well, in parts of North Philadelphia, which are heavily Latino, he made up some ground there. So I think that`s going to be a fascinating part of the city to watch.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to take a long time before we see those final numbers, though.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, we have got all night together, guys.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s what is so great.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Thanks to all of you for being here as well.\par \par \uc2 (PHONE RINGING)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: \uc2 David Brooks\uc2  is getting an important call. We`re going to ask him who`s calling him at this hour.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: In the meantime, we`re going to take a short break so our \uc2 PBS\uc2  stations can report on some of their state and local races.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We will be right back at 9:00 p.m. Eastern, which is when polls close in many more states, including Michigan, Wisconsin, and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 NewsHour Productions, LLC.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 VIQ Media Transcription,  Inc., which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the  transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of  this material except for the user`s personal or internal use and, in such  case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for  commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon NewsHour  Productions, LLC, and VIQ Media Transcription, Inc`s copyrights or other  proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal  transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241106ekb50005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for November 4, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeffrey Brown, Nick Schifrin, James Mates, Lisa Desjardins, Laura  Barron-Lopez, Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: This election eve, the presidential candidates and their running mates make their final push in the swing states that will determine who wins the White House.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: A new PBS News poll shows \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ahead of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nationally, but the path to 270 electoral votes remains as tight as ever. We game out the candidates` potential paths to victory.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And we examine how various legal challenges could potentially prolong or disrupt this presidential election.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER, Executive Director, Center for Election Innovation and Research: We have seen a lot of preelection litigation. It`s very likely it is the most preelection litigation we have ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Welcome to the "News Hour" on this election eve.\par \par \uc2 With only hours left for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to reach voters, the candidates are crisscrossing crucial swing states tonight that could decide the election.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: More than 80 million Americans have already cast their ballots in this year`s election. And, tonight, there are new signs of momentum for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . But neither side is taking anything for granted in the waning hours of this unprecedented election cycle.\par \par \uc2 Our coverage tonight begins with Laura Barron-Lopez.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: In a mad dash to the finish, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rallying their supporters tonight to get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Here`s my only purpose in even being here today. Get out and vote. You know that.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: Are we ready to do this?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : We`re ready to get out the vote?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : We`re ready to win?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Trying to convince any remaining undecided voters to come to their side. Most national polls show the race in a dead heat, but there are some signs that voters may be opting for Harris.\par \par \uc2 The latest PBS News/NPR/Marist poll shows Harris up four points against Trump among likely voters nationally. It`s the first time she`s shown a lead greater than the margin of error. Harris even appears to have a slight lead in decidedly red Iowa.\par \par \uc2 The final Des Moines Register-Mediacom poll shows Harris ahead by three points among Iowa voters.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS: Momentum is on our side. Momentum is on our side.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS: Can you feel it? We have momentum, right?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : You know, they have an expression. I hate the expression, actually, but it`s ours to lose.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Both campaigns are engaging in an all-out battleground blitz today, former President Trump fanning out across three states with two stops in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 But Vice President Harris is holding every single one of her five final events in the Keystone State, the biggest Electoral College prize of the swing states.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS: We need everyone in Pennsylvania to vote, because you are going to make the difference in this election.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : You will. So here`s how I think about things. We are all here together because we love our country. And when you love something, you fight for it.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Trump and Vance were also out today in North Carolina and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`d like to begin by asking a very simple question. Are you better off now than you were four years ago?\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: No!\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), Vice Presidential Candidate: You don`t have to agree with everything that I say, every policy proposal that we have. You don`t have to agree with everything that \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  says. But what we know is that, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, you could afford to pay your bills.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: It builds on a weekend of campaigning by both candidates at breakneck pace, including media appearances.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris popped in for a lighthearted surprise on "Saturday Night Live."\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH, Actress: Now, Kamala, take my palmala.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MAYA RUDOLPH: The American people want to stop the chaos...\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : ... and end the dramala.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: For equal airtime, NBC played a 90-second special message from Trump during two major Sunday sporting events, a NASCAR race and "Sunday Night Football."\par \par \uc2 At a Sunday rally while surrounded by bulletproof glass, Trump made menacing remarks toward the press.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And I have this piece of glass here. But all we have really over here is the fake news, right?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And to get me, somebody would have to shoot through the fake news. And I don`t mind that so much.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Trump`s campaign tried to defend the remark, claiming that the former president, who regularly calls reporters the enemy of the people, was actually looking out for the press.\par \par \uc2 And despite running well behind Harris with women voters, Trump laughed when a supporter of his compared the vice president to a sex worker.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She`s a significant liar. And when you lie about something so simple, so she never worked there.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She worked on a corner!\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : But I did -- I did a little bit.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: This weekend, Trump also appeared to endorse a proposal to remove fluoride from public water utilities, an idea floated by \uc2 Robert F. Kennedy Jr.\uc2 , who frequently spreads public health conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 In the final stretch, Trump has repeatedly said he will put Kennedy in charge of health policy if he wins.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We have a mess in our hands. We got a bunch of cheaters. That all they do is think about how they can cheat.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: And as Trump lays the groundwork to call the election stolen with baseless claims of cheating, Harris is warning voters that Trump may prematurely claim victory Tuesday night, like he did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : I would ask, in particular people who have not yet voted, to not fall for his tactic.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: The former president wraps up tonight with a rally in Grand Rapids, Michigan, the same city where he ended both his 2016 and 2020 campaigns. And Harris will hold a star-studied event from the Rocky Balboa Steps in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: With more on the candidates` final sprint to Election Day, let`s bring in Laura Barron-Lopez, who`s been covering the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And Lisa Desjardins, who has been reporting on former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, we will start with you. What has been the final message from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ? Have you noticed a shift?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: We have noticed the shift.\par \par \uc2 In the final month, Harris was being much more explicit when she was talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what she sees as a threat to democracy that he poses, calling him unstable, looking for unchecked power. She was repeatedly quoting former Trump officials like his longest-serving Chief of Staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , as well as the former Chiefs -- chief of the -- sorry -- Joints Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 And their words, as they described him as -- they described \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as fascist and as dangerous. But in her closing remarks, starting this weekend, Harris has not been naming \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  specifically. She`s been trying to create more of this unity message. And that is a notable shift.\par \par \uc2 I asked \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , her campaign chair, about that today. And the campaign chair said that she wants to end with optimism, this theme of unifying the country, that she will be a next generation.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Lisa, what about the Trump campaign`s final messages here? Any big shifts?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: As you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has very many messages, most often. But one that they like as their final message is this idea that Harris broke it, Trump will fix it.\par \par \uc2 That does a couple of things when you talk to senior campaign advisers for Trump. They say, first of all, it tries to tag Harris to the Biden administration, but it also leaves a much more vague opening, so that any remorse, any grievance that voters have, they will blame on her, whatever it is that`s broken.\par \par \uc2 And it also helps with his brand which, of course, is a get it done kind of businessman. So they like that message, she broke it, we will fix it. Something else they`re doing in ads, though, is, they`re focusing on the economy and immigration and, as Laura has been reporting, in some specific markets and online, especially anti-transgender rights commercials.\par \par \uc2 Talking to campaign advisers about what`s going on with that, they say they`re trying to make up ground that they concede they have lost with women, suburban women, over abortion and trying to see if that helps. Not clear if it will, but that`s part of their final strategy.\par \par \uc2 They do feel like they gained ground earlier this fall. They think the vice presidential debate may have been a turning point for them for momentum. They also like to point out that Harris has not had a traditional news conference, but overall, right now, they`re trying to portray Harris as someone who`s not ready to be president.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Laura, how has the Harris campaign been spending these final days? What are your sources telling you?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: The Harris campaign is feeling pretty good about the election right now at this point, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 They point to their expansive ground game, specifically that, in the final weekend they had volunteers knocking on hundreds of thousands of doors across the battleground states. In total, campaign officials said that the campaign has knocked on 16 million doors across the battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is that Harris` campaign and Democrats are relying big on turnout amongst women, and they`re also really hoping that they are going to be getting a bigger margin of those disenchanted Republicans than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got in 2020, those people who voted for \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  in the primary, those \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 -type Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But when I asked \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , the campaign chair, today about those remaining undecided fence-sitters, she said that the campaign is still consistently seeing them in the final two weeks breaking for Harris, and she has in the past specifically noted the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally and Trump`s rhetoric in the final stretch.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Lisa, how is former President Trump looking at this moment? It was striking to see Vice President Harris say she feels she has the momentum and he was saying, well, this feels like ours to lose. What does this moment look like to them?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Well, they have an interesting communication strategy. They have gone dark, essentially, with the press. They haven`t had a press call in the way that the Harris campaign has. One of them said, if you`re spinning, you`re not winning.\par \par \uc2 But they still weren`t giving me numbers like we have from the Harris campaign on how many door knocks. Part of that is because their get-out- the-vote and canvassing strategy all along has been unorthodox.\par \par \uc2 They have changed the playbook that the Republicans had four years ago. It was a role of the dice. They have been trying to go for low-propensity voters, but also from Trump volunteers, some of them, people who`ve never canvassed before. So it`s a question of whether those folks will get out the vote or not.\par \par \uc2 How many doors they have knocked on, we don`t know, because they`re also relying on a myriad independent groups to get out there on the abortion issue, Americans for Progress. There are many -- for Prosperity.\par \par \uc2 There are many different groups doing this out. One thing that they like right now, the Republicans and the Trump campaign, is the early vote turnout. They like that rural counties have turned out, as we have reported here. They think that that is going to be a boost for them. They admit they don`t know, are those new voters or are those just voters who would have come out anyway?\par \par \uc2 But they think they feel some momentum in the early vote going their way. But any source that I trust is one who will admit it is very close. They see many paths, Trump campaign, to winning, but they also admit they don`t know.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, Lisa Desjardins, Laura Barron-Lopez on the campaign`s views before this last day of voting, good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The day`s other headlines start in Eastern Spain, where heavy rain has battered Barcelona.\par \par \uc2 It comes just days after catastrophic floods pummeled the Valencia region down the coast, killing more than 200 people. The search for bodies continues there as frustration builds over the government`s response.\par \par \uc2 ITV Europe editor James Mates has this report.\par \par \uc2 JAMES MATES: Deep under a shopping center in the suburbs of Valencia, the water is as deep as ever,this car park still barely accessible to rescue workers. In some places, only a canoe is good enough.\par \par \uc2 It`s in places like these that they fear many more victims are still to be found. Being caught in your car when the floods hit was often fatal. Finally now, but much too late, the full resources of the Spanish state are being deployed in the city. The anger here is that it took five days for the army and its heavy equipment to be on their streets in these sort of numbers; 7,800 are now at work here.\par \par \uc2 What a difference it might have made to Valencians if they`d seen them the morning after the floods. Instead, they`re not just desperate at the loss of almost everything they own. They`re angry too, anger they took out on their king and queen yesterday afternoon, spattering them with clods of the mud that had invaded their homes.\par \par \uc2 "We have to give them hope," the king said late last night, "give them hope and guarantee to them that the state in all its power is present."\par \par \uc2 Almost unbelievably, it`s still raining torrentially on Spain`s Mediterranean coast,Barcelona Airport partially evacuated today, roads nearby turning into minor rivers, though nothing like as dangerous as the wall of water that hit Valencia. Some of the streets of Tarragona, south of Barcelona, are also awash.\par \par \uc2 Back in Valencia, the search has moved downstream not so much for survivors as for bodies in vehicles swept miles away by the water. Nighttime infrared pictures show the moment a sniffer dog alerts its handlers to the presence of a possible victim along a riverbed. These searches could be going on for many days yet.\par \par \uc2 Longer still, though, will be the anger and recrimination at both the lack of warning and lack of help.\par \par \uc2 James Mates, \uc2 ITV\uc2  news.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: In the Middle East, Israel says it`s terminated a decades-old agreement that officially recognizes \uc2 UNRWA\uc2 , the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  agency providing aid and services to the Gaza Strip.\par \par \uc2 That comes a week after Israel`s legislature passed two laws that would sever ties with the agency and stop its operations there. Israel says the organization has been infiltrated by \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists. \uc2 UNRWA\uc2  says it`s purged any suspected \uc2 Hamas\uc2  from its ranks.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, health officials in Gaza say today there are no emergency crews left in the north, where Israel has waged a renewed offensive for nearly a month.\par \par \uc2 EYAD ZAQOUT, Palestinian Health Ministry (through translator): There are no longer any ambulances in the Northern Gaza Strip governorate to carry out the duty of transporting the injured. A large number of injured people are bleeding on the roads and in the places of various targets. And there are no civil defense or first aid crews to perform humanitarian duties and transport and provide the necessary medical assistance to them.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Separately, health officials in Lebanon say that more than 3,000 people have been killed during the last 13 months of fighting between Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . The Iran-backed group began firing rockets into Northern Israel the day after the October 7 \uc2 Hamas\uc2  attacks on Israel. Last month, Israel began a ground invasion into Lebanon to target \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  fighters there.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to India, authorities say at least 36 people were killed when an overcrowded bus plunged into a gorge earlier today. The accident happened in the remote Almora district and the mountainous north of the country. Onlookers at the base of the 200-foot ravine watched as emergency crews pulled bodies from the wreckage and attended to the survivors. At least 20 people were injured, in addition to those killed.\par \par \uc2 Officials say that the bus was poorly maintained and may have skidded before swerving off the road.\par \par \uc2 Western leaders have welcomed the reelection of Moldova`s pro-European president. \uc2 Maia Sandu\uc2  defeated a Russia-friendly opponent in a race that was marred by claims of Russian interference and voter fraud. In a statement, U.S. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  congratulated Sandu on her historic reelection, saying -- quote -- "Russia sought to undermine Moldova`s democratic institutions and election processes, but Russia failed."\par \par \uc2 Sandu campaigned on a promise to push forward Moldova`s candidacy for \uc2 European Union\uc2  membership. The country applied to join the E.U. shortly after Russia`s invasion of Ukraine in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will take up a long-running dispute over redistricting in Louisiana involving two mostly Black districts. The decision marks at least the third time the justices will step into the battle over voting lines in the state. A lower court had invalidated the most recent map, saying it relies too heavily on race. But the justices allowed it to be used in this election cycle after civil rights groups appealed.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will hear arguments early next year, with the decision expected by the summer of 2025.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, a white former police officer has been convicted of murder in the fatal shooting of an unarmed Black man. A jury delivered the verdict to Adam Coy in a Columbus courtroom today. Coy had testified that he thought 47-year-old Andre Hill was holding a silver revolver when he shot him four times in a garage nearly four years ago.\par \par \uc2 The object turned out to be keys. Coy faces at least 15 years in prison. His sentencing is set for later this month.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks ended lower as investors hold their breath for tomorrow`s election. The Dow dipped about 250 points. The Nasdaq and S&P 500 also lost ground.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": election security experts warn about misinformation and militant groups trying to sow chaos; Tamara Keith and Amy Walter break down the latest political news on this election eve; And we remember the towering legacy of the late music producer Quincy Jones.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Both candidates are using the final hours of campaigning to make their messages clear in key battlegrounds, which we will be keeping a close eye on tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Our Lisa Desjardins is at the PBS News super screen for a look at the various paths to victory for both candidates.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: All right, let`s talk about Pennsylvania. We have heard about Pennsylvania, Pennsylvania, Pennsylvania. Why is it so critical for both candidates on their path to 270?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: The Keystone State living up to its name.\par \par \uc2 Let`s look at how The Cook Political Report rates these races, as you know, seven key swing states. And if you look at what both campaigns see as a potential likelihood in the way states are leaning right now, then you have North Carolina, Georgia going for Trump, Arizona going for Trump. And let`s even say Nevada, which right now is about even goes for Trump.\par \par \uc2 In this situation, if \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is able to win Wisconsin and Michigan, the so-called blue wall, her only way of getting to the presidency is through Pennsylvania. Now, even if she was able to win here in Nevada, if she loses Pennsylvania, Trump still wins. Same thing. Even Arizona, if she`s able to win there, she still needs to win in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So, for the Democrats, the Keystone State is absolutely critical.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So if Pennsylvania is a must win for Harris, what are the must-wins for Trump?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Right.\par \par \uc2 Let`s look at the alternate scenario here. So let`s say that Trump is able to win in Arizona and in Nevada. Now, here we go. Let`s leave Pennsylvania off the board for just a second and leave it blank.\par \par \uc2 Now, he -- if he can`t win in North Carolina and Georgia, his path gets much more difficult. I will show you what I mean, 16 points here in North Carolina. Let`s say it goes to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . That puts her at 267, just three points away from winning at all. So, in other words, if Trump wants to win, he`s got to win in Pennsylvania and in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a strange combination. If he`s able to do well enough in Pennsylvania and still lose North Carolina, that`s a scenario Republicans don`t really see happening because of the way voter groups overlap. In other words, some of these early states in the night, North Carolina and Georgia, the South, the Southern wall, Republicans really want that to come in for them.\par \par \uc2 If Trump can`t win in both of these states, he really may have a problem for the rest of the night.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, Lisa, move us away from the battlegrounds for a second, because Iowa and New Hampshire are back in the headlines, some surprising polling over the weekend and some shifts. What happens if those states are in play?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Two different sets of expectations and effects from these states. Now, let`s talk first about Iowa. Again, let`s start with this base scenario where Harris is able to win the blue wall and that second district in Nebraska, Trump wins these other states in the Sun Belt. Let`s say Harris is able to switch Iowa and its six electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 That gets her up to 276, but it really doesn`t change the map for her. She can still win here, lose here. It really doesn`t affect her win or loss ability as much as it would give her more points on the board. New Hampshire is a different situation, because this is the situation Republicans fear is Harris running the blue wall through these three states, getting 270 electoral votes. They need another path.\par \par \uc2 This is why you saw vice presidential candidate J.D. Vance go to New Hampshire, try to pick up these four electoral votes, gets them to 272. They just want another potential pathway to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Now, is this likely? Both parties say maybe not, but Republicans think they have a real chance in New Hampshire, where they don`t have early voting, and voting will be starting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, let`s shift our focus back to the battlegrounds, Lisa. How are things shaping up there for the campaign?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: OK, so where the heck are these battlegrounds?\par \par \uc2 Folks, it is unbelievably close. Let`s take a look at these races. Arizona, Trump plus-two, Georgia plus-one. Look at this, Michigan, Harris plus-one, Trump plus-one. Even in North Carolina, even in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin plus-one.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if I have ever seen a \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  battleground states this close. Now, of course, this isn`t a statement of the exact state of the race, but it tells you that it`s close. It`s a virtual tie, all of these races within the margin of error, most certainly as we stand and look at it right now.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Lisa, meanwhile, some new polling from PBS News as well. What stands out to you?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Right, some big numbers.\par \par \uc2 As we heard earlier, look at this. Right now, in national likely voters, we see Trump down by four points over Harris, and that is Harris outside the margin of error. So that`s a trend that the Democrats would certainly like to see.\par \par \uc2 And something else Democrats like to see, the gender gap in terms of men has certainly changed just since October. Look at this, it was a 16-point gap Harris had with men. Now look at that, just four points. That`s an incredible 12-point difference.\par \par \uc2 But I will also say Trump and Vance have also made up ground with women. They have started to close that gap on their end.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa Desjardins, our thanks to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Today, federal officials responsible for securing the election and U.S. critical infrastructure said that, while they were tracking small- scale disruptions to tomorrow`s vote, they saw no evidence of activity that could affect the outcome.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The threats to influence and interfere with the vote include disinformation, hacking and even attempts to foment violence by both domestic and foreign actors.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with Nick Schifrin, who`s reporting on this year`s foreign threat.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start there, Nick.\par \par \uc2 What is the biggest concern this election about foreign interference, foreign actors?\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Geoff, the intelligence community believes that Russia favors former President Trump, that Iran favors Vice President Harris, and that China`s focused on downballot races.\par \par \uc2 But all are united in wanting to sow chaos and deepen division in the U.S. And so one of the main concerns is actually after Election Day, that if, for example, former President Trump were to declare victory, his campaign declares widespread fraud, or even as there are allegations of any kind of fraud after the election, Russia, Iran would then amplify any of those claims of fraud and even try and foment violence.\par \par \uc2 And so one of the main officials -- one of the main messages from officials tonight who want to secure the vote is listen to state and local authorities, not any candidate, not any campaign, and that election night results are unofficial.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Let`s talk more about Russia. What`s the specific concern?\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Intelligence community officials say that Russian actors are the most sophisticated, even knowing which swing states to target, and they have generated the most viral content.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  called out a new video you see there. It makes false allegations about early voting. The intelligence community has not identified this video as Russian-created, but multiple researchers today told me it was likely by the Russian group Doppelganger.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the intelligence community says Russian actors have released a -- quote -- "fire hose of disinformation," including videos like this one, about Haitian voters that undermine Americans` faith in the election results.\par \par \uc2 And officials say many videos like this one, false claims about ballots in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, are designed to help Trump and amplify his claims of fraud and at the same time denigrate Vice President Harris. You see another one there with a false claim about Harris and rare rhinos.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s attempts are largely through proxies, Geoff, with ties to Russian intelligence, with an attempt to launder their Russian origin using P.R. companies, unwitting, witting Americans, even influencers.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: What about Iran?\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: The Department of Justice indicted three Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps officials earlier this year for hacking the Trump campaign, stealing e-mails and documents, and trying to leak those e-mails and documents to the media and even to the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 Intelligence officials and election researchers also say that Iran has used fake news articles, fake online personas, even offered people money to go to pro-Palestinian protests. But those efforts largely have not been considered effective. And a former senior military official told me that Cyber Command inside the military is -- quote -- "hammering" these Iranian groups online to try and stymie their operations.\par \par \uc2 Another point, Iran`s efforts, Russia`s efforts are not new at all. But what is new, the speed at which the intelligence community has been able to call out some of these fake disinformation and some of these fake accounts.\par \par \uc2 That said, it`s not clear social media companies have all complied with some of these findings, especially X, which is run by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the world`s richest man, who`s endorsed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 What about China? How is China trying to influence this election?\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Much more focused on downballot races, rather than the presidential race. Intelligence officials, researchers say that China`s disinformation has, frankly, largely been ineffective, a sign that China simply doesn`t understand the American electorate or American voting patterns, as much as especially Russia.\par \par \uc2 But, Geoff, that doesn`t mean that they`re not trying.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Nick Schifrin, our thanks to you, as always.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, as we have been discussing, the threat of political violence still hangs over the country nearly four years after a violent mob stormed the U.S. Capitol and tried to overturn the will of the voters.\par \par \uc2 With just one day left of voting, what lessons have we learned, and how does the threat compare to the last cycle?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Mary McCord. She`s a former acting assistant attorney general for national security, now teaches at Georgetown Law School.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Mary.\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD, Former Justice Department Official: Nice to be here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, law enforcement, we know, is on alert for Election Day. We have seen election offices and other locations ramping up security. How worried are you about violence on Election Day or the days to follow?\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD: I`m more worried about the days to follow.\par \par \uc2 Many things have come into play to protect voters and election workers on Election Day. First of all, unlike four years ago, I mean, we have seen this movie before, right? So election officials at the local level, at the state level, law enforcement across parties, community organizations, mayors have all worked together really over these four years to make sure that they can provide a safe voting venue, not only for the voters, but also for the poll workers, right?\par \par \uc2 And if that means more security, they have it. If it means different facets of law enforcement presence, again, carefully, so it doesn`t intimidate voters, because law enforcement in voting sometimes itself can be intimidation. But they have worked individually, community by community, to create plans that will work.\par \par \uc2 I also think that the effect of over 1,500 prosecutions coming out of the January 6 attack has made -- has been a real deterrent for those who would otherwise maybe think about engaging in armed and even unarmed forms of voter intimidation.\par \par \uc2 There`s been tons and tons of public education and statements and people in positions of authority talking about voter intimidation. DOJ has been forward-leading. And a lot of these people, I think they see others who`ve gotten in trouble. That wasn`t voter intimidation. That was a violent attack.\par \par \uc2 But, still, they know this could cross -- things could cross the line. I`m more worried about after the election.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, let me ask you about the scenario that Nick reported on as well. It`s likely we may not have a result on the night on the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 If former President Trump comes out and declares victory, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , his supporter, amplifies that on X, does that change or alter the potential for violence?\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD: So this is exactly what I think -- why I`m more worried about the post-election period, particularly during the time of uncertainty, because, as you said, and as we saw in 2020, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I think will, as he did before, come out and say he won, he won in a landslide, the voting should stop, and that votes counted after this period are fraudulent.\par \par \uc2 He will attack mail-in voting, absentee voting, drop boxes, election workers, et cetera. And false -- and that -- he will be aided because we know from Nick`s reporting and from the intelligence community`s warnings that Russia and Iran and China, all our adversaries, have an interest in seeding additional false information that, frankly, we as Americans do a great job of propounding and spreading.\par \par \uc2 And those false narratives are what become the justification for people to engage in political violence. That`s what we saw in 2020. That`s what led to January 6. And that might be individual acts. It might be physical. It might be threats, intimidation, harassment. It might be pressuring election officials not to certify, governors not to certify, state legislatures to try to somehow take their own action and send up their own slates of electors.\par \par \uc2 All of those are things that could be -- could happen if this false narrative spreads and people either believe it or pretend to believe it.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There`s been some reporting and tracking of online chatter, in particular in the kinds of social media platforms that are very lightly moderated, where we know a lot of far right and extremist groups gather and share information, like Telegram and Gab.\par \par \uc2 There was one nonprofit that said in October that election denialism increased by 317 percent on Telegram, by 105 percent on Gab. How likely is that online chatter to fuel real-world violence or action of any kind?\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD: So what we`re not seeing as much of -- or I should say we. I get all my information through researchers, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, I`m a lawyer and a former prosecutor and former national security official. But I talk with researchers regularly. And what we are not seeing is actual mobilization, logistics, planning. Now, some of that could be because there`s more operational security now than there was back in 2020, they`re more careful about not speaking on public forums and public chat rooms about those plans.\par \par \uc2 But, still, if it was big enough and massive enough, these kind of things would start to leak out. So I do think there`s probably planning going on, again, at that hyper-local level, and there will be some disruptions tomorrow and disruptions going forward.\par \par \uc2 But something massive like a January 6, we`re not seeing that kind of mobilization.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Mary, briefly, before we let you go, people want to know that they can vote, and vote safely, and they`re worried about what`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to folks out there as we head into this last day of voting?\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD: I do not think voters should be concerned about going to the polls tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But even armed groups who have agitated online, a lot of it is all talk. We see very little uptick in those who actually want to show up. And, again, they are worried about prosecution. I think voting -- I think this election will be safe for voters. I think it`ll be secure in terms of the results. And we just have to take a breath and wait for those results to come.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Good advice.\par \par \uc2 Mary McCord, always good to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARY MCCORD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The tight race for the White House is fueling a wave of election litigation by both Democrats and Republicans as they spar over the ground rules for early voting, mail-in ballots, and voter eligibility.\par \par \uc2 For a closer look at that and what`s to come, we`re joined now by election law expert David Becker. He`s the executive director and founder of the nonpartisan Center for Election Innovation and Research.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER, Executive Director, Center for Election Innovation and Research: Thanks for having me, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So more than 80 million Americans have already voted, key swing states like North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Nevada, Michigan experiencing a high volume of mail-in and absentee voting.\par \par \uc2 Have you spotted any disruptions, irregularities, any problems that could lead to legitimate questions about the integrity of the vote or the vote count?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: It`s remarkable.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, we have seen about 80 million ballots cast already. States like North Carolina and Georgia are over four million ballots cast. That`s likely around 75 to 80 percent of their total turnout already voting. And we have seen hardly any problems at all, certainly nothing that would involve voter safety or any kind of conflicts at the polling places.\par \par \uc2 We`re really even not hearing many stories about lines. Most of the stories about lines occurred on the last day of early voting in some states. And, of course, that always happens. That`s just reflecting enthusiasm and the fact people waited until the last minute a little bit, which is always understandable.\par \par \uc2 But it`s remarkable how smoothly voting has gone, how few problems there have been. And that`s particularly so while we`re dealing with things like our foreign adversaries spreading disinformation and the highly divisive environment we`re currently in. It`s really remarkable, a testament to our election officials.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: I want to ask you about that in a bit.\par \par \uc2 But, first, both parties are preparing massive legal operations to file and respond to litigation on and after Election Day. 2024, according to one estimate, is the most litigated election ever. What are you tracking in particular?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, I think we have seen a lot of preelection litigation. It`s very likely it is the most preelection litigation we have ever seen before.\par \par \uc2 And we should put this in context. In 2020, there was a lot of preelection litigation that was trying to clarify rules, particularly changes in adaptations that were made due to COVID. And it should be noted that Republicans won about 85 percent of those cases preelection in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In the 2024 election, we`re seeing some cases like that, we`re seeking to clarify rules, but we`re seeing Republicans push last-minute changes a lot more, and we`re seeing them lose a majority of cases. We`re seeing things like cases raised about the voter lists, which they have known about for days, weeks, months, years, and could have brought at any given point in time.\par \par \uc2 We see challenges about mail voting laws that have been around in some cases for decades in states like Nevada. And then we`re seeing challenges to even military and overseas voting procedures that have been around in some cases for as long as 40 years.\par \par \uc2 So those kinds of cases have been dismissed by courts, even by judges appointed by former President Trump himself. And we will be then moving on to the post-election period. We will have to see what happens, obviously.\par \par \uc2 In a really close election, there is legitimate litigation that can be brought. Think Florida 2000, when it`s that close, hundreds of votes. But when we see an election that`s decided by margins like we saw in 2020, it`s very, very unlikely any post-election litigation will succeed.\par \par \uc2 And that`s largely because the candidate who`s behind will still have to bring evidence that they can somehow make up the margin. And once you get over a few hundred votes, it`s almost impossible to do that.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, I was going to ask you that. Does the number of cases floated by the GOP and Republican-affiliated entities, does that suggest anything about the number of legitimate concerns connected to the vote?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: So I think what`s been surprising is, it hasn`t reflected legitimate concerns.\par \par \uc2 It seems to be -- have the effect -- we have the normal kind of litigation that seeks to clarify rules that need clarifying. But there`s a lot of litigation, as I mentioned, that seeks to challenge rules that were never going to be successfully challenged. They were never going to win a lot of those cases.\par \par \uc2 And you have to wonder if what we will see after the election is that the losing candidate might bring up some of these same dismissed claims to try to suggest the election was stolen. That would be very dangerous if that happens. There are a lot of sincere supporters of a losing candidate that could be incited to anger or potentially even violence.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we remember that to some degree from 2020.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Returning to the issue of foreign interference, which you mentioned earlier, has the effort by U.S. intelligence agencies to expose Russian disinformation, has that been effective in disrupting them and diminishing the impact?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, I think our adversaries are highly motivated.\par \par \uc2 They see a big reward in potentially disrupting our elections. And, again, they`re not disrupting our elections by attacking actual election infrastructure. We`re way too decentralized. We have paper ballots. We have a lot of protections and redundancies and checks and balances in place so that no foreign adversary or domestic actor could change or alter the votes in an election.\par \par \uc2 We should say, for everyone in the United States, don`t trust social media. Don`t trust videos. Don`t trust claims about election fraud or something that they`re seeing just on the Web. Trust your local and state election officials. Go to those sites. Find out what`s really going on. Do not rely upon social media and the Internet right now, because there`s a lot of disinformation.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: That`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 David Becker with the nonpartisan Center for Election Innovation and Research, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Thanks, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: For more on the tight race for the White House, I`m joined by our Politics Monday team. That`s Amy Walter of The Cook Political Report With Amy Walter and Tamara Keith of NPR.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH, National Public Radio: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Can`t believe it`s finally here.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, The Cook Political Report: I know it.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Election eve, my favorite eve of all time.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Of all the eves.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Of all the eves.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I want to ask you about that new PBS News poll we have got, Tam, because you saw there, as Lisa reported earlier, Harris with her first national lead that`s outside the margin of error, just four-point lead among likely voters at a 3.5 percent margin of error on that poll.\par \par \uc2 But you have been in the battleground states. You were in Arizona, Wisconsin, Nevada. That`s where the races are won or lost. Are you seeing any shift that tells you which way this race is going?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: I have spoken to a lot of people who believe it`s really, really close.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t know that you -- when you`re out there in these states, it`s like you`re looking at the toenail of an elephant, but you don`t know whether it`s a rock or an elephant. And so what I will say is that I was covering Harris. I was going to some of her events.\par \par \uc2 The enthusiasm is quite intense among her supporters who are showing up at these events, 10,000, 12000 people showing up. One thing that really stood out to me was, we were in Wisconsin. She did a rally in a town called Little Chute, which is a population-12,000 town in a county that went for Trump.\par \par \uc2 And there was a lot of enthusiasm in that gymnasium. Driving up, there were a supporters outside waving and banners. But then you get a little bit closer and you see this long line of people who didn`t get into the event because it was oversold. Inside, there was a lot of enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 That is part of their effort to lose by less in areas, more rural areas outside of the urban centers. Now, is this part of a big trend and we`re going to see, oh, well, gosh, she lost by less in all these counties out there, and that`s how she won? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: No idea.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Amy, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: It still feels to me like we`re coming down to Pennsylvania, like we did last time.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: And, once again, it is a combination of for both candidates losing by a little bit less in certain areas, winning by a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 There are going to be some swing counties that may tell us the story early on in the night, especially in the northeastern part of the state in those places around Scranton, in Northampton County, in Bucks County, that could give us a sense of who`s doing a little bit better, a little bit worse than the numbers were in 2020.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I have to ask you, though, about Iowa.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I know. Everyone is talking about Iowa.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Because this poll by -- she`s a renowned pollster...\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I know. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: ... Ann Selzer, we should say, highly respected.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: This poll that shows her -- that shows Harris leading Trump in a state that he won easily twice, what does that say to you about Iowa or what it means for others?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I know it. And she -- it`s not just that she`s well-renowned. She`s also well-renowned for being an out -- what looks like an outlier in 2020 and in 2016.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: She was showing in Iowa basically the opposite of what a lot of people thought that was going to happen, not just in Iowa, but nationally. And she ended up being right.\par \par \uc2 So I think that`s why she gets a lot more weight than any other poll. Part of the challenge we have with Iowa, because it`s not a swing state, we don`t have a lot of other pieces of data. What we do have, though, are competitive House races there that are showing Harris doing a little bit better...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... than what -- how Biden did in those competitive districts.\par \par \uc2 So there is, there is a sense that, in those places where Democrats are competing, Harris is also doing a little bit better there. Those areas where Harris is doing a little bit better, they are in more urban areas like around Des Moines or college towns like Iowa City.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that I think is really interesting is, this is driven, the lead for Harris in this poll, by independent women, older women.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: You know where else we have seen some swings to Harris in states that aren`t very -- aren`t polled very often? Kansas, Nebraska, Plains states, which also have a overwhelmingly white population, where older women are going to have a bigger part,they play a bigger part because of the demography of that state, and also places where abortion has played a big role, the Kansas initiative, of course, in 2022.\par \par \uc2 And in Iowa, there`s a six-week ban that is very unpopular. A poll that came out, I think, in September showed 70 percent of women in Iowa disapprove of that six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Tam, I know you have reported on this. We have talked about the gender gap before. We know abortion is on the ballot in 10 different states, including Arizona and Nevada. How much of an issue is it going to be tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: I don`t see how it can`t be a big issue.\par \par \uc2 It is a defining issue in this race. It`s a defining issue in the way Harris has campaigned. It`s also defined Trump`s campaign and his effort -- when he the other day said, "I`m going to protect women, whether they like it or not," he was talking about his -- his policy on abortion and other issues.\par \par \uc2 He continues to try to figure out a place to be on this issue, which is challenging ground. Republicans won. He -- Trump got the Supreme Court that he wanted. The Republicans got this issue where they wanted it. And now it`s a challenge for them, because a lot of voters, especially women, are upset.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: You agree with that, Amy?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes. Yes, I do think that is part of it.\par \par \uc2 And I have said sort of fundamentally what this race really is about for -- especially for the undecided voters right now...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... but what it`s been about for a lot of voters all along, is the sense of, who is the bigger risk?\par \par \uc2 If you don`t like either candidate, which candidate is going to be less risky and what does it mean to have control and choice? And some of it is about abortion. Some of it too is about the kind of country that they want to live in.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to cut different ways. The other thing that`s really hard in polling the issue of abortion is, it doesn`t necessarily show up as abortion. This is why you hear Harris talking about it as control, freedom, right, that this is less about the issue of abortion and more about this idea that, when I talk about -- an individual voter would say, when I talk about control or freedom, I mean this.\par \par \uc2 And for many voters, it means reproductive control.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Tam, as you know, you have been covering this race. It`s been largely stuck at 50/50 for many, many months. Lisa reported earlier on those key battlegrounds in particular, where we have not seen a lot of movement, one or two points the -- one direction or the other, within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 As you`re watching tomorrow, as results start to come in, as we get a sense of where things go, what are you going to be tracking? What are you looking at most closely.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Well, and I will say that our poll shows Trump at 47 percent, which is where he was in 2020. It`s where he was in 2016.\par \par \uc2 For all of the things that this country and this campaign has been through, for him to be in exactly the same place is quite remarkable. Whether he lands there, I don`t know. But I`m -- that is one thing I am definitely watching.\par \par \uc2 And I will be watching North Carolina and Georgia, because their returns are likely to come in earlier than some of the other states. I will be watching there to see how that goes. If Trump wins those states, then all eyes go to the blue wall.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: And if he doesn`t, then this is a different race.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: What does that say to you if he doesn`t?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: If he doesn`t, then there`s a path, but it`s not an easy path.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Exactly. I think that`s right.\par \par \uc2 And there are also congressional races in those places that can also give us a clue maybe to how the presidential race is going, especially if the House races get called earlier, but also could give us -- at least tip the hand of where House control is going. It`s unlikely we`re going to know until long after Wednesday, because so many of the competitive House races are on the West Coast in states like California that do take longer time to count their ballots.\par \par \uc2 But I`m -- there are competitive House races in some of these battlegrounds, Michigan, Pennsylvania, that will also give us early on in the night at least a sense, if the race is breaking one way or the other, which way it`s going.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I got to ask, even though I don`t know if either of you will answer, do you care to make a prediction about what we`re going to see tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Absolutely no.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: No. No.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: I don`t even predict whether we will know by the end of the night. So it`s probably a safe bet that we...\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I can predict it will be earlier -- I will make a just prediction, that it will not be Saturday by the time we know...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: OK.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: ... like it was last time.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We will take that.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: OK.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: From Amy Walter`s mouth to our...\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Amy Walter, Tamara Keith, great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And we hope that you will join us for a live election night coverage tomorrow as the votes are counted.\par \par \uc2 Our livestream online begins at noon Eastern, and then special coverage right here on your \uc2 PBS\uc2  station starts at 7:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: A giant in the music and entertainment industry has died.\par \par \uc2 Quincy Jones was revered as a record producer, songwriter, composer, arranger and performer throughout his seven decades in the business. Through his vast range of projects, from the producer of the bestselling album of all time to composer of some of the most recognized music in film, he racked up 80 Grammy nominations and 28 Grammy Awards.\par \par \uc2 Senior arts correspondent Jeffrey Brown has this for remembrance, part of our arts and culture series, Canvas.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: From Michael Jackson`s biggest hits to some of Frank Sinatra`s most classic arrangements, few loomed larger in American popular culture or had more success than Quincy Jones.\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JONES, Record Producer: Love, laugh, live and give, that`s what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: Born on Chicago`s South Side in 1933, Jones began his life in music as a jazz trumpeter. He became fast friends with Ray Charles when both were in still in their teens.\par \par \uc2 He soon began composing and arranging, including 1962`s classic "Soul Bossa Nova" and Lesley Gore`s hit "It`s My Party," which arrived a year later. Across a career that spanned more than 70 years, he worked with seemingly everyone, including jazz legends like Ella Fitzgerald and Duke Ellington, and rappers such as Dr. Dre and Snoop Dogg.\par \par \uc2 His most impactful moment, a three-album run with Michael Jackson that included the bestselling record of all time, 1982`s "Thriller," along with "Off the Wall" and "Bad."\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JONES: Anyone who says they have figured out how to make records that sell more than 50 million records is lying and smoking Kool-Aid.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JONES: Doesn`t work like that. You just find a group of songs that touch you and give you goose bumps. And that`s why I go by my goose bumps. I don`t need survey groups or anything like that, and let the rest take care of itself.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: In 2010, Jones was awarded the National Medal of Arts by former President Obama.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For his extraordinary contributions to American music as a musician, composer, and arranger.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: And he is one of just 27 members of the rarefied EGOT club, winner of an Emmy, Grammy, an honorary Oscar and Tony. The Oscar came in 1995.\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JONES: I did not engineer this journey. To tell you the truth, I don`t think I could even see this far.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: He composed dozens of memorable soundtracks for film and television, including "The Color Purple," a film he also produced, another prominent role he played in popular culture.\par \par \uc2 In 1985, in a role only someone of his stature could have filled, he organized the recording of the smash-hit single "We Are the World," a session that featured music`s biggest stars and would become one of the bestselling songs ever.\par \par \uc2 Jones had seven children and married three times. One of his daughters, the actress Rashida Jones, directed a 2018 documentary on his extraordinary life.\par \par \uc2 QUINCY JONES: I have a feeling that God will prevail and the light will prevail. You have to think like that, because we have come a long way. Got a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY BROWN: In a statement, his family said he died peacefully Sunday evening surrounded by loved ones. Quincy Jones was 91.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Jeffrey Brown.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And that is the "News Hour" for tonight.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget to join us tomorrow evening for our live election night coverage here on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . And that starts at 7:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 With that, I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team, thank you for joining us and we hope to see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241105ekb400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for October 7, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Rogin, Jack Hewson, Nick Schifrin, Geoff Bennett \par \uc2 9,152 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC, which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the user's personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and ASC Services II Media, LLC's copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett. Amna Nawaz is away.\par \par \uc2 On the "NewsHour" tonight: Israel marks one year since \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` deadly attack, as war rages on multiple fronts.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI, Kibbutz Be`eri, Israel, Resident: Still, I`m here. So they will have to understand this nobody is going because they kill us. Nobody is going.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The recovery from Hurricane Helene is complicated by lies, hoaxes and conspiracy theories spread by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 And residents of a Ukrainian town face an agonizing choice as Russian forces advance within a mile of their homes.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 It was one year ago today that \uc2 Hamas\uc2  gunman launched a surprise terror attack on Israel, killing and wounding thousands and abducting hundreds more. In Israel and around the world, memorials marked a year of loss, a year of trauma, and a year of war that is still unfolding.\par \par \uc2 It was not yet dawn. Still, families of Israel`s victims had already gathered to remember, lighting candles and choking back tears for the more than 360 people who were killed at the site of a music festival one year ago.\par \par \uc2 When the sun finally rose, club music echoed. It was the last song that festivalgoers heard on October 7, 2023, before the shooting started, and in pain laid bare the solemn ceremony pierced by the anguished wail of a victim`s relative.\par \par \uc2 Families spoke of the moment that everything changed.\par \par \uc2 SHIMON BUSKILA, Father of Victim: It seems to be like yesterday was the last day we saw him. For us, yesterday was the last day of the old life.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: In Jerusalem, sirens blared at 6:29 a.m., believed to be the precise moment \uc2 Hamas\uc2 -led militants began their attack, massacring more than 1,200 people and taking over 200 hostages. Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  today visited a memorial, the names of the victims etched forever in iron, and surrounded by his Cabinet held a minute of silence before insisting on strength in the resulting war.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , Israeli Prime Minister (through translator): Since that black day, we have been fighting. This is the war of our existence, the war of resurrection. This is how I would like to officially call the war. We will end the war when we complete all the goals we have set.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, indeed, across the world, others paid tribute. President Biden and the first lady honored those lost with a prayer, "God full of mercy," and the lighting of a single candle.\par \par \uc2 The solemn anniversary was also front of mind on the campaign trail. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comforted Jewish leaders and visited a memorial grave site in Queens. His running mate, J.D. Vance, in a separate event in Washington, D.C., said he and so many others share in Israel`s pain.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), Vice Presidential Candidate: We can`t ever let our fellow citizens forget that October the 7th was not just an attack on Israel and it was not just an attack on Jews. It was an attack on Americans.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  planted a pomegranate tree, which represents hope and righteousness in Judaism, on the grounds of her residence and offered words of support and comfort to Israel.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: Doug and I pray for the family and loved ones of all of those who were lost. And may their memories be a blessing.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And in an interview airing tonight on \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "60 Minutes," Harris said this when pressed on the U.S. relationship with Netanyahu.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER, CBS News Correspondent: Do we have a real close ally in Prime Minister Netanyahu?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : I think, with all due respect, the better question is, do we have an important alliance between the American people and the Israeli people? And the answer to that question is yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: For many Palestinians, there was grief for the 42,000 Gazans that local health officials say have been killed since the war began. And there was anger for what many Palestinians are calling a genocide at the hands of the Israelis.\par \par \uc2 NASRAH AL-QABALANI, Ramallah Resident (through translator): A year has passed and Israel is stumbling. It is committing massacres against our people, our children and our houses.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: In Gaza, this day was not so much an anniversary, but a day like all the others. Many were still reeling from Israeli airstrikes that tore through a mosque and a shelter for displaced people early Sunday that killed at least 26 and wounded nearly 100 others.\par \par \uc2 And as the war rages on multiple fronts, Israel continued its relentless aerial assault on neighboring Lebanon in its pursuit of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  militants.\par \par \uc2 What were entire blocks in Beirut`s southern suburbs are now craters.\par \par \uc2 ALI ASILI, Beirut Resident (through translator): Nothing is left. Our whole lives have changed. We have been displaced to the streets, all of us together.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  launched some 175 rockets into Northern Israel in return, a tit-for-tat on a tense day in which Israel promised to launch more operations in Lebanon along the southern coast and as the Israeli military told Gazans across the north to flee south, and told the displaced in Khan Yunis already to the south to flee anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 SHAIMA ABU LUDBA, Displaced From Khan Yunis (through translator): They said, evacuate, and we evacuated. And every time we`re evacuated to the streets, I wish they would eliminate us and end our suffering.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: For some, today marks a grim milestone, for others, another day in a life upended by a war with no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 The day`s other headlines start with Hurricane Milton, which has rapidly intensified to a Category 5 storm. Milton is on track to make landfall in the Tampa Bay area this week, bringing a life-threatening storm surge of up to 12 feet, a torrent of rain and high winds. Authorities and residents there are racing to prepare.\par \par \uc2 For many today, that meant filling sandbags. Others have already heeded evacuation orders. All this comes less than two weeks after Hurricane Helene battered the same area.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We had a lot of debris left from Hurricane Helene on Florida`s Gulf Coast. That creates a huge hazard if you have a major hurricane hit in that area this week.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Helene caused at least 230 deaths. About half of those fatalities were in North Carolina, where apocalyptic scenes still cover the western part of the state.\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 FEMA\uc2  administrator once again responded to unsubstantiated claims that her agency isn`t doing enough to help the storm`s victims.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , \uc2 FEMA\uc2  Administrator: Absolutely false. We have thousands of people on the ground, not just federal, but also our volunteers in the private sector. And, frankly, that type of rhetoric is demoralizing to our staff that have left their families to come here and help the people of North Carolina. And we will be here as long as they`re needed.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Shortly after that press conference, Criswell traveled to Florida, where she met with state and local officials ahead of Hurricane Milton.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  kicked off its new term today. In the months ahead, the justices will hear cases about ghost guns and transgender rights, among others. Today, the court declined to hear a Biden administration appeal over emergency abortions in Texas. In doing so, the justices left in place a lower court ruling saying hospitals can`t be required to perform abortions that go against Texas law.\par \par \uc2 The court also turned away an Alabama fertility clinic`s bid to avoid a wrongful death claim over the destruction of a couple`s frozen embryo. The case raised broader concerns about legal protections for in vitro fertilization nationwide. The justices also declined to hear appeals by singer R. Kelly on his child`s sex crime conviction and the social media platform X over a search warrant in an election interference case.\par \par \uc2 A Russian court sentenced a 72-year-old American man today to nearly seven years in prison charges of fighting for the Ukrainian military. Prosecutors say Stephen Hubbard signed a contract as a mercenary and fought for two months back in 2022 before being captured. He`s the first American to be convicted on such charges. Hubbard is originally from Michigan, but had reportedly been living in Ukraine since 2014.\par \par \uc2 Russian arrests of Americans have become increasingly common amid concerns that authorities are targeting U.S. citizens for future prisoner swaps.\par \par \uc2 Georgia`s Supreme Court has temporarily put back in place a ban on nearly all abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy. The order is meant to give the justices time to consider an appeal of last week`s ruling by a lower court judge that found the ban violates the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Georgia law was passed in 2019, but didn`t take effect until the fall of Roe v. Wade in 2022. It prohibits most abortions once a detectable human heartbeat is present, usually around six weeks, which is before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 And 2023 was the driest year for the world`s rivers in more than three decades. That`s according to a new report by the U.N.`s weather agency. Prolonged droughts have drained water levels in some areas to all-time lows, like here in the \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . The report also noted that glaciers, which feed many of the world`s rivers, suffered the largest loss of mass in half- a-century last year.\par \par \uc2 The agency`s director said that rising global temperatures are partly to blame and called the state of water -- quote -- "the canary in the coal mine of climate change."\par \par \uc2 And a pair of Americans have won the Nobel Prize for Medicine. The Massachusetts-based duo of Victor Ambros and Gary Ruvkun were recognized for the discovery of tiny bits of genetic material known as microRNA. A panel in Stockholm said their findings, initially studied in worms, are fundamentally important for how organisms develop and function, including humans.\par \par \uc2 After hearing the news, Ruvkun said a Nobel Prize amounts to a quantum leap in terms of recognition for their work. And Ambros explained the potential real-world health implications of microRNA.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR AMBROS, 2024 Nobel Laureate: The deeper we understand living systems, the better equipped we are to figure out what`s wrong when these systems go awry in the context of disease.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Experts say learning how to manipulate microRNA could one day lead to powerful treatments for diseases like cancer. Tomorrow, the Nobel for Physics will be announced.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks struggled to start the week. The Dow Jones industrial average dropped about 400 points, or nearly 1 percent. The Nasdaq gave back more than 200 points. The S&P 500 also ended lower on the day.\par \par \uc2 And a passing of note. Cissy Houston, the mother of the late Whitney Houston and a successful singer in her own right, has died. The two-time Grammy winner performed alongside the likes of Elvis Presley and Aretha Franklin. She saw early success as part of the vocal group The Sweet Inspirations, who sang backup for several soul acts and featured on Van Morrison`s "Brown Eyed Girl."\par \par \uc2 Branching out on her own, Houston was an in-demand session singer. Her vocals can be heard on tracks by Jimi Hendrix, Luther Vandross, and Beyonce, among others. Cissy Houston was 91 years old.\par \par \uc2 And still to come on the "News Hour": Tamara Keith and Amy Walter break down the latest political headlines; Russian forces advance on a Ukrainian town, leaving residents with the painful choice of whether to stay or go; and a college trains students to become automotive technicians and works to increase diversity in the field.\par \par \uc2 Among the first targets on the morning of October 7 were communities across Southern Israel, hit first by rockets, then infiltrated by gunmen, who went house to house, killing and kidnapping civilians.\par \par \uc2 Among those communities, Kibbutz Be`eri, where Nick Schifrin looks back on a day that has changed the country and the region and upended the lives of its 1,000 residents.\par \par \uc2 A warning: Some images in this story may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: One year later, the Bachar home in Kibbutz Be`eri three miles from the Gaza Strip is frozen in horror, evidence of a community that will never be the same, a family that will never be whole, and a safe room that couldn`t keep them safe.\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR, Kibbutz Be`eri, Israeli, Resident: They get into the house. You hear the steps on the floor. And they know that people still alive, they are in the safe room. So they go straight to the safe room.\par \par \uc2 They try to open and you close, and they try to open. And you close and they told me, "Open the door."\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Avida Bachar lived here with Dana, his wife of 32 years, their son, Carmel, and daughter Hadar.\par \par \uc2 The home stands as it was 365 days ago, a clock frozen to the time of the attack on this house and a safe room where Hadar Bachar recorded these messages for a local chat group.\par \par \uc2 HADAR BACHAR, Victim (through translator): They are burning us. We can`t - - they burn us. Come now, now, now. We`re being shot. They shot us. Come now.\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR: They shot me through the door. So, I saw one bullet took my arm here in the side, and a few seconds later, I collapsed and fell out of the safe room. Carmel, both hands go, because he holding like that, and the bullets hit his arm.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: The bullet holes and his or his son`s blood still stay in the door.\par \par \uc2 HADAR BACHAR (through translator): Help us please. They threw three grenades at us, please help us We`re dying here. My whole leg is injured. My whole leg is punctured, dad, all his legs, Carmel too. Mom is going to die, I`m telling you. Help us, anyone who can help us, please.\par \par \uc2 I love you. We love you. We all love you. Carmel, stay with us. Don`t close your eyes. Mom, stay with us, don`t die.\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR: Dana, she told me: "I -- I can`t breathe."\par \par \uc2 Hadar, she tried to clean her a little bit, and she told Hadar: "Hadar, it`s done. I don`t have nothing in this world," and she died.\par \par \uc2 Carmel told me: "Dad, when you bury me, please do it with my surfboard." A few seconds later, he got his short breath, and he died.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: A few weeks later, he and Avida led the funeral, a family split in half, a country in mourning and shock, and a son`s final wish granted, Carmel buried with his surfboard.\par \par \uc2 Bachar`s leg was amputated, and he spent five months in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 How does your leg feel today?\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR: Good. Practicing.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Today, he`s increasingly sure of his step and of a lesson taught by tragedy.\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR: When you say thank you for something, I`m not believe in God, but I can say thank you to Dana about 32 years with me. I met her, she was 17. And you got your wife for 32 years. Why you cry? You got everything. You got more than rich people on a boat. You got everything.\par \par \uc2 So I can thank you for her about this time. And I can thank you also to Carmel, about 15 years, because I got a great son.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Be`eri`s 1,000 residents lived in about three square miles, invaded by more than 340 gunmen, some wearing Israeli uniforms, civilians killed point blank, dead bodies discarded or denied any dignity and taken back to Gaza, a couple embracing each other burned alive.\par \par \uc2 In total, 101 civilians, more than one-tenth of Be`eri`s population and more than 30 security personnel, were killed, including a friend who fought alongside Yonatan Alfia.\par \par \uc2 YONATAN ALFIA, Former Israeli Paratrooper (through translator): The chaos was so high, none of us understood what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Alfia is a reservist and former paratrooper. He and others came from all over to join a handful of Israeli special forces to fight back. It took them nearly two days to declare a Be`eri liberated and bring shocked survivors out of their safe rooms.\par \par \uc2 YONATAN ALFIA (through translator): The main feeling of us fighters in this area was that we came to the battle unprepared. I, for example, came only with my personal handgun, when the other side, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  terrorists, fight us with RPGs, rockets, suicide drones, automatic weapons, mortar bombs and well-prepared command.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: The IDF`s own investigation into actions here in Be`eri admitted that it failed the residents here, that the early response was characterized by a lack of coordination and command-and-control, and that the residents of Be`eri were largely left on their own for the first seven hours.\par \par \uc2 Be`eri`s founders came here in 1946, when it was nothing but Negev Desert. They were pioneers, hoping to help create a country and build an agricultural settlement with residents who live communally.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI, Kibbutz Be`eri, Israel, Resident: This kibbutz, there was nothing here because there was no water. Everything that you see here is planted. Everything is manmade.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Nili Bar Sinai has lived here for a half-century, lured by Yoram Bar Sinai, one of the kibbutz`s original architects. They started a family and advocated for the kibbutz`s founding principle, cooperation.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI: He`s cooperative and not competitive, but he wants to live together. And it`s the saddest irony of all that we think in such cooperative terms, and this is what they do to us.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: You live on the opposite side of the kibbutz.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And your husband came here on the morning of the 7th.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: One year ago, Yoram Bar Sinai rushed to this house, their daughters, to help defend her and the grandkids.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI: They stopped the car and went up, seven of them, and he shot and they shot. And that was it. My daughter was there with three kids and she heard the sound. And then she heard steps coming and she hoped it was him. But it wasn`t. Somebody opened this thing, and she slammed the door on him, and she stayed there holding the handle for 20 hours, I think.\par \par \uc2 He was a cooperative guy. He could handle anyone, including me. He could take me for 50 years, so you can imagine. He was a good father. He was like a local kibbutznik. He was an army officer, and he was playing guitar. He was drawing. He was just the best you could get.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Twenty-five miles to the east, another kibbutz, Hatzerim, has opened its doors to Be`eri`s displaced and built them new prefab houses, including for Ayelet Hakim.\par \par \uc2 AYELET HAKIM, Displaced Resident of Kibbutz Be`eri, Israel: What was done to us on the 7th of October is unimaginable, and I don`t think anybody invented a word for it. And my life since, it`s just the same. I have a house, but not a home.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Hakim knows how lucky she is to be here and have her family whole. That`s her sister, Raz Ben Ami, abducted to Gaza one year ago today, alongside her brother-in-law, Ohad Ben Ami, two of 30 hostages taken from Be`eri. Raz was released during last November`s cease-fire, but Ohad remains a hostage.\par \par \uc2 AYELET HAKIM: I try to tell myself, convince myself he`s still alive, because we know about dead hostages. So, if we know about the dead hostages, the other ones that we don`t know about are still alive, as far as we know. And hopefully they will do something about it and bring him back.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Back in Be`eri today, at the epicenter of their loss, residents rallied for the hostages and hoped they heard a message.\par \par \uc2 "You are not alone," they chant. That`s also a reminder for themselves.\par \par \uc2 AVIDA BACHAR: We`re back to Be`eri because this is our place. I`m born here. I`m going to die here. We`re going to rebuild Be`eri again.\par \par \uc2 NILI BAR SINAI: My mother was killed 50 years ago at the Lod Airport, and now my husband. And still I`m here. So they will have to understand this. Nobody is going because they kill us. Nobody is going.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Determination despite devastation. But this community and country are still traumatized. And one year later, Be`eri and October the 7th remains an open wound.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Nick Schifrin in Kibbutz Be`eri, Israel.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has for several days now spread lies and spouted conspiracy theories about the federal government`s response to Hurricane Helene, disinformation that`s causing real-time confusion among some of those most desperate for help and answers.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by Juliette Kayyem, former assistant secretary at the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . Her latest piece in "The Atlantic" is titled "The Fog Of Disaster Is Getting Worse."\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM, Former \uc2 U.S. Department of Homeland Security\uc2  Assistant Secretary: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: As you note in the piece, the spread of rumors and misinformation has always been a problem during major disasters, especially when the usual channels of communication break down. But what`s the real- world harm inflicted by lies and conspiracy theories about the hurricane relief effort?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: Well, there`s a couple of direct impacts. The first is how the government works and functions. It needs the support of communities and populations.\par \par \uc2 If there`s distrust, rumors, all sorts of rampant lies being spread, in particular by the former President Trump, it makes the work of government more difficult. The second is sort of the driving of resources.\par \par \uc2 In normal disaster management,a lot of what the government finds out comes from local sources, a mayor, a local newspaper, a citizen saying, look, this is here, or we have a problem there. And they drive resources that way. If there`s a lot of noise in the system, not just sort of disinformation, someone gets something wrong, but actual misinformation, it actually impedes the ability to move resources.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I have talked to people in \uc2 FEMA\uc2 . The concern or the animus towards the government that`s being bred and spun up by so many right now is causing some of them to have to be deployed in pairs. So that just basically means that you`re wasting resources on safety and security issues that don`t normally exist in a crisis, when people generally do come together.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Donald Trump`s claims have focused on undermining confidence in the federal response and trying to tie that to his political opponent, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So here`s a sampling of some of the false things that he said in recent days.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Kamala spent all her \uc2 FEMA\uc2  money, billions of dollars, on housing for illegal migrants, many of whom should not be in our country.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They`re offering them $750 to people whose homes have been washed away.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And yet we send tens of billions of dollars to foreign countries that most people have never heard of.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So here`s the fact-check. There is zero basis for claiming that \uc2 FEMA\uc2  disaster money is being diverted to undocumented immigrants. And the $750 he mentioned is merely the immediate up-front aid that survivors can get to cover basic supplies in the days after disaster hits.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned that you know a lot of people at \uc2 FEMA\uc2 . There`s this other false notion, this conspiracy theory that the federal government is trying to swoop in and buy up land from people? Tell me more about that and how it`s resonating.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 So there`s there`s all of these rumors that basically undermine trust. And that is so essential in a crisis,trust not just in your government, but trust in your other citizens, that people will come together. And so all of these lies breed distrust amongst ethnic groups or racial groups or red states and blue states.\par \par \uc2 That`s been a rumor that -- or that`s been a lie that Trump has been pushing that resources aren`t going to red states. One very harmful lie that is being spread is that the government -- this is the opportunity for the government to take your house.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is actually not true. \uc2 FEMA\uc2  cannot take your house. They can pay you and urge you to be bought out, so that you don`t build your home in the same place. It`s a long process. It doesn`t happen overnight.\par \par \uc2 But you can imagine people worrying about, well, my home is going to be taken away. They believe these lies. And then what do they do? They don`t evacuate, as we have a new hurricane, a deadly hurricane coming to Florida right now. The idea that people are going to stay put because of a lie that the government is going to take their home if they leave it is going to cost lives.\par \par \uc2 And this is why it`s not just, oh, these are lies being spread around. They have direct impact on the life and health and safety of millions of people either impacted by Helene or all of these hurricanes that are coming through now.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And as a sign of the times, \uc2 FEMA\uc2  now has part of its Web site devoted to dispelling rumors. What do you make of the federal government`s efforts to combat misinformation and disinformation?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: I mean, it`s much better than it has been before. I think there used to be a belief, well, no one`s going to, no one`s going to actually believe this.\par \par \uc2 We now know that these lies, they fill the vacuum that has been created by platforms like X that sort of contribute to the misinformation, political leadership like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that then amplify it. And then remember a communications network in particular in Helene that`s essentially down.\par \par \uc2 It is hard for people to get online. It is hard for them to get those media resources that they used to have. So there`s just all sorts of rumors at this stage. So \uc2 FEMA\uc2  has taken a step. I commend Republican governors and mayors that are pushing back against a narrative that is being asserted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as part of his election campaign.\par \par \uc2 It`s obscene at this stage, but we`re just going to see more of it.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And lastly, Juliette, you write in your piece that emergency managers regularly urge people to stockpile 72 hours` worth of food and water, but Americans should also be planning their disaster media diet with similar care.\par \par \uc2 What does that look like, a disaster media diet?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: This is where, no offense to us, national media matters less, that you want to talk -- you want to follow local emergency managers, local media, radio, whether people who are in the community and are telling you what to do, and then, of course, to take -- to listen to what is being asked of you.\par \par \uc2 So, the evacuations are not done casually. The kinds of evacuations we`re seeing in Florida right now are serious. There`s a lot of weighting going into them. But this is where people before the disaster, much like that they would retain 72 hours of water or food, really think about, well, how am I going to get my resources and my information in real time? And you can build for that now, even before the disaster.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Juliette Kayyem, thanks so much for sharing your insights with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE KAYYEM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: With just under a month to go before Election Day, all eyes are on the key battleground states set to decide the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Our Politics Monday duo is following it all. That is Amy Walter of The Cook Political Report With Amy Walter and Tamara Keith of NPR, who joins us from one of those key states, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, Tam, you are in Raleigh, this evening. You know, J.D. Vance has said that it would be very hard for the Trump campaign to win this election if they don`t hold on to North Carolina. So, how are things looking there?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH, National Public Radio: Yes, he`s absolutely right about that. It is one of these key states.\par \par \uc2 It`s been very close at the presidential level, going back several cycles. Republicans here still feel very confident about this state. However, we know that the Trump campaign realizes that they could lose. That`s why Trump and Vance keep traveling here.\par \par \uc2 And what I have been learning about today as I have been here and I will be looking into more in particular, the state is working on figuring out how to respond to the hurricane, and the state election board -- elections board met today to talk about making sure that people can actually vote when early voting begins later this month.\par \par \uc2 And, certainly, the campaigns are also working on figuring out how to mobilize voters in areas that have been affected by the storm, where getting to a polling place just isn`t a top priority or the easiest thing right now.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Amy, this race is still exceedingly close with less than 30 days to go. What stands out for you in terms of polling and the early voting so far?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, The Cook Political Report: To me, what stands out is the fact that we have had a lot happen over the course of a few months.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Fact-check, true.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Very true.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: This is true.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: A lot of things. It seems every week we have one of these events that is really quite remarkable.\par \par \uc2 And yet the numbers just really don`t move that much. What you saw -- the most movement in this race was from the time that Biden drops out. Harris is on the ticket. She consolidates much of the Democratic base. And now we just haven`t seen much movement one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 It`s just like continuing and continually on this very marginal -- it`s the knife`s edge of polling. So what we`re looking at for every single one of these states, this is why, to Tam`s point in North Carolina, literally, every single one of those voters out in a place that`s been destroyed by a hurricane, making sure that they are able to cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are, what, I think 13 counties that have been impacted. It`s obviously not as many people as, say, a big urban area, but that`s still -- in a state that was decided by 70,000 voters, that`s important.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that really matters, and it`s mattered all along, but it`s even in sharper focus now, is the state of Pennsylvania. It`s the one place where both campaigns are basically spending equal amounts of money and time and energy.\par \par \uc2 It is -- both candidates have a path to win without Pennsylvania, but it is very difficult. And so when we think about where is the place where this election will be decided, you look at the kinds of voters in Pennsylvania that are the most important, and we will be talking about them, I`m sure, going into the next election, but in between suburban voters, the gender gap and these working-class voters, all seeming to move in different directions.\par \par \uc2 And at what pace they move is going to tell us who wins.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Let`s talk in more detail about the candidates, because Vice President Harris is spending the rest of the week hitting the airwaves with a series of major media interviews.\par \par \uc2 She`s going to sit down with Howard Stern, Stephen Colbert, the hosts of "The View." And she also sat for an interview with the very popular, I`m told, "Call Me Daddy" podcast -- I`m sorry -- "Call Her Daddy" podcast. We said we were going to get the name of this podcast wrong.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Oh, I know. We did.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The "Call Her Daddy" podcast.\par \par \uc2 And she was asked about Arkansas Governor \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 ` criticism that somehow \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  didn`t have anyone to keep her humble because she doesn`t have biological children. Here`s how she responded.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: I don`t think she understands that there are a whole lot of women out here who, one, are not aspiring to be humble. Family comes in many forms, and I think that increasingly all of us understand that this is not the 1950s anymore.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, this appearance was aimed at reaching younger women.\par \par \uc2 Tam, walk us through the campaign strategy of having Vice President Harris speak to different media figures and different media platforms.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: So that podcast is, I think, the number two \uc2 Spotify\uc2  podcast. It is a hugely popular podcast with young people, in particular young women.\par \par \uc2 And the struggle that the Harris campaign has had -- and there`s a corollary to the Trump campaign, but the struggle the Harris campaign has had is reaching voters who do not want to be reached. These are people who are actively avoiding politics.\par \par \uc2 Well, that is a podcast where people go to talk about sex and other things. And politics came to them in the form of this interview with Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the swathe of interviews that she is doing this week, she is hitting a lot of demographics. Men, younger men, listen to Howard Stern. "The View" is another audience. "60 Minutes" has the largest news audience out there. So, for a large part of the Harris campaign, she had a lot of earned media.\par \par \uc2 She was just getting -- she was getting a lot of attention just for being new, then for her convention, the debate. And now it`s a phase of the campaign where their campaign is now going out there and trying harder to get to voters, like, everywhere. And it -- especially also this Univision town hall is another area, another key demographic of voters.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Amy, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returned to Butler, Pennsylvania, the site of the first assassination attempt. And he and some members of his family suggested that Democrats are somehow behind these attempts on his life. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Over the past eight years, those who want to stop us from achieving this future have slandered me, impeached me, indicted me, tried to throw me off the ballot, and, who knows, maybe even tried to kill me.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), Vice Presidential Candidate: With all the hatred they have spewed at President Trump, it was only a matter of time before somebody tried to kill him.\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, Executive Vice President, \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 : And then, guys, they tried to kill him. They tried to kill him. And it`s because the Democratic Party, they can`t do anything right.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So claims without regard for evidence or propriety. I mean, what are your -- what are your takeaways?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 Geoff, remember when we were at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 ,and it was just days after that assassination attempt, and we had Republican official after Republican official say, this event has changed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , this is going to be a new \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ,this is going to be a more unifying, a more somber \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 And I think, as we saw in his final speech at the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , that is not -- that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  showed up for about half of it, and then the sort of regular \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  showed up. But this was also a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who was feeling incredibly confident. And the party was feeling very confident that they were going to win.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that returned to Butler is a candidate that feels like they are not winning, or at least it`s too close for comfort. And so going back into, how am I going to be able to get my team, my side fired up, where he traditionally goes back to is not a unifying message, not how we`re going to go together to bring the country back from a really traumatic place.\par \par \uc2 It`s -- it has to be us versus them. And I think we`re going to see this just continue for the next 30 days.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Tam, there`s one last topic I want to get to. It`s \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  putting a focus on Donald Trump`s age and mental cognition.\par \par \uc2 Our friend Peter Baker and his colleagues over there write that: "Donald Trump`s speeches have grown darker, harsher, longer, angrier, less focused, more profane, and increasingly fixated on the past."\par \par \uc2 There is this question of, where`s the outrage and the criticism that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saw when the question was focused on his age and mental capacity?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Certainly, that debate performance that former -- that President Biden had really opened the floodgates of conversations about his health, conversations that voters had been having already.\par \par \uc2 I think that former President Trump, his health, his age are fair game. He is now the oldest candidate in the race, and he has never released a fulsome health report. He has said he`s aced his cognitive exams, but he hasn`t ever released non-superlative medical reports.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Tamara Keith and Amy Walter, we covered a lot of ground today. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 The front lines in Ukraine`s east are long and deadly, but all along them, as Russian and Ukrainian forces hammer each other day in and day out, civilians are caught in between.\par \par \uc2 And as we now too often see in war, it`s the civilians who sometimes suffer the most, in this case, one of the most agonizing choices, to stay and face the unknown or flee.\par \par \uc2 Special correspondent Jack Hewson reports from Eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: The wind is blowing against Ukraine in the deserted streets of Myrnohrad, a name which means Peace Town. The familiar signs of Russia`s destructive advance are everywhere, ruins, rubble, and the distant sound of shellfire.\par \par \uc2 Russia is on the brink of taking this town two miles east of the vital strategic hub of Pokrovsk, but there is little left to occupy; 28,000 people have been evacuated from here in the last two weeks alone, and that`s because the Russians have been making gains towards here at 300 meters to a kilometer a day, the fastest gains they have made since the original invasion in 2022.\par \par \uc2 As so often in this war, it is civilians that suffer most. At the daily evacuation bus are some of the last to leave. Among them is Inna. She already escaped bombardment in Avdiivka, a city 25 miles southeast that fell in February.\par \par \uc2 INNA, Displaced From Avdiivka, Ukraine (through translator): After I survived all those horrible things, when I was woken up by the glass that was shattering and falling on me, I`m not ready to feel that fear again. When I heard the shots here, my body jerked each time uncontrollably. My nervous system can`t stand that. I`m scared. I just want to wake up and see the sunshine and hear birds twittering, you know? I want silence.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: JACK HEWSON: It`s scenes like these that Inna is fleeing. In the Pokrovsk area, shelling has terrorized civilians in recent weeks, prompting a government evacuation.\par \par \uc2 But some residents are determined to stay. We drive to the town`s southern limits to find them. Past these tank defenses, it`s open road until the front. Russians guns are just a few miles from here, and drones may be watching, so we seek cover in the back streets.\par \par \uc2 Searching for water and basic supplies, we find Valentina and Oleksandr.\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA, Myrnohrad, Ukraine, Resident (through translator): Oh, God, this shelling. I just don`t know what to say.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: We ask them, for the all the dangers and their obvious distress, why do they choose to stay?\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDR, Myrnohrad, Ukraine, Resident (through translator): Let it be what it will be. We don`t have anywhere to go.\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA (through translator): We`re old and we are afraid to lose our house.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDR (through translator): Also, money is needed to leave. To rent an apartment somewhere else is too expensive, and we don`t have money for that.\par \par \uc2 VALENTINA (through translator): It`s scary in here and we are very afraid.\par \par \uc2 OLEKSANDR (through translator): So, let it just be what it comes.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: But while some are unable to leave, others are not inclined. The people who wait, or zhduny, a term meaning Russian sympathizer. Many don`t want to show their faces. They have turned their backs on Kyiv. Fearing judgment, they refuse to talk.\par \par \uc2 But even for those that hate the Russians, bitterness and paranoia are growing in Donbass. And back at the evac bus, there is anger at how the area`s defenses have crumbled.\par \par \uc2 MAN (through translator): Why weren`t the Russians stopped? Why weren`t those bastards stopped in Avdiivka? People here are afraid and suffering.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: We try to interview this man further.\par \par \uc2 MAN (through translator): And then rumors spread that Donbass was given up to the Russians. but that`s not the point. The point is that it was necessary to defend it in advance.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: As we put a microphone on him, he is pulled away by his wife. Even in their anger, some fear being judged pro-Russian if they are critical of the war effort.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s surprise incursion into Kursk in August has exacerbated disquiet in Donbass. Images of Ukrainian soldiers occupying Russian towns and villages has provided the country a much-needed morale boost and challenged the narrative that Kyiv is losing.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, Ukrainian President (through translator): Commander in Chief Syrskyi has already reported several times on the front-line situation and on our actions to push the war out into the aggressor`s territory.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: But military analysts have questioned the wisdom of this move. While Ukraine has gained in Kursk, losses have accelerated across Donbass, notably in Vuhledar, Toretsk,Chasiv Yar, and here in the Pokrovsk area.\par \par \uc2 If Russia takes Pokrovsk, it cuts off the region`s most strategic supply route and threatens to split the eastern front in two. For now, the evacuation continues, but with Pokrovsk train station in artillery range, evacuees instead are bused 60 miles northwest.\par \par \uc2 We`re at Pavlohrad train station, where a large number of evacuees from nearby Pokrovsk and other areas under threat from the Russians have come to get out of here. There`s a general sense obviously of resentment against the invading Russian forces, but some are also angry with how the handling of the defense has gone in recent months.\par \par \uc2 As troops have been pushed to Kursk, they wonder, has it come at the expense of defending places like Pokrovsk?\par \par \uc2 We asked Mariia, who recently fled the town of Ukrainsk 75 miles southeast of here, if she supported the Kursk offensive.\par \par \uc2 MARIIA, Displaced From Mariia, Ukraine (through translator): I have some thoughts about that, but I don`t want to share and say them out loud. But they shouldn`t have done that.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: Outmanned and outgunned, there are only hard choices for Ukraine`s leadership. But, for Mariia, losing her home has been harder.\par \par \uc2 MARIIA (through translator): I never cried so hard. Even at my husband`s funeral, I wasn`t crying as hard as I was crying while leaving my city. Pardon my tears.\par \par \uc2 JACK HEWSON: Mariia and many others journey into an uncertain future. Waving goodbye to homes they may never see again.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Jack Hewson in Donbass, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: From self-driving cars to electric vehicles, automobiles are becoming more technologically advanced, and there`s a shortage of workers who have the diverse skills needed to keep them on the road.\par \par \uc2 Ali Rogin visited one college focused on training the next generation of the automotive industry, including many people who may have once thought there wasn`t a place for them there.\par \par \uc2 It`s part of our series, Rethinking College.\par \par \uc2 CAID KROEGER, Instructor, Department of Automotive Technology, \uc2 Weber State University\uc2 : Hey, guys, for this assignment, we`re going to be tearing down the engines, much like you guys are.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: At \uc2 Weber State University\uc2  in Layton, Utah, it`s no coincidence that the automotive program shares a building with computer science.\par \par \uc2 CAID KROEGER: We have autonomous vehicles now. We have adaptive cruise control. We have a full-on hybrid and full-on electric vehicles now. So, the old days of just being able to pick up a wrench and work on your car are falling farther and farther behind.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Weber State instructor Caid Kroeger trains his students for the cars and the jobs of the future.\par \par \uc2 CAID KROEGER: You are no longer just your average mechanic that`s diagnosing base engine concerns. You are I.T., you are an engineer, you are a tech expert at this point.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Weber has been keeping students on the cutting edge of the auto industry since 1922, not long after Henry Ford launched the Model T.\par \par \uc2 The university works with companies like \uc2 Toyota\uc2 , GM, and Chrysler to make sure students are learning the most in-demand skills. Many students already work in the field and can translate their professional certifications into credits towards an associates degree. They can stop there, or keep going, or come back later for their bachelor of science in automotive technology.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN RAGUE, Associate Dean of College of Engineering, Applied Science, and Technology at \uc2 Weber State University\uc2 : Fundamental knowledge about their particular field is important to us and we build that up. But we build it up in a kind of a gradated or stackable way, so that students can earn degrees and credentials as they move along.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Brian Rague is the associate dean of the college of engineering, applied science and technology.\par \par \uc2 It seems like this hybrid approach to higher education is really well- geared to this national moment that we`re in.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN RAGUE: It requires of the university to be attuned to what the needs are out there. And you get that information from the industry partners, and then you make the curriculum kind of fit those needs. And that is not an easy thing to do at a university.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: One of the biggest needs is the work force. According to one study, the U.S. auto industry will need more than 300,000 new auto techs over the next four years to keep up with demand. Many of those jobs will need to be more technical than mechanical, making it an opportune time for the horsepower industry to rethink manpower.\par \par \uc2 Women make up about half the American work force, but only about a quarter of the automotive industry. Experts say that`s a missed opportunity.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SLATER, Department Chair, Department of Automotive Technology, \uc2 Weber State University\uc2 : I think there are a lot of fantastic women and there are a lot of brilliant women that are in other industries that we are missing.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Jessica Slater is the department chair of Weber`s automotive program.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SLATER: Where would the automotive industry be now if those women had been a part of it? If maybe they had entertained a STEM or an engineering field, where would we be now? It`s a curious thought. I think we would be somewhere else entirely.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Today, women are a little over 10 percent of the undergrad auto program at Weber, and the college hopes to boost those numbers in part by offering college-level courses to students at 16 local high schools.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SLATER: We want to get in front of these young women, again, before any preconceptions are developed on this is what I`m capable of doing because I`m a woman. We want to get to them when they`re 12 years old and they think they can do whatever a boy can do, because they can.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: It can be easy for young girls to lose that attitude. Luckily, 20-year-old Delanie Long held on to it.\par \par \uc2 DELANIE LONG, Student: Since I was young, my dad always worked on cars and stuff like that. And he would actually try and show my brother everything and get him into it. So, I just tagged along and I enjoyed it more than my brother.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: You`re a woman going into potentially a very right now male- dominated industry. How do you feel about that?\par \par \uc2 DELANIE LONG: At first, it was really intimidating, to be honest. But I do feel welcome here. It`s fun to show, like, the boys what`s up kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Not just the boys. Her father too.\par \par \uc2 DELANIE LONG: It`s also fun, like teaching him some things I have learned here that he didn`t know. He`s shocked, but proud of me, for sure.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: But attitudes take longer to change than tires. A 2020 survey of the auto industry found that 91 percent of female respondents believed there was a bias towards men for leadership positions. Only 47 percent of men felt that way.\par \par \uc2 But Weber alum Beth Miya, who now works for the \uc2 Cummins\uc2  engine and generator company, says the industry has improved since she started out over two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 BETH MIYA, Weber State University Graduate: I couldn`t get a job turning wrenches when I first started out in the automotive industry, even though I had more experience than some of my male counterparts. It`s obviously a very, very different world. I mean, I graduated from Weber State in like `04. I think that just the assumptions that females shouldn`t be there is gone.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: But Miya says she still deals with sexism in the industry.\par \par \uc2 BETH MIYA: Everywhere I go, they usually assume I am the marketing girl or the secretary that`s helping. And then I get up and I`m presenting the technical information. I always feel that I have to prove myself, when my male counterparts can walk up and they just listen. I have to kind of show them why I`m there.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: So, Miya is paying it forward, speaking when she can to younger students who are curious about the automotive field.\par \par \uc2 BETH MIYA: It would have been nice for me to see a female instructor. You know, that would have been encouraging for sure. As cheesy as it sounds, anywhere I can actually appear and be a female is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Caid Kroeger says the industry would benefit from more women joining.\par \par \uc2 CAID KROEGER: I have found that our female students, the women in the industry, approach diagnostic in a very unique way and they end up being great at solving problems.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Plus, these days, the auto industry isn`t just looking for gearheads.\par \par \uc2 CAID KROEGER: Much like the days of old, we still get the students that love automotive for the horsepower, the loud noises, the -- like, what people, what some students end up coming to the automotive industry in the first place.\par \par \uc2 But the other side of that, though, too, is now I have a lot of students that are interested in I.T. that are still interested in cars or interested in battery design.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: Jessica Slater says there`s more opportunity in automotive than outsiders realize.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA SLATER: There`s a misconception, if you go into automotive, you`re just going to be a grease monkey, that`s all you`re good for. And that is entirely inaccurate. There are so many channels and avenues for this industry that people just don`t know about.\par \par \uc2 ALI ROGIN: That`s why Weber State is working to change stereotypes about the industry and the people who work in it.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Ali Rogin in Layton, Utah.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And join us again here tomorrow night for the latest, as Hurricane Milton charts a path towards Florida`s already storm-battered Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 For all of us here at the "PBS News Hour," thanks for spending part of your evening with us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 CQ-Roll Call, Inc., which  takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the  user`s personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be  printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any  fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and CQ-Roll Call,  Inc.`s copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material.  This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020241008eka700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS NewsHour for\b0\par\par\uc2 Lisa Desjardins, Judy Woodruff, Nick Schifrin, Geoff Bennett, Amna  Nawaz \par \uc2 10,103 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC, which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the user's personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and ASC Services II Media, LLC's copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: Israel and \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  move closer to an all-out war after Israeli airstrikes kill hundreds in Lebanon.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ramp up efforts to win over voters in critical swing states as the election draws closer.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And late show host Stephen Colbert and his wife, Evie, on their new cookbook, the family stories behind the food and how to laugh in tough political times.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, Host, "The Late Show With Stephen Colbert": Toxicity itself is worth making fun of. Like, that`s just -- the fact of how bad things are is something you have to make fun of.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 It is the deadliest day in the Middle East since the October 7 terror attacks. Lebanese authorities say Israeli airstrikes killed nearly 500 people and wounded more than 1,600.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Israel said it struck 1,100 targets where Iran-backed \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  hid weapons in Lebanon, including thousands of rockets and missiles in a stash often fired at Israel.\par \par \uc2 The tensions have led the U.S. Defense Department to send additional troops to the region.\par \par \uc2 Nick Schifrin starts our coverage.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: In towns across southern and Eastern Lebanon today, at more than 1,000 sites, an avalanche of airstrikes. Lebanese across the region said today felt like the beginning of war. Thousands of families fled, cramming everyone they could fit into every vehicle they could find, even those shattered by shrapnel.\par \par \uc2 AHMED, Southern Lebanon Resident (through translator): Strikes, warplanes, destruction. No one is left there. Everyone has fled. We took our belongings and left.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Since October the 8th, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has fired thousands of rockets into Israel, including yesterday outside Israel`s third largest city. And Israel says its goal is to return some 60,000 residents to now empty Northern Israeli towns.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , Israeli Prime Minister (through translator): I promised that we would change the security balance, the balance of power in the north. That is exactly what we are doing. We`re destroying thousands of missiles and rockets directed at Israeli cities and Israeli citizens.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: And Israel expanded its targets today, warning residents to leave any homes with weapons in the Beqaa Valley, where \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  was founded decades ago near the border with Syria.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. DANIEL HAGARI, Spokesperson, \uc2 Israeli Defense Forces\uc2 : We advise civilians from Lebanese villages located in and next to buildings and areas used by \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  for military purpose, such as those used to store weapons, to immediately move out of harm`s way for their own safety.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Israel said the targets were \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 , but Lebanese officials say Israel also struck hospitals, medical centers and other civilian infrastructure. It is a dangerous, violent escalation one day after \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  leaders promised their own escalation.\par \par \uc2 NAIM QASSEM, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  Deputy Secretary-General (through translator): We don`t need to make threats, and we won`t specify how we will respond to aggression. We have entered a new phase titled open-ended battle of reckoning.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But over the last week, it`s been \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  that`s faced unprecedented attacks, their walkie-talkies and pagers turned into bombs, injuring thousands and killing nearly 40. And on Friday, an Israeli airstrike in Southern Beirut killed the founder of \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s special forces unit, but also dozens of civilians, who have faced the brunt of the violence, the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  said today.\par \par \uc2 IMRAN RIZA, \uc2 U.N.\uc2  Deputy Special Coordinator For Lebanon: What we have seen over the last week has been dramatic, has been dramatic. The consequences on civilians has been huge.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Today in Washington, for at least the fifth time in the last week, President Biden urged restraint.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , President of the United States: My team is in constant contact with their counterparts, and we`re working to de-escalate in a way that allows people to return to their homes safely.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But if Israel`s hope is that \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  chooses diplomacy, analysts say this week`s attacks will produce the opposite effect, and is already leading to the very escalation from both sides the U.S. has been trying to prevent.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Nick Schifrin.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: For perspective on the spiraling violence between Israel and Lebanon, we get two views.\par \par \uc2 Aaron David Miller is a senior fellow at the \uc2 Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\uc2  and a longtime State Department official in both Democratic and Republican administrations. And Hussein Ibish is a senior resident scholar at the Arab Gulf States Institute in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Hussein, I will start with you.\par \par \uc2 Lebanon`s Health Ministry says the strikes have killed 492 people, including 35 children and 58 women. What`s your assessment of how Israel is conducting this military operation?\par \par \uc2 HUSSEIN IBISH, Senior Resident Scholar, Arab Gulf States Institute: Well, it`s obviously aimed at not just \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  and its weapons depots, but anything that Israel thinks might be of use to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . It`s very similar to the doctrine applied in Gaza, where dual-use, triple-use facilities are all targeted, and so are anything that could kind of maybe be on that list.\par \par \uc2 There is a real deference towards authorization rather than non- authorization of strikes and a willingness to do tremendous harm. Part of it is psychological warfare. Part of it is generalized vengeance. Israel`s approach to these things is wars between societies, and they want to make sure everybody gets the point and everybody suffers.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Aaron David Miller, how does this move Israel closer to its goal of facilitating the return of some 60,000 people, civilians to their homes in the north?\par \par \uc2 AARON DAVID MILLER, \uc2 Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\uc2 : You know, it`s great to be with you. And it`s always great to be with Hussein.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure about the war against societies. So I think the Israelis are faced with a legitimate problem, and that is, October 8, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  decided, for their own reasons, perhaps encouraged by Iran, to become part of the Axis of Resistance. And in doing so, they struck Israel.\par \par \uc2 We have been living on borrowed time ever since. And \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  understands that it`s -- like \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , it`s placed the entire population of Lebanon in the dock for its actions.\par \par \uc2 As far as Israel`s strategy, I mean, I do raise a concern. I`m not sure the Israelis believe for a minute, frankly, that all this military pressure is going to get to somehow roll over, let alone create a pathway that the Biden administration would very eagerly take advantage of to de-escalate under the able and very talented management of \uc2 Amos Hochstein\uc2 , who negotiated a maritime border with -- two years ago, with -- under -- with \uc2 Hamas\uc2 ` and presumably with Iran`s acquiescence.\par \par \uc2 I think the Israelis, frankly -- I don`t think they want a major escalation, which is going to expose their population centers and infrastructure to an enormous amount of destruction. But they are taking a major whack at \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think they`re prepared to continue that, perhaps even without a sustainable objective.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Do you expect Israel to launch a ground operation in Southern Lebanon?\par \par \uc2 HUSSEIN IBISH: Not yet.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I do think that, yes, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is responsible, for sure, for dragging yet again Lebanon and its people into a conflict which is not connected to Lebanese interests. But that`s changed now with the pager explosion, et cetera. I think there were all over 4,000 bombs randomly put all over Lebanon, killed a lot of people, including children.\par \par \uc2 I think the perspective of a lot of Lebanese has shifted to be more synthetic to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 . I think that a land invasion is not yet likely. But Israel may come to find that it cannot create the illusion of security, because that`s what they`re chasing.\par \par \uc2 And none of this, not the bombing, not some kind of a security barrier they might try to create by seizing and creating a new occupation in a swathe of Southern Lebanon, none of it would provide security for Northern Israel.\par \par \uc2 In fact, it would mire Israel in yet another open-ended occupation and conflict counterinsurgency in the north, as well as in the south. But I do think that it could create, for a short period of time, the illusion of security. And I think it`s possible that the Israelis may turn to that, if they cannot get the illusion of security through this bombing, which is undoubtedly a form of coercive diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, they`re trying to force \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  into some kind of formal back- down, while degrading their rockets and rocket launchers. But none of that translates into greater security for Northern Israel. At most, it buys you time.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Aaron David Miller, what about Iran? You say neither Israel nor Iran wants a full-blown direct conflict. But how might this escalation by Israel into Lebanon, how might this prompt Iran to be more provocative in response?\par \par \uc2 AARON DAVID MILLER: Well, if in fact you actually got a major escalation, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has not yet crossed the key red line, which is the use of their precision-guided missiles again, which they have hundreds, not thousands, but, nonetheless, they have sufficient quantity in order to do tremendous damage to Israeli population centers, infrastructure, the electricity grid.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Israel gets a lot of its water from desal plants. Those would go out. This could become a major nightmare. And, remember, \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  has the capacity now to launch 3,000 missiles a day. In summer of 2006, in the 34- day war, they launched 3,000 during the entire campaign.\par \par \uc2 Look, the Iranians, I think, want to cause the Israelis as much difficulty as they can. They`d love to see an Israeli occupation of Southern Lebanon. They`d love to see the Israelis in the West Bank and in Gaza in perpetuity.\par \par \uc2 But what they don`t want to see is an Israeli and/or American strike against Iran proper. And you could end up in -- with that eventuality if, in fact, you have got a major Israeli-\uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  escalation, and Iran felt the need to come to \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2 `s defense. Israelis and perhaps even the Americans could get involved.\par \par \uc2 And you could see something the Middle East has never experienced before, which is a multifront war, Iraq, Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Iran. The Houthis (AUDIO GAP) got really bad and Iranians striking oil targets in the Gulf, which, as we know, they have done in 2019.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 What does de-escalation look like at this point, and what more can the U.S. do, if anything?\par \par \uc2 HUSSEIN IBISH: I think the U.S. could do a lot to restrain Israel, because Israel has achieved early on in this conflict that began right after October 7 escalation dominance.\par \par \uc2 They made it clear very early on, and especially during this year, that they don`t mind a broader war that Aaron has described, that of all the parties involved, other than \uc2 Hamas\uc2 , which has no ability to influence things really, that Israel is the one that is most interested in a potential war, that they wouldn`t mind getting the United States to actually bomb Iranian nuclear facilities through the process Aaron just described.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of people in Israel who are enthusiastic about that, and a bigger number in the Israeli government who are ambivalent about it. They`re concerned about it, but, at the same time, they can see upsides, which is why they have been so reckless and so escalatory in recent months.\par \par \uc2 De-escalation looks like \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  backing off with rocket attacks and making maybe some conciliatory noises about decoupling their conflict with Israel from the war in Gaza, which is the one thing they have been harping on, is that they won`t change any of their behavior until there`s a cease- fire in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t actually make any sense from their point of view, except sort of in a very vague, ideological way. And it also certainly means the United States restraining Israel. I think both Tehran and Washington, who would say, and I think with good reason, they don`t want a broader war, have a big responsibility to restrain their allies.\par \par \uc2 And, in particular, I think Washington really does need to say to the Israelis in a more open way, if you force a war here, because you are the party driving it this time -- it wasn`t true in 2006, but now it is -- we - - you`re going to be on your own. We -- don`t rely on us at the \uc2 U.N.\uc2  for more munitions or anything.\par \par \uc2 Our policy has been that the war should not spread. And if it spreads because you insist it must spread for whatever reason, then we`re not responsible.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Aaron, in the 30 seconds we have left, is there anything that President Biden could say to \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  at the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  this week that might make a difference?\par \par \uc2 AARON DAVID MILLER: Yes, I will make this brief.\par \par \uc2 In 11 months, the administration, for any number of reasons, some legitimate, some perhaps not, has been reluctant and/or refused to impose a single cost or consequence that normal humans, like the three of us, would regard as sustained and serious pressure.\par \par \uc2 Fifty -- are we 50 days out from November 5? There is absolutely no way, in my judgment, given the consequential nature of this election, that this administration, with Iran perceived to be pulling the strings, even though it may want de-escalation -- \uc2 Hezbollah\uc2  is an Iranian proxy -- is going to pressure the Israelis in the 50-plus days that remain before this very consequential election.\par \par \uc2 HUSSEIN IBISH: And the Israelis know this and are cynically using that situation, rather, to give themselves a freer hand.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Hussein Ibish and Aaron David Miller, we thank you both for your insights and for your time this evening.\par \par \uc2 HUSSEIN IBISH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AARON DAVID MILLER: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We start the day`s other headlines with the latest on the apparent assassination attempt of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at his golf course earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors said today that the suspect, Ryan Routh, had engaged in a premeditated plot to kill the former president. They say the 58-year-old left behind a prewritten note that he addressed to the world, appearing to say he may not succeed in killing Trump and offering a reward to anyone willing to -- quote -- "complete the job."\par \par \uc2 Routh appeared at a West Palm Beach courthouse today. He will remain in jail awaiting trial on two gun-related charges. Prosecutors will seek a more serious charge of attempted assassination of a major political candidate.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration is proposing a ban on Chinese software and hardware in autonomous vehicles operating on U.S. roads. The Commerce Department cites national security concerns for the move and says Russian technology would also be prohibited. Under the proposal, the software would be banned from vehicles starting with model year 2027 and hardware in 2030.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after a similar ban on communications equipment from \uc2 Huawei\uc2  in 2022 and amid a push by U.S. lawmakers to ban TikTok if its Chinese owners don`t sell their state.\par \par \uc2 Officials in Alabama are urging members of the public to come forward with any information related to a weekend mass shooting in Birmingham. The search is still on for the gunmen who opened fire outside a nightclub on Saturday night, killing four people and injuring 17 others. Police have described it as a targeted hit, but say that innocent bystanders were among those shot.\par \par \uc2 Today, authorities said they`re offering rewards totaling $100,000 for credible information that helps lead to an arrest or conviction.\par \par \uc2 RANDALL WOODFIN\uc2  (D), Mayor of Birmingham, Alabama: We cannot give safe harbor or shelter to people who want to just simply kill people. We can`t give them cover. They should not feel safe in our community. They should not feel safe anywhere.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Saturday`s shooting was the city`s third quadruple homicide of the year. In fact, according to an Associated Press database that tracks shootings, three of the nation`s 31 mass killings this year have taken place in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 Murder and other violent crime dropped across the nation last year. That`s according to an annual report from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Overall, violent crime fell by an estimated 3 percent compared to 2022. Murders and manslaughter dropped nearly 12 percent, and reports of rape fell more than 9 percent. However, reports of hate crimes rose last year by 2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Public safety has been a central issue in this year`s presidential race, with the Republican ticket claiming, despite evidence to the contrary, that crime is out of control under the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Two Russians and one American returned from the International Space Station today, ending a record-breaking stay for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Touchdown.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Their capsule landed in a cloud of dust in Kazakstan about 3.5 hours after it undocked from the station. Oleg Kononenko and Nikolai Chub were aboard the ISS for 374 days, a new record for the longest continuous stay.\par \par \uc2 American astronaut Tracy Dyson was there for six months. Eight astronauts are still on board, including Butch Wilmore and Suni Williams. Those Americans won`t return until next year following tech problems with Boeing Starliner.\par \par \uc2 Two new reports on the state of book banning in America paint a mixed picture of the practice as the annual Banned Books Week begins nationwide. The \uc2 American Library Association\uc2  reported 414 complaints in the first eight months of 2024. That`s compared to 695 challenges over the same period last year. The meantime, data from PEN America shows the number of books actually being pulled from shelves tripled over the previous year to more than 10,000.\par \par \uc2 Experts say the two surveys don`t necessarily contradict each other, and taken together they point to an ongoing surge in book bans in recent years.\par \par \uc2 And on Wall Street, small gains across the board helped build on recent record highs. The Dow Jones industrial average added around 60 points, well above that 42000-point level. The Nasdaq added about 25 points on the day, and the S&P 500 tacked on 16 points to close at a new record.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": our Politics Monday team breaks down the latest from the campaign trail; California sues \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  over allegedly deceiving the public about recycling plastics; and Judy Woodruff discusses the election with voters from across the political spectrum in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Congress is returning to town with the election hovering, and funding for most of the government is set to run out a week from today.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Congressional leaders announced another short-term proposal to punt more partisan fights over spending until after the election.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Desjardins is tracking the movements on Capitol Hill and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Lisa, so here we go again.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: What`s in this deal exactly?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: OK, let`s talk about this. This came together over the weekend from the Republican and Democratic leaders of Congress.\par \par \uc2 First thing people should know, this would extend government funding to a new date, December 20. Of course, that is after the election, but before a new president would be sworn in. This includes $231 million for Secret Service, and it explicitly says that can be spent now on the current campaign season, obviously, concern from both parties, protection, including former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 What is not in here, there will not be any increased funding for Veterans Affairs. That is something that Democrats say they particularly tried to get in this bill. The VA is constrained, constrained right now for resources, but Republicans, they say, would not agree to it.\par \par \uc2 As you said, this is standard procedure, but coming now in such a tense election year, both parties` leadership realized they need to get this through. Republican leaders don`t want to shut down and Democrats want to focus on the election.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So what happens now? What are you watching for next?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: OK, so the first thing is, we`re waiting to see how this happens. We do expect this to pass both chambers. It`s a question of, how dramatic is it?\par \par \uc2 The House is likely to move on this tomorrow or Wednesday. Now, we are watching in the Senate some senators, conservatives like \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  of Utah, who often brings up his objections to spending and other things because he knows he can try and run out the Senate clock, get some attention for it.\par \par \uc2 This would -- could come close to the wire to next Monday`s deadline, but all expect in the end it will likely pass. The other person to watch very closely is former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He said he would oppose. He told Speaker Johnson to oppose a deal like this without attached legislation he wanted.\par \par \uc2 Is he going to fight again against this deal? Is he going to push for a shutdown? And, more importantly, is he going to raise problems for Speaker Johnson? We know recent Republican speakers of the House do not have an easy job, and their job security also is not the greatest.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: That is indeed true.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Desjardins, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And turning now to the presidential race, with just over 40 days until Election Day, both Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are ramping up their campaigning in swing states during the final stretch of the race.\par \par \uc2 A week after a second attempt on his life, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  spent the afternoon in Western Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Nobody`s done for farmers what I have done.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Speaking to voters about the potential threat from China to U.S. agriculture.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m going to call up President Xi. I`m going to say, you have to honor the deal you made. We made a deal you would buy $50 billion worth of American farm product, and I guarantee you he will buy it, 100 percent. He will buy it.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: As Trump and Vice President Harris barnstorm battlegrounds just six weeks before Election Day, new polling from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and Siena College shows the former president taking the lead in three of those crucial states, Arizona, Georgia and North Carolina, with Trump`s five- point lead in Arizona outside the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 While the polls may show a Trump advantage in the Sun Belt, Harris is outpacing her Republican rival when it comes to money in the bank. The vice president received a $27 million cash injection from a Wall Street fund- raising event on Sunday, adding to the $361 million the campaign raised last month.\par \par \uc2 That same day, she also racked up 700 endorsements in a signed letter from both Republican and Democratic national security leaders, who called the choice between Harris and Trump a choice between -- quote -- "democracy and authoritarianism."\par \par \uc2 On official vice presidential business today, Harris will pick up campaigning in key swing states throughout the week with a focus on the economy.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: I have named it an opportunity economy, which really, in short form, it`s about what we can do more to invest in the aspirations, the ambitions and the dreams of the American people, while addressing the challenges that they face.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The vice president also accepted an invitation from CNN for a second debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who swiftly declined the invitation at a weekend rally.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It`s just too late. Voting has already started.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And in an interview on a conservative Sunday show, Trump was candid about his plans not to run again in 2028.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: If you`re not successful this time, do you see yourself running again in four years?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : No, I don`t. No, I don`t. I think that will be -- that will be it. I don`t see that at all.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: His running mate, Senator J.D. Vance, meanwhile, campaigned in North Carolina today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. J.D. VANCE (R-OH), Vice Presidential Candidate: Thanks to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` policies, you have got a lot of American families and a lot of American cities that can`t afford to provide basic necessities to their citizens.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: But all eyes are on a different Republican candidate in the Tar Heel State, gubernatorial nominee Mark Robinson. On Sunday, several of Robinson`s top campaign staffers quit after a CNN report uncovered numerous inflammatory and derogatory comments that the lieutenant governor allegedly made on a porn Web site.\par \par \uc2 The Harris campaign quickly turned out campaign ads tying Robinson directly to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I have gotten to know him and he`s outstanding.\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Mark Robinson, they`re both wrong for North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: With early voting already under way in some states, the race remains a tight one, leaving a grueling sprint ahead for the candidates in the weeks until November.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And new developments in two key states could affect the presidential candidates` potential paths to victory, as each tries to clinch the electoral votes needed to win.\par \par \uc2 To dive into it all on this Politics Monday, we have Tamara Keith of \uc2 NPR\uc2  and Jasmine Wright of NOTUS. That`s a new publication from the nonprofit nonpartisan Allbritton Journalism Institute. Amy Walter is away.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH, National Public Radio: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT, NOTUS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So we are heading into the final six-week sprint to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Tam, you have been keeping tabs on Donald Trump`s travel schedule. What does that tell you about the campaign strategy six weeks out?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Well, what I can tell you is that he is in Pennsylvania. He was just in North Carolina. He`s going back to North Carolina again, and he`s also going to Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So his strategy is this East Coast strategy. If he can win in Pennsylvania and Georgia and North Carolina, but especially if he can win in Pennsylvania, then Harris has a very difficult path to the 270 electoral votes she needs.\par \par \uc2 And so Trump is focusing on these states. The fact that he is spending a lot of time in North Carolina says that he`s worried about it, because, if he wasn`t worried about it, he wouldn`t be spending time there. But he is. In fact, I think he`s spending more time there than Harris has spent since she became the nominee.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: I want to talk a bit more about North Carolina in a second.\par \par \uc2 But, Jasmine, over the weekend, Vice President Harris said she accepted a debate proposal from CNN for a debate late in October. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has ruled it out. He can always change his mind. We know that.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: Right.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: But what`s the strategy? Why does the vice president feel like she needs another debate?\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: Look, well, I think that the vice president had a massively successful debate earlier this month. We saw her not just only quell the competency questions that she was absolutely getting from both parties or whether or not she was actually ready to be president, but also they made a lot of money in that 24 hours after the debate, $47 million.\par \par \uc2 So they want to recreate those moments. And, of course, it`s a little bit of goading Trump, something that they have seen just in the debate and since the debate that has been successful, trying to poke him, really kind of playing this game of, are you or are you not a chicken?\par \par \uc2 And so I think we`re going to continue to see her up until really it`s basically too late and those dates expire, trying to get Trump again on that debate space, because there is no -- or at least they believe that there is no downside into them doing this.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Well, and one thing I will note is that the vice presidential debate is scheduled for October 1. In the last president -- the first presidential debate between Biden -- or between Trump and Harris, Trump was like, J.D. Vance, he doesn`t speak for me.\par \par \uc2 Well, so is he going to let J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  have the last word? I guess we will find out. But part of what the Harris campaign is betting is that, this -- Trump may say it`s a closed question.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: And I think every time that J.D. Vance has gotten the last word, the former president wasn`t happy about it.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: So I think that we will see you after October 1.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it appears, is trying to shift and soften his approach toward women. Speaking in North Carolina over the weekend, he said: "I will protect women at a level never seen before. They will finally be healthy, hopeful, safe, and secure."\par \par \uc2 And this comes as NBC has a new pull out that shows Harris leading Trump by 58 percent to 37 percent among women. That is quite a gap.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: This is a historically large gender gap. And Trump is doing better with men than Harris is doing with men. That was what makes the gap so gaping.\par \par \uc2 Trump, in those remarks, also said, and I just want to read this: "Women will no longer be thinking about abortion because it is now where it has always had to be, with the states."\par \par \uc2 So he is essentially saying that you`re not going to have to worry about abortion. Your life is going to be great if I am elected president again. And what he`s saying is that it`s with the states, there`s going to be these ballot measures, it`ll be figured out.\par \par \uc2 I have to say, though, women are not going to stop thinking about it. In fact, ever since Roe was overturned with Supreme Court justices that Trump proudly appointed, ever since Roe was overturned, abortion has become a campaign issue like it wasn`t before.\par \par \uc2 Before, it was a motivator for Christian conservatives, but it was not a broad-based issue that Americans thought was front of mind in the last couple of decades. Now it is a front-of-mind campaign issue. There are going to be these ballot measures.\par \par \uc2 And I was traveling with Vice President Harris on Friday, when she went to Georgia with an -- a late added rally in the Atlanta area. Specifically, the entire speech was about reproductive health care and what she calls Trump abortion bans in nearly two dozen states. He was responding to that. That -- it was a cause and effect.\par \par \uc2 She did that speech and then he put out this post on social media and then literally read it at his rally.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: Because he knows he`s vulnerable on the issue of abortion ever since Roe v. Wade was struck down. This is something that he has actually been probably the most aggressive in his party in trying to figure out how to position himself on abortion.\par \par \uc2 And we have seen that he just can`t figure out where he wants to be. And so I think you`re going to continue to hear these kind of slightly unserious proclamations that women will never have to worry about abortion again if he`s president because he`s trying to figure out the right language in real time to tell women that he can be trusted on this issue, which is absolutely opposite of the message that the vice president is offering.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Well, we got some news today out of Nebraska. And this relates to the Republican effort to change how Nebraska awards its Electoral College votes.\par \par \uc2 This was an effort by Republicans to boost former President Trump. And it hit a roadblock in the form of a state legislator named Mike McDonnell. He put out a statement, part of which I will read.\par \par \uc2 He says: "In recent weeks, a conversation around whether to change how we allocate our Electoral College votes has returned to the forefront." He also says: "After deep consideration, it is clear to me that, right now, 43 days from Election Day, is not the moment to make this change."\par \par \uc2 Tam, how big a deal is this for both campaigns?\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: This is -- I mean, in some ways, this returns it to where it was, which is that this was kind of going nowhere. They have been trying to do this. The Republican legislature has tried a few times to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 What is interesting is that, in the past week, former President Trump, his close ally \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  have been talking to these legislators, trying to put pressure on them, trying to make it happen one more time. This is because Trump allies readily admit that he is not winning in this one congressional district.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Harris and her campaign have spent a lot of time and money and sent -- Doug Emhoff is like the second mayor of Omaha.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: Living there. He`s got an apartment.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: Yes, he`s got an apartment. Yes.\par \par \uc2 They have spent a lot of time and effort on this one Electoral College vote, because if it comes down to just the blue wall states, then she would also need this blue dot, the Second Congressional District in Nebraska, in order to get to 270. And, obviously, Trump and his allies do not want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: I mean, there`s no doubt this is a major win for the Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this guy can change his mind, that we may go through two more rotations of this before we get to November. But, certainly, I have been talking to Harris aides. I was with them in North Carolina two weeks ago, where I asked an aide, really, what`s their path? And they say that they are most comfortable, they feel most bullish about the blue wall path plus Omaha.\par \par \uc2 And that is where that congressional district is. And so they want to maintain that. And so him basically ruling out switching it, saying that we`re too close to November to do this, this is a win for them, even though it still maintains what is the status quo.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Jasmine Wright of NOTUS, we should say, a former "News Hour" producer.\par \par \uc2 JASMINE WRIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Great to have you back. And, Tamara Keith, of course, of \uc2 NPR\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: A new lawsuit filed today accuses \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  of contributing to the world`s plastic pollution. The oil and gas company is one of the largest producers of the material used to make single-use plastics.\par \par \uc2 California`s Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  filed that lawsuit after a two-year investigation, and I spoke with him moments ago.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Bonta, welcome back to the "News Hour." Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA (D), California Attorney General: Thanks for having me, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So, in this lawsuit, you`re accusing \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  of what you call decades-long deception, and essentially lying to the public about the recyclability of plastics.\par \par \uc2 What specifically have they been saying over the last 50 years that you`re alleging just isn`t true?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: Well, since the 70s, for decades, they have been telling the world, Americans, Californians, that plastics are recyclable and are being recycled, and that consumers can participate in a single-use throwaway culture, and it`s OK because all those plastics that you throw away will be reclaimed, recycled, will show up in someone else`s household as a useful plastic product, utensil or a container or something else.\par \par \uc2 And that is absolutely false. And in many occasions, most occasions, the item is completely not recyclable and never going to be recycled.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: An \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  spokesperson did push back on parts of your lawsuit, saying in a statement to Reuters in part that -- quote -- "Suing people makes headlines, but it doesn`t solve the plastic waste problem. Advanced recycling is a real solution."\par \par \uc2 They`re referencing their program that they actually had to convert plastic waste into petrochemical feedstock. What`s your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: First, litigation can certainly solve problems. It can stop \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  from lying. They have been lying for decades. They`re still lying today. Advanced recycling is part of that lying.\par \par \uc2 Here`s why; 92 percent of what is produced by advanced recycling is not any other plastic product. It is mostly transportation fuel and other items. So they promise this circular recycling experience for their plastics through advanced recycling. They can take any plastic, they say, and it`ll come back into your household as a useful plastic product.\par \par \uc2 A water bottle will come back as another water bottle or maybe a container or utensil, something else useful. And it`s just not true, and they know it. Only 8 percent of the plastics that they recycle through advanced -- so-called advanced recycling are actually turned into recyclable material.\par \par \uc2 Advanced recycling is not a solution. It is another lie that they are telling. It is completely predictable that they`re telling it, because that`s what they have done for decades. But it`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: It`s worth pointing out \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  does not actually produce or manufacture a lot of the single-use plastics that you`re talking about here.\par \par \uc2 They are the largest producer of the plastic polymers that are used to then produce those single-use plastic products, right, the building blocks of the plastics.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So why not go after the companies that actually make the single-use plastics that we agree are the core of this problem?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: We`re going to the source. \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  is the biggest producer, as you mentioned, of the building blocks, the raw materials, the plastic pellets that they sell to their corporate clients, who then through melting and molding, through adding of chemicals, turn them into the products that we see.\par \par \uc2 But they`re the biggest producers of the building blocks of the polymers, as you mentioned. And so -- and they`re one of the biggest liars, one of the biggest deceivers that have been engaged on their own and through their trade industry front groups perpetuating this myth of recycling.\par \par \uc2 So they must be held responsible. And, in this case, it`s unique. It`s pioneering. It`s trailblazing. It`s groundbreaking in the sense that it`s the first time a public entity like the state of California has sued a petrochemical company like \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  that produces the plastic pellets, the polymers.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There have also been campaigns over the years for consumers to reduce their plastic consumption, not just reuse and recycle, as you point out, which has abysmally low rates across the country.\par \par \uc2 But our usage as consumers has actually gone way up over the years. So doesn`t that play a role in creating more demand that manufacturers will say, we`re just trying to meet?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: When it comes to usage, no surprise that it`s gone up. That`s exactly what \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  wants. That`s exactly what the myth of recycling is designed to manifest.\par \par \uc2 When people have comfort that they can use freely plastic products as much as they want and put them in the blue bin because it`ll come back as a recycled product and not harm the environment -- in fact, they think they`re being good stewards of the environment -- that increases use.\par \par \uc2 That`s by design. That`s what \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  wanted. And given that only 5 percent of the plastic waste in the United States of America is actually recycled, less use of plastic products might be something that a fully informed public might want to engage in and reuse of either plastic products or non-plastic products like cloth or fabric bags when you go shopping might be something people want to adopt as well.\par \par \uc2 But the myth of recycling was designed to drive purchasing -- purchases of and use of plastics up, up, up and also drive profits of \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  up, up, up. And it`s worked. Last year, \uc2 ExxonMobil\uc2  made $36 billion in profit.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That is California`s Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Attorney General, thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA: Thanks for having me, Amna. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Stephen Colbert has been making America laugh for decades and, since 2015, on \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` "The Late Show With Stephen Colbert."\par \par \uc2 Over the years, his wife, Evie McGee Colbert, has often gotten a mention and makes regular cameos on the show. And now the couple is sharing the ingredients that make their partnership work, especially in the kitchen. Their new cookbook "Does This Taste Funny?" is out now.\par \par \uc2 And we met up recently to talk food, family and politics for our arts and culture series, Canvas.\par \par \uc2 At New York City`s Porchlight bar,\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT, Co-Author, "Does This Taste Funny?: Recipes Our Family Loves": I have never been interviewed in a bar before. This is nice.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT, Co-Author, "Does This Taste Funny?: Recipes Our Family Loves": What have we got here?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That`s a great question. What is this dessert?\par \par \uc2 Over a spread of Southern specialties\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Oh, nice.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: And then biscuits and honey.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Stephen and Evie McGee Colbert open up about their shared love of their shared hometown, Charleston, South Carolina, when they first met in their 20s and that inspired this cookbook together.\par \par \uc2 You say in the book that you guys live in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: I`m curious how that works.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Well...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Does someone take the lead? Is there like a chef/sous-chef situation?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Every family in some ways -- like, the whole thing about, like, we`re talking about kitchen table issues and sitting around the kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: We`re always hanging out in there, like everybody does.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Right. Right. One of us is a little bossy.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: One of us.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: We`re not going to say who. No names.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: One of us enjoys cooking more than the other one does. That`s all. I don`t think I`m bossy. I think I`m just...\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: It`s true. You do. You do enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I`m more enthusiastic about it.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Yes. We say in the cookbook...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So there`s a chef/sous-chef vibe, or no?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: No.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: No, we`re not good together.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: How dare I? I apologize.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: You know what? The cookbook changed that. Before that, we didn`t cook together. But then, of course, we had to write a cookbook together. And it turns out we do it pretty well, I think.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The book, a compendium of everything from seafood specials and party food to desserts and drinks, started coming together during COVID.\par \par \uc2 The Colberts, along with their three mostly grown children, were locked down together in their Charleston home. The family turned their focus to a joint project, compiling recipes of the food they loved and the stories behind it.\par \par \uc2 It feels like it`s as much about your family history in this book as it is about the food. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Absolutely. Very fair.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes, it`s about our -- yes, it`s about the lives we lived growing up there, not just the food.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Well, and when we started, we gathered up recipes that we have fed our kids for years, but we also reached out to family members.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that was fun for me is, I worked on it with my mother, so we would go hunting. She couldn`t remember. Like, for example, her pickled shrimp recipe is in the cookbook. I think we made it for her four times and she kept saying, that`s not quite right.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: That`s not right.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: The book is dedicated to Evie`s mom, not just because she was a great hostess and a cook of her own, but because we were writing this book near the end of your mother`s life.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Right. She wasn`t well, so I would be with her, a medical appointment or at the hospital or something. And it was something else to talk about, which was really nice for her and nice for me.\par \par \uc2 And I think neither one of us sort of knew where we were headed in the future, but it gave us a present moment to be together.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Food is at the heart of major moments for the couple, including the moment they first met.\par \par \uc2 You have told about the moment you saw Evie, you knew, you knew she was the one that you were going to marry.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Did you cook for her? Is that how you help to woo her, or no?\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Oh, no, you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: No. Actually, I don`t think I was very good. Like, I -- there`s a recipe in Charleston, it`s a shrimp dip, or -- but we call it shrimp paste, because everything in Charleston has to have an old name.\par \par \uc2 And so my family made shrimp paste, and our recipe was terrible. And the first thing I ever made for Evie was shrimp paste, which is not the most attractive-sounding dish.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Well, you use canned shrimp, because that`s all we could afford.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I used canned shrimp. And it was terrible.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: There were budget constraints.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There were budget constraints.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The first dish Evie cooked for Stephen? Her family`s famous cheese biscuits.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Her family would make thousands and thousands of cheese biscuits and distribute them around.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That it`s a family recipe.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Yes, it`s a family recipe. My mother did it every Christmas all year long.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: They were in the house. It was her go-to. Someone`s coming over. You put a plate of those out, just like our first date. I wasn`t ready.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: She was late. So, I went into the bar with her dad.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: On purpose. You got to make them wait a little.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: You, Miss. You, Miss, right there.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Hello. Hi. Lovely to meet you.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Lovely to meet you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: What is your name?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The book isn`t their first collaboration. That came during pandemic lockdowns when Evie jumped in to help Stephen host "The Late Show" from their South Carolina home on Sullivan`s Island.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We called it the Ed Sullivan`s Island Theater.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Was that was your first professional time working together?\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: It was.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes. And we were terrified. I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Her eyes went wide when she said, yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: It was. It really was. And there were very funny moments that now are funny at the time were slightly stressful, where he would turn to me and say something like, "I can`t hear Tom Hanks."\par \par \uc2 And I would say, "I don`t know what to do."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: She was my crew on the couch with a headset and all these wires around her. She`s like, I don`t know what button to push.\par \par \uc2 The polls are showing we won by numbers you have never even heard of. We got smeventeen percent, 100 didgeridoo.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Back in his studio, Stephen Colbert is back to doing what he does best, bringing levity to a heavy time and sometimes heavy topics.\par \par \uc2 Is there such toxicity around the discourse sometimes?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Do you find it`s harder today to be funny about what`s going on in the world?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: It`s a gift to us that we get to go out there and do the jokes for the audience, and we get to realize that we`re not crazy, and that these things that are driving us crazy or making us anxious are also resonating with the audience.\par \par \uc2 But toxicity itself is worth making fun of. Like, that`s just -- the fact of how bad things are is something you have to make fun of.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Yes. Is it the kind of thing where, Evie, he leaves it all at work? Or do you talk about these things at home?\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Oh, we talk...\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: We talk about it.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Unfortunately, we talk about it a lot.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: All the time, yes. We`ve always been news junkies.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Always been news junkies.\par \par \uc2 But yes, we do talk about it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: "PBS News Hour," number one.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: He`s long blended current events and comedy, but this year, with this particular election cycle, Colbert says there`s just a lot more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Right now, what`s interesting is, no matter what happens with the election, it`s something new.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: You know, the excitement of as much -- as I admire Mr. Biden, is that the excitement that, well, there is a change. And this change is as good as a holiday sometimes. And there`s something new to talk about.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: One of the jokes that I did after the switch-off between Biden and Vice President Harris was that, in the monologue, I just found myself dancing around.\par \par \uc2 I was just kind of excited, and I just turned to the camera and said -- it was first thing in my mind. I said, this is the dance of a guy who gets to talk about something new for the first time in five years.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: His own politics lean left. He refused to even say former President Trump`s name for a couple of years.\par \par \uc2 Do you have red lines, like people you won`t talk to. Like, would you have former President Trump on the show?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I have had him before, and he was kind of boring. So, no.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: No.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes. No. No. I don`t like to have people on the show who I don`t think are going to be honest agents of their own ideas.\par \par \uc2 Politicians always represent an idea. So if I think a politician is disingenuous, not to point out Mr. Trump specifically, because there are many politicians I wouldn`t want to talk to that I think do not honestly represent what they actually believe, but are rather just playing to a crowd. So, that`s just no fun.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: "The Late Show" on \uc2 CBS\uc2  is number one among all late-night shows, some of which are struggling with a changing landscape. Audiences may come to Colbert for the laughs, but seem to be staying for more.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): We have to learn more about trust and peace and learning about each other, rather than...\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Rare moments of engaged conversation, like this from his recent interview with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: As you can see with the protest out here, that answer is unsatisfying to some people.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There were these pro-Palestinian protesters and she said, I can`t hear what they are saying. And you took a moment to make sure she heard the questions that they were asking. Why?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Well, I want to be respectful to my guests, but I promised the people who had protested during the first act of the interview with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  that I would ask the question if they would sit and listen to what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 And, also, there`s no way to move on from a subject unless you address the subject.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: That feels like something you may have learned in 30-plus years of marriage together, no?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Yes. It is good to address the subject.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Well, and listening. And I think what you did so well at that moment, that`s what conversation is about, that we have to listen to the issue that is bothering someone, so you can respond to it.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s also in our marriage. You have to listen and then be able to respond. And I think we do. We`ve managed to figure out how to do that.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I`m sorry. I missed some of that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: There are more collaborations and laughs to come as the two run a production company together.\par \par \uc2 Stephen and Evie Colbert, thank you so very much. Such a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Oh, thank you.\par \par \uc2 EVIE MCGEE COLBERT: It`s been lovely. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 And we have more from Stephen and Evie Colbert online. Taking some lessons learned while cooking together, they discuss the art of an apology and how people who disagree can find a way to get along. That`s on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Meantime, eight in 10 Americans say the country`s political divisions pose a serious threat to the future of our democracy. That is according to the most recent PBS News/Marist poll.\par \par \uc2 Judy Woodruff has been exploring many of these divisions for her series America at a Crossroads.\par \par \uc2 And she recently hosted a town hall in the swing state of Wisconsin to find out how people are feeling at this moment. Here`s a preview of her special airing later tonight on \uc2 PBS\uc2  stations.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: On a warm September night at the historic Pabst brewery in downtown Milwaukee, 50 Wisconsinites with different political views gathered to discuss their differences and whether common ground is possible.\par \par \uc2 RYN BOTSFORD, Harris Supporter: I am a gay person. I have a son. I have a wife. And I cannot compromise with somebody who says that my child would be better not with my wife and I, but in a foster system or with a mom and a dad.\par \par \uc2 JIM BLUEMEL, Trump Supporter: Ninety-nine percent of the people are happy that you have a child and you`re raising a child in a good environment and giving that child a good education.\par \par \uc2 What I disagree with is that I don`t want to have a drag show at my kids` grade school. I don`t want to have a drag school -- show at the library. And I don`t want my children, my girls competing against someone that is changed over from a male to a female.\par \par \uc2 I have a major problem with that. That`s where I am not willing to compromise. But as all the rest of the stuff, you go on and live your life. You be you, I will be me.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: I moderated a sometimes tense, sometimes surprising conversation about the most difficult topics our society faces today.\par \par \uc2 AMY TUETENBERG, Trump Supporter: I believe that those who believe in open borders are using our neighbors from the southern border to bring them here in a situation where they will not be given any rights or visas.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Antonio, you are an immigrant yourself. You came as a child of parents who arrived here without documentation. Do you think the system did you wrong?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIO ALCAZAR, DACA Recipient: No. Yes, I think she`s 100 percent right. Like, we`re on opposite ends of the political divide, but she`s right.\par \par \uc2 Immigrants are used largely as a slave labor force. There`s an enormous amount of people that work with no rights, no ability to say anything, because, if they say something, they could get in trouble. And so they just have to take what they`re given.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Some shared painful personal stories.\par \par \uc2 EMILY SCHULTZ, Harris Supporter: I had to walk around for two weeks knowing I had a baby that I could feel kicking inside me who would never survive birth, who would never get to come home with me. And I couldn`t be induced into labor because it would be considered termination.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And others responded thoughtfully to those they disagreed with.\par \par \uc2 MARK HOLLAND, Trump Supporter: I think that Republicans and Democrats are actually closer than we think about this issue.\par \par \uc2 As far as abortion, I do believe that some people use it as a -- like an oops kind of thing. Oh, you know, we got pregnant. Oops, let`s get an abortion. That`s the issue I have with it, as opposed to the areas where, if a woman is raped or if there is a danger of the mother or if there is something like -- then I would say, yes, it would be OK.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: With the help of a panel, we tried to make sense of the difference between what we see in Washington and the conversations taking place in the room.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE CRAMER, \uc2 University of Wisconsin-Madison\uc2 : Our political divisions are mapping on to so many other divisions in our lives, whether were talking about race or sometimes gender.\par \par \uc2 CMDR. THEODORE JOHNSON (RET), New America: We have a politics that will find a way to weaponize anything that`s different among us and then turn us against one another in hopes of out of expedience or in hopes of winning elections.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES, Editor at Large, The Bulwark: They want us to be at each others throat. They want an issue. They want us to fear and dislike one another.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Please join me tonight at 9:00 p.m. Eastern/8:00 Central for "Crossroads: A Conversation With America."\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And you can get a look behind the scenes of how we produced that town hall. See that at PBS.org/NewsHour.\par \par \uc2 And that is the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 CQ-Roll Call, Inc., which  takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the  user`s personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be  printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any  fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and CQ-Roll Call,  Inc.`s copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material.  This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020240924ek9n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for September 11, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Schifrin, Paul Solman, Stephanie Sy, William Brangham, Judy  Woodruff, Laura Barron-Lopez, Lisa Desjardins, Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 9,771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC, which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the user's personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and ASC Services II Media, LLC's copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Good evening. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On the "News Hour" tonight: the key takeaways from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Donald Trump`s debate. We delve into the issues and fact-check a debunked story repeated by the former president.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: More turmoil in the Middle East. An Israeli airstrike hits a \uc2 U.N.\uc2  school in Gaza, while President Biden speaks out about the death of an American activist in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And Judy Woodruff explores the role social media is playing in spreading extremism and misinformation in an already divided political landscape.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL, Polarization Lab, \uc2 Duke University\uc2 : When we wander on to social media, we can wrongly conclude that everyone is extreme and everyone is sort of out to get everyone else.\par \par \uc2 (BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Tens of millions of Americans tuned into last night`s consequential presidential debate between Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: The contentious debate may be the only face off of the campaign season. Vice President Harris tried to walk a fine line between being both an incumbent and a change candidate all while fending off attacks from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the former president was repeatedly fact- checked for inaccuracies.\par \par \uc2 Laura Barron-Lopez reports.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Nice to see you. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: The night began cordially before the gloves came off.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : World leaders are laughing at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I have talked with military leaders, some of whom worked with you, and they say you`re a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She got zero votes, and when she ran, she was the first one to leave because she failed.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: It was their first face-to-face meeting, and their first head-to-head clash on the issues, like on the economy.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : I have a plan, $6,000 for young families for the first year of your child`s life to help you in that most critical stage of your child`s development. I have a plan.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She copied Biden`s plan, and it`s like four sentences, like run, Spot, run. Four sentences that are just, oh, we will try and lower taxes.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: The former president tried to link Harris to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : She is Biden. She`s trying to get away from Biden. I don`t know the gentleman she says. She is Biden.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : Clearly, I am not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and I am certainly not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: And Harris, a former prosecutor, repeatedly baited Trump.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : They`re so clear they can manipulate you with flattery and favors.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: From highlighting his fondness of dictators to needling Trump about the crowd size at his rallies.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : I`m going to invite you to attend one of Donald Trump`s rallies, because it`s a really interesting thing to watch. You will see during the course of his rallies, he talks about fictional characters like Hannibal Lecter. He will talk about windmills cause cancer.\par \par \uc2 And what you will also notice is that people start leaving his rallies early out of exhaustion and boredom.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : People don`t leave my rallies. We have the biggest rallies, the most incredible rallies in the history of politics. That`s because people want to take their country back.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: For much of the night, Trump was on defense, not answering whether he`d sign or veto a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC News Live "Prime" Anchor: If I could just get a yes or no, because you`re running mate, J.D. Vance, has said that you would veto if you did come to your desk.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Well, I didn`t discuss it with J.D., in all fairness. Look, we don`t have to discuss it.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Refusing to say if he thought defending Ukraine against Russia was in America`s national security interests.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, "World News Tonight" Anchor and Managing Editor: I want to ask you a very simple question tonight. Do you want Ukraine to win this war?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R) FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND PRESIDENTIAL NOMINEE: I want the war to stop. I want to save lives that are being uselessly -- people being killed by the millions. It`s the millions.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR: Just to clarify in the question, do you believe it`s in the U.S. best interests for Ukraine to win this war, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I think it`s the U.S. best interests to get this war finished and just get it done.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: And taking no responsibility for his role in the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR: Is there anything you regret about what you did on that day, yes or no?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I had nothing to do with that, other than they asked me to make a speech.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Vice President Harris used that moment to appeal to undecided voters.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 : It`s time to turn the page. And if that was a bridge too far for you, well, there is a place in our campaign for you to stand for country, to stand for our democracy, to stand for rule of law, and to end the chaos.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Moments after the debate ended, Harris picked up a superstar endorsement, Taylor Swift. And in a rare move for a presidential candidate, Trump went to the spin room afterward.\par \par \uc2 Over the last 24 hours, in multiple FOX News interviews, Trump questioned whether he`d do another debate, and said ABC should lose its license.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : It was three to one. It was a rigged deal, as I assumed it would be. I think ABC took a big hit last night. I mean, to be honest, they`re a news organization. They have to be licensed to do it. They ought to take away their license for the way they did that.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Trump and Republicans were quick to attack the moderators for fact-checking him in real time, including on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Her vice presidential pick says abortion in the ninth month is absolutely fine. He also says execution after birth. It`s execution, no longer abortion, because the baby is born.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS: There is no state in this country where it is legal to kill a baby after it`s born.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: And on a debunked conspiracy theory about Haitian immigrants in an Ohio town eating pets, one that`s been peddled by far right activist and 9/11 truther Laura Loomer, who traveled with Trump to the debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : In Springfield, they`re eating the dogs, the people that came in. They`re eating the cats. They`re eating -- they`re eating the pets of the people that live there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR: ABC News did reach out to the city manager there. He told us there had been no credible reports of specific claims of pets being harmed, injured or abused by individuals within the immigrant community.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Well, I have seen people on television.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR: Let me just say...\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Katie Sanders of PolitiFact defended the moderators.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDERS, Editor in Chief, PolitiFact: When a candidate is lobbying conspiracy theories, that`s kind of a softball for a moderator, and it`s particularly glaring if you don`t address that.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: She said Harris was not immune from critique for leaving out finer details, but it was Trump who required more immediate fact-checking.\par \par \uc2 KATIE SANDERS: I think one candidate, former President Trump, was repeating conspiracy theories that are pretty well-known to be false at this point and so it`s almost easier to call those out in real time.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: At Ground Zero today, disagreements were put aside. President Biden and Vice President Harris stood just feet away from former President Trump and running mate J.D. Vance as the nation remembered the nearly 3,000 lives lost on this day 23 years ago, the former president and Vice President Harris even shaking hands at the remembrance.\par \par \uc2 But, tomorrow, the race is back on with less than eight weeks to go before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And Laura joins us now, alongside Lisa Desjardins, who`s been covering the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 So, Laura, the Harris campaign feels good about how last night went both in terms of Harris` performance and the degree to which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost his composure multiple times last night. How does the debate in their view help her position in this campaign moving forward?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Well, the campaign feels as though it helps her immensely, that essentially the momentum she has had since she jumped into the race, they feel as though it`s only going to be furthered by the debate.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what a lot of Democrats that I spoke to said. They feel as though it`s totally different than how they felt after that June debate between President Biden and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They have particularly pointed out direct appeals that they feel Harris was wise to make to key voting blocs, specifically appealing to women on abortion, appealing to centrists and undecideds on January 6, as we reported, and also a direct appeal to some 800,000 Polish Americans in Pennsylvania itself, specifically in her defense of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I also spoke to Geoff Duncan, a former lieutenant governor of Georgia, one of those Republicans that is backing Harris, and he said that Harris is making it hard for level-headed Republicans to vote for Trump again and is giving them a strong permission structure to vote for her.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa, meantime, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is touting his debate performance. I think he said it was his best ever.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: It would be news if he didn`t say that, right?\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , one of his top allies, said it was a missed opportunity. How are other Republicans talking about his performance last night?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: You`re right. The former president said he won the debate. He also said to Vice President Harris` -- the campaign idea that there will be another debate, he said he would consider it, but he said that`s a sign that she lost because she wants a rematch.\par \par \uc2 I haven`t heard anyone else say that except for the former president. But you`re right. From other Republicans, there are three reactions,one, silence. And I will say that is the majority reaction that I found even talking to members of Congress today and trying to reach out to their offices.\par \par \uc2 The other reaction that you hear, some are saying, well, there should have been more details from Harris, maybe the moderators were the problem, finding some other thing to talk about.\par \par \uc2 But there is a significant amount, like Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who are saying, this was a missed opportunity. And it`s significant to understand what the problems might be for them. Let`s look at the polling that we have from our PBS/\uc2 NPR\uc2 /Marist poll just this week about where the generic congressional ballot would go.\par \par \uc2 It is split almost right down the middle whether people would vote for a generic Republican or Democrat for Congress. But look at that, 8 percent undecided. This -- the race for Congress can really still swing. The undecided number for the race for president right now in our poll, 1 percent. So all these members of Congress and Republicans know that this debate could affect who runs Congress.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, Laura, what are you hearing from voters about their initial reactions to last night`s debate?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: We sat in on two focus groups run by never-Trumper Sarah Longwell`s firm. One was swing state voters who backed Trump in 2016 and then voted Biden in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And they praised how Harris conducted herself. And one Georgia voter in particular, Justin, so that the debate impacted his views about Harris.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN, Georgia Voter: I wanted some answers, which, in the beginning, Kamala didn`t seem like she was going to give, but she got there. She was definitely very presidential. I feel like I got all the answers from Kamala, but Trump just sat there taking her bait, like what was said, and not answering questions. I feel like she was the clear winner.\par \par \uc2 I think every time I watch Trump get on TV and rail and go nuts about a topic, it just pushes me further and further towards the Democratic Party. I was born and raised a Republican.\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: In this group of swing voters, they were asked if the election were held today, who would they vote for? And all nine of them said that it would be Harris. But the election isn`t today. And so there were a few of them that said they still wanted a bit more time to think about it.\par \par \uc2 And another group of people who voted for Trump in 2020, they weren`t as enthusiastic about either candidate and some expressing frustration with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as well, saying that they still wanted more policy details from her.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa, same question. What have you picked up from voters over the course of your reporting?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: A quick note. I spoke to two Republicans who were formerly undecided and now leaning more toward Harris today.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: So, Laura, the Harris campaign -- Harris campaign aides, as I understand it, they spent the day in Wilmington huddled trying to figure out how best to strategize on this momentum as they see it.\par \par \uc2 What are they telling you?\par \par \uc2 LAURA BARRON-LOPEZ: Well, most Democrats say that they don`t want to make the mistakes of 2016, so they think that she still needs to run like an underdog.\par \par \uc2 And to the point earlier about the fact that she`s trying to appeal to the centrists, two lifelong Pennsylvania Republicans in those focus groups today said that the recent endorsement from Cheney is pushing them more towards Harris. So she`s going to be hitting the ground in Pennsylvania, North Carolina in the days to come. \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  will be in Michigan and Wisconsin, so they`re not taking any breaks right now, Geoff.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And, Lisa, while we have you here, I have to ask you a question about what`s happening on the Hill, because House Republicans have a new problem. We are weeks away from a funding deadline. What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Well, today House Speaker Mike Johnson pulled the funding bill that Republicans wanted to pass through the House to have that stopgap measure go.\par \par \uc2 He could not get enough Republican votes to make it through. Attached to that was the Republican SAVE Act, which would require a national I.D. voter law. There were many different reasons. Different groups of Republicans oppose this. We have seen this playbook before. They just cannot get 217 votes, even though they have a majority.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what Speaker Johnson told reporters today.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA): No vote today because we`re in the consensus- building business here in Congress. With small majorities, that`s what you do, two primary obligations, responsibly fund the government, and make sure that our elections are free and fair and secure. And that`s what we`re working on.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Consensus-building is not going so well right now. They only have, depending on how you count, six to eight legislative days after this week to make that September 30 funding deadlines.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Democrats just expect to have another measure. They think this advantages them in a few ways, but we will see.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: We have seen this movie before.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Lisa Desjardins, Laura Barron-Lopez, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 LISA DESJARDINS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Well, as you just heard, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doubled down on unsupported claims that Haitian immigrants are abducting and eating people`s pets in the small city of Springfield, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 William Brangham recently visited Springfield, where he spoke to city officials and members of the Haitian community there.\par \par \uc2 He joins us now.\par \par \uc2 William, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Hi, Amna.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: So we heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  there repeat those assertions to tens of millions of people who watched the debate last night. As you have been reporting, there is no factual evidence for those claims. So what is actually happening in Springfield?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: Three weeks ago, our wonderful producer Mary Fecteau and I were in Springfield doing a story about what is going on there.\par \par \uc2 And Springfield is like a lot of Midwestern Rust Belt cities. It had a declining population for a long time, but in recent years, they have had an influx of Haitians moving to that community. This is part of the wave of people that are fleeing the violence in Haiti. Tens of thousands of people have left that country as it has fallen into chaos.\par \par \uc2 And about 12,000 to 15,000 of them have ended up in Springfield, Ohio. And they`re here legally under the Temporary Protected Status program. But their arrival in this community has been rocky, to say the least. There was an accident that killed a young boy last year. But, in addition, if you just grow a city`s population by 20 percent in just a few years, that causes strains.\par \par \uc2 We talked to the mayor about this, Rob Rue. Here`s what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 ROB RUE, Mayor of Springfield, Ohio: The infrastructure of the city, our safety forces, our hospitals, our schools. Springfield is a close community and has a big heart. But at the same point, we have had this influx that has taxed all these services.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: While we were there, we heard that there had been these \uc2 Facebook\uc2  rumors of ducks and geese being taken by Haitians out of city parks. County commissioners said that there were no -- no evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 But then this pets rumor that was amplified last night really took off when the senator from Ohio, J.D. Vance, put out this tweet earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 He said this: "Reports now show that people have had their pets abducted and eaten by people who shouldn`t be in this country."\par \par \uc2 Again, we asked the city manager, and the city manager in a city like Springfield, like in many cities, is like the CEO of the city. And he again reiterated there are no credible reports that immigrants had taken pets, abducted pets, or harmed pets in any way.\par \par \uc2 And on some level, there`s a logical question you have to ask here. If you believe that your beloved pet had been stolen and killed and eaten by someone, who would you first contact, the police or your local senator? The local senator here, J.D. Vance, argues he`s received multiple calls about this.\par \par \uc2 The local officials say they have received none. Even today, after the debate, they double down saying all of these things are -- quote -- "rumors" that have been spread by misinformation.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: William, these very ugly rumors do have some historical context we should get into here, right? Like, how have these kinds of claims been used against other groups in the past?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: That is exactly right, Amna.\par \par \uc2 Researchers who study this field say rumors like this are often used to demonize people who are seen as different, whether you are from another country, speak another language, or simply look different.\par \par \uc2 We talked earlier, Mary and I, to Cynthia Miller-Idriss. She`s a researcher at American university. Here`s what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MILLER-IDRISS, \uc2 American University\uc2 : There`s always been this idea of a threat from the other, from immigrants, from people of color to the nation, to white families, to civilization itself, right, this idea that there`s some terrible threat coming that has to be defended against, and then, as evidence of that, this idea that your beloved pets are being stolen and eaten, which is patently false.\par \par \uc2 There`s no evidence whatsoever that that`s happening. And it`s the kind of thing that makes people scared of the other. It demonizes and it dehumanizes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: So, even though there is no evidence that immigrant communities in America commit violence at greater rates than native-born Americans, the former president and his running mate keep leaning into this argument that we are being invaded by a violent immigrant horde, and it is not true.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: It`s great reporting from you and producer Mary Fecteau.\par \par \uc2 William Brangham, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BRANGHAM: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: Extreme weather is affecting two parts of the country tonight.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Francine is making landfall tonight in Louisiana as a Category 2 hurricane. Meteorologists say it could lead to a deadly storm surge, fierce winds and widespread flooding. Meanwhile, out West, scores of large wildfires have prompted tens of thousands of evacuations.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Sy has the latest.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: In the Los Angeles area today, crews fought to contain massive blazes racing toward foothill communities. Some longtime Southern California residents told reporters they`d never seen fires like this before.\par \par \uc2 LAURIE NOWOCINSKI, California Resident: This is the biggest one and the closest one so far.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: In the 33 years?\par \par \uc2 LAURIE NOWOCINSKI: In 33 years, yes sir.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Three large wildfires outside of L.A. are barely contained, the Bridge Fire to the north, the Line Fire to the east, and the Airport Fire to the south.\par \par \uc2 The Line Fire`s location in the San Bernardino Mountains has complicated suppression efforts.\par \par \uc2 ARNOLD MENJIVAR, Redondo Beach, California, Fire Department: The terrain is very difficult terrain, some inaccessible areas for the crews to reach to be able to fight this fire aggressively and safely.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: The Airport Fire, ignited by a spark from heavy machinery used by public workers, quickly exploded in size.\par \par \uc2 JENNIE BROWNING, Airport Fire Evacuee: We hardly had any time.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Some Orange County residents evacuated as the fire reached homes.\par \par \uc2 JENNIE BROWNING: We had a packing list. We have done this a couple times in the past. So we grabbed all the stuff we needed and it`s in the car down here at the bottom of the hill.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: To the north, the Bridge Fire tore through a popular ski area and destroyed dozens of homes in the villages of Mount Baldy and Wrightwood.\par \par \uc2 But no state is battling more large wildfires than Oregon with 24 blazes burning right now. Idaho is dealing with 22. While tinderbox conditions plague the West, the Southeast, particularly Louisiana, is bracing for an onslaught of water from Hurricane Francine.\par \par \uc2 GARY DRISKELL, Saint Mary Parish, Louisiana, Sheriff: For days now, we have been prepping for the storm. We have high-water vehicles, boats, you name it, extra deputies out on patrol right now until this is concluded.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE SY: Now they`re hunkering down for a storm forecasters say could bring up to 12 inches of rain and a storm surge as high as 10 feet.\par \par \uc2 For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Stephanie Sy.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The U.S. is sending $700 million in new humanitarian aid to Ukraine to help the country`s battered energy grid. The announcement came as Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and the new British foreign secretary, \uc2 David Lammy\uc2 , traveled together to Ukraine. The U.K. is also providing hundreds of millions of dollars in assistance.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  has been asking for permission to use Western-supplied weapons to strike deep inside Russia, which the U.S. has so far denied. At a press conference today, Secretary Blinken said he would take the issue of missiles back to Washington to brief the president.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. Secretary of State: We have adjusted and adapted as needs have changed, as the battlefield has changed. And I have no doubt that we will continue to do that as this evolves.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: The question of whether Ukraine can fire deeper into Russia is also due to come up when Britain`s Prime Minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2  visits Washington later this week. He`s due to meet with President Biden at the White House on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Back in this country, a group of state and local election officials from across America is warning the problems with the \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  could disrupt voting in the upcoming presidential election.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the head of the USPS today, they cited widespread delays receiving mail-in ballots during primary elections as well as mail being returned as undeliverable, which could disenfranchise voters. In an interview last month, U.S. Postmaster General \uc2 Louis DeJoy\uc2  insisted the USPS is ready to handle the flood of election mail come November.\par \par \uc2 Inflation reached a three-year low last month, thanks largely to cooling gas prices. Today`s report showed consumer prices rising just 2.5 percent in August, compared to the same month last year. That`s down from July`s reading of 2.9 percent and a far cry from the 9.1 percent inflation we saw back in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Today`s report comes as \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials prepare to cut interest rates next week for the first time since early 2020.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street today, stocks ended higher following that inflation data. The Dow Jones industrial average shook off early losses to end more than 100 points higher. The Nasdaq jumped nearly 370 points, or more than 2 percent. The S&P 500 gained ground for a third straight session.\par \par \uc2 And a passing of note. Singer-songwriter Frankie Beverly, who fronted the group Maze, has died. In the 1970s, Beverly brought the soulful sounds of his hometown of Philadelphia to the music scene of the Bay Area. From there, the group climbed the R&B charts and were dubbed by "Ebony" magazine as Black America`s favorite band.\par \par \uc2 Anthems like "Before I Let Go" played at countless summer cookouts and family reunions over the years. His friend Beyonce covered the song in 2019 and many hip-hop artists have sampled his melodies. Frankie Beverly and Maze finished a farewell tour earlier this year. He was 77 years old.\par \par \uc2 And commemorations have been held across the country to mark 23 years since the 9/11 terrorist attacks, which killed nearly 3,000 people. The day started in New York, where flowers were laid and tributes paid for the lives lost when two planes hit the Twin Towers that morning.\par \par \uc2 Then, around midday, President Biden and Vice President Harris visited the site where the hijacked Flight 93 went down near Shanksville, Pennsylvania. And this afternoon at the Pentagon, Biden and Harris joined Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  to pay respects to the 184 people killed there.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on the "News Hour": a look at how Donald Trump`s proposal to increase tariffs on China would affect U.S. consumers; President Biden responds to Israel`s claim that the killing of an American in the West Bank was unintentional; and how social media has fundamentally changed the way Americans engage in politics.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Tariffs are a hallmark of former President Donald Trump`s economic agenda, and some of them have picked up bipartisan support as a way of dealing with China.\par \par \uc2 But there are still key differences between Trump and Vice President Harris when it comes to how large and wide-ranging they should be.\par \par \uc2 As economics correspondent Paul Solman reports, the potential impact of imposing more tariffs was debated on stage last night and is very much on the minds of some voters in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Other countries are going to finally, after 75 years, pay us back for all that we have done for the world.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Last night, former President Trump began by defending his aggressive tariff policies both from his first term and what he would do if he wins again. That includes a 10 to 20 percent tariff on all foreign goods and a 60 percent tariff, that is, import tax, on goods from China.\par \par \uc2 It was the very first policy spat in last night`s debate.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: My opponent has a plan that I call the Trump`s sales tax, which would be a 20 percent tax on everyday goods that you rely on to get through the month.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: To which Trump responded in part:\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : The tariff will be substantial in some cases. I took in billions and billions of dollars, as you know, from China. In fact, they never took the tariff off because it was so much money.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Almost anyone would dispute that explanation, but one thing is true. The Biden administration maintained most of the tariffs on more than $300 billion worth of goods from the original Trump regime and added tariffs on another $18 billion worth of items from steel to medical supplies, including a 100 percent tariff on Chinese electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Harris We are fighting for middle-class families.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: And Vice President Harris supports tariffs too, she said, so long as they`re much smaller and targeted. You will hear more about that in a bit.\par \par \uc2 What`s so striking is that protective tariffs were once a partisan point of stark conflict, but now both candidates are in favor of tariffs, which, of course, have such obvious appeal to workers afraid of losing their jobs, many of them swing-state voters in import-threatened industries.\par \par \uc2 Look the best-selling BYD Chinese E.V. goes for $16,000 or less in China. They`re not yet sold here, but even with Biden`s 100 percent tariff, they`d still be a bargain. And despite a plunge in Chinese consumer demand, manufacturing activity across all sectors remained strong until just recently, resulting in a glut of Chinese goods being offered on Web sites like Temu at unbelievably low prices.\par \par \uc2 I bought this cap with a watercolor by Swiss artist Paul Klee for $25 from a us firm some time ago, this one from China for grandson Will $1.69, free shipping.\par \par \uc2 Small wonder that at a Detroit Teamsters union local, there was plenty of support for protective tariffs.\par \par \uc2 JEFF TRICOFF, Member, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 283: Short-term discomfort for long- term relief.\par \par \uc2 CASIMER GUZDZIOL, Member, \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 283: One way to ensure that jobs are going to be at least starting back here in the United States. good- paying jobs, jobs that they outsourced overseas, maybe it`s not something that`s going to happen immediately, but, over time, it`s probably something I think is going to be very effective at bringing jobs back to our country.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: But there were tariff skeptics as well.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MINNIS, Member, Teamsters Local 283: I would say it probably wouldn`t happen right away. So we might not benefit from it.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Professor Kim Clausing worked for the Biden administration, has studied the impact of past and would-be future tariffs as an academic.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING, UCLA School of Law: Our trading partners may retaliate against our tariffs with tariffs of their own, which hurts our export sectors.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: So when the Trump administration slapped tariffs on imports from multiple countries, including China, it did spur some domestic production and manufacturing. Trump`s team also cites a rise in median household income during that period, before the pandemic hit, as evidence of success.\par \par \uc2 But, overall, says economist Clausing:\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: When you take a hard look at the data, they really aren`t worth it for jobs and they raise costs for consumers.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: How much would Trump`s latest proposal cost per year?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: That comes out for a median family in the middle of the income distribution to be about $1,700.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: So how do the workers like them apples?\par \par \uc2 DAN HAMMON, Member, Teamsters Local 283: Seventeen hundred dollars isn`t going to kill me. That`s all there is to it. And if it`s enough to bring jobs back here, I`m willing.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Dan Hammon is a Democrat. So is Jared Jackson.\par \par \uc2 JARED JACKSON, Teamsters Local 283: Some things do take time. And if that were to work, I don`t know what other repercussions would come from that and stuff. And I`m all for it.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: But Clausing says the $1,700 or so will mainly and really hurt low income households.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: What`s really driving the fact that tariffs hit lower- and middle-income people harder than they hit those that are well-off is the fact that low- and middle-income people are going to the store and spending most of their money every week and every month.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Moreover, she says $1,700 is a low estimate.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: This is really an underestimate because it`s just looking at the fact that when imported prices go up, consumers take that hit. But imagine you`re going to buy something like a bottle of wine.\par \par \uc2 Well, the French wine and the Spanish wine are more expensive and that gives the Californian wine growers the opportunity to raise their prices too.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: In fact, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  argued last night the impact of Trump`s tariffs could be even larger, as much as $4,000 per household per year. But are we to do nothing about the tsunami of cheap Chinese imports?\par \par \uc2 Trump economic adviser and former U.S. trade representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  has written that we have to take action -- quote -- "In circumstances where the exporting country`s trade distortions are systemic, broad tariffs may be the only way to offset them and reinstate market forces."\par \par \uc2 Well, not broad tariffs, says economist Clausing.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: I think you can reach those industries and those trade practices that you think are unfair with much more targeted, careful and strategic tariffs, but I don`t think there`s any reason or any argument to have such broad-based across-the-board tariffs.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: And that`s what the Democrats are proposing, says Clausing.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: I don`t think that means you want to completely unilaterally disagree to never have a tariff, because, as our trading partners in Europe and elsewhere realize, it`s important to have tools at your disposal when other countries are doing unfair trade practices.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Tax expert Jim Hines says there`s a basic problem with all tariffs, though, that most people never think about.\par \par \uc2 JAMES HINES, University of Michigan Law School: If you put on a tariff, it increases the value of the dollar, and that makes it harder to export.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Why does the value of the dollar go up if you impose tariffs?\par \par \uc2 JAMES HINES: One way to think about it is we have less demand for foreign currency now.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Now, stick with us here. That`s because we need to buy foreign currency in order to buy foreign goods. We buy that currency with dollars, but if we buy fewer foreign goods:\par \par \uc2 JAMES HINES: We`re not buying foreign currency, and so the dollar gets stronger relative to the foreign currencies.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: As an inevitable result, dollar-priced American goods become more expensive, and thus American exports will go down.\par \par \uc2 JAMES HINES: It`s a tough argument, but it`s true.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Moreover, says Clausing:\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY CLAUSING: Even in those heartland regions, where you would expect people to be pro-tariff, the job losses from the retaliation and the shocks were higher than the job gains due to the protection from the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: But let`s face it, these are tough arguments, as is the argument for free trade in general. In the short run, workers feel threatened by foreign competition or, as Trump enthusiast Brian Pannebecker says:\par \par \uc2 BRIAN PANNEBECKER, Retired Autoworker: I want to have a job, and that`s what I think what President Trump is going for, is protecting American jobs.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: Does Jim Hines not sympathize?\par \par \uc2 JAMES HINES: Your heart goes out to people who lose their jobs because of competition from foreign imports. It absolutely does. And I`m not saying there shouldn`t be assistance for those people and those industries and those communities. I think there should be.\par \par \uc2 But if the question is, should we adopt bad economic policies thinking that, by doing so, we`re helping American workers, that`s just confusion.\par \par \uc2 PAUL SOLMAN: For the "PBS News Hour," Paul Solman in and around Detroit.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: It was a violent day across Gaza and the West Bank. Palestinians say Israeli strikes that hit a United Nations school turned shelter killed dozens in Gaza. The \uc2 U.N.\uc2  said six of its staff died in the attack.\par \par \uc2 In the West Bank, an Israeli soldier died when a Palestinian truck rammed a bus stop. And Palestinians say Israeli raids backed by airstrikes killed at least five.\par \par \uc2 Nick Schifrin looks at the ongoing violence starting in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: At this school turned shelter today, the aftermath was too much to bear and too graphic to show. Survivors collected body parts in whatever they could carry after a bomb gutted the bedroom that had once been a classroom.\par \par \uc2 Israel said the target was \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militants planning attacks. Palestinians said among the dead displaced women and children. Israel has pushed the displace to Al-Mawasi, where early yesterday morning first responders dug through soft sand in the crater left by an Israeli bomb.\par \par \uc2 By day, three craters, each at least 40-feet-wide, suggested Israel had dropped large bombs on what Israel calls a safer zone, tents that lead to the sea and have become a sea of humanity. The bombs blew through flimsy canvas and left behind the artifacts of a family`s displaced life.\par \par \uc2 Mohammad Nasser is 12 years old.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMAD NASSER, 12 Years Old (through translator): It was like any other night here, and then we heard rockets coming down right next to us. I found myself 1.5 meters under the ground and dirt above me. I wasn`t expecting to be saved from under the dirt because I could feel the weight of the ground above me and how strong the attack was. I didn`t think I`d be saved. Thank God for those who helped my dad who worked to get us out.\par \par \uc2 FADY ABED, MedGlobal: People were digging and searching for bodies under the sand. And there was at least more than 20 tents that they were gone.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Fady Abed works for the international medical humanitarian organization MedGlobal. Before the war, he was a dentist. Since October the 7th, he`s been displaced four times.\par \par \uc2 FADY ABED: The fact that we left Gaza City just seeking safety and we know that Gaza is not safe anymore, so we went to the yellow area, they call it, or humanitarian area, but, at the same time, no one is safe even in the humanitarian area, makes you think, why did I take the decision of evacuating in the first place?\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But Israel says the targets were three senior \uc2 Hamas\uc2  commanders who were directly involved in October the 7th and embedded inside the humanitarian zone.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MENCER, Spokesperson, Israeli Prime Minister`s Office: Israel is trying its hardest, more than any other nation on earth, more than any other army has in the history of armed conflict to get civilians out of harm`s way and to go for the terrorist targets, even though they embed themselves in civilian areas.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But it`s the death of an American civilian in the occupied West Bank that has added tension between the U.S. and Israel. On Friday, Israeli soldiers shot dead Aysenur Eygi, 26-year-old born in Turkey who traveled to the West Bank to protest Israeli settlements.\par \par \uc2 Israel says the bullet that struck her was a ricochet aimed at the -- quote -- "instigator of the riot" and has expressed its deepest regret, an explanation that President Biden endorsed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , President of the United States: We`re finding more detail. Apparently, it was an accident. It ricocheted off the ground and got hit by accident, but we`re working that out now.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: But today, in a statement, President Biden said: "The shooting that led to her death is totally unacceptable. There must be full accountability and Israel must do more to ensure that incidents like this never happen again."\par \par \uc2 And Vice President Harris said: "Aysenur was peacefully protesting in the West Bank, standing up against the expansion of settlements when her young life was senselessly cut short."\par \par \uc2 But, today, Eygi`s family said those statements weren`t enough and accused President Biden of -- quote -- "complicity" in Israeli occupation and whitewashing the killing of an American.\par \par \uc2 For more, I`m joined by Juliette Majid and Kelsie Nabass, friends of Aysenur Eygi.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much to both of you. Welcome to the "News Hour."\par \par \uc2 Kelsie Nabass, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell me about your friend?\par \par \uc2 KELSIE NABASS, Friend of Aysenur Eygi: Yes, so I met Aysenur a couple years ago on a trip to Istanbul in Turkey. She was such a breath of fresh air and such a light in any room that she walked into. I knew that immediately I was going to be friends with her when I was introduced to her just because of how silly and fun and goofy she was.\par \par \uc2 And I know that, after a few conversations, we really hit it off and became super close friends.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Juliette Majid, what do you remember?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE MAJID, Friend of Aysenur Eygi: Yes, so I met Aysenur Eygi at the \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 . We both graduated this past June, she with a bachelor`s in psychology and a minor in Middle Eastern cultures and languages.\par \par \uc2 She was an incredibly hard worker. She was a very staunch activist. She was a community and a campus organizer spanning many different solidarity movements and programs across the Seattle area. It`s a profound loss that we are feeling in our community here in Seattle.\par \par \uc2 And also it`s a profound loss for the American public in general to lose such a staunch American human rights activist.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Kelsie Nabass, why did she travel to the occupied West Bank?\par \par \uc2 KELSIE NABASS: As you just heard, Juliette said she was a staunch activist, and she knew that anything that she could have possibly done here on the ground between the U.W. encampment camp met and going to protests and advocating for the liberation of the Palestinian people, she knew that going and being on the ground and observing the suffering and observing and witnessing what the Palestinian people were facing would be good for her self-conscience and also just because she felt comfortable going and being a part of that community.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Juliette Majid, you were talking to her until the end. What did you understand and what do you understand today about what happened?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE MAJID: I talked to Aysenur on Monday before she arrived in the West Bank. And I just know that she was so honored to be able to be in the West Bank in a land where she understood the complex and horrible conditions that the Palestinians in the West Bank face at the hands of the Israeli military and illegal Israeli settlements.\par \par \uc2 And she was so honored to be able to experience and then come back and share these stories and help create a stronger movement here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Kelsie Nabass, as you know, the Israeli military says that it is not defending illegal settlements and is in fact trying to crack down on what they call illegal outposts.\par \par \uc2 And as you have seen, the Israeli military has said that she was not the target of this bullet, that the -- what they called "the instigator" of the riot was the actual target and it was a ricochet. What`s your response to that Israeli military statement?\par \par \uc2 KELSIE NABASS: I think what`s important here today is that anyone should have the right to peacefully protest and peacefully observe a demonstration from an illegal settlement.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that there was any right to have any live ammunition at that type of -- at that type of place. As we know, they were peacefully having their Friday prayer and peacefully demonstrating against the occupation of farmland in the region.\par \par \uc2 And now they`re saying that it was an accident, which is completely deplorable for my best friend.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Juliette Majid, what is your message? What is the family`s message as well to the Biden administration today?\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE MAJID: Yes, so I -- for me personally, I think that the Biden administration`s statement today was incredibly shameful.\par \par \uc2 To release comment about and support the evidence that the Israeli military gave without conducting an independent U.S. investigation into the killing of an American citizen is very shameful. And I don`t accept that. I don`t accept that we will accept the Israeli military`s evidence or proof.\par \par \uc2 What I want to see, what the family wants to see is this independent us investigation. Aysenur was an American citizen. She was an incredible person and she deserves justice. Her family deserves justice. And they won`t -- they won`t get that without an investigation and without the evidence that they are requiring.\par \par \uc2 NICK SCHIFRIN: Kelsie Nabass, Juliette Majid, thank you very much to you both.\par \par \uc2 JULIETTE MAJID: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELSIE NABASS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Half of all American adults say they sometimes get their news from social media. At the same time, almost two-thirds say they view social media as a bad thing for democracy, which raises the question, what responsibility do social media companies bear for our divided political climate?\par \par \uc2 Judy Woodruff explores that question as part of her ongoing series America at a Crossroads.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH, Former Extremist: Social media was my sad little life. I was a far right radical crazy young person who was a jerk. My personality was not pleasant.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Less than a decade ago, Katie McHugh was a prominent figure in the online world of far right extremism. She was a writer and editor at the deeply conservative publication Breitbart, where she was especially known for her vitriolic tweets.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: Whenever I was saying the really vile racist things that went viral, you find other right-wing people on social media, on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and you -- if you follow each other, you boost each other, and then the network just expands.\par \par \uc2 And you say outlandish things to push the conversation that way. So you have this -- the real-time interaction, real-time radicalization.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: McHugh has since rejected the far right. Today, she worries about her address being revealed, which is why we agreed to interview her in this hotel room.\par \par \uc2 Some of her viral tweets are still well-known.\par \par \uc2 I`m quoting: "Funny how Europeans assimilated, unlike Third Worlders, demanding welfare while raping, killing Americans."\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: Yes, that`s what we believed. They`re taking our money, our taxes, people of color will rape the white women.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: You wrote: "It`s important to keep families together. We must deport anchor babies along with their illegal alien parents."\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: Yes, the dehumanizing language I was using, they are alien, and they are dangerous, and they are existential threat. That`s what you believe on the right.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And that`s what you believed at the time?\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: That`s what I believed at the time, yes, strongly.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: And then the day came when you tweeted this: "There would be no deadly terror attacks in the U.K. if Muslims didn`t live there."\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: Yes, and that was the tweet that got me fired, and that was one of the best things that ever happened to me, to get me out of that environment.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL, Polarization Lab, \uc2 Duke University\uc2 : Most of the things we try to do to discourage this tiny group of people who ruined the Internet have very limited impact.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Chris Bail is the founder of Duke University`s Polarization Lab. His research focuses on how social media can be a driver of and a solution to political divisions. He says posts like McHugh`s get outsized attention.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: When we look at people who are highly politically active on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , we find that about 70 percent of the content about politics is generated by just 6 percent of people, and those 6 percent of people are disproportionately very liberal or very conservative.\par \par \uc2 And so when we wander on to social media, we can wrongly conclude that everyone is extreme, and everyone is sort of out to get everyone else.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Contrary to popular belief, Bail says, the problem is not so-called echo chambers, online bubbles where people only have their views reinforced.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: We recruited a large group of Republicans and Democrats who were using \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and we thought, hey, if we could just show them some messages from the other side, that surely they would come to realize that there are two sides to every story.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, what we found at that time was that exposing people to the other side made them a little more polarized, not less.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Which is fascinating, because that`s been the common assumption.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: Think about the last time that you saw a message from people you don`t agree with. Did it produce a kind of calm, rational deliberation about whether the idea had merit, or did it make you mad?\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Bail says the incentive structure on social media platforms leads to more extreme content rising to the top, as algorithms promote what gets high engagement, reactions, comments, and shares.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: We have made it all but impossible for people to gain status for sharing and voicing the moderate views that many of us think our country needs right now.\par \par \uc2 TAYLEN HARP, Mental Health Counselor: I have seen one too many posts talking negatively, namely about Black women. So I`m like, OK, this is when we turn this off.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Taylen Harp is a mental health counselor in Raleigh, North Carolina, with expertise in serving people of color and members of the LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 She struggles with what she sees online.\par \par \uc2 TAYLEN HARP: If there is a lot of negative comments being made about Black women, if there`s a lot of negative commentary being made about the LGBTQ community, it`s one of those things where I`m disconnecting, I`m blocking those pages so that I am not constantly being fed with that.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: But she says social media has also helped her connect with her community.\par \par \uc2 TAYLEN HARP: Anything having to do with Black, indigenous people of color and also LGBTQ rights, different things happening within the LGBT community, that`s really where my focus comes to on social media.\par \par \uc2 MATT PERAULT\uc2 , University of North Carolina: I do remain an optimist that, on balance, the technology, the developments that we will see will make our lives better.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: \uc2 Matt Perault\uc2  was the head of global policy development at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  until 2019. He`s now the director of the University of North Carolina`s Center on Technology Policy, which receives funding from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s parent company, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , among others.\par \par \uc2 MATT PERAULT\uc2 : I was never in a meeting where someone said, here`s something that we could do that`s good for the world, and here`s another approach that`s going to be good for our bottom line that`s going to make us money, and people said, let`s just do the money approach and not the good for the world approach.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Is there an inherent conflict here in -- you have -- these are for-profit companies that want to grow audience, they want to increase engagement. And what their mission is not always going to be consistent with promoting accurate information.\par \par \uc2 MATT PERAULT\uc2 : I agree with what you`re hinting at in your question that isn`t necessarily aligned with the public good. It might not be good for the world if it`s a more connected world.\par \par \uc2 That, I think, is a good rationale for smart regulation of the tech industry where there are those kinds of market failures that produce harms.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: I asked him about Meta`s decision to reinstate former President Trump after he had been banned for praising people involved in the January 6, 2021, attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 MATT PERAULT\uc2 : It is not clear to me that censorship results in stronger outcomes down the road. President Trump didn`t disappear just because his content wasn`t hosted on \uc2 Meta\uc2 . He started his own social network. He has lots of followers on that social network and he posts there regularly.\par \par \uc2 BECKY LEW-HOBBS, Moms For \uc2 Liberty\uc2 : There`s the truth and there`s your opinion. And so people have muddied that.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Becky Lew-Hobbs chairs the Wake County, North Carolina, chapter of the conservative advocacy group Moms For \uc2 Liberty\uc2 . She says she`s gotten hateful messages online after her group called for removing school library books that include descriptions of sex or discussions of gender identity.\par \par \uc2 BECKY LEW-HOBBS: There`s a difference between not liking a statement I make and making somebody angry versus somebody wishing harm, death on you, a family member.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: I mean, is it your sense that the people in charge of content with these different social media platforms should do a better job of policing themselves, monitoring themselves?\par \par \uc2 BECKY LEW-HOBBS: It gets really slippery when some bot or programmer tries to determine without true thought. What is offensive and what is not. That`s not a free platform.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: At the end of the day, these are businesses, these are corporations. And even though many of us don`t like the fact that corporations are now in charge of democracy`s public square, it`s de facto what has happened.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Bail`s Polarization Lab has begun to develop tools they think could reduce conflict online, including an A.I.-powered assistant.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: Last year, \uc2 Nextdoor\uc2 , the social media platform that allows neighbors to connect to each other, came to us and they said, we have got a lot of toxic language going on. How can we identify solutions that are both good for society and profitable?\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: Bail`s team helped \uc2 Nextdoor\uc2  employ A.I. to suggest less divisive language to its users.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: And this resulted in a 15 percent decrease in the use of toxic language on their platform. So these are real solutions that are low- hanging fruit that I think could be implemented with minimal cost to social media companies.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: So you`re saying these are things that companies would have to be persuaded would, frankly, raise revenue, right?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: Yes, I do think they -- of course, they care about revenue. They`re beholden to shareholders. Much as I might not like that and many people might not like that, that is the reality.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: These social media platforms, they`re disincentivized from cutting down on right-wing rhetoric, because again, it`s a moneymaker. It`s a huge moneymaker. Racism is very profitable.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: To help her work through her own painful experience, Katie McHugh reached out to counselors who help individuals leave hate groups and move beyond extremism.\par \par \uc2 KATIE MCHUGH: I was very lucky and blessed to have people who I trusted completely helped me extricate myself from everything. And I described it as pulling shrapnel out of your brain. What I believed was vile, and I didn`t want to be that person anymore.\par \par \uc2 Having that community in place stops and defuses so much of the hatred, because a lot of online isolation will cause that.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BAIL: There are a lot of angry people in our country, and one way to understand what social media does is, it gives those people more of a platform.\par \par \uc2 And I worry that if we point all the blame at social media and don`t do a little bit of introspection, that we will be unhappy with the result.\par \par \uc2 JUDY WOODRUFF: For the "PBS News Hour," I`m Judy Woodruff in Durham, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: And that`s the "News Hour" for tonight. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the entire "News Hour" team," thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 CQ-Roll Call, Inc., which  takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the  user`s personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be  printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any  fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and CQ-Roll Call,  Inc.`s copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material.  This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 Document NSHR000020240911ek9b0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 PBS News Hour for September 10, 2024\b0\par\par\uc2 Lisa Desjardins, Laura Barron-Lopez, Geoff Bennett, Amna Nawaz \par \uc2 21,509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 September 2024\par \uc2 PBS: PBS NewsHour\par \uc2 NSHR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2024 MacNeil/Lehrer Productions. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2024 ASC Services II Media, LLC, which takes sole responsibility for the accuracy of the transcription. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. No license is granted to the user of this material except for the user's personal or internal use and, in such case, only one copy may be printed, nor shall user use any material for commercial purposes or in any fashion that may infringe upon MacNeil/Lehrer Productions and ASC Services II Media, LLC's copyrights or other proprietary rights or interests in the material. This is not a legal transcript for purposes of litigation. \par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: Welcome to PBS News` special live coverage of the ABC News presidential debate in Philadelphia. I`m Geoff Bennett.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ: And I`m Amna Nawaz.\par \par \uc2 Tonight is a high-pressure moment for both Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Polls show that the race is a coin flip, and a good performance could provide momentum for either candidate as they enter the final sprint to Election Day now just 55 days away.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT: This is the first and perhaps only time we will see these two candidates share a stage before the election, and the debate begins right now.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: ... and Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, "World News Tonight" Anchor and Managing Editor: Good evening. I`m David Muir, and thank you for joining us for tonight`s ABC News presidential debate. We want to welcome viewers watching on ABC and around the world tonight.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are just moments away from taking the stage in this unprecedented race for president.\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC News Live "Prime" Anchor: And I`m Linsey Davis.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s meeting could be the most consequential event of their campaigns, with Election Day now less than two months away.\par \par \uc2 For Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , this is her first debate since President Biden withdrew from the race on July 21. Of course, that decision followed his debate against President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in June. Since then, this race has taken on an entirely new dynamic.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: And that brings us to the rules of tonight`s debate, 90 minutes with two commercial breaks. No topics or questions have been shared with the campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The candidates will have two minutes to answer questions. And this is the clock. That`s what they will be seeing, two minutes for rebuttals and one minute for follow-ups, clarifications or responses.\par \par \uc2 Their microphones will only be turned on when it`s their turn to speak, no prewritten notes allowed. There is no audience here tonight in this hall at the \uc2 National Constitution Center\uc2 . This is an intimate setting for two candidates who have never met.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: President Trump won the coin toss. He chose to deliver the final closing statement of the evening.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris selected the podium to the right.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: So let`s now welcome the candidates to the stage, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , Vice President of the United States (D) and U.S. Presidential Candidate: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Let`s have a good debate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , Former President of the United States (R) and Current U.S. Presidential Candidate: Nice to see you. Have fun.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: Welcome to you both. It`s wonderful to have you. It`s an honor to have you both here tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Good evening. We are looking forward to a spirited and thoughtful debate.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: So let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin tonight with the issue that voters repeatedly say is their number one issue. And that is the economy and the cost of living in this country.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris, you and President Trump were elected four years ago, and your opponent on the stage here tonight often asks his supporters, are you better off than you were four years ago? When it comes to the economy, do you believe Americans are better off than they were four years ago?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: So, I was raised as a middle-class kid. And I am actually the only person on this stage who has a plan that is about lifting up the middle class and working people of America.\par \par \uc2 I believe in the ambition, the aspirations, the dreams of the American people. And that is why I imagine and have actually a plan to build what I call an opportunity economy, because here`s the thing. We know that we have a shortage of homes and housing. And the cost of housing is too expensive for far too many people.\par \par \uc2 We know that young families need support to raise their children. And I intend on extending a tax cut for those families of $6,000, which is the largest child tax credit that we have given in a long time, so that those young families can afford to buy a crib, buy a car seat, buy clothes for their children.\par \par \uc2 My passion, one of them, is small businesses. I was actually -- my mother raised my sister and me, but there was a woman who helped raise us. We call her our second mother. She was a small business owner. I love our small businesses.\par \par \uc2 My plan is to give a $50,000 tax deduction to start-up small businesses, knowing they are part of the backbone of America`s economy.\par \par \uc2 My opponent, on the other hand, his plan is to do what he has done before, which is to provide a tax cut for billionaires and big corporations, which will result in $5 trillion to America`s deficit.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: My opponent has a plan that I call the Trump sales tax, which would be a 20 percent tax on everyday goods that you rely on to get through the month.\par \par \uc2 Economists have said that that Trump sales tax would actually result for middle-class families in about $4,000 more a year because of his policies and his ideas about what should be the backs of middle-class people paying for tax cuts for billionaires.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, ABC NEWS MODERATOR: President Trump, I`ll give you two minutes.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: First of all, I have no sales tax. That`s an incorrect statement. She knows that. We`re doing tariffs on other countries. Other countries are going to finally, after 75 years, pay us back for all that we`ve done for the world. And the tariff will be substantial in some cases.\par \par \uc2 I took in billions and billions of dollars, as you know, from China. In fact, they never took the tariff off because it was so much money they can`t. It would totally destroy everything that they`ve set out to do. They`ve taken in billions of dollars from China and other places. They`ve left the tariffs on. When I had it, I had tariffs and yet I had no inflation.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`ve had a terrible economy because inflation has -- which is really known as a country buster. It breaks up countries. We have inflation like very few people have ever seen before. Probably the worst in our nation`s history. We were at 21 percent. But that`s being generous because many things are 50 percent, 60 percent, 70 percent, and 80 percent higher than they were just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 This has been a disaster for people, for the middle class but for every class. On top of that we have millions of people pouring into our country from prisons and jails, from mental institutions and insane asylums. And they`re coming in and they`re taking jobs that are occupied right now by African-Americans and Hispanics and also unions. Unions are going to be affected very soon. And you see what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 You see what`s happening with towns throughout the United States. You look at Springfield, Ohio. You look at \uc2 Aurora\uc2  in Colorado. They are taking over the towns. They`re taking over buildings. They`re going in violently. These are the people that she and Biden let into our country. And they`re destroying our country. They`re dangerous. They`re at the highest level of criminality. And we have to get them out. We have to get them out fast.\par \par \uc2 I created one of the greatest economies in the history of our country. I`ll do it again and even better.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: We are going to get to immigration and border security during this debate. But I would like to let Vice President Harris respond on the economy here.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I would love to. Let`s talk about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst unemployment since the Great Depression. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst public health epidemic in a century. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left us the worst attack on our democracy since the Civil War. And what we have done is clean up Donald Trump`s mess. What we have done and what I intend to do is build on what we know are the aspirations and the hopes of the American people.\par \par \uc2 But I`m going to tell you all in this debate tonight you`re going to hear from the same old tired playbook, a bunch of lies, grievances and name- calling. What you`re going to hear tonight is a detailed and dangerous plan called Project 2025 that the former president intends on implementing if he were elected again.\par \par \uc2 I believe very strongly that the American people want a president who understands the importance of bringing us together knowing we have so much more in common than what separates us. And I pledge to you to be a president for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: President Trump, I`ll give you a minute here to respond.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Number one, I have nothing to do, as you know and as she knows better than anyone, I have nothing to do with Project 2025. That`s out there. I haven`t read it. I don`t want to read it purposely. I`m not going to read it. This was a group of people that got together, they came up with some ideas. I guess some good, some bad. But it makes no difference. I have nothing to do.\par \par \uc2 Everybody knows I`m an open book. Everybody knows what I`m going to do. Cut taxes very substantially and create a great economy like I did before. We had the greatest economy. We got hit with a pandemic. And the pandemic was not since 1917 where 100 million people died has there been anything like it. We did a phenomenal job with the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 We handed them over a country where the economy and where the stock market was higher than it was before the pandemic came in. Nobody has ever seen anything like it. We made ventilators for the entire world. We got gowns. We got masks. We did things that nobody thought possible. And people give me credit for rebuilding the military. They give me credit for a lot of things. But not enough credit for the great job we did with the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But the only jobs they got were bounce-back jobs. These were jobs, bounce- back. And it bounced back and it went to their benefit. But I was the one that created them. They know it and so does everybody else.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: Vice President Harris, I`ll let you respond.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no plan for you. And when you look at his economic plan, it`s all about tax breaks for the richest people. I am offering what I`ve described as an opportunity economy.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And the best economists in our country, if not the world, have reviewed our relative plans for the future of America. What \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  has said is that Donald Trump`s plan would make the economy worse. Mine would strengthen the economy.\par \par \uc2 What the \uc2 Wharton School\uc2  has said is Donald Trump`s plan would actually explode the deficit. Sixteen Nobel laureates have described his economic plan as something that would increase inflation and by the middle of next year would invite a recession.\par \par \uc2 You just have to look at where we are and where we stand on the issues. And I`d invite you to know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually has no plan for you because he is more interested in defending himself than he is in looking out for you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : That`s just a sound bite. They gave her that to say. Look, I went to the Wharton School of Finance and many of those professors, the top professors, think my plan is a brilliant plan. It`s a great plan. It`s a plan that`s going to bring up our worth, our value as a country. It`s going to make people want to be able to go and work and create jobs and create a lot of good, solid money for our company -- for our country.\par \par \uc2 And just to finish off, she doesn`t have a plan. She copied Biden`s plan and it`s like four sentences, like run, spot, run, four sentences that are just, oh, we`ll try and lower taxes. She doesn`t have a plan. Take a look at her plan. She doesn`t have a plan.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: Mr. President, I do want to drill down on something you both brought up. The Vice President brought up your tariffs. You responded. And let`s drill down on this because your plan is what she calls is essentially a national sales tax. Your proposal calls for tariffs, as you pointed out here, on foreign imports across the board. You recently said that you might double your plan, imposing tariffs up to 20% on goods coming into this country.\par \par \uc2 As you know, many economists say that with tariffs at that level, costs are then passed on to the consumer. Vice President Harris has argued it`ll mean higher prices on gas, food, clothing, medication, arguing it costs the typical family nearly $4,000 a year. Do you believe Americans can afford higher prices because of tariffs?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They`re not going to have higher prices. What`s going to have and who`s going to have higher prices is China and all of the countries that have been ripping us off for years. I charge -- I was the only president ever. China was paying us hundreds of billions of dollars. And so were other countries. And, you know, if she doesn`t like them, they should have gone out and they should have immediately cut the tariffs. But those tariffs are there three and a half years now under their administration.\par \par \uc2 We are going to take in billions of dollars, hundreds of billions of dollars. I had no inflation, virtually no inflation. They had the highest inflation, perhaps in the history of our country, because I`ve never seen a worse period of time. People can`t go out and buy cereal or bacon or eggs or anything else. These -- the people of our country are absolutely dying with what they`ve done. They`ve destroyed the economy.\par \par \uc2 And all you have to do is look at a poll. The polls say 80 and 85 and even 90% that the Trump economy was great, that their economy was terrible.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: Vice President Harris, I do want to ask for your response. And you heard what the president said there because the Biden administration did keep a number of the Trump tariffs in place. So how do you respond?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, let`s be clear that the Trump administration resulted in a trade deficit, one of the highest we`ve ever seen in the history of America. He invited trade wars. You want to talk about his deal with China. What he ended up doing is under Donald Trump`s presidency, he ended up selling American chips to China to help them improve and modernize their military, basically sold us out when a policy about China should be in making sure the United States of America wins the competition for the 21st century, which means focusing on the details of what that requires, focusing on relationships with our allies, focusing on investing in American based technology so that we win the race on A.I. on quantum computing, focusing on what we need to do to support America`s work force so that we don`t end up having -- on the short end of the stick in terms of workers` rights.\par \par \uc2 But what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did -- let`s talk about this with COVID, is he actually thanked President Xi for what he did during COVID. Look at his tweet. Thank you, President Xi. Exclamation point. When we know that Xi was responsible for lacking and not giving us transparency about the origins of COVID.\par \par \uc2 MUIR: President Trump, I`ll let you respond.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: First of all, they bought their chips from Taiwan. We hardly make chips anymore because of philosophies like they have and policies like they have. I don`t say her because she has no policy. Everything that she believed three years ago and four years ago is out the window. She`s going to my philosophy now.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I was going to send her a MAGA hat. She`s gone to my philosophy. But if she ever got elected, she`d change it, and it will be the end of our country.\par \par \uc2 She`s a Marxist. Everybody knows she`s a Marxist. Her father`s a Marxist professor in economics, and he taught her well. But when you look at what she`s done to our country, and when you look at these millions and millions of people that are pouring into our country monthly, where it`s, I believe, 21 million people, not the 15 that people say, and I think it`s a lot higher than the 21, that`s bigger than New York state pouring in.\par \par \uc2 And just look at what they`re doing to our country. They`re criminals. Many of these people coming in are criminals. And that`s bad for our economy, too. You know, you mentioned before, we`ll talk about immigration later. Well, bad immigration is the worst thing that can happen to our economy. They have and she has destroyed our country with policy that`s insane, almost policy that you`d say they have to hate our country.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR, ABC NEWS REPORTER: President Trump, thank you. Linsey?\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC NEWS REPORTER: I want to turn to the issue of abortion. President Trump, you`ve often touted that you were able to kill Roe v. Wade last year. You said that you were proud to be the most pro life president in American history. Then last month, you said that your administration would be great for women and their reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 In your home state of Florida, you surprised many with regard to your six week abortion ban because you initially had said that it was too short. And you said, quote, I`m going to be voting that we need more than six weeks. But then the very next day, you reversed course and said you would vote to support the six week ban.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris says that women shouldn`t trust you on the issue of abortion because you`ve changed your position so many times. Therefore, why should they trust you?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, the reason I`m doing that vote is because the plan is, as you know, the vote is they have abortion in the ninth month. They even have, and you can look at the Governor of West Virginia, the previous Governor of West Virginia, not the current Governor, who`s doing an excellent job, but the Governor before, he said, the baby will be born and we will decide what to do with the baby. In other words, we`ll execute the baby.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I did that, because that predominates, because they`re radical. The Democrats are radical in that. And her Vice Presidential pick, which I think is a horrible pick, by the way, for our country, because he is really out of it. But her Vice Presidential pick says abortion in the ninth month is absolutely fine. He also says execution after birth. It`s execution.\par \par \uc2 No longer abortion because the baby is born is OK. And that`s not OK with me. Hence the vote. But what I did is something, for 52 years, they`ve been trying to get Roe v. Wade into the states. And through the genius and heart and strength of six Supreme Court Justices, we were able to do that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I believe in the exceptions for rape, incest, and life of the mother. I believe strongly in it. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  did also. 85% of Republicans do. Exceptions, very important. But we were able to get it. And now, states are voting on it. And for the first time, you`re going to see, look, this is an issue that`s torn our country apart for 52 years.\par \par \uc2 Every legal scholar, every Democrat, every Republican, liberal, conservative, they all wanted this issue to be brought back to the states where the people could vote. And that`s what happened. Now, Ohio, the vote was somewhat liberal. Kansas, the vote was somewhat liberal, much more liberal than people would have thought.\par \par \uc2 But each individual state is voting. It`s the vote of the people now. It`s not tied up in the federal government. I did a great service in doing it. It took courage to do it, and the Supreme Court had great courage in doing it. And I give tremendous credit to those six justices.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: There is no state in this country where it`s legal to kill a baby after it`s born. Madam Vice President, I want to get your response to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES AND 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, as I said, you`re going to hear a bunch of lies, and that`s not actually a surprising fact. Let`s understand how we got here. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hand selected three members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  with the intention that they would undo the protections of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And they did exactly as he intended. And now, in over 20 states, there are Trump abortion bans, which make it criminal for a doctor or nurse to provide health care. In one state, it provides prison for life. Trump abortion bans that make no exception, even for rape and incest, which understand what that means. A survivor of a crime of violation to their body does not have the right to make a decision about what happens to their body next.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That is immoral. And one does not have to abandon their faith or deeply held beliefs to agree the government and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  certainly should not be telling a woman what to do with her body. I have talked with women around our country. You want to talk about this is what people wanted, pregnant women who want to carry a pregnancy to term suffering from a miscarriage being denied care in an emergency room because the health care providers are afraid they might go to jail and she`s bleeding out in a car in the parking lot? She didn`t want that. Her husband didn`t want that.\par \par \uc2 A 12 or 13-year-old survivor of incest being forced to carry a pregnancy to term. They don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 And I pledge to you, when Congress passes a bill to put back in place the protections of Roe v. Wade, as president of the United States, I will proudly sign it into law.\par \par \uc2 But understand, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to be re-elected, he will sign a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Understand in his Project 2025, there would be a national abortion -- a monitor that would be monitoring your pregnancies, your miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 I think the American people believe that certain freedoms, in particular the freedom to make decisions about one`s own body, should not be made by the government.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you, Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, there she goes again. It`s a lie. I`m not signing a ban. And there`s no reason to sign a ban because we`ve gotten what everybody wanted.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, Republicans and everybody else, at every legal scholar, wanted it to be brought back into the States. And the States are voting. And it may take a little time.\par \par \uc2 But for 52 years, this issue has torn our country apart. And they`ve wanted it back in the States. And I did something that nobody thought was possible. The States are now voting.\par \par \uc2 What she says is an absolute lie. And as far as the abortion ban, no, I`m not in favor of abortion ban, but it doesn`t matter, because this issue has now been taken over by the states.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Would you veto a national abortion ban if you could?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, I won`t have to because again, two things. Number one, she said she`ll go back to Congress. She`ll never get the vote. It`s impossible for her to get the vote, especially now with the 50/50 and essentially 50/50 in both Senate and the House. She`s not going to get the vote. She can`t get the vote. She won`t even come close to it. So it`s just talk.\par \par \uc2 You know what it reminds me of when they said